ATSWA.put("PS", {"intro":"Public Sector Accounting is the most legalistic paper in Part II. Almost every answer rests on a statute, a regulation or an IPSAS, and the examiners reward candidates who can cite the authority as well as describe the practice. The chapters below therefore name the section of the Constitution, the Act or the Standard wherever one governs — and set out the statutory financial statements in the exact form in which they must be presented.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Nature and Scope of Public Sector Accounting","brief":"What the public sector is, the six-stage process public sector accounting actually runs through, why it exists (its real objectives, not just \"accountability\" as a slogan), who reads it, the five bases it can be done on, and the alphabet soup of international and Nigerian bodies that set its rules — including the FRCN, whose full establishing Act this study text quotes almost in its entirety.","outcomes":["Define and explain terminology in public sector accounting","Identify the objectives of public sector accounting","Understand and appreciate the users of public sector accounting information","Identify the concepts and principles applicable to public sector accounting","Identify the five bases of public sector accounting","Distinguish a non-profit-oriented organisation from a profit-oriented one","Explain the objectives and details of the professional pronouncements on public sector accounting, including the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria"],"secs":[{"n":"1.1","t":"What \"public sector\" means, and the six things public sector accounting actually does","b":[{"p":"The simplest definition of **\"public sector\"** is \"all organisations which are not privately owned and operated, but which are established, run and financed by government on behalf of the public.\" This definition conveys the idea that the public sector consists of organisations where control lies in the hands of the public, as opposed to private owners, and whose objectives involve the provision of services, where profit-making is not a primary objective. Performance measurement in the public sector is made harder by three things at once: the lack of a profit motive, the presence of multiple objectives pursued simultaneously, and the presence of **intangible services**, whose benefits are difficult to quantify — you can price a bag of cement; you cannot straightforwardly price \"a safer neighbourhood.\""},{"def":{"t":"Public sector accounting","d":"a process of recording, communicating, summarising, analysing and interpreting government financial statements and statistics in aggregate and in detail; it is concerned with the receipt, custody, disbursement and rendering of stewardship on public funds entrusted."}},{"p":"That definition is worth learning in full — examiners frequently ask for it close to verbatim, and \"receipt, custody, disbursement and rendering of stewardship\" is where the marks sit. But the definition alone is compressed; the study text unpacks it into **six stages**, and it is worth understanding each one rather than only reciting the label, because a question can just as easily ask \"what does recording involve?\" as it can ask for the definition itself."},{"ol":["**Recording** — documenting the financial transactions and activities in the necessary books of account, e.g. the cashbook, the ledger and the vote book.","**Analysing** — separating transactions according to their distinct nature, and posting them under the appropriate heads and sub-heads.","**Classifying** — grouping the transactions into revenue and expense descriptions, and bringing them under major classes as \"Revenue Head\" and \"Sub-heads\" with their relevant code numbers of accounts.","**Summarising** — bringing together all the classes of accounts and preparing them into reports periodically, as statutorily or organisationally required.","**Communicating** — making available financial reports on all the government financial activities, from the necessary accounting summaries, to various interested parties. The style of communication adopted should be unambiguous, lucid and, as far as possible, devoid of jargon.","**Interpreting** — the process that ends the chain, giving explanations on what has been reported in the various financial statements and reports, as regards the overall operations and performance of the relevant government organisation(s). This is to enable the necessary parties and users to take relevant decisions based on their assessments of the reports."]},{"key":"Notice the chain ends at **interpretation**, not at reporting — exactly the same lesson as Chapter 1 of Financial Accounting, where accounting only finishes once somebody has been told something useful. A government that publishes a report nobody can understand has completed five of these six stages and failed at the one that actually mattered."}]},{"n":"1.2","t":"Nature, objectives and characteristics","b":[{"h3":"Nature"},{"p":"The term **\"Public Sector\"** refers to all organisations which are created, administered and financed by Government, from the taxpayers' money, on behalf of the members of the public. Such establishments are referred to as the **\"three tiers\"** — Government, Companies, Parastatals and other public agencies created by the Nation's Constitution, Acts of Parliament and Bye-Laws."},{"h3":"The main objectives of public sector accounting"},{"p":"Five objectives, each worth understanding rather than memorising as a bare phrase:"},{"ol":["**Determining the legitimacy of transactions and their compliance with the statutes and accepted norms.** Public sector disbursements should accord with the provisions of the Appropriation Act and Financial Regulations. There should be due authorisation for all payments so as to avoid misappropriation.","**Providing evidence of stewardship.** The act of rendering stewardship is being able to account transparently and diligently for resources entrusted. Government and public sector officers are obliged to display due diligence and a sense of probity in the collection and disposal of public funds.","**Assisting planning and control.** The future is full of risks and uncertainties. Therefore, mapping out strategic plans prevents an organisation from \"drowning in the tides.\" Plans of action provide the focus of activities which are being pursued — the unforeseen circumstance is built into plans so as to prevent, or at least reduce, corporate failure. Public sector establishments should act in accordance with the **\"mandate theory\"** of governance. Control measures are adjuncts to skilful planning; they assist in avoiding unnecessary deviations from the pursuit of the original objectives set.","**Ensuring objective and timely reporting.** Users of public sector accounting information are anxious to bridge their knowledge gaps on government activities. They therefore treasure prompt and accurate statistics with which to evaluate government performance.","**Evaluating the costs incurred and the benefits derivable.** In public sector organisations, it is difficult to measure costs and benefits in financial terms. The analysis of cost-benefit assesses the economic and social advantages (benefits) and disadvantages or inconveniences (costs) of alternative courses of action, to ensure that the welfare of the citizens is well provided for."]},{"p":"Beyond these five, the study text lists a further set of **other objectives**:"},{"ol":["Enhancing the appraisal of the efficiency of management.","Highlighting the various sources of revenue receivable and expenditure incurred.","Identifying the sources of funding for capital projects.","Evaluating the economy, efficiency and effectiveness with which public sector organisations pursue their goals and objectives.","Ensuring that costs are matched by at least equivalent benefits accruing therefrom.","Providing the details of outstanding long-term commitments and financial obligations.","Providing the means by which actual performance may be compared with the set target.","Proffering solutions to the various bottlenecks and/or problems identified.","Providing the basis for decision-making."]},{"key":"The four italicised words in objective (v) above — **economy, efficiency and effectiveness** — are the \"three Es\" of value-for-money, and they resurface throughout this paper (and in Chapter 15 of Financial Accounting's interpretation work) as the standard framework for judging whether public money was well spent, not merely lawfully spent."},{"h3":"Characteristics of public sector accounting"},{"p":"Three characteristics distinguish public sector accounting from an ordinary business's accounting:"},{"ol":["There are **distinct aspects of accounting information, classification and procedures**, which apply only to transactions made by the government. Examples are the budgeting system and applicable procedures, fiscal policy, accounting methods and sources of revenue. The peculiar nature of government transactions makes it desirable — and indeed mandatory — to treat them in accordance with specific, but cohesive and standardised, measurement approaches and rules.","In view of the requirement to obtain **legislative approval** for government revenue and expenditure, budgeting largely determines the structure of Public Sector Accounting. The government sometimes finds it necessary to segregate its resources into specific or special-purpose compartments — a set-up of receipts and expenditure known as **\"Funds.\"** The method of accounting adopted in recording and measuring the Funds is referred to as **\"fund accounting.\"**","Another peculiarity of government operations is that the accounting system is traditionally maintained on a **\"cash basis.\"** Only transactions involving the movement of cash come into reckoning. Although the approach has its inadequacies, the general practice has been to adopt the \"cash-basis\" of accounting. All assets are written off as they are regarded as consumed at the point they are paid for. Accordingly, government's statement of assets and liabilities does not contain information on tangible assets, and neither is depreciation charged in the revenue and expenditure accounts."]},{"note":"This is your first sight of \"fund accounting\" and of the Consolidated Revenue Fund family of concepts — the study text only names them here in passing, as an example of characteristic (ii). The full treatment of the Constitution's specific funds — the Consolidated Revenue Fund, the Development Fund, the Contingencies Fund and the Federation Account — belongs to Chapter 2, on the constitutional and regulatory framework. Because this chapter's own quiz already draws on that material as essential context, a compact version is given at the end of this chapter (§1.8) so you are not left without it until Chapter 2 is reached."}]},{"n":"1.3","t":"Users of public sector accounting information","b":[{"p":"There are two groups of users of Public Sector Accounting information: **\"Internal\"** and **\"External\"** users, whose peculiarities and areas of interest are as follows."},{"h3":"Internal users and their interest areas"},{"ol":["**The Labour Union** in the public service, which will press for improved conditions of employment and security of tenure for its members;","**Members of the Executive Arm of Government**, such as the President, Ministers and Governors, Commissioners and Chairmen of Local Governments. Their interest areas are to ensure probity and accountability through record-keeping and performance control, which are achieved through accounting information; and","**Top management members**, such as Permanent Secretaries of various Ministries and Chief Executives of Parastatals. They are the conduit of accounting information generation and transmission, and serve as liaison officers between Government, employees and the public."]},{"h3":"External users and their areas of interest"},{"ol":["**Members of the Legislature**, at National, State and Local Government levels. Information in the accounts of Governments is the major medium through which politicians render stewardship to their constituencies and apprise them of the endeavours of governance;","**Members of the public**, to demonstrate accountability and assist the people to appreciate — or otherwise — the efforts of Governments;","**Researchers and financial journalists.** Researchers are expected to develop new and better ideas of governance; financial journalists cherish accounting information to advise existing and potential investors;","**Financial institutions**, such as Commercial Banks, the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF). Accounting information assists them to evaluate the credit rating of a borrowing Nation;","**Governments, apart from the one reporting.** Governments collaborate on ideas of investment and research; they require accounting information on the well-being, or otherwise, of each other;","**Suppliers and contractors**, who are eager to ascertain the ability of a government to pay for goods and services delivered — only accounting information can be revealing on this point;","**Political Parties, Trade Unions and Civil Liberty Organisations**; and","**Foreign countries and foreign financial institutions**, such as the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank. Like other external users, they require accounting information to ascertain the financial viability of the public sector organisations and the efficiency and effectiveness of management — and to know whether the accounting information enhances the quality, consistency and transparency of public sector financial reporting."]},{"note":"A question asking for \"users and their information needs\" wants both halves of each pair — the user, and specifically what that user is looking for. Listing users without saying what each wants typically scores only about half the available marks. Notice also that \"financial institutions\" and \"foreign financial institutions\" appear as two separate, if overlapping, entries in the source's own list (iv) and (viii) — the second emphasises the *foreign* and *comparative* angle (comparing this government's standing against others') rather than simply repeating the first."}]},{"n":"1.4","t":"Concepts and principles applicable to public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"**Concepts** have been defined as broad basic assumptions underlying the preparation of financial statements of an enterprise. Public sector accounting is an integral but separate branch of financial accounting, sharing in common many concepts and principles applicable in the private sector. The study text names six such concepts — materiality, periodicity, duality, entity, historical cost and going concern — without itself re-defining them, on the assumption you already met them in Financial Accounting Chapter 1. Since they are examinable here too, and it costs nothing to be certain, each is restated below in the specifically public-sector reading:"},{"ul":["**Materiality** — information is material if its omission or misstatement could influence the decisions users take on the basis of the financial statements. In a public sector context, materiality is judged against the size of the vote or the fund concerned, not against the size of government as a whole — a ₦50 million discrepancy is immaterial to the Federation Account, but may be highly material to a single Local Government's budget.","**Periodicity** — the endless life of government is divided into reporting periods (financial years) so that performance and position can be reported at regular intervals, rather than only once, at the end of time. Nigeria's public sector financial year runs from 1 January to 31 December.","**Duality** — every transaction has two effects, exactly as in double-entry bookkeeping. This underlies the double-entry cash books and ledgers kept in government accounting units, even where the reporting basis itself is cash rather than accrual.","**Entity** — the government unit or agency is accounted for as a unit separate and distinct from the individuals — ministers, civil servants, contractors — who transact with it. A Permanent Secretary's personal bank account is never part of the Ministry's accounts, for exactly the same reason a sole trader's personal spending is never part of the business's accounts.","**Historical cost** — assets and transactions are recorded at the amount of cash, or cash equivalent, actually paid or received. Under a cash-basis system this concept does unusually heavy lifting, since — as characteristic (iii) in §1.2 above already noted — no later remeasurement (depreciation, revaluation, impairment) is applied at all: the historical cost figure is also, in effect, the only figure that ever exists for the asset.","**Going concern** — the reporting government entity is assumed to continue operating for the foreseeable future. Unlike a company, which can be wound up, a government is, for accounting purposes, presumed perpetual — this is part of why public sector accounting has historically felt less urgency about asset valuation than the private sector: there is no liquidation scenario to prepare the statements for."]},{"key":"Learn these by the same discipline used throughout Financial Accounting: you are far more often asked which concept a described situation illustrates or breaches than asked to define one cold. A ministry that fails to record a transaction because \"it was paid from a special account outside the normal ledger\" has breached the **entity** concept, not merely made a bookkeeping slip."}]},{"n":"1.5","t":"Bases of public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"The following are the bases under which the financial statements of a public sector enterprise are compiled:"},{"ol":["**Cash basis;**","**Accrual basis;**","**Commitment basis;**","**Modified cash basis; and**","**Modified accrual basis.**"]},{"h3":"Cash basis"},{"p":"It is the basis of accounting under which revenue is recorded only when cash is received, and expenditure recognised only when cash is paid, irrespective of the fact that the transactions might have occurred in the previous accounting period. This is a simple method of recording financial transactions in the public sector."},{"h4":"Advantages of the cash basis"},{"ol":["It is simple to understand;","It eliminates the existence of debtors and creditors;","It permits easy identification of those who authorise payments and collect revenue;","It allows for comparison between the amount provided in the budget and that actually spent;","It saves time and is easy to operate;","It permits the delegation of work in certain circumstances; and","The cost of tangible assets is written off in the year of purchase, resulting in fewer accounting entries."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the cash basis"},{"p":"It takes an unrealistic view of financial transactions, as only the settlement of liabilities is recognised. There are, in fact, **five stages** through which a spending decision passes:"},{"ol":["Management decision to spend money;","Issue of order or contract for the supply of goods or services;","Supply of goods or services — acknowledgment of liability;","Settlement of the amount of the good or service received; and","Consumption of value."]},{"key":"The **cash basis** records only stage (v). The **accrual basis** takes care of stages (iii), (iv) and (v). The **commitment basis** records stages (ii) through (v). This one diagram is the fastest way to answer almost any exam question that asks you to compare two of these bases — work out which stage each one starts recording from."},{"ul":["It does not provide for depreciation, since assets are written off in the year of purchase.","It does not convey an accurate picture of the financial affairs at the end of the year.","The cash basis cannot be used for economic decisions, as it tends to hide basic information, such as information relating to tangible assets, debtors and creditors.","It does not accord with the **matching concept**."]},{"h3":"Modified cash basis"},{"p":"Under this basis, the books of accounts are left open for a maximum of three months after the end of the year, so as to capture a substantial amount of income or expenses relating to the year just ended."},{"h3":"Accrual basis"},{"p":"Under this basis, revenue is recorded when earned, and expenditure acknowledged as a liability when known or when the benefit is received — notwithstanding the fact that the receipts or payments of cash have taken place wholly or partly in other accounting periods. The accrual basis is practised in the private sector and in all parastatals, such as the Central Bank of Nigeria and the Federal Inland Revenue Service. The reason for this is that private-sector concerns are profit-oriented — it is therefore necessary to estimate how much profit has been earned in each period, with a view to keeping invested assets intact and making periodic distributions to shareholders by way of dividends. However, the main consideration in the public sector is the **improvement in the standard of living of the citizens.**"},{"h4":"Advantages of the accrual basis"},{"ol":["It takes a realistic view of financial transactions;","It reveals an accurate picture of the state of financial affairs at the end of the period;","It could be used for both economic and investment decision-making, as all parameters for performance appraisal are available;","It aligns with the \"matching concept\"; and","It makes allowance for the diminution in the value of assets used to generate the revenue of the enterprise."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the accrual basis"},{"ol":["It is very difficult to understand, especially by non-accountants.","It does not permit easy delegation of work in certain circumstances."]},{"h3":"Modified accrual basis"},{"p":"This is the basis under which revenue is recorded when received (not earned), while expenditure is recorded once its liability is incurred. It means that the cash basis is used for recording revenue while the accrual basis is used for expenditure."},{"p":"**Revenue** is recorded when cash is received, except for:"},{"ol":["Revenue which is susceptible to accrual; and","Revenue of a material amount which has not been received at the time it should have been."]},{"p":"**Expenditure** is recorded on the accrual basis, except in the cases of:"},{"ol":["Disbursements for inventory items, which may be considered as expenditure at the time the items are utilised; and","Interest on long-term debt, commonly accounted for in debt service funds and recorded as expenditure on its due date."]},{"h3":"Commitment basis"},{"p":"It is a basis that records **anticipated expenditure** evidenced by a contract or a purchase order. In public sector financing, budgetary and accounting systems are closely related to the commitment basis."},{"warn":"The study text's own list of \"advantages of the commitment basis\" (and the model answer it gives to the matching end-of-chapter question) simply **repeats, word for word, four of the five listed advantages of the accrual basis** above — \"it reveals an accurate picture...\", \"it could be used for economic and investment decision-making...\", \"it aligns with the matching concept,\" and \"it makes allowance for the diminution in the value of assets.\" This cannot be right as written: the commitment basis exists specifically to record **anticipated** expenditure at the point an order or contract is raised, for budgetary control purposes — it says nothing about matching revenue to expenses, and nothing at all about asset valuation or depreciation, which is precisely the sort of remeasurement a commitment-basis (order-stage) system has not yet reached. This is a copy-paste artefact in the source, not a real property of the commitment basis. Only the genuinely distinct advantages below are commitment-basis advantages; treat the other four as belonging to the accrual basis alone, above, and not as also true here."},{"h4":"The genuine advantages of the commitment basis (kept on a memorandum vote-book basis)"},{"ol":["A separate payment tabulation is available when required.","Adjustments occurring when actual expenditure has been obtained do not affect the final accounts.","It is an aid to financial control — a commitment is regarded as a charge which has been made on a budget provision, so it prevents a spending unit from over-committing an appropriation before the cash has even moved."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the commitment basis"},{"ol":["The system involves extra work: actual figures have to be substituted for the commitment provisions to finally determine the running balances under the sub-heads of expenditure.","There is a risk of over-expenditure under the commitment basis, in the expectation that government may finally release funds to settle the legal obligations.","At the year end, all commitments that are the subject of unfulfilled orders will have to be written back to reflect the exact picture of the transactions which took place during the year.","Balances which ought to have lapsed in the Vote Book at the end of the year may instead be spent by issuing local purchase orders, purely to exhaust the votes before they lapse."]}]},{"n":"1.6","t":"Non-profit-oriented organisations compared with profit-oriented organisations","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis","Non-profit oriented","Profit oriented"],"rows":[["Profit","The main objective is to provide adequate welfare to the people at reasonable costs.","The main objective is to maximise profit."],["Revenue","Revenue is derived from the public in the form of taxation, fines, fees, etc.","Obtain their income principally from sales of goods and services."],["Legal formation","They are created by Acts of the Legislature, i.e. National/State Assemblies.","Registered with the Corporate Affairs Commission."],["Budget","The public is more interested in the annual budget than the annual report.","The public is more interested in the annual reports than the annual budgets."],["Budgetary approach","Public entities are rigid on the budgetary approach.","Budgets are internal affairs of these organisations."],["Accounting","They strictly operate on International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS), cash or accrual. They operate substantially on fund accounting.","They strictly operate on International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS). The proprietary approach is adopted for their funding."],["Annual general meeting","There is no annual general meeting, but government can hold public briefing on specific issues.","There is an annual general meeting of the shareholders to discuss the performance of these organisations."],["Dissolution","On dissolution, any income raised or asset acquired cannot be distributed to, or benefit, members or directors or officers of the organisation, because all the proceeds must revert to the state.","Income realised on dissolution can be distributed for the benefit of members, directors or officers of the organisation."]]}},{"note":"This table is the study text's own way of drawing the public/private distinction — notice it uses the labels \"non-profit oriented\" and \"profit oriented\" rather than \"public sector\" and \"private sector\" outright, because a handful of public sector bodies (Government Business Enterprises, met later in the syllabus) are run on proprietary, profit-oriented lines despite being government-owned. The table's **dissolution** row is the one examiners most often lift into a short-answer question on its own."}]},{"n":"1.7","t":"Professional pronouncements on public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"The **International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC)**, formed in 1973, was the first international standards-setting body. In 2001, IASC was re-organised to become the **International Accounting Standards Board (IASB)** — an independent international standard-setter. Professional pronouncements on public sector accounting have three objectives:"},{"ol":["To develop and harmonise public sector financial reporting, accounting and auditing practices;","To put into practice the same accounting standards throughout the world, in order to make comparisons possible and meaningful;","To make guidelines available for practitioners, in order to maintain high reporting standards."]},{"h3":"International Federation of Accountants (IFAC)"},{"p":"IFAC is the global organisation for the accountancy profession, dedicated to serving the public interest by strengthening the profession and contributing to the development of strong international economies. Founded in **1977**, IFAC comprises 173 members and associates in 129 countries and jurisdictions, including Nigeria. The mission of IFAC is to serve the public interest by:"},{"ol":["Contributing to the development, adoption and implementation of high-quality international standards and guidance;","Contributing to the development of strong professional accountants;","Promoting the value of professional accountants worldwide; and","Speaking out on accounting public interest issues."]},{"p":"In spite of the importance of public sector accounting in the economic development process, significant attention was not always given to it. The development of the private sector depends largely on activities in the public sector, and in developing countries such as Nigeria, the public sector is not only the biggest actor but also the hub of economic activities — moreover, government has the responsibility for the efficient management of the environment of commercial transactions, through the maintenance of law and order and the enactment of legislation, and political stability is essential for the growth of the economy. It is in the realisation of this, and to bridge the gap between the public and private sectors, that many international pronouncements have been made:"},{"ol":["A **United Nations survey** was conducted, and recommendations made for improvements in the government accounting systems of \"third world\" countries, especially in budgeting practices, training, data classifications, methods and accounting procedures.","In the **United States of America**, the National Committee on Government Accounting issued a manual titled *\"Government Accounting, Auditing and Financial Reporting (GAAFR)\"* — generally referred to as **\"The Blue Book.\"** The Blue Book and other pronouncements of the committee set forth basic accounting and reporting principles covering: basis of accounting; legality; funds and fund accounting; budgetary, planning and control; tangible assets and depreciation; terminologies and accounting/classifications; and financial reporting. The National Committee on Government Accounting recommended the **accrual basis** of accounting for public enterprises, capital projects and trust funds; the **modified accrual basis** for special revenue and debt service funds; and that depreciation is not chargeable on tangible assets."]},{"p":"**Other pronouncements:**"},{"ol":["The **International Federation of Accountants (IFAC)** issued International Public Sector Accounting Standards, effective from the year 2003.","The **Nigerian Public Sector Auditing Standards**, effective from December 1997, were issued by the Auditor-General for the Federation and the Auditors-General for the States.","The **American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA)** recommended that the financial statements of each governmental unit should be prepared in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, while supplementary schedules should accord with legal compliance.","The **International Organisation of Supreme Audit Institutions (INTOSAI)**, the association of all Auditors-General in the world, meets annually and draws up resolutions on accounting and audit practices and procedures to be followed by member countries."]},{"h3":"The International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB)"},{"p":"The IPSASB (formerly the Public Sector Committee, PSC) is a Board of IFAC formed to develop and issue, under its own authority, International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSASs). IPSASs are high-quality global financial reporting standards for application by public sector entities **other than** Government Business Enterprises (GBEs)."},{"p":"The main objective of the IPSASB is to serve the public interest by developing high-quality public sector financial reporting standards. IPSASs are the authoritative requirements established by the authoritative publications, including studies, research reports and occasional papers, that deal with particular public sector financial reporting issues."},{"h4":"Membership of the IPSASB"},{"p":"The IPSASB consists of **18 members**, of which 15 are drawn from IFAC member bodies, and the remaining three are public members with expertise in public sector financial reporting. The IFAC Board, on the recommendation of the IFAC Nominating Committee, appoints all members of the IPSASB, including the chair and deputy chair."},{"h4":"Due process"},{"p":"The IPSASB adopts a due process for the development of IPSASs that provides the opportunity for comments by interested parties, including IFAC member bodies, auditors, preparers (including finance ministries), standard-setters and individuals. IPSASB's due process for projects normally, but not necessarily, takes the following stages:"},{"ol":["Study of national accounting requirements and practice, and exchange of views about the issues with national standard-setters;","Consideration of pronouncements issued by: (a) the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB); (b) national standard-setters, regulatory authorities and other authoritative bodies; (c) professional accounting bodies; and (d) other organisations interested in financial reporting in the public sector;","Formation of Steering Committees (SC), Project Advisory Panels (PAPs) or subcommittees to provide input to the IPSASB on a project;","Publication of an exposure draft for public comment, usually for at least four (4) months. This provides an opportunity for those affected by IPSASB's pronouncements to comment before they are finalised and approved as an IPSAS — the exposure draft will include a basis for conclusions;","Consideration of all comments received within the comment period on discussion documents and exposure drafts, and modification of the proposed standard as considered appropriate in light of them;","Publication of an IPSAS, which includes a basis for conclusions that explains the steps in IPSASB's due process and how the Board reached its conclusions."]},{"p":"The adoption of IPSASs by government will improve both the quality and the comparability of financial information reported by public sector entities around the world. The IPSASB recognises the right of governments and national standard-setters to establish accounting standards and guidelines for financial reporting in their jurisdictions, and encourages the adoption of IPSASs and the harmonisation of national requirements with them. Financial statements should be described as complying with IPSASs only if they comply with **all** requirements of each applicable IPSAS."},{"h3":"Pan African Federation of Accountants (PAFA)"},{"p":"PAFA is the continental body representing Africa's professional accountants. Established in **May 2011**, PAFA aims to accelerate the development of the accountancy profession in Africa and strengthen the voice of the accountancy profession within Africa and worldwide. PAFA's strategic objectives, and their economic and societal benefits, are aimed at creating long-term value and contributing to the realisation of the African Union's Agenda 2063 and the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)."},{"h4":"The Seven Aspirations of Agenda 2063"},{"ol":["A prosperous Africa based on inclusive growth and sustainable development.","An integrated continent that is politically united, based on the ideals of Pan-Africanism and the vision of the African Renaissance.","An Africa of good governance, democracy, and respect for human rights, justice and the rule of law.","A peaceful and secure Africa.","An Africa with a strong cultural identity, common heritage, values and ethics.","An Africa whose development is people-driven, relying on the potential offered by the African people, especially its women and youth, and caring for children.","An Africa that is strong, united, resilient and an influential global player and partner."]},{"h3":"African Professionalization Initiatives (API) in the public sector"},{"p":"The African Professionalization Initiative (API) is a partnership between Accountants-General, Professional Accountancy Organisations and Supreme Audit Institutions, whose core objective is to professionalise accounting and auditing in the public sector in Africa."},{"h3":"The Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria (FRCN)"},{"p":"There is established the **Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria**. The Council shall be: (i) a body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal; and (ii) may sue and be sued in its corporate name. The Council may acquire, hold or dispose of any property, movable or immovable, for the purpose of carrying out its functions."},{"h4":"Composition"},{"p":"There is established for the Council a **Board**, which has overall control of the Council. The Board shall consist of:"},{"ol":["A Chairman who shall be a professional accountant with considerable professional experience in accounting practices;","Two representatives from the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria;","Two representatives from the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria;","One representative from each of: the Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation; the Office of the Auditor-General for the Federation; the Central Bank of Nigeria; the Chartered Institute of Stockbrokers; the Chartered Institute of Taxation of Nigeria; the Corporate Affairs Commission; the Nigeria Revenue Service; the Federal Ministry of Commerce; the Federal Ministry of Finance; the Nigerian Accounting Association; the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industries, Mines and Agriculture; the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation; the Nigerian Institute of Estate Surveyors and Valuers; the Securities and Exchange Commission; the National Insurance Commission; the Nigerian Stock Exchange; and the National Pension Commission; and","The Executive Secretary of the Council."]},{"p":"The President, on the recommendation of the Minister, shall appoint the Chairman, and the members shall be recommended by their various professional or statutory bodies to the Minister for appointment by the President. The Chairman and other members of the Board shall each hold office for a term of 4 years in the first instance, and may be re-appointed for a further term of 4 years and no more."},{"h4":"Cessation and disqualification from membership"},{"p":"A person shall not be appointed or continue to hold office as a member of the Board if:"},{"ol":["He becomes bankrupt, suspends payment or compounds with his creditors;","He is convicted of a felony or any offence involving dishonesty or fraud;","He becomes of unsound mind, or incapable of carrying out his duties;","He is guilty of serious misconduct in relation to his duties; or","Being a person who possessed professional qualifications, he is disqualified or suspended (other than at his own request) from practising his profession anywhere in the world by order of a competent authority."]},{"p":"The seat of a member becomes vacant if:"},{"ol":["He resigns;","He becomes disqualified from membership under section 4 of the Act;","He no longer holds the office by virtue of which he became a member; or","He has been absent from three consecutive meetings, or three-quarters of the meetings of the Board during a financial year, without leave."]},{"p":"A vacancy so created must be filled not later than 30 days from the date it occurs, and the replacement holds office only for the remainder of the term of the vacating member."},{"h4":"Powers of the Council"},{"p":"The Council shall have power to:"},{"ol":["Enforce and approve enforcement of compliance with accounting, auditing, corporate governance and financial reporting standards in Nigeria;","Enter into such contracts as may be necessary or expedient for discharging its functions;","Borrow such sums of money, or raise such loans, as it may require to discharge its functions;","Co-operate with, or become a member or affiliate of, any similar international body;","Exercise such powers as are necessary or expedient to give effect to the provisions of the Act;","Require management assessment of internal controls, including information systems controls, with independent attestation;","Require a code of ethics for financial officers and certification of financial statements by the Chief Executive Officer and Chief Financial Officer;","Require entities to provide real-time disclosures on material changes in financial condition or operations; and","Pronounce forfeiture, by Chief Executive Officers and Chief Financial Officers, of certain bonuses received and profits realised from the sale of company shares owned by them, where the company is required to prepare an accounting restatement."]},{"h4":"Functions of the Council"},{"p":"Among a longer list, the Council shall:"},{"ol":["Develop and publish accounting and financial reporting standards to be observed in the preparation of financial statements of public interest entities;","Review, promote and enforce compliance with the standards it adopts;","Receive notices of non-compliance from preparers, users, other third parties or auditors;","Receive copies of annual reports and financial statements of public interest entities within 60 days of the Board's approval;","Advise the Federal Government on matters relating to accounting and financial reporting standards;","Maintain a register of professional accountants and other professionals engaged in the financial reporting process;","Monitor compliance with the reporting requirements of the adopted code of corporate governance;","Promote compliance with standards issued by IFAC and the IASB;","Monitor and promote education, research and training in accounting, auditing, financial reporting and corporate governance;","Conduct practice reviews of registered professionals;","Review financial statements and reports of public interest entities;","Enforce compliance with the Act and the Council's rules;","Establish such systems, schemes or activities, alone or with any other organisation, as needed to discharge its functions;","Receive copies of all qualified audit reports together with detailed explanations within 30 days of the qualification (such reports are not announced to the public until all accounting issues relating to them are resolved by the Council);","Adopt and keep up to date accounting and financial reporting standards, ensuring consistency with IFRS;","Specify, in those standards, the minimum requirements for recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure that every public interest entity must comply with;","Develop or adopt and keep up-to-date auditing standards, ensuring consistency with the pronouncements of the International Auditing and Assurance Standards Board; and","Perform such other functions as, in the Board's opinion, are necessary or expedient for the efficient performance of the Council's functions."]},{"h4":"Functions of the Board"},{"p":"The Board shall:"},{"ol":["Determine broad strategies and priorities;","Set out the budget, secure the necessary funding and monitor expenditure;","Appoint the directors and other senior management staff;","Oversee the delivery by each directorate of its functions, through regular reports from the directorates' coordinating directors;","Oversee the performance of the executive through regular reports from the Chief Executive Officer;","Ensure that the Council and its directorates achieve high levels of accountability and transparency;","Undertake an annual assessment of the risks to the success of the Council's operations and oversee the necessary risk-mitigation plan; and","Undertake an annual evaluation of its own performance, and that of its committees and operating bodies, against its objectives, including a review of the schedule of matters reserved to the Board."]},{"h4":"Objectives of the Council"},{"p":"The objectives of the Council are to:"},{"ol":["Protect investors' and other stakeholders' interests;","Give guidance on issues relating to financial reporting and corporate governance to the bodies it regulates;","Ensure good corporate governance practices in the public and private sectors of the Nigerian economy;","Ensure the accuracy and reliability of financial reports and corporate disclosures, pursuant to the various laws and regulations currently in existence; and","Harmonise the activities of relevant professional and regulatory bodies as they relate to corporate governance and financial reporting."]},{"key":"Do not try to memorise the FRCN material as one undifferentiated block. Organise it into the same five buckets the source itself uses — **establishment, composition, powers, functions of the Council, functions of the Board, and objectives** — and learn which bucket a given fact belongs to. An exam question naming \"objectives\" wants the five-item list above, not the much longer \"functions\" list, even though both talk about broadly similar things."},{"note":"[Every abbreviation in the syllabus, in one place →](guides/ps-abbreviations.html) — bodies, the Acts that create them, digital systems and technical terms, organised by chapter."}]},{"n":"1.8","t":"Fund accounting — a compact preview ahead of Chapter 2","b":[{"p":"As flagged in §1.2, this study text's own Chapter 1 does not itself define the constitutional funds — that material belongs to Chapter 2, on the constitutional and regulatory framework, where it will be treated in full alongside the sections of the 1999 Constitution that establish each one. This section exists only so that the concepts this chapter's quiz assumes are not left completely undefined until then."},{"def":{"t":"Fund","d":"a self-balancing set of accounts recording cash and other resources, together with the related liabilities and balances, segregated for the purpose of carrying out a specific activity or attaining certain objectives in accordance with special regulations or limitations."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Fund","What it is"],"rows":[["**Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF)**","Established by section 80(1) of the 1999 Constitution. All revenues of the Federation are paid into it, except revenues payable into another fund established for a specific purpose. Withdrawals require appropriation by the National Assembly."],["**Development Fund**","Established by section 81(2). Receives transfers from the CRF and finances capital expenditure."],["**Contingencies Fund**","Established by section 83. Meets urgent and unforeseen expenditure for which no other provision exists; the amount advanced is later replaced by a supplementary appropriation."],["**Federation Account**","Established by section 162(1). All revenue collected by the Government of the Federation is paid into it, and distributed among the Federal, State and Local Governments on the basis prescribed by the National Assembly."]]}},{"warn":"The **Federation Account** and the **Consolidated Revenue Fund** are different things and are very easy to confuse. The Federation Account is the pool from which all three tiers of government are paid; the CRF is the Federal Government's **own** account, and it receives the Federal Government's share out of the Federation Account. Section 162 governs the first; section 80 governs the second."}]},{"n":"1.9","t":"Further reading","b":[{"note":"[IFAC](https://www.ifac.org) and its board the [IPSASB](https://www.ipsasb.org) publish the full text and handbook of every IPSAS, free to read, along with the IPSASB's current work plan. The [Pan African Federation of Accountants](https://pafa-af.org) has more on Agenda 2063 and its own initiatives across the continent. For Nigeria specifically, the [Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria](https://www.frcnigeria.gov.ng) is the primary source for the FRCN's current Board composition, standards and enforcement activity, and the [Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria](https://icanig.org) publishes guidance and past questions relevant to this paper. If any of these links have moved, search the organisation's name directly — these sites reorganise from time to time."}]},{"n":"1.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, reproduced with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer in each case."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is **not** an external user of public sector accounting information: (A) Members of the Legislature  (B) Governments, apart from the one reporting  (C) The members of the public  (D) Financial institutions  (E) Governments, including the one reporting.","Which stage, through which a spending decision passes, does the **cash basis** of accounting record? (A) Management decision to spend money  (B) Issue of order or contract  (C) Supply of goods or services — acknowledgment of liability  (D) Settlement of the amount  (E) Consumption of value.","Which of the following is **not** a public sector organisation? (A) Federal Government  (B) State Government  (C) National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control  (D) Nigeria Theological Seminary and Chaplaincy  (E) Nigerian Civil Aviation Authority.","One of the following is **not** a basis under which the financial statements of a public sector enterprise are compiled: (A) Cash basis  (B) Accrual basis  (C) Profit maximisation basis  (D) Commitment basis  (E) Modified cash basis.","Which of the following is **not** an advantage of the cash basis? (A) It is simple to understand  (B) It takes a realistic view of financial transactions  (C) It eliminates the existence of debtors and creditors  (D) It saves time and is easy to operate  (E) It allows comparison between the amount budgeted and that actually spent.","The user of public sector accounting information whose interest is in evaluating the credit rating of a borrowing nation is called …","The basis of accounting under which the books are left open for a maximum of three months after year end, to capture substantial income or expenditure relating to the year just ended, is called …","The accounting basis that records anticipated expenditure evidenced by a contract or a purchase order is called …","The name of the first international standards-setting body, formed in 1973, is …","All organisations which are created, administered and financed by government from taxpayers' money on behalf of the members of the public are called the …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**E.** The reporting government itself acts through the executive arm and is an *internal* user; every other option is external.","**E — Consumption of value.** The cash basis recognises a transaction only on payment, i.e. stage (v). Accrual covers stages (iii)–(v); commitment covers stages (ii)–(v).","**D — Nigeria Theological Seminary and Chaplaincy.** A private religious body, not established, run or financed by government.","**C — Profit maximisation basis.** The five bases are cash, accrual, commitment, modified cash and modified accrual.","**B — \"It takes a realistic view of financial transactions.\"** That is an advantage of the *accrual* basis; the cash basis is criticised for taking an *unrealistic* view. The other four are genuine advantages of the cash basis.","**Financial institutions** — commercial banks, the World Bank and the IMF use the accounts to assess a borrowing nation's credit rating.","**Modified cash basis.**","**Commitment basis.**","**International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC)** — reorganised in 2001 into the IASB.","**Public sector.**"]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["IPSASB adopts a due process for developing IPSAS, with opportunity for comment by interested parties. Enumerate any **four stages** of the IPSASB's due process for project execution.","Discuss the comparison between a non-profit-oriented organisation and a profit-oriented organisation under: (a) profit  (b) legal formation  (c) budgetary approach  (d) dissolution  (e) revenue.","The commitment basis records anticipated expenditure evidenced by a contract or a purchase order. Give any **three advantages** and **two disadvantages** of the commitment basis of accounting.","Public sector accounting has three unique characteristics. **List and explain** them."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Stages of the IPSASB due process (any four)"},{"ol":["Study of national accounting requirements and practice, and exchange of views on the issues with national standard-setters.","Consideration of pronouncements issued by the IASB, national standard-setters and regulatory/authoritative bodies, professional accounting bodies, and other bodies interested in public financial reporting.","Formation of **Steering Committees**, **Project Advisory Panels** or subcommittees to provide input on a project.","Publication of an **exposure draft** for public comment, usually for at least **four months**, including a basis for conclusions.","Consideration of all comments received within the comment period and modification of the proposed standard as appropriate.","Publication of the **IPSAS**, including a basis for conclusions explaining the steps in the due process and how the Board reached its conclusions."]},{"h4":"2  Non-profit-oriented vs profit-oriented organisation"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis","Non-profit-oriented","Profit-oriented"],"rows":[["**Profit**","Main objective is to provide adequate welfare to the people at reasonable cost","Main objective is to maximise profit"],["**Legal formation**","Created by Acts of the legislatures (National / State Assemblies)","Registered with the Corporate Affairs Commission"],["**Budgetary approach**","Public entities are rigid on the budgetary approach","Budgets are an internal affair of the organisation"],["**Dissolution**","Income raised or assets acquired cannot be distributed to members, directors or officers — all proceeds revert to the State","Income realised on dissolution can be distributed to members, directors or officers"],["**Revenue**","Derived from the public as taxation, fines, fees, etc.","Derived principally from the sale of goods and services"]]}},{"h4":"3  Commitment basis"},{"p":"**Advantages (any three):**"},{"ol":["A separate payment tabulation is available on demand;","Adjustments when actual expenditure is known do not affect the final accounts;","It is an aid to financial control (a commitment is a charge already made on a budget provision);","It takes a realistic view of transactions;","It reveals an accurate year-end position;","It serves economic and investment decision-making;","It aligns with the matching concept;","It allows for the diminution in value of assets."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages (any two):**"},{"ol":["The system involves extra work (actual figures must later be substituted for the commitment provisions);","It encourages over-expenditure in the expectation that government will release funds to settle legal obligations;","At year end, unfulfilled orders must be written back;","Balances that ought to lapse in the Vote Book may be spent by issuing local purchase orders to exhaust the votes."]},{"h4":"4  Three unique characteristics of public sector accounting"},{"ol":["**Distinct information, classification and procedures** apply only to government transactions — the budgeting system, fiscal policy, accounting methods and revenue sources — which must be treated by specific, cohesive, standardised rules.","**Budgeting largely determines the structure** of public sector accounting, because government revenue and expenditure need legislative approval; resources are often segregated into special-purpose **Funds**, recorded and measured by **fund accounting**.","**The system is maintained on the cash basis** — only movements of cash are recognised; all assets are written off when paid for, so the statement of assets and liabilities shows no tangible assets and no depreciation is charged."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Two or three Section A marks every diet on definitions, objectives, the five bases and the professional bodies, and a Section B question in most diets — usually \"distinguish the bases of accounting\", \"state the users and their information needs\", or \"state the objectives of public sector accounting\". These are recall questions and should be scored close to in full. The five-stages-of-a-spending-decision diagram in §1.5 is the fastest way to answer almost any question comparing two bases of accounting — learn it as a diagram, not as prose.","errors":["Confusing the Federation Account (section 162) with the Consolidated Revenue Fund (section 80).","Listing users without stating what each of them needs from the report.","Describing the commitment basis as recording the same things as the accrual basis — it records orders and contracts placed (stage ii onward), not liabilities incurred (stage iii onward).","Giving the accrual basis's advantages (matching, asset valuation) as advantages of the commitment basis — the source itself makes this exact error, and it is worth being able to say why it is wrong.","Stating that the public sector has no objective, rather than that its objectives centre on accountability and service delivery rather than profit.","Giving advantages of the accrual basis when a question asks for advantages of the cash basis, or vice versa."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under section 80(1) of the 1999 Constitution, all revenues of the Federation are paid into the","o":["Federation Account","Consolidated Revenue Fund","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Excess Crude Account"],"a":1,"w":"Section 80(1) establishes the Consolidated Revenue Fund. The Federation Account is established separately by section 162(1) and is the pool shared among the three tiers.","src":"Chapter 1.8","sec":"1.8"},{"q":"Under which basis of accounting is expenditure recognised when an order is placed, before any invoice is received?","o":["Cash basis","Commitment basis","Accrual basis","Modified cash basis","Modified accrual basis"],"a":1,"w":"The commitment basis records the obligation at the point of order so that the vote is not over-committed. It is a budgetary control device.","src":"Chapter 1.5","sec":"1.5"},{"q":"The primary objective of public sector accounting is to","o":["maximise government revenue","demonstrate accountability for public resources","compute the profit of government","value government assets","reduce the cost of governance"],"a":1,"w":"Because public money is raised compulsorily, accountability rather than profit measurement is the central purpose.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"Which fund meets urgent and unforeseen expenditure for which no other provision exists?","o":["Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Consolidated Revenue Fund","Federation Account","Ecological Fund"],"a":1,"w":"Section 83 of the Constitution establishes the Contingencies Fund; the advance is subsequently replaced by a supplementary appropriation.","src":"Chapter 1.8","sec":"1.8"},{"q":"A disadvantage of the cash basis of accounting is that it","o":["is difficult to audit","ignores assets and liabilities","requires extensive professional judgement","is not comparable with the budget","is expensive to operate"],"a":1,"w":"Recording only receipts and payments means receivables, payables and non-current assets never appear, so the financial position is not disclosed. The other four options are in fact advantages of the cash basis.","src":"Chapter 1.5","sec":"1.5"},{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from","o":["January 2012","January 2016","January 2014","January 2020","January 2010"],"a":1,"w":"Cash basis IPSAS was adopted from January 2014 and accrual basis IPSAS from January 2016, subject to the transitional provisions of IPSAS 33.","src":"Chapter 1.5","sec":"1.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a basis on which the financial statements of a public sector enterprise are compiled?","o":["Cash basis","Accrual basis","Profit maximisation basis","Commitment basis","Modified cash basis"],"a":2,"w":"The five bases are cash, accrual, commitment, modified cash and modified accrual. There is no \"profit maximisation\" basis — the public sector has no profit motive.","src":"Chapter 1.10 (study text Section A Q4)","sec":"1.10"},{"q":"The cash basis of accounting recognises a spending decision only at which stage?","o":["Management decision to spend","Issue of the order or contract","Supply of goods or services (acknowledgment of liability)","Consumption of value","Approval of the budget"],"a":3,"w":"A spending decision passes through five stages; the cash basis records only stage (v), consumption of value (payment). Accrual covers stages iii–v; commitment covers stages ii–v.","src":"Chapter 1.10 (study text Section A Q2)","sec":"1.10"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an advantage of the cash basis of accounting?","o":["It is simple to understand","It takes a realistic view of financial transactions","It eliminates the existence of debtors and creditors","It saves time and is easy to operate","It allows comparison of budgeted with actual spending"],"a":1,"w":"Taking a realistic view of transactions is an advantage of the accrual basis; the cash basis is criticised for the opposite. The other four are genuine advantages of the cash basis.","src":"Chapter 1.10 (study text Section A Q5)","sec":"1.10"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between public sector accounting and private sector accounting, and explain the users of public sector financial reports and their information needs.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"The distinction"},{"p":"Public sector accounting records and reports the receipt, transfer and disposition of government funds and property. Private sector accounting reports the financial performance and position of a business. They differ in the following respects."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis of comparison","Public sector","Private sector"],"rows":[["Objective","Delivery of services for the welfare of citizens","Maximisation of profit and of shareholder wealth"],["Ownership","Vested in the citizens collectively; no transferable interest","Shareholders holding transferable shares"],["Funding","Taxation, statutory allocation, levies, grants and borrowing","Share capital, retained earnings and borrowing"],["Nature of contribution","Compulsory, and unrelated to the benefit an individual receives","Voluntary, and made in expectation of a return"],["Regulation","The 1999 Constitution, the Finance (Control and Management) Act, the Fiscal Responsibility Act, Financial Regulations and Treasury Circulars","CAMA 2020 and the company's own constitution"],["Reporting framework","IPSAS","IFRS"],["Accounting basis","Historically cash; now accrual under IPSAS","Accrual"],["Performance measurement","Value for money — economy, efficiency and effectiveness — which resist quantification","Profit, earnings per share, return on capital employed"],["Budget","A legal authority to spend; exceeding it is unlawful","A management plan with no legal force"],["Audit","Auditor-General for the Federation, reporting to the National Assembly","External auditors, reporting to the members"],["Continuity","Government is presumed to continue indefinitely","The entity may be wound up"]]}},{"p":"The differences all flow from the first. Because there is no profit to measure and no shareholder to satisfy, the public sector substitutes **accountability and budgetary compliance** for profitability as the test of stewardship."},{"h4":"Users and their information needs"},{"ol":["**The general public and taxpayers** — whether the taxes and levies they pay are properly collected, applied to lawful purposes and translated into services.","**The legislature** — whether the executive spent only on the purposes appropriated and within the sums appropriated; the National Assembly needs this to discharge its oversight function and to consider the following year's estimates.","**The executive and public sector managers** — cost and revenue information for planning, budgetary control, resource allocation and day-to-day operating decisions.","**Creditors, contractors and lenders** — the government's capacity to pay for goods and services supplied and to service and repay its debt.","**Investors in government securities** — the security of their investment and the certainty of interest and redemption.","**International agencies and donors** (the World Bank, the IMF, bilateral donors) — whether grants and concessionary loans were applied to the agreed purposes, and whether the overall fiscal position justifies further support.","**Employees and their unions** — the capacity of government to meet salaries, allowances, pensions and gratuities, which informs negotiation.","**Other tiers of government** — State and Local Governments need information on the Federation Account and on the basis of the statutory allocations they receive.","**Researchers, analysts and the press** — data for economic analysis, policy evaluation and public debate.","**The Auditor-General** — evidence on which to base the audit opinion and the report submitted to the legislature."]},{"note":"The distinguishing feature of the public sector user group is that most of its members cannot obtain the information any other way. A shareholder dissatisfied with a company's disclosure can sell the shares; a citizen cannot resign from the state. That asymmetry is the reason public sector reporting obligations are imposed by statute rather than left to the entity."}],"src":"Chapter 1.2–1.3","sec":"1.2"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"The Constitutional and Regulatory Framework of Public Sector Accounting","brief":"Public sector accounting is guided by the Constitution and the financial regulations of a nation — this chapter is the study text's own index of every section, Act, regulation and circular that does the guiding, for both Nigeria and Ghana, plus the detail behind the two instruments (the Fiscal Responsibility Act and IPSAS adoption) the source itself covers too thinly to answer an exam question from alone.","outcomes":["Understand the legal framework of public sector accounting","Discuss the administrative framework of public sector accounting","Cite the sections of the 1999 Constitution governing public finance","State the purpose of the Finance (Control and Management) Act","Explain the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 and the Public Procurement Act 2007","Describe the status of the Financial Regulations, Treasury Circulars and Financial Memoranda","List other laws and regulations guiding public sector accounting","State the constitutional and legal provisions guiding public sector accounting in Ghana"],"secs":[{"n":"2.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Public Sector Accounting is guided by the Constitution and financial regulations of a Nation. It is concerned with the recording of the mobilisation and prudent utilisation of the finances of a country. Everything in this chapter is, in effect, one long answer to a single question: **on whose authority, exactly, may government collect or spend a naira?** Each instrument below answers that question at a different level — the Constitution first, then Acts of the National Assembly, then Regulations, then Circulars — and an exam question is very often really asking you to place a described fact at the correct level."}]},{"n":"2.2","t":"Constitutional and legal provisions in Nigeria","b":[{"h3":"2.2.1 — The 1999 Constitution"},{"p":"The 1999 Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria (as amended) is one of the legal frameworks that regulate the receipts and disbursements of public funds. The sections quoted below are authorities on receipts and payments procedures of government, the allocation of revenue, the audit of public accounts, and other financial matters."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section(s)","Details"],"rows":[["80 and 120","Establishment of the Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF)"],["81 and 121","Authorisation of expenditure from the CRF"],["82 and 122","Authorisation of expenditure in default of appropriations"],["83 and 123","Establishment of the Contingencies Fund"],["84 and 124","Remuneration of statutory officers"],["84(4) and 124(4)","Comprehensive list of statutory officers"],["85 and 125","Audit of public accounts"],["86 and 126","Appointment of the Auditor-General"],["87 and 127","Tenure of office of the Auditor-General"],["88 and 128","Power to conduct investigation by the legislatures"],["89 and 129","Power as to matters of evidence"],["149","Declaration of assets and liabilities, and oaths of office"],["153","List of statutory commissions"],["162","Establishment of the Federation Account"],["163","Allocation of other revenue"],["164","Federal grants-in-aid of State revenue"],["165","Cost of collection of certain duties"],["166","Set-off of loans made by the Federation to the State"],["167","Sums charged on Consolidated Revenue Fund"],["168","Provisions with regard to payments"]]}},{"key":"The pattern in the left column is itself worth noticing: every \"Federal\" section has a paired \"State\" section exactly 40 higher (80↔120, 81↔121, ... 89↔129) — the Constitution simply repeats the same federal-level scheme for each State. Learn the federal numbers and the +40 rule together, rather than memorising twenty section numbers as an unrelated list."},{"warn":"Sections **80** and **162** are the two most frequently examined, and the two most frequently confused. **80 = Consolidated Revenue Fund** (the Federal Government's own account, into which its own revenues are paid). **162 = Federation Account** (the shared pool, fed by centrally-collected revenue, out of which all three tiers — Federal, State and Local — are paid). The Federal Government's share of the Federation Account is itself paid into its Consolidated Revenue Fund — the two are related, not interchangeable."},{"h3":"2.2.2 — The Finance (Control and Management) Act"},{"warn":"The study text is inconsistent with its own citation of this Act in two different places: its own chapter-opening contents list cites it as \"Finance (Control and Management) Act of 1958, **Cap. 144, 1990**,\" while the body text and the model answer to the matching end-of-chapter question both cite it as \"**Cap. F.26 Laws of the Federation 2004**.\" Since the body text and the answer key agree with each other twice, **Cap. F.26 LFN 2004** is treated as the reliable citation here — the \"Cap. 144, 1990\" reference in the contents page appears to be either an older edition's citation or a straightforward slip."},{"p":"This Act governs the management and operation of government funds. It regulates the accounting system, the books of account to be kept, and the procedures to be followed in the preparation of accounts and financial statements."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section","Details"],"rows":[["3","Legislative control and management of the public finances"],["4","Minister's instructions to be complied with, and powers to inspect, etc."],["5","Management of the Consolidated Revenue Fund"],["6","Authorised issues from the Consolidated Revenue Fund"],["7","Erroneous receipts"],["8","Losses"],["9","Authorisation of investments"],["10","Investments — general procedure"],["11","Income of investments — general"],["12","Fluctuation in value of investments — general"],["13","Annual estimates in appropriation bill"],["14","Supplementary provisions"],["15","Contingencies Fund"],["16","Unexpended votes to lapse"],["17","Provision if Appropriation Act not in force"],["18","Specification of certain public funds allocated by law"],["19","Carrying forward of annual balance"],["20","Interest and investment fluctuation to accrue to certain funds"],["21","Interest and investment fluctuation to accrue to CRF in certain cases"],["22","Fluctuation in value of investments"],["23","Rules for management of funds"],["24","Annual accounts of all funds"]]}},{"note":"Two provisions here are worth remembering by name rather than only by number, because they answer questions the Constitution itself does not: **section 16** — that an unspent vote **lapses** at the year end rather than carrying forward automatically, which is the legal root of the \"year-end spending rush\" criticised under the cash basis (Chapter 1 §1.5) — and **section 17**, which is the Act's own version of Constitution section 82: what happens if the Appropriation Act is not yet in force when the financial year begins."}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Annual and Supplementary Appropriation Acts","b":[{"p":"Money bills, when passed into law, become **Appropriation Acts**. They regulate financial matters, including the payment or withdrawal from the Consolidated Revenue Fund. Appropriation Acts are passed yearly, for the release of public money so as to render services in the years to which they relate. Appropriation Acts may direct a change in the way of operating any fund, apart from the Contingencies Fund and the Consolidated Revenue Fund."},{"key":"An Appropriation Act is what turns a *budget* — a plan — into a **legal authority to spend**. Spending outside its terms is unlawful, which is the single biggest difference between a government budget and a private company's budget: a company's budget binds nobody legally; a government's does."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"Fiscal Responsibility Act, 2007","b":[{"p":"This Act provides for prudent management of the nation's resources and ensures long-term macro-economic stability of the national economy. It secures greater accountability and transparency in fiscal operations within a medium-term fiscal policy framework. The Act also established the **Fiscal Responsibility Commission** to ensure the promotion and enforcement of the nation's economic objectives. The Act emphasises the preparation of the medium-term expenditure framework (MTEF), the annual budget, budget execution, collection of public revenue, public expenditure, debt and borrowings."},{"h4":"The functions of the Fiscal Responsibility Commission"},{"p":"The Act established the Commission to carry out, among others, the following functions:"},{"ol":["Monitoring and enforcing the provisions of the Act, thereby promoting the economic objectives of the Nation;","Disseminating standard national and international practices that will ensure greater efficiency in the allocation and management of public expenditure, revenue collection, debt control and transparency in fiscal matters;","Undertaking fiscal and financial studies, analysis and diagnosis, and disseminating the result to the general public."]},{"p":"Beyond this three-item list, the Act's own detailed machinery is worth knowing in full, since the source text's own treatment of it stops at the paragraph above — far too thin, on its own, to answer the Section B essay questions this Act regularly attracts:"},{"ul":["The Federal Government must prepare a **Medium Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF)** covering three financial years, approved by the Federal Executive Council and endorsed by the National Assembly before the estimates are laid.","The MTEF contains a macroeconomic framework, a fiscal strategy paper, an expenditure and revenue framework, and a consolidated debt statement.","The **annual budget must be derived from the MTEF**; an estimate inconsistent with it is invalid.","Aggregate expenditure may not exceed estimated aggregate revenue plus a deficit of more than **3% of GDP**, save in exceptional circumstances such as a threat to national security.","Borrowing must be for capital expenditure and human development only, at concessional terms, and subject to a debt limit set by the National Assembly.","Scheduled corporations must prepare and submit annual budgets and remit **80% of their operating surplus** to the Consolidated Revenue Fund — this is the provision that addressed the long-standing practice of agencies retaining internally generated revenue without remitting any of it.","Revenue collected in excess of the budget benchmark is to be **saved rather than spent** — the statutory root of the Excess Crude Account and, later, the Sovereign Wealth Fund."]},{"note":"In practice, this Act's framework has strengthened *design* more than *enforcement*: budgeting is now formally tied to a three-year horizon and explicit benchmarks, and borrowing is formally tied to capital projects and a debt limit — but the Commission has limited power to sanction a breach, the 3% deficit rule has been exceeded in practice using the \"exceptional circumstances\" exemption, and States were expected to pass their own equivalent laws with uneven take-up. An essay question that asks you to \"assess\" the Act, rather than merely describe it, is asking for exactly this design-versus-enforcement distinction."}]},{"n":"2.5","t":"Public Procurement Act, 2007","b":[{"p":"This is an Act which established the **National Council on Public Procurement (NCPP)** and the **Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP)** as the regulatory authorities responsible for monitoring and oversight of public procurement, harmonising existing government policies by regulating, setting standards and developing the legal framework and professional capacity for public procurement in Nigeria. The Act sets standards for organising procurements, methods of procurement of works, goods, consultancy and non-consultancy services, as well as procurement approval thresholds for the Bureau of Public Procurement, tenders boards and accounting officers for all ministries, departments and agencies."},{"note":"This chapter only introduces the Public Procurement Act as one item in the regulatory framework. The methods of procurement, the fundamental principles, and the special and restricted procurement methods it sets out are covered in full later in this paper, under public procurement specifically."}]},{"n":"2.6","t":"Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act, 1982 Cap. A.15 LFN 2004","b":[{"p":"The Act prescribes:"},{"ol":["The basis for distribution of revenue accruing to the Federation Account between the Federal, States and Local Governments;","The formula for distribution amongst the States;","The proportion of the total revenue of each State to be contributed to the State Joint Local Government Account; and","Other purposes connected therewith."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section","Details"],"rows":[["1","Distribution of the Federation Accounts, etc."],["2","Formula for distribution between the Federal and State Governments"],["3","Formula for distribution between Local Governments"],["4","Proportion of revenue to be paid by each State to the State Joint Local Government Account"],["5","Allocations under Special Funds"],["6","Establishment of the Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC), and its functions"],["7","Establishment of a Joint Local Government Account for each State, and its functions"],["8","Limit on the power of State Governments to borrow money"],["9","Report by the Accountant-General of the Federation"]]}},{"key":"This is the Act that gives the **Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)** its legal footing — the body that actually meets, monthly, to share the Federation Account among the three tiers under the formula this Act (and its accompanying schedules) sets. FAAC resurfaces later in this paper as one of the intergovernmental bodies examined in its own right."}]},{"n":"2.7","t":"The Financial Regulations (2009 Edition)","b":[{"p":"The Financial Regulations are powerful control tools used in public sector fund management. They are the accounting manuals of the three tiers of government, designed to guide the management of public funds. The rules spell out the system concerning receipts and disbursements of funds and the procedures to ensure good accountability, prevention and early detection of frauds, errors and other financial malpractices."},{"p":"These are the accounting manual of government ministries and extra-ministerial departments, which deal with financial and accounting matters. They set out the procedures and steps to be followed in treating most of government's transactions. According to **FR 105**, the Minister of Finance shall issue, from time to time, financial regulations, which shall be in accordance with existing laws and policies of government. The financial regulations so issued shall generally apply to the federal public service, ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government."},{"h4":"Objectives of the Financial Regulations"},{"ol":["To guide the day-to-day financial operation of government MDAs.","To ensure an appropriate system of information flow from management to finance and accounts staff.","To ensure prudence in carrying out government business.","To provide common standards, procedures and guides by which auditors and treasury inspectors can ascertain that ministries are able to control and maintain up-to-date records of financial transactions.","To promote fiscal accountability, management accountability and programme-result accountability in government financial management and control.","To ensure the applicability of the principles of good government."]}]},{"n":"2.8","t":"Finance / Treasury circulars","b":[{"p":"These are administrative tools which are used to amend the existing provisions of Financial Regulations, public service rules and the introduction of new policy guidelines."},{"key":"The hierarchy of authority runs: **Constitution → Acts of the National Assembly → Financial Regulations → Treasury/Finance Circulars → internal instructions.** An instrument lower in the hierarchy cannot override one above it, and it is exactly this hierarchy that makes a circular a fast, flexible way to amend a detail of the Financial Regulations without having to amend an Act of the National Assembly every time a procedure needs updating."}]},{"n":"2.9","t":"Revised Financial Memoranda for Local Government, 1991","b":[{"p":"The Financial Memoranda for Local Government is a publication by the Federal Government which contains the administrative guidelines, the existing systems of checks and balances, as well as the roles of all the officers from the Chief Accounting Officer, the Chairman, to the officer at the lowest cadre."},{"h4":"The objectives of the Financial Memoranda"},{"ol":["To expressly highlight the implications of disbursing government fund and property without proper authority and approval;","To serve as administrative guidelines which facilitate the day-to-day running of local governments;","To facilitate the recording of local government financial transactions using the appropriate accounting method; and","To serve as a learning tool for officers on first appointment or on transfer to a new section."]},{"h4":"The contents of the Local Government Financial Memoranda"},{"ol":["The format of budget and budgetary control;","The financial responsibilities of the Chairman and other accounting officers of a local government;","The responsibilities of the local government Secretary, Treasurer and Heads of Departments;","The powers and functions of the Auditor-General for Local Government;","The various financial offences and their respective sanctions;","The means of revenue collection and control;","Main books of accounts kept in the local government; and","The custody, accounting and control of stores."]},{"note":"This is the Local Government equivalent of the Financial Regulations in §2.7 — same purpose (an accounting manual with the force of administrative authority), one tier lower. Do not mix the two up: the Financial Regulations bind the Federal (and, by adoption, State) public service; the Financial Memoranda bind Local Government."}]},{"n":"2.10","t":"Other laws and regulations guiding public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"Other laws guiding public sector accounting include the following:"},{"ol":["Revenue Mobilisation and Fiscal Allocation Commission Act (Cap. R.7 LFN 2004);","Pension Reform Act of 2014;","The Independent Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Commission (ICPC) Act of 2000;","Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (Establishment) Act, 2002;","Nigeria Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative (NEITI) Act, 2007;","Ministry of Finance Incorporated (MoFI) Act of 1959;","Financial Reporting Council Act, 2011;","Freedom of Information Act, 2011;","International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS);","International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS);","Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal Act, 1991;","Money Laundering (Prohibition) Act, 1995;","Bye-laws of local government as related to revenue and expenditure; and","Nigeria Tax Act, 2025."]},{"note":"This is a \"know the name, know roughly what it does\" list rather than a deep-detail list — several of these (the Pension Reform Act, IPSAS itself) get their own full chapters later in this paper. Treat this section as the index that tells you those chapters exist and where they sit in the wider legal landscape, and revisit it once you have studied each one in full."}]},{"n":"2.11","t":"Constitutional and legal provisions in Ghana","b":[{"h3":"2.11.1 — 1992 Constitutional provisions of Ghana"},{"p":"Chapter 13 of the 1992 Constitution of Ghana gives the authorisation for the generation of the country's revenue, and stipulates that the Nation's Auditor-General shall prepare and forward his report to the National Assembly within a specified period of time."},{"h3":"2.11.2 — Ghana's Financial Administration Act No. 654 of 2003"},{"p":"The Act, and the Financial Administration Regulation of 2004, L.I. 1802, provide the legal basis or authority, while the Accounting Manual and Departmental Accounting Instructions give the operational guidelines. Chapter 13 of Ghana's Constitution of 1992 dwells on the finances of the country."},{"key":"The Ghana material mirrors the Nigerian structure exactly, one level at a time: a **constitution chapter** (Chapter 13, playing the same role as sections 80–168 of Nigeria's Constitution), a **primary Act** (the Financial Administration Act, playing the same role as the Finance (Control and Management) Act), and **subsidiary instruments** (the Financial Administration Regulation and the Accounting Manual, playing the same role as Nigeria's Financial Regulations and Treasury Circulars). Recognising this parallel structure is the fastest way to answer a question that asks you to compare the two countries' frameworks, rather than memorising the Ghanaian instruments as an unrelated new list."}]},{"n":"2.12","t":"The standard-setting framework — IPSAS in Nigeria","b":[{"note":"[Every IPSAS this course treats, indexed by number →](guides/ipsas.html) — what each one covers, the two supersessions (17→45, 25→39), the \"IPSAS 49\" numbering anomaly, and exactly which chapter deals with which standard."},{"p":"The chapter's own list in §2.10 names IPSAS as one instrument among many, without saying anything further about it — the detail below fills that gap, since IPSAS adoption is one of the most heavily examined topics to come out of this chapter's material in practice."},{"p":"**IPSAS** — International Public Sector Accounting Standards — are issued by the International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB), a board of the International Federation of Accountants (IFAC), as already met in Chapter 1 §1.7. They exist in two series: a single cash basis standard, and a suite of accrual basis standards largely converged with IFRS but adapted for the public sector."},{"ul":["Nigeria adopted **cash basis IPSAS from 1 January 2014** and **accrual basis IPSAS from 1 January 2016**, on the recommendation of the Federation Account Allocation Committee and by decision of the National Executive Council.","The **Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria**, established by the FRC Act 2011 (already met in full in Chapter 1 §1.7), is the national standard-setter and adopts IPSAS for the public sector.","The **Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)**, whose legal footing was given in §2.6 above, established a Sub-Committee on roadmap implementation, which produced the National Chart of Accounts and the General Purpose Financial Statements templates — met in full in Chapter 3.","IPSAS are **not law in themselves**: they acquire force in Nigeria through adoption by the FRC and through Treasury Circulars directing their use — exactly the same \"acquires force by adoption, not automatically\" point already made about IFRS in Financial Accounting Chapter 1 §1.7."]},{"h4":"Benefits of adopting IPSAS"},{"ul":["Comparability of government financial statements across states and internationally.","Improved transparency and accountability, and hence a stronger basis for the fight against corruption.","Better information for decision-making, since assets and liabilities are recognised.","Enhanced credibility with international lenders and donor agencies.","A uniform basis of consolidation across ministries, departments and agencies."]},{"h4":"Challenges of implementation"},{"ul":["The cost and difficulty of identifying and valuing legacy assets, many of which have no reliable records.","Shortage of trained accountants familiar with accrual concepts.","Weak information systems in many entities, and the cost of upgrading them.","Resistance to change, and the loss of discretion that greater transparency entails.","The need to reconcile accrual reporting with a budget still prepared and appropriated on a cash basis."]}]},{"n":"2.13","t":"Further reading","b":[{"note":"The [Corporate Affairs Commission](https://www.cac.gov.ng) and the [Federal Ministry of Finance](https://www.finance.gov.ng) publish the current text of many of the Acts named here. The [Fiscal Responsibility Commission of Nigeria](https://www.frc.gov.ng) publishes its own annual reports and MTEF compliance monitoring, useful for updating the \"assessment\" material in §2.4 with more recent examples. The [Bureau of Public Procurement](https://www.bpp.gov.ng) is the primary source for current procurement thresholds under the Public Procurement Act — worth bookmarking ahead of the dedicated procurement chapter later in this paper."}]},{"n":"2.14","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer. The printed key is numbered one higher than the questions and drops the answer to Q1 — it is restored below."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is **not** part of the \"other laws and regulations\" guiding public sector accounting: (A) Revenue Mobilization and Fiscal Allocation Commission Act (Cap. R.7 LFN 2004)  (B) Pension Reform Act 2014  (C) Companies and Allied Matters Act 2020  (D) Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal Act 1991  (E) Financial Reporting Council Act 2011.","Which of the following are the administrative tools used to amend existing provisions of the Financial Regulations and public service rules and to introduce new policy guidelines? (A) Finance / Treasury circulars  (B) Financial Regulations  (C) Revised Financial Memoranda for Local Government 1991  (D) Medium-Term Expenditure Framework  (E) Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007.","One of the following is **not** among the objectives of the Financial Memoranda: (A) to serve as a learning tool for officers on first appointment  (B) to serve as an administrative guideline for the day-to-day running of local government  (C) to facilitate recording of local government transactions using appropriate accounting methods  (D) to highlight the implications of disbursing government funds without proper authority  (E) to make Local Government Chairmen accountable to their communities.","Which of the following laws regulates financial matters and payment or withdrawal from the Consolidated Revenue Fund? (A) Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007  (B) Public Procurement Act 2007  (C) The Financial Regulations (2009 Edition)  (D) Annual / Supplementary Appropriation Act  (E) Finance and Treasury circulars.","The Act that prescribes the basis for distribution of revenue accruing to the Federation Account between the Federal, State and Local Governments is …","… governs the management and operation of government funds.","The Act which provides for prudent management of the nation's resources and long-term macro-economic stability is …","The powerful control tool used in public sector fund management is …","The document containing the administrative guidelines and the checks and balances on local government financial matters is …","The body charged with procurement issues under the Public Procurement Act 2007 is …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — Companies and Allied Matters Act 2020.** CAMA is the private-sector companies statute; it is not in the study text's list of other laws guiding public sector accounting. (The printed key omits this answer.)","**A — Finance / Treasury circulars.**","**E — \"to make Local Government Chairmen accountable to their communities.\"** Not one of the stated objectives of the Financial Memoranda.","**D — the Annual / Supplementary Appropriation Act.** Money bills become Appropriation Acts and regulate withdrawals from the Consolidated Revenue Fund.","**C — the Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act 1982, Cap. A.15 LFN 2004.**","**The Finance (Control and Management) Act 1958, Cap. F.26 LFN 2004.**","**The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007.**","**The Financial Regulations (2009 Edition).**","**The Revised Financial Memoranda for Local Government, 1991.**","**The Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP).**"]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 established the Fiscal Responsibility Commission. State the **functions of the Commission** as contained in the Act.","The Nigerian public sector finances are guided by the Constitution and several other laws. **List any ten** of such laws.","The Financial Regulations (2009 Edition) is the accounting manual of government for managing public funds. **List the objectives** of the Financial Regulations.","The Revised Financial Memoranda for Local Government contains the administrative guidelines and the checks and balances in local government finance. **List its contents.**"]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Functions of the Fiscal Responsibility Commission"},{"ol":["**Monitoring and enforcing the provisions of the Act**, thereby promoting the economic objectives of the nation.","**Disseminating standard national and international practices** that ensure greater efficiency in the allocation and management of public expenditure, revenue collection, debt control and transparency in fiscal matters.","**Undertaking fiscal and financial studies, analysis and diagnosis**, and disseminating the results to the general public."]},{"h4":"2  Other laws guiding public sector accounting (any ten)"},{"ol":["Revenue Mobilisation and Fiscal Allocation Commission Act (Cap. R.7 LFN 2004).","Pension Reform Act 2014.","Independent Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Commission (ICPC) Act 2000.","Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (Establishment) Act 2002.","Nigeria Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative (NEITI) Act 2007.","Ministry of Finance Incorporated (MoFI) Act 1959.","Financial Reporting Council Act 2011.","Freedom of Information Act 2011.","International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS).","International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS).","Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal Act 1991.","Money Laundering (Prohibition) Act 1995.","Bye-laws of local government relating to revenue and expenditure.","Nigeria Tax Act 2025."]},{"h4":"3  Objectives of the Financial Regulations"},{"ol":["To guide the day-to-day financial operation of government MDAs.","To ensure an appropriate system of information flow from management to finance and accounts staff.","To ensure prudence in carrying out government business.","To provide common standards, procedures and guides by which auditors and treasury inspectors can ascertain that ministries control and maintain up-to-date records of financial transactions.","To promote fiscal accountability, management accountability and programme-result accountability in government financial management and control.","To ensure the applicability of the principles of good government."]},{"h4":"4  Contents of the Local Government Financial Memoranda"},{"ol":["The format of budget and budgetary control.","The financial responsibilities of the Chairman and other accounting officers of a local government.","The responsibilities of the local government Secretary, Treasurer and Heads of Departments.","The powers and functions of the Auditor-General for Local Government.","The various financial offences and their respective sanctions.","The means of revenue collection and control.","The main books of accounts kept in the local government.","The custody, accounting and control of stores."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One of the most heavily examined chapters in the paper. Expect two or three Section A marks on section numbers and Act titles, and a Section B question in most diets on the Fiscal Responsibility Act, the regulatory framework generally, or the benefits and challenges of IPSAS adoption. Learn the section numbers: an answer that says \"section 80 of the 1999 Constitution\" scores where \"the Constitution\" does not. The federal-to-State \"+40\" pattern in §2.2.1 is the fastest way to hold twenty section numbers in your head at once.","errors":["Attributing the Federation Account to section 80 or the Consolidated Revenue Fund to section 162.","Stating that IPSAS are law; they take effect through adoption by the FRC and by circular.","Confusing the Financial Regulations (federal) with the Financial Memoranda (local government).","Giving the deficit limit under the Fiscal Responsibility Act as a naira figure; it is 3% of GDP.","Confusing the two Fiscal Responsibility Act lists: the Commission's three statutory functions (monitor/enforce, disseminate practice, undertake studies) are not the same list as the Act's substantive requirements (MTEF, deficit limit, borrowing rules, remittance)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Consolidated Revenue Fund is established by which section of the 1999 Constitution?","o":["Section 162(1)","Section 80(1)","Section 81(2)","Section 83(1)","Section 85(1)"],"a":1,"w":"Section 80(1) establishes the CRF. Section 162(1) establishes the Federation Account, section 81(2) the Development Fund and section 83 the Contingencies Fund.","src":"Chapter 2.2","sec":"2.2"},{"q":"Under the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007, the aggregate expenditure deficit may not exceed","o":["1% of GDP","3% of GDP","5% of GDP","3% of aggregate revenue","10% of GDP"],"a":1,"w":"Section 12(1) caps the deficit at 3% of estimated GDP, save in exceptional circumstances.","src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"Where the Appropriation Act has not been passed at the beginning of a financial year, the President may authorise withdrawals from the Consolidated Revenue Fund for a maximum period of","o":["three months","six months","one month","twelve months","nine months"],"a":1,"w":"Section 82 permits withdrawals for up to six months or until the Appropriation Act is passed, whichever is earlier.","src":"Chapter 2.2","sec":"2.2"},{"q":"Scheduled corporations are required by the Fiscal Responsibility Act to remit to the Consolidated Revenue Fund","o":["50% of operating surplus","80% of operating surplus","100% of operating surplus","25% of gross revenue","80% of gross revenue"],"a":1,"w":"The Act requires 80% of the operating surplus, computed in accordance with the Commission's template, to be paid over.","src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"Financial Regulations derive their authority from the","o":["1999 Constitution directly","Finance (Control and Management) Act","Audit Act","Public Procurement Act","Financial Reporting Council Act"],"a":1,"w":"The Minister of Finance issues them under the Finance (Control and Management) Act 1958, which vests in him the supervision of the finances of the Federation.","src":"Chapter 2.7","sec":"2.7"},{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from","o":["1 January 2014","1 January 2016","1 January 2012","1 January 2018","1 January 2020"],"a":1,"w":"Cash basis IPSAS took effect from 1 January 2014 and accrual basis IPSAS from 1 January 2016.","src":"Chapter 2.12","sec":"2.12"},{"q":"Which law regulates financial matters and payment or withdrawal from the Consolidated Revenue Fund?","o":["Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007","Public Procurement Act 2007","The Financial Regulations (2009 Edition)","The Annual / Supplementary Appropriation Act","Finance and Treasury circulars"],"a":3,"w":"Money bills, once passed, become Appropriation Acts, which regulate withdrawals from the Consolidated Revenue Fund for the year to which they relate.","src":"Chapter 2.14 (study text Section A Q4)","sec":"2.14"},{"q":"The administrative tool used to amend existing provisions of the Financial Regulations and public service rules, and to introduce new policy guidelines, is the","o":["Financial Regulations","Finance / Treasury circular","Revised Financial Memoranda for Local Government","Medium-Term Expenditure Framework","Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007"],"a":1,"w":"Finance / Treasury circulars are administrative instruments issued to amend the Financial Regulations and public service rules and to introduce new policy.","src":"Chapter 2.14 (study text Section A Q2)","sec":"2.14"},{"q":"The Act that prescribes the basis for distributing revenue in the Federation Account among the Federal, State and Local Governments is the","o":["Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007","Public Procurement Act 2007","Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act 1982","Finance (Control and Management) Act 1958","Financial Reporting Council Act 2011"],"a":2,"w":"The Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act 1982 (Cap. A.15 LFN 2004) prescribes the vertical and horizontal distribution formulae.","src":"Chapter 2.14 (study text Section A Q5)","sec":"2.14"}],"theory":[{"q":"The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 was enacted to promote prudent management of the nation's resources. Explain the principal provisions of the Act and assess the extent to which it has strengthened fiscal discipline in Nigeria.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Purpose of the Act"},{"p":"The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 provides for prudent management of the nation's resources, ensures long-term macroeconomic stability, secures greater accountability and transparency in fiscal operations within a medium-term fiscal framework, and establishes the Fiscal Responsibility Commission to enforce these objectives."},{"h4":"Principal provisions"},{"ol":["**The Medium Term Expenditure Framework.** The Federal Government must prepare an MTEF covering the next three financial years, containing a macroeconomic framework, a fiscal strategy paper, an expenditure and revenue framework, and a consolidated debt statement. It must be approved by the Federal Executive Council and laid before the National Assembly not later than four months before the end of the financial year.","**The annual budget must derive from the MTEF.** The estimates of revenue and expenditure laid before the National Assembly must be consistent with the approved framework, and any estimate inconsistent with it is invalid. This converts the budget from an annual improvisation into the first year of a rolling medium-term plan.","**A limit on the deficit.** Aggregate expenditure may not exceed estimated aggregate revenue by more than 3% of estimated Gross Domestic Product, or such other percentage as the National Assembly may approve in exceptional circumstances such as a threat to national security.","**Control of borrowing.** Government at all tiers may borrow only for capital expenditure and human development, only on concessional terms with low interest and a reasonably long amortisation period, and only within the debt limit set by the National Assembly. A cost-benefit analysis of the project to be financed must precede the borrowing.","**Remittance of operating surpluses.** Corporations listed in the Schedule must prepare annual budgets and estimates, submit them to the Minister, and pay 80% of their operating surplus into the Consolidated Revenue Fund. This addressed the long-standing practice of agencies retaining substantial internally generated revenue.","**Budget execution and cash management.** Ministries must prepare disbursement schedules, and a cash plan is drawn up so that releases follow revenue rather than creating arrears.","**Savings from excess revenue.** Revenue in excess of the budget benchmark is to be saved rather than spent, a provision that gave rise to the Excess Crude Account and later the Sovereign Wealth Fund.","**Transparency and public access.** The Act requires publication of the budget, the MTEF and audited accounts, and gives any person the right to enforce its provisions in court after giving written notice to the Commission.","**The Fiscal Responsibility Commission.** An independent body to monitor and enforce compliance, to compel remittance of surpluses, and to investigate breaches."]},{"h4":"Assessment"},{"p":"**Where it has strengthened discipline:**"},{"ul":["Budgeting is now framed within a three-year horizon linked to explicit oil price and production benchmarks, which has reduced the purely incremental budgeting of earlier years.","The requirement to remit operating surpluses has recovered substantial sums from agencies that previously retained them, and the Commission publishes the amounts due and paid.","Borrowing is now formally tied to capital projects and to a debt limit, and the debt position is published.","Publication requirements have improved the availability of fiscal information to the legislature, analysts and the public."]},{"p":"**Where it has fallen short:**"},{"ul":["**Weak enforcement powers.** The Commission may investigate and report but has limited power to sanction. Breaches by ministries have generally attracted no penalty.","**Persistent breach of the deficit rule.** The 3% limit has been exceeded in several years, using the \"exceptional circumstances\" exemption widely.","**Incomplete adoption by the States.** The Act binds the Federal Government; States were expected to enact their own fiscal responsibility laws, and adoption has been uneven, so the framework does not cover the whole of general government.","**Late budgets.** The requirement that the MTEF precede the estimates has often been observed late, compressing legislative scrutiny.","**Incomplete remittance.** Many agencies remain outside the Schedule or dispute the computation of operating surplus, and arrears persist."]},{"p":"**Conclusion.** The Act has materially improved the *framework* of fiscal management — medium-term planning, published rules, an identified enforcement body. Its weakness is in enforcement rather than design: rules that carry no effective sanction and that admit a broad exemption are followed only when it is convenient. Strengthening the Commission's sanctioning powers, extending the Act to all tiers, and narrowing the exceptional-circumstances exemption would do more than further legislation."}],"src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Standardisation of Reporting Format","brief":"Why every tier of Nigerian government now shares one chart of accounts, what its six segments actually classify, and the full, statement-by-statement shape of a General Purpose Financial Statement under both cash IPSAS and accrual IPSAS — plus GIFMIS and the Treasury Single Account, which this study text's own chapter never quite reaches, even though the reform they represent is the whole reason the chart of accounts and the reporting formats below could be standardised at all.","outcomes":["Understand the purpose and process of implementation of the National Chart of Accounts (NCOA)","Describe the structure of the National Chart of Accounts","Explain the financial reporting format under Cash IPSAS","Explain the financial reporting format under Accrual IPSAS"],"secs":[{"n":"3.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Nigeria, a leading African nation with a population of over 150 million and a foremost OPEC member with a public-sector-dominated economy, has identified the need to consider the value of IPSAS and has implemented same in order to remain relevant in the comity of nations."},{"note":"Read past the somewhat grand phrasing to the actual claim underneath it: Nigeria's public sector is large enough, relative to its whole economy, that international lenders, rating agencies and investors treat the *credibility of government accounts* as a proxy for the credibility of the economy as a whole. That is the practical reason a chapter on chart-of-accounts standardisation matters at all — it is not bureaucratic tidiness for its own sake, it is what makes Nigeria's fiscal statistics legible, and therefore trusted, internationally."}]},{"n":"3.2","t":"The National Chart of Accounts (NCOA) for General Purpose Financial Reporting and budgeting","b":[{"p":"The FAAC sub-committee developed a comprehensive and standardised National Chart of Accounts. The National Chart of Accounts is an integrated budget and accounting classification system prepared primarily for the implementation of a uniform accounting system that is IPSAS compliant."},{"note":"This section's own heading is examinable in its own right: **General Purpose Financial Reports (GPFRs)** is the umbrella term — every report a public-sector entity issues for users who cannot demand information to meet their own specific needs. **General Purpose Financial Statements (GPFS)** are one part of that: the formal statements of financial position, performance, cash flows and so on. Every GPFS is a GPFR; not every GPFR (e.g. a budget or a service-performance report) is a GPFS."},{"def":{"t":"National Chart of Accounts (NCOA)","d":"a complete list of budget and accounting items, where each item is uniquely represented by a code and grouped into tables of related data, for the purposes of tracking, managing and reporting budgetary and accounting items in an orderly, efficient and transparent manner."}},{"p":"It is also a created list of codes, which can be represented by numeric, alphabetic, or alphanumeric symbols. This is to enable the entity to define each item of revenue, expenditure, asset, liability, location and other parameters, in order to give interested parties a better understanding of the entity."},{"h3":"Objectives of the NCOA"},{"ol":["To give ample opportunity for comparability;","Unification and harmonisation of the coding, budgeting, accounting and reporting system;","To bring about global interpretation of Nigeria's General Purpose Financial Statements (GPFS);","Nationally consistent financial reporting;","Improvement in transparency and accountability;","To facilitate ease of computerisation of the accounting system."]},{"h3":"Features of the NCOA"},{"p":"NCOA is based on the following key features:"},{"ol":["NCOA was designed after due consultations with all Local Government Councils, States and the Federal Government of Nigeria, taking into consideration their peculiar needs;","Expandable / flexible;","Each item has a unique code;","Used for both budgeting and accounting;","IPSAS cash and accrual basis compliant;","Government Financial Statistics (GFS) 2001 compliant; and","Compliance with the Classification of the Functions of Government (COFOG)."]},{"warn":"The source text's own list of features prints the seventh item as **\"Incompliance with the classification of functions of government (COFOG)\"** — the word \"In\" before \"compliance\" breaks the pattern of everything around it: items (v) and (vi) both list a standard the NCOA **complies** with (IPSAS, GFS 2001), so a chart of accounts feature list would not suddenly, and without any explanation, list *non*-compliance with a third standard as a \"feature\" to be proud of. This is corrected above to \"**Compliance** with COFOG\" — matching both the pattern of the list and the fact that COFOG is exactly the \"functional\" segment described next."},{"h3":"Structure of the National Chart of Accounts"},{"p":"The NCOA is built from **six segments**, each classifying the same transaction along a different, independent dimension — so a single posting can be reported by ministry, by economic nature, by government function, by programme, by fund and by location, all at once, without ever being re-analysed."},{"ol":["**Administrative segment** — assigns responsibility for each transaction, whether a revenue centre (receipt) or a cost centre (payment).","**Economic segment** — every receipt must be from a particular source, e.g. contractor registration fee; likewise, every expense must be on a particular item or object, e.g. purchase of drugs and medical supplies. It answers the **\"What\"** question of every transaction.","**Functional segment** — functional classification categorises expenditure according to the purposes and objectives for which it is intended. Functional classification, or **classification of the functions of government (COFOG)**, is defined as a detailed classification of the functions, or socio-economic objectives, that general government units aim to achieve through various kinds of outlays. It organises government activities according to their broad objectives or purposes (for example, education, social security, housing, etc.). Government expenditure is measured according to internationally recognised functional categories, and this classification is especially useful in analysing the allocation of resources among sectors. Functions and sub-functions are assigned at the point of budget and planning for every transaction, or at initial set-up.","**Programme segment** — the programme classification identifies the various sets of activities used to meet specific policy objectives of the government, e.g. pre-primary education, poverty alleviation and food security.","**Fund segment** — the fund segment addresses the **\"Financed by\"** element of a transaction. A fund refers to the various pools of resources for financing government activities. It fast-tracks the implementation of IPSAS, particularly with respect to the full disclosure of government revenue, including external assistance.","**Geographic segment** — addresses the **\"Where\"** (location/station) element of every transaction. It is for the location or physical existence of a transaction, so that an analysis of government budget and expenditure along the various geo-political zones, states and local government councils in the country can be done. The use of geographic codes makes it easier for agencies with an oversight function, such as monitoring and evaluation (M&E) mandates, to locate projects across the country."]},{"key":"Learn the six segments by the **question each one answers**, not just its name: Administrative = **who**; Economic = **what**; Functional = **why / for what purpose**; Programme = **under which policy**; Fund = **financed by what**; Geographic = **where**. A question that gives you a scenario and asks \"which segment is this?\" is testing exactly this mapping."},{"h3":"Advantages of the NCOA"},{"ol":["NCOA harmonised all the various charts of accounts of the Federal, States and Local Government Councils into a standardised chart of accounts that enhances the attainment of a minimum reporting requirement in line with international best practice.","NCOA comprises the coding of items used for classification, budgeting, accounting and reporting within the financial year.","It serves to facilitate the recording of all transactions and is directly linked to the General Purpose Financial Statements (GPFS)."]}]},{"n":"3.3","t":"Financial reporting format — Cash IPSAS","b":[{"p":"Financial statements are a structured representation of the financial position and financial performance of an entity, which may be the government or the private sector. General Purpose Financial Statements (GPFS) are those intended to fulfil the information needs of users who are not in a position to demand reports tailored to meet their particular information needs — taxpayers, suppliers, employees, etc."},{"p":"Cash basis GPFS are financial statements prepared based on the cash basis of accounting. The cash basis of accounting is the recognition and recording of income and expenses only when cash (income) is actually received and the expenses are actually paid."},{"h3":"Structure of cash basis IPSAS"},{"p":"IPSASB issued only **one** standard on cash basis IPSAS, whereas **40** standards have been issued on accrual basis IPSAS. The cash basis standard is structured so that it meets two requirements: **mandatory requirements**, and **additional disclosures**."},{"ul":["**Mandatory requirements** are requirements that are applicable to all entities preparing cash-based GPFS, and must be complied with by entities which claim to be reporting in accordance with the cash basis IPSAS.","**Additional disclosures** identify additional accounting policies and disclosures that an entity is *encouraged* to adopt, and include explanations of alternative methods of presenting certain information. Cash IPSAS encourages the disclosure of additional information that is useful to the understanding of the GPFS."]},{"h3":"Objectives of cash basis IPSAS"},{"ol":["Cash basis IPSAS prescribes the manner in which the GPFS on the cash basis should be presented;","Accountability about the cash receipts, cash payments and cash balances;","Transparency about the allocation of cash resources;","Assessment of the ability of the entity to generate adequate cash in the future;","Enhances comparability; and","Provides a sound base for transition to the accrual basis."]},{"h3":"Components of cash basis IPSAS"},{"p":"Cash basis IPSAS provides for the presentation of the following statements in the GPFS:"},{"ol":["Statement of cash receipts and payments (Cash Flow Statement);","Accounting policies and explanatory notes; and","Comparison of budget and actual amounts."]},{"h3":"The financial reports currently prepared in the public sector in Nigeria"},{"p":"At present, the following financial statements are prepared as financial reports in the public sector in Nigeria:"},{"ol":["**Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund** — a statement of the recurrent income and expenditure of an entity over a financial period. It shows the overall financial performance of a reporting entity after deduction of the appropriate expenses. All revenues accruing to government are paid into this account. The CRF statement will eventually be replaced with the Statement of Financial Performance when the entity migrates to accrual basis IPSAS.","**Statement of Assets and Liabilities** — presents the government's financial position at a specific point in time. It includes assets and liabilities: assets are grouped into two categories, namely liquid assets and investments, and other cash assets; liabilities are grouped into two, namely public funds, and external and internal loans.","**Statement of Capital Development Fund** — one of the statutory financial statements of the three tiers of government. It shows funds transferred from the Consolidated Revenue Fund and other borrowings (loans), used to finance capital-oriented projects for an accounting period. It is also known as the Capital Income Statement.","**Cash flow statements** — the cash flow statement (statement of receipts and payments) is one of the statements required under cash IPSAS. It identifies the sources of cash inflows, the items on which cash was expended during the reporting period, and the cash balance as at the reporting date. Cash flows are reported under three separate activities: (a) operating activities; (b) investing activities; and (c) financing activities.","**Notes to the accounts** — additional information to support items disclosed in the statements presented in the GPFS.","**Performance reports** — show the performance of a reporting entity, comparing actual performance with the approved budget. Though not recognised as one of the principal statements in GPFS, this is very essential, as the reports enable users to ascertain the budget performance of the reporting entity.","**Statistical report** — also useful to certain categories of users of GPFS for their decision-making. Statistical reports include: functional report; programme report; and geo-location report.","**Accounting policies** — the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices adopted by an entity in preparing and presenting financial statements. They are part of the financial statements. Entities should select and apply accounting policies so that the financial statements comply with the requirements of each applicable International Public Sector Accounting Standard."]},{"h3":"What an entity must additionally disclose"},{"p":"An entity should disclose the following, if not disclosed elsewhere in information published with the financial statements:"},{"ol":["The domicile and legal form of the entity, and the jurisdiction within which it operates;","A description of the nature of the entity's operations and principal activities;","A reference to the relevant legislation governing the entity's operations, if any;","An entity should be in a position to issue its financial statements within **six months** of the reporting date, although a timeframe of not more than **three months** is strongly encouraged;","Under IPSAS cash basis, an entity should disclose the date when the financial statements were authorised for issue, and who gave that authorisation. If another body has the power to amend the financial statements after issuance, the entity should disclose that fact."]}]},{"n":"3.4","t":"Financial reporting format — Accrual IPSAS","b":[{"def":{"t":"Accrual based GPFS","d":"financial statements prepared based on the accrual basis of accounting — a basis of accounting under which transactions and other events are recognised when they occur (and not only when cash or its equivalent is received or paid)."}},{"h3":"The difference between cash GPFS and accrual GPFS"},{"p":"The difference between Cash and Accrual GPFS stems from the basis of accounting adopted by the reporting entity. Under the accrual basis, transactions and other events are recognised when they occur; the cash basis recognises transactions and other events only when cash or its equivalent is received or paid. Accrual-based GPFS discloses information on tangible assets, accounts receivable and payables, while cash-based GPFS does not disclose them. Accrual-based GPFS also differs from cash-based GPFS in terms of its components — the principal statements in accrual-based GPFS are different from those of the cash-based set."},{"p":"IPSAS 1 covers presentation of financial statements as a whole, while issues of recognition, measurement and presentation of line items in the financial statements are covered by the other issued standards (IPSAS 2–41)."},{"h3":"A complete set of accrual-based GPFS"},{"p":"A complete set of accrual-based GPFS includes the following components:"},{"h4":"(a) Statement of financial position"},{"p":"A statement that shows assets, liabilities and net assets/equity of an entity. Both assets and liabilities are categorised as current and non-current in the statement of financial position."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["","Includes"],"rows":[["Current assets","Cash and its equivalents; receivables; inventories; prepayments; and other current assets"],["Non-current assets","Loans granted (receivables); investments; infrastructure; plant and equipment; investment property; and intangible assets"],["Current liabilities","Deposits; short-term loans and advances; unremitted deductions; accrued expenses; and the current portion of borrowings"],["Non-current liabilities","Public funds and long-term borrowings"]]}},{"note":"All the above sub-groupings, as a minimum requirement, must be disclosed on the face of the statement of financial position. Assets are, however, treated in the statement of financial position **net of all provisions**, while the details are disclosed in the notes to the financial statements."},{"h4":"(b) Statement of financial performance"},{"p":"The statement of financial performance (income and expenditure account) shows income accrued to the entity from all sources, and expenditure incurred, during the period. As a minimum requirement, it should include the following line items:"},{"ol":["Revenue from operating activities;","Surplus or deficit from operating activities;","Finance costs;","Share of net surpluses or deficits of associates and joint ventures accounted for using the equity method;","Surplus or deficit from ordinary activities;","Extraordinary items;","Minority interest share of net surplus or deficit; and","Net surplus or deficit for the period."]},{"p":"Expenses are classified either **by nature** or **by function** within the entity. If an entity decides to classify expenses by function, it must also provide a presentation by nature of expenses in the notes."},{"h4":"(c) Statement of cash flow"},{"p":"One of the statements required by IPSAS 1 to be presented in the GPFS. The cash flow statement identifies the sources of cash inflows, the items on which cash was expended during the reporting period, and the cash balance as at the reporting date. The preparation and presentation of the cash flow statement is covered by IPSAS 2. Cash flows are reported under three separate activities:"},{"ul":["**Operating activities** — activities of an entity that are not investing or financing activities. These are the day-to-day activities of an entity.","**Investing activities** — the acquisition and disposal of long-term assets, and other investments not included in cash equivalents.","**Financing activities** — activities that result in changes in the size and composition of the contributed capital and borrowings."]},{"p":"IPSAS 2 recognises two methods of preparing the cash flow statement:"},{"ol":["**Direct method** — major classes of gross cash receipts and gross cash payments are disclosed. Entities using the direct method should provide a reconciliation of the surplus/deficit with the net cash from operating activities.","**Indirect method** — net surplus/deficit is adjusted for the effect of, for instance, transactions of a non-cash nature, like depreciation."]},{"key":"**IPSAS 2 recommends the direct method.** This mirrors IAS 7 in Financial Accounting (Chapter 14 there), where the direct method is likewise the presentation the standard encourages, even though the indirect method is what is far more commonly seen in practice, because it can be built directly from the other two statements without a separate analysis of gross receipts and payments."},{"h4":"(d) Statement of changes in net assets/equity"},{"p":"Net assets/equity refers to assets less liabilities. Net assets/equity is financed by reserves, accumulated surpluses/deficits, minority interest, etc. The statement is important in GPFS because it enables users to ascertain the causes of movement in the net equity of an entity. Changes in net assets/equity are normally caused by:"},{"ol":["Significant changes in accounting policies;","Correction of prior years' errors;","Revaluation of assets;","Surplus or deficit for the period;","Changes in currency translation."]},{"h4":"(e) Notes and other disclosures to GPFS"},{"p":"Notes and other disclosures to GPFS are additional information presented in the GPFS to enable users to understand the financial statements better and compare them with those of other entities. Notes include narrative descriptions, or more detailed schedules or analyses of amounts shown on the face of the statement of financial performance, statement of financial position, cash flow statement and statement of changes in net assets/equity, as well as additional information such as contingent liabilities and commitments."},{"p":"IPSAS has not specifically provided formats for the preparation of notes and other disclosures to the financial statements, but, as a minimum requirement, notes are normally presented in the following order:"},{"ol":["Statement of compliance with IPSAS;","Statement of the measurement basis (bases) and accounting policies applied;","Supporting information for items presented on the face of each financial statement, in the order in which each line item and each financial statement is presented; and","Other disclosures, including contingencies, commitments, and other financial and non-financial disclosures."]},{"h4":"(f) Statement of accounting policies"},{"p":"Accounting policies are the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices adopted by an entity in preparing and presenting financial statements. They are part of the financial statements. Entities should select and apply accounting policies so that the financial statements comply with all the requirements of each applicable International Public Sector Accounting Standard (IPSAS)."}]},{"n":"3.5","t":"GIFMIS and the Treasury Single Account","b":[{"warn":"This study text's own Chapter 3 stops at the reporting formats above — it never actually names GIFMIS, IPPIS or the Treasury Single Account, even though the reform those three represent is what made the National Chart of Accounts, and the standardised reporting formats above, achievable in the first place. Because this is essential, closely related context — and because it is genuinely examinable — a compact treatment is given here rather than leaving the gap unfilled."},{"def":{"t":"GIFMIS","d":"the Government Integrated Financial Management Information System — an integrated computerised platform covering budget preparation, budget execution, commitment control, payments, accounting, cash management, payroll (through IPPIS) and reporting, built on the National Chart of Accounts."}},{"h4":"Benefits of GIFMIS"},{"ul":["A single, real-time record of government financial transactions, eliminating reconciliation between disconnected ledgers.","Automatic **commitment control**: an order cannot be raised where the vote is exhausted, which prevents the accumulation of unfunded arrears.","Faster and more reliable production of the monthly transcript and the annual accounts.","A stronger audit trail, since every transaction is time-stamped and attributed to a user.","Better cash management, because balances across government are visible.","Reduction of ghost workers when combined with the **IPPIS** payroll platform."]},{"def":{"t":"Treasury Single Account (TSA)","d":"a unified structure of government bank accounts, operated through the Central Bank of Nigeria, which gives a consolidated view of government cash resources. Implemented fully in Nigeria from September 2015, with revenue collected through the Remita platform sweeping into the TSA daily."}},{"h4":"Benefits of the TSA"},{"ul":["Consolidation of idle balances that were previously spread across thousands of accounts in commercial banks, reducing the need to borrow while holding cash.","Elimination of the interest earned privately on public funds and of the associated incentives.","A complete daily picture of government cash, which makes cash planning possible.","Blocking of unauthorised accounts and of revenue diversion.","Reduced cost of debt service, since borrowing no longer coexists with idle balances."]},{"note":"The examinable criticism of the TSA is the strain it placed on deposit money banks, which lost substantial public sector deposits, and the administrative difficulty faced by agencies with genuine operational needs for local payments. A balanced answer states both sides."}]},{"n":"3.6","t":"Further reading","b":[{"note":"The [IPSASB handbook](https://www.ipsasb.org/publications) publishes the full text of the Cash Basis IPSAS and every accrual IPSAS free of charge — worth reading IPSAS 1 and IPSAS 2 alongside this chapter, since this chapter is built directly on them. The [Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation](https://oagf.gov.ng) publishes Nigeria's current General Purpose Financial Statements and, where available, documentation on the National Chart of Accounts and GIFMIS."}]},{"n":"3.7","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer. The printed Section A key is misnumbered in the scan; it is realigned below."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is **not** a user of the National Chart of Accounts (NCOA): (A) Finance / Accounts officer in ministries/MDAs  (B) Legislators and other political leaders  (C) Nigeria Bottling Company Plc  (D) Donors and other development partners  (E) Budget / Planning officers in ministries/MDAs.","One of the following is **not** a member of the Law & Justice sector of the NCOA: (A) Judicial Council  (B) National Population Commission  (C) Ministry of Justice  (D) ICPC  (E) Code of Conduct Bureau.","Revenue should be classified by fund and source to provide the necessary information. One of the following is **not** among that necessary information: (A) prepare and control the budget  (B) conduct an independent examination of financial statements and schedules  (C) record the collection of revenues  (D) prepare financial statements and schedules  (E) prepare financial statistics.","Which of the following is **not** part of the structure of the NCOA? (A) Functional segment  (B) Historic segment  (C) Geographic segment  (D) Programme segment  (E) Economic segment.","The following, except one, are objectives of Cash Basis IPSAS: (A) accountability for cash receipts, payments and balances  (B) transparency on the allocation of cash resources  (C) it enhances comparability  (D) it recognises transactions when they occur, not when paid for  (E) it provides a sound base for transition to the accrual basis.","The National Chart of Accounts was developed by …","The document giving a complete list of budget and accounting items, each uniquely represented by a code, is the …","The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices adopted by an entity in preparing and presenting financial statements are the …","Functional reports, programme reports and geo-location reports are an integral part of the …","The statement showing funds transferred from the Consolidated Revenue Fund and other borrowings to finance capital projects is the …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — Nigeria Bottling Company Plc.** A private company, not a user of the NCOA.","**B — National Population Commission.** Not part of the Law & Justice sector.","**B — \"conduct an independent examination of financial statements and schedules.\"** That is the auditor's function, not information provided by revenue classification.","**B — Historic segment.** The six NCOA segments are **Administrative, Economic, Functional, Programme, Funds and Geographic**.","**D — \"it recognises transactions when they occur, not when paid for.\"** That describes the *accrual* basis, not an objective of Cash Basis IPSAS.","**The FAAC Sub-committee** (Federation Account Allocation Committee sub-committee).","**The National Chart of Accounts (NCOA).**","**Accounting policies** (the Statement of Accounting Policies).","**Statistical reports** (part of the GPFS).","**The Statement of Capital Development Fund** (also called the Capital Income Statement)."]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Name any **five** financial statements prepared as financial reports in the Nigerian public sector, and explain any three.","Identify the differences between **GPFS-Cash** and **GPFS-Accrual**.","List **eight line items** contained in the Statement of Financial Performance.","State the causes of changes in net assets/equity, and give the order in which the Notes to the GPFS are arranged.","Under IPSAS 2, state and explain the separate activities under which cash flows are reported."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Financial statements in the Nigerian public sector (any five)"},{"ul":["**Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund** — recurrent income and expenditure over the period; shows overall financial performance after expenses; all government revenues are paid in. It becomes the **Statement of Financial Performance** on migration to accrual IPSAS.","**Statement of Assets and Liabilities** — the government's financial position at a point in time; assets split into *liquid assets/investments* and *other cash assets*, liabilities into *public funds* and *external and internal loans*.","**Statement of Capital Development Fund** — funds transferred from the CRF plus borrowings, used to finance capital projects; the Capital Income Statement.","**Cash flow statement** (statement of cash receipts and payments) — sources of cash inflows, items of expenditure and the closing cash balance, under operating, investing and financing activities.","**Notes to the accounts** — additional information supporting the statements.","**Performance reports** — actual performance against the approved budget (not a principal statement, but essential).","**Statistical reports** — functional, programme and geo-location reports.","**Accounting policies** — the principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices adopted."]},{"h4":"2  GPFS-Cash vs GPFS-Accrual"},{"ul":["The difference stems from the **basis of accounting** adopted by the reporting entity.","Under **accrual**, transactions are recognised **when they occur**; under **cash**, only when cash or its equivalent is received or paid.","Accrual GPFS **disclose** tangible assets, receivables and payables; cash GPFS **do not**.","The two also differ in their **components** — the principal statements of accrual GPFS differ from those of cash GPFS."]},{"h4":"3  Line items in the Statement of Financial Performance"},{"ol":["Revenue from operating activities.","Surplus or deficit from operating activities.","Finance costs.","Share of net surpluses or deficits of associates and joint ventures accounted for by the equity method.","Surplus or deficit from ordinary activities.","Extraordinary items.","Minority interest share of net surplus or deficit.","Net surplus or deficit for the period."]},{"h4":"4  Changes in net assets/equity, and the order of the Notes"},{"p":"**Causes of changes in net assets/equity:**"},{"ol":["Significant changes in accounting policies;","Correction of prior-year errors;","Revaluation of assets;","Surplus or deficit for the period;","Changes in currency translation."]},{"p":"**Order of the Notes:**"},{"ol":["Statement of compliance with IPSAS;","Statement of the measurement bases and accounting policies applied;","Supporting information for items on the face of each statement, in the order the line items and statements are presented;","Other disclosures — contingencies, commitments, and other financial and non-financial disclosures."]},{"h4":"5  Cash flow activities (IPSAS 2)"},{"ul":["**Operating activities** — the day-to-day activities of the entity that are not investing or financing activities.","**Investing activities** — the acquisition and disposal of long-term assets and other investments not included in cash equivalents.","**Financing activities** — activities that change the size and composition of the contributed capital and borrowings."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One or two Section A marks on the segments of the chart of accounts, on the components of cash versus accrual GPFS, or on the meaning of GIFMIS, IPPIS and TSA. In Section B it appears as a discussion question — the objectives and segments of the NCOA, the components of a complete set of accrual GPFS, or the benefits and problems of the Treasury Single Account. Learn the six NCOA segments as a list of six with the question each answers — the source text does not have a seventh \"financing/donor\" segment, whatever an older or more general source might suggest.","errors":["Listing seven NCOA segments, or inventing a \"financing/donor\" segment; the source names six: administrative, economic, functional, programme, fund and geographic.","Confusing GIFMIS (the financial management system) with IPPIS (the payroll platform) or with the TSA (the banking arrangement).","Omitting the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts from the required statements under either basis.","Describing the functional segment as the ministry; the ministry is the administrative segment, the function is health, education, defence and so on.","Naming the indirect method as the one IPSAS 2 recommends; it recommends the direct method, even though the indirect method is more common in practice."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"In the National Chart of Accounts, classification by ministry, department or agency is the","o":["economic segment","administrative segment","functional segment","programme segment","fund segment"],"a":1,"w":"The administrative segment answers \"who spent it\". The economic segment answers \"on what, by nature\" and the functional segment \"for what purpose of government\".","src":"Chapter 3.2","sec":"3.2"},{"q":"The full meaning of the acronym GPFRs is","o":["General Public Financial Regulations","Government Public Finance Reports","General Purpose Financial Reports","General Purpose Financial Returns","Government Purpose Financial Reports"],"a":2,"w":"GPFRs is the umbrella term for every report issued to users who cannot demand information to meet their own needs. GPFS (financial statements) is one part of it, not a synonym for it.","src":"Chapter 3.2","sec":"3.2"},{"q":"GIFMIS stands for","o":["Government Integrated Fiscal Management Information Service","Government Integrated Financial Management Information System","General Integrated Financial Monitoring Information System","Government Internal Financial Management and Internal Systems","Government Institutional Financial Management Information Service"],"a":1,"w":"It is the integrated platform covering budgeting, commitment control, payments, accounting and reporting, built on the National Chart of Accounts.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"The Treasury Single Account was fully implemented in Nigeria in","o":["2012","2015","2014","2016","2011"],"a":1,"w":"Full implementation followed the presidential directive of 2015, with collections sweeping into the CBN through Remita from September of that year.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"Under a complete set of accrual-based GPFS, \"revenue from operating activities\" is a minimum line item required in the","o":["Statement of financial position","Statement of financial performance","Statement of changes in net assets/equity","Statement of cash flow","Notes to the accounts"],"a":1,"w":"The statement of financial performance (the accrual-basis income and expenditure account) must show, as a minimum, revenue from operating activities through to the net surplus or deficit for the period.","src":"Chapter 3.4","sec":"3.4"},{"q":"Which financial statement is required under BOTH cash basis and accrual basis IPSAS?","o":["Statement of Financial Performance","Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","Statement of Cash Flows","Statement of Changes in Net Assets","Statement of Financial Position"],"a":1,"w":"Comparison with the budget is required under both bases, because the budget is a legal authority to spend rather than an internal plan.","src":"Chapter 3.3","sec":"3.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six segments of the National Chart of Accounts?","o":["Administrative segment","Historic segment","Functional segment","Programme segment","Funds segment"],"a":1,"w":"The six segments are Administrative, Economic, Functional, Programme, Funds and Geographic. There is no \"Historic\" segment.","src":"Chapter 3.6 (study text Section A Q4)","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of Cash Basis IPSAS?","o":["Accountability for cash receipts, payments and balances","Transparency on the allocation of cash resources","Recognising transactions when they occur rather than when paid","Enhancing comparability","Providing a sound base for transition to the accrual basis"],"a":2,"w":"Recognising transactions when they occur is the accrual basis. Cash Basis IPSAS records transactions only on receipt or payment of cash.","src":"Chapter 3.6 (study text Section A Q5)","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"The National Chart of Accounts (NCOA) was developed by the","o":["Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation","FAAC Sub-committee","Central Bank of Nigeria","Budget Office of the Federation","Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria"],"a":1,"w":"A sub-committee of the Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC) developed the standardised, IPSAS-compliant National Chart of Accounts.","src":"Chapter 3.6 (study text Section A Q6)","sec":"3.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the objectives of the National Chart of Accounts, describe its segments, and discuss the benefits which the Treasury Single Account has brought to public financial management in Nigeria, together with the problems it has created.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Objectives of the National Chart of Accounts"},{"ol":["To provide a **uniform classification** of all government receipts and payments across the Federal, State and Local Governments, in place of the incompatible classifications each previously operated.","To make **consolidation** of the accounts of all tiers into government-wide financial statements possible.","To align government reporting with the **IMF Government Finance Statistics Manual** and the UN Classification of the Functions of Government, so that Nigerian fiscal statistics are internationally comparable.","To integrate the **budget with the accounts**, since the same codes are used for appropriation, for commitment and for recording actual expenditure, permitting direct comparison of budget and actual.","To provide the asset, liability and net-asset codes necessary for **IPSAS-compliant accrual reporting**, which a purely cash-based classification did not contain.","To supply the coding structure required by **GIFMIS**."]},{"h4":"The segments"},{"p":"The chart is multi-dimensional: each transaction carries a code in every segment simultaneously, so one posting supports every dimension of reporting."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Segment","Classifies by","Example"],"rows":[["Administrative","Ministry, department or agency incurring the transaction","Federal Ministry of Education"],["Economic","The nature of the receipt or payment","Personnel cost; overhead; capital expenditure"],["Functional","The purpose of government served","Education; health; defence"],["Programme","The policy programme or project","Universal Basic Education"],["Fund","The fund from which the money comes","Consolidated Revenue Fund; Development Fund"],["Geographic","The location of the activity","State; local government area"]]}},{"h4":"Benefits of the Treasury Single Account"},{"ol":["**Consolidation of cash.** Balances formerly held in many thousands of accounts across commercial banks are now visible in one structure at the Central Bank, so that government knows daily what cash it actually has.","**Reduced cost of borrowing.** Government previously borrowed at commercial rates while simultaneously holding large idle deposits. Netting the two has reduced debt service costs materially.","**Elimination of leakage.** Revenue collected by agencies now sweeps automatically to the TSA rather than resting in accounts under agency control, which removed both the private interest earned on public money and much of the scope for diversion.","**Better cash planning and budget execution.** A complete daily cash position permits realistic disbursement schedules rather than releases made in ignorance of the aggregate position.","**Improved accountability and audit.** Every receipt and payment passes through a traceable channel, and unauthorised accounts were closed on implementation.","**Support for the wider reform.** The TSA supplies the transaction data on which GIFMIS and IPSAS reporting depend."]},{"h4":"Problems created"},{"ol":["**Liquidity shock to the banking system.** Deposit money banks lost very large public sector deposits over a short period, which tightened liquidity, raised interbank rates and contributed to job losses in the sector.","**Operational difficulty for agencies.** Institutions with genuine day-to-day payment needs — teaching hospitals, universities, embassies, agencies operating in remote areas — found centralised payment processing slow, and delays in releases disrupted operations.","**Dependence on a single platform.** Reliance on one payment gateway and on the Central Bank's systems creates a single point of failure; downtime halts government payments nationally.","**Transaction charges and disputes** over the fees payable on collections through the platform.","**Incomplete coverage.** Some funds and some tiers of government remained outside the arrangement for a considerable period, limiting the completeness of the consolidated view."]},{"p":"**Conclusion.** The Treasury Single Account has been among the most effective of the public financial management reforms, chiefly because it removed opportunity rather than relying on exhortation: money that sweeps automatically cannot be diverted by an official who intends to divert it. The costs it imposed on the banking sector and on agency operations were real but largely transitional, and are outweighed by the improvement in control and in the cost of debt."}],"src":"Chapter 3.2–3.5","sec":"3.2"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Pensions and Gratuity","brief":"The legal framework for pensions in Nigeria — the 1999 Constitution, the Armed Forces Pension Act, and the Pension Reform Act 2014 — and the institutions, contributions, Retirement Savings Account, benefits, deficiencies and offences of the Contributory Pension Scheme, including the Micro Pension Plan for the informal sector.","outcomes":["Discuss the provisions of the 1999 Constitution relating to pensions","Discuss the Armed Forces Pension Act No. 103 of 1979 as amended","Outline the objectives, scope and contribution rates of the Pension Reform Act 2014","Identify the categories of persons exempted from the Pension Reform Act 2014","Discuss the powers and functions of the key pension administration institutions","Discuss offences, penalties and enforcement under the Act"],"secs":[{"n":"4.1","t":"Definition of terms","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Pension**","A monthly payment made to a retired officer who has served for a statutory period. Pension is payable for a minimum period of five years or till death."],["**Gratuity**","A lump sum of money paid once to a retired officer who has served for a minimum of five years in service."],["**Pensionable emoluments**","The gross salary (basic salary and allowances) attached to a retiring officer’s substantive rank."],["**Withdrawal of service**","The cessation of service after an officer has served for a minimum of five years, but below ten years, qualifying him only for gratuity."],["**Retirement**","The cessation of service after an officer has served for a minimum of ten years, qualifying him for both gratuity and pension."],["**Qualifying service**","Service after an officer has served for a period of not less than the minimum qualifying years — five years for gratuity, ten years for gratuity and pension. Qualifying service determines the qualification, or otherwise, of the person for pension and gratuity."],["**Next of kin**","Those persons whose names were furnished by the deceased officer on his record of service kept in the Records Office of the Establishment, or furnished by him to the Ministry, in writing, at any time before his death."],["**Public service**","Any service or employment under the government of the Federation in a civil position, recognised as such by the establishment. It includes employment declared as approved service by the Pension Act, as updated."]]}},{"note":"Read “withdrawal of service” and “retirement” together: five to under ten years’ service yields **gratuity only**; ten years or more yields **both gratuity and pension**. This five/ten-year boundary recurs throughout the chapter and is a common source of examination marks."},{"h4":"Abbreviations used in this chapter"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Abbreviation","Full meaning"],"rows":[["**CPS**","Contributory Pension Scheme (§4.4)"],["**RSA**","Retirement Savings Account (§4.6)"],["**PenCom**","National Pension Commission (§4.7)"],["**PFA**","Pension Fund Administrator (§4.8)"],["**PFC**","Pension Fund Custodian (§4.9)"],["**PTAD**","Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (§4.16) — administers the old Defined Benefit Scheme for those exempted from the CPS"],["**RLA**","Retiree Life Annuity — one of the two ways (with PW) a retiree may draw down an RSA: a life annuity purchased from a NAICOM-licensed insurer (§4.6)"],["**PW**","Programmed Withdrawal — the other of the two draw-down options: scheduled monthly/quarterly payments calculated on expected life span (§4.6)"]]}}]},{"n":"4.2","t":"The 1999 Constitutional provisions","b":[{"p":"The Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 1999 (as amended) makes direct provision for pensions in the public service."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section","Provision"],"rows":[["173(3)","“Pensions shall be reviewed every five years or together with any Federal civil service salary reviews, whichever is earlier.”"],["173(4)","“Pensions in respect of service in the public service of the Federation shall not be taxed.”"],["84(5)","“Any person who has held office as President or Vice President shall be entitled to pension for life at a rate equivalent to the annual salary of the incumbent President or Vice-President, provided that such a person was not removed from office by the process of impeachment or for breach of any provision of this Constitution.”"],["84(6)","“Any pension granted by virtue of subsection (5) of this section shall be a charge upon the Consolidated Revenue Fund of the Federation.”"]]}},{"key":"Section 173(3) and (4) apply to public servants generally: five-yearly review, and exemption from tax. Section 84(5) and (6) are a separate, narrower provision for a former President or Vice President specifically, charged directly on the Consolidated Revenue Fund rather than paid from a funded scheme."}]},{"n":"4.3","t":"The Armed Forces Pension Act No. 103 of 1979","b":[{"def":{"t":"War services","d":"Services in connection with the internal security, maintenance of law and order, between 27 May 1967 and 15 January 1970, and other services as may be declared by the President of Nigeria."}},{"p":"Under the Act, as amended by CAP 119 and 212 LFN 2004, the calculation of **war service years** follows special rules:"},{"ol":["Each completed year of war service counts as **two years**.","A period of war service exceeding 4 months but below 6 months is counted as 6 months.","A period of war service below 4 months is counted as 6 months.","A period of war service exceeding 6 months is counted as 1 year.","A period of service **other than war service** exceeding 6 months is approximated to one year, provided the officer has served the qualifying service in the first instance."]},{"eg":{"t":"War service years","q":[{"p":"An officer has served 14 years, 8 months and 10 days. A second officer has served 9 years, 11 months and 28 days. Determine each officer’s entitlement."}],"a":[{"p":"The first officer is deemed to have rendered **15 years** in service (the 8 months 10 days rounds up under the approximation rule), and so qualifies for both gratuity and pension."},{"p":"The second officer, despite being just 2 days short of 10 years, is entitled to **gratuity only**, because he has not rendered the minimum qualifying service of 10 years. The rule is applied strictly at the 10-year boundary."}]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Rates of contribution under the Armed Forces Pension Act","rows":[{"lb":"By government","tex":"12.5\\% \\text{ of basic salary and allowances}"},{"lb":"By military staff","tex":"8\\% \\text{ of basic salary and allowances}"}]}},{"warn":"These 12.5%/8% rates apply specifically to **military staff** under this older, separate Act. Do not confuse them with the 10%/8% rates that apply generally under the Pension Reform Act 2014 (section 4.4)."}]},{"n":"4.4","t":"The Pension Reform Act 2014 — scope, objectives and contributions","b":[{"p":"The **Pension Reform Act 2014** repeals the Pension Reform Act 2004 and continues to govern and regulate the administration of a **uniform contributory pension scheme** for both the public and private sectors in Nigeria."},{"h4":"Scope"},{"ul":["Any employment in the public service of the Federation, the public service of the Federal Capital Territory, the public service of state governments, the public service of local government councils, and the **private sector**.","In the private sector, the scheme applies to employees of an organisation in which there are **15 or more employees**.","Employees of organisations with fewer than three employees, and self-employed persons, may participate under the scheme in accordance with guidelines issued by the Commission (the **Micro Pension Plan**, section 4.17)."]},{"h4":"Objectives of the Act"},{"ol":["Establish a uniform set of rules, regulations and standards for the administration and payment of retirement benefits for the public service of the Federation, the FCT, state governments, local government councils and the private sector.","Make provision for the smooth operation of the contributory pension scheme.","Ensure that every person who worked in either the public service of the Federation, the FCT, state governments, local government councils or the private sector receives his retirement benefits as and when due.","Assist improvident individuals by ensuring that they save in order to cater for their livelihood during old age."]},{"h4":"Rates of contribution"},{"fbox":{"h":"Minimum contribution rates (relating to monthly emoluments)","rows":[{"lb":"Employer, minimum","tex":"10\\%"},{"lb":"Employee, minimum","tex":"8\\%"},{"lb":"Combined minimum","tex":"18\\%"},{"lb":"Employer alone, if it elects to bear full responsibility","tex":"\\ge 20\\%"}]}},{"ul":["Deductions are made from the worker’s salary at source; government’s contribution is a **first charge on the Consolidated Revenue Fund of the Federation**.","The rates of contribution may, upon agreement between an employer and employee, be revised **upwards**, and the Commission must be notified of the revision.","An employee may, in addition to the statutory contributions, make **voluntary contributions** to his retirement savings account.","An employer may elect to bear full responsibility for the scheme, provided its own contribution is then not less than **20% of the employee’s monthly emoluments** (the employee then contributes nothing).","In addition to the contribution rates, employers must maintain **life insurance policies** in favour of employees for a minimum of **three times the annual total emoluments** of the individual.","Where the employer fails, refuses or omits to make payment as and when due, the employer must make arrangements to meet claims arising from the death of any staff during the period of default."]},{"note":"The Act itself refers only to “monthly emoluments” without spelling out what the term comprises in this chapter of the text. Section 4(1) of the Pension Reform Act 2014 defines it as **basic salary, housing allowance and transport allowance** — the same three components used in the computation example below. Other allowances are excluded unless the employer elects to include them."},{"eg":{"t":"Computing the contributions","q":[{"p":"An officer’s annual emoluments are: basic salary ₦2,400,000; housing allowance ₦600,000; transport allowance ₦300,000; utility allowance ₦180,000; meal subsidy ₦120,000. Compute the monthly employer and employee pension contributions and the total annual contribution to the officer’s Retirement Savings Account."}],"a":[{"p":"Only basic salary, housing and transport enter monthly emoluments. The utility allowance and meal subsidy are excluded."},{"stmt":{"t":"Annual pensionable emolument","rows":[["Basic salary",2400000],["Housing allowance",600000],["Transport allowance",300000],["Total annual emolument@tot",3300000]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Monthly emolument} = \\frac{3{,}300{,}000}{12} = ₦275{,}000"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Monthly (₦)","Annual (₦)"],"rows":[["Employer contribution at 10%","27,500","330,000"],["Employee contribution at 8%","22,000","264,000"],["**Total credited to the RSA**","**49,500**","**594,000**"]]}},{"p":"The employer’s ₦330,000 is a charge in the statement of financial performance for the year; the employee’s ₦264,000 is deducted from salary. The employer must also carry group life cover of at least $3 \\times ₦3{,}600{,}000 = ₦10{,}800{,}000$, taking the total annual emolument including the utility and meal allowances."},{"warn":"Applying the percentages to **gross pay** rather than to basic, housing and transport is the standard error. Here gross pay is ₦3,600,000 and would give an employer contribution of ₦360,000 instead of ₦330,000."}]}}]},{"n":"4.5","t":"Exemptions and retirement benefits under the 2014 Act","b":[{"h4":"Exemption from the scheme — section 5(1)"},{"ul":["The categories of persons mentioned in **section 291 of the Constitution**, including members of the Armed Forces and the Intelligence and Secret Services of the Federation.","An employee who is entitled to retirement benefits under any pension scheme existing before **25 June 2004** and who has **3 or fewer years to retire** (i.e. a fully funded pension scheme).","Any person falling within the two categories above continues to derive retirement benefit under the existing pension scheme, as provided in the Second Schedule to the Act.","Where an officer exempted under the second category dies in service or in the course of duty, the Federal Government Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate and the FCT Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate pay, en bloc, to his next of kin or designated survivors, a gratuity and pension calculated under the existing pay-as-you-go scheme."]},{"warn":"The source text’s own list of exemptions is lettered a), b), c), e) — it skips the letter d) entirely. This is preserved here as printed; it does not indicate a missing item, only an inconsistency in the source’s lettering."},{"h4":"Supervision of retirees exempted from the scheme"},{"p":"Administration of the retirement benefits of employees exempted under section 5(1)(b) is subject to the supervision and regulation of the Commission. Professors covered by the Universities (Miscellaneous Provisions) (Amendment) Act 2012, and political appointees entitled by their terms of employment to retire with full benefits, have their retirement benefits administered under guidelines issued by the Commission, with any shortfall funded from budgetary allocations by the employer. The Accountant-General of the Federation and the FCT Treasury pay retirement benefits directly into the individual bank accounts of retirees covered here, reporting details to the Commission and the relevant Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate."},{"h4":"Payment of retirement benefits"},{"p":"A holder of a retirement savings account shall, upon retirement or attaining the age of **50 years**, whichever is later, utilise the balance credited to the account for the following:"},{"ol":["**Programmed Withdrawal (PW)** — monthly or quarterly withdrawals, calculated on the basis of an expected life span.","**Withdrawal of a lump sum** from the account, provided the amount left after the withdrawal is sufficient to procure programmed withdrawals or an annuity for life, in accordance with extant Commission guidelines.","**Retiree Life Annuity (RLA)** — an annuity for life, purchased from a life insurance company licensed by the National Insurance Commission (NAICOM), with monthly or quarterly payments.","For **professors** covered by the Universities (Miscellaneous Provisions) (Amendment) Act 2012, benefits are paid according to the University Act.","Other categories of employee entitled, by their terms of employment, to retire with full retirement benefits continue to have that entitlement apply."]},{"ul":["Where an employee retires before age 50, he may request a lump sum of not more than **25% of the RSA balance**, provided the withdrawal is made only after **six months** of retirement and the retired employee has not secured other employment.","Where an employee has already accessed a lump sum under (ii) above, he may subsequently access the balance only under the programmed-withdrawal/annuity route of (i)."]},{"h4":"Death of an employee"},{"ul":["The entitlement under the life insurance policy maintained is paid into the deceased’s retirement savings account.","The Pension Fund Administrator adds this amount to the RSA balance and pays it out in favour of the beneficiary under a will, or the spouse and children of the deceased, or in their absence the recorded next of kin, or any person designated by the deceased during his lifetime, or, in the absence of any such designation, whoever the Probate Registry appoints as administrator of the estate — in line with the payment of retirement benefit."]},{"note":"Where an employee is **missing** and not found within one year of being declared missing, and a Board of Inquiry set up by the Commission concludes it is reasonable to presume death, the normal death-benefit process is followed: the life assurance entitlement is paid to the retirement savings account."},{"h4":"Exemption from taxes"},{"ul":["Contributions to the scheme are exempted from tax, and form part of tax-deductible expenses in computing tax payable by an employer or employee.","All interest, dividends, profits, investment and other income accruable to pension funds and assets under the Act are not taxable.","Any amount payable as a retirement benefit under the Act is not taxable.","A **voluntary contribution** is, however, subject to tax at the point of withdrawal, where the withdrawal is made before the end of 5 years from the date the voluntary contribution was made."]}]},{"n":"4.6","t":"The Retirement Savings Account (RSA)","b":[{"h4":"RSA and remittance of contribution"},{"ul":["Every employee to whom the Act applies must maintain a **Retirement Savings Account** in his own name, with any Pension Fund Administrator of his choice, and must notify his employer of the PFA and RSA chosen.","The employer must **deduct the employee’s contribution at source**, and, not later than **seven working days** from the day the employee is paid his salary, remit both the employee’s and employer’s contributions to the Pension Fund Custodian specified by the employee’s PFA.","On receiving the remittance, the PFC notifies the PFA, which then credits the employee’s RSA.","Where an employee fails to open an RSA within **six months** of assuming duty, the employer must, subject to Commission guidelines, request a PFA to open a **nominal RSA** for the employee.","An employer who fails to deduct or remit within the seven-working-day period is, in addition to the remittance itself, liable to a **penalty of not less than 2% of the total unpaid contribution per month** (or part of a month) of default, recoverable as a debt owed to the employee’s RSA.","An employee has no access to his RSA, and no dealing with the PFC in respect of it, except **through the PFA**.","The Commission determines the cost of recovering unremitted contributions and the source to defray that cost, which may include amounts recovered as penalty."]},{"h4":"Contributions of the Federal Government and FCT Administration"},{"p":"Contributions of the Federal Government and the FCT Administration to the retirement benefits of Public Service of the Federation employees, under section 11(3) of the Act, are a charge on the Consolidated Revenue Fund of the Federation and the Revenue Fund of the FCT respectively. The Accountant-General of the Federation and the FCT Treasury make these deductions."},{"h4":"Transfer of the account"},{"ul":["Subject to Commission guidelines, an RSA holder may **transfer his account from one PFA to another not more than once in a year**.","Where an employee transfers employment from one employer to another, the same RSA continues to be maintained, or may be transferred subject to the rule above."]},{"h4":"Transfer of entitlement from a defined benefit scheme into the scheme"},{"p":"As from 25 June 2004, the accrued pension right of any employee already under a pension scheme existing before that date, with over 3 years to retire, is treated as follows:"},{"ol":["For employees of the Public Service of the Federation, where the existing scheme is **unfunded**: recognised as an amount acknowledged through **Federal Government Retirement Benefits Bonds** issued by the Debt Management Office, redeemed upon retirement and added to the RSA balance.","For employees of the FCT, where the scheme is unfunded: recognised through **FCT Retirement Benefits Bonds**, redeemed and added to the RSA balance in the same way.","For employees of the Public Service of the Federation, the FCT or the private sector, where the scheme is **funded**: the RSA is credited with the fund to which the employee is entitled; any shortfall becomes an immediate debt of the employer, with priority over other claims."]},{"ul":["Where such a debt exists, the employer must issue a written acknowledgement of it to the employee and take steps to meet the shortfall; the debt is not subject to any limitation law.","The employer must notify the Commission of any such written acknowledgement, and of any step taken or planned to meet the shortfall.","Accrued pension rights of Public Service of the Federation employees are reviewed by the Federal Government from time to time, in line with section 173(3) of the Constitution, with the resulting variation credited directly into the individual retiree’s RSA; the FCT does the same for its own employees."]},{"h4":"Withdrawal conditions"},{"ul":["Withdrawal is not allowed until the attainment of **50 years** of age.","An officer who retires before 50 may withdraw where a suitably qualified physician or properly constituted Medical Board certifies he is no longer mentally or physically capable of carrying out his office.","Withdrawal is allowed if the officer is retired due to total or permanent disability of mind or body.","Where an employee retires before 50 in accordance with the terms of his employment, he is entitled to make withdrawals.","The Medical Board or physician may, at the employee’s request, review his fitness once every two years; where he is certified fit again, he may re-enter the scheme upon securing another employment.","An employee who disengages, or is disengaged, before age 50 and is unable to secure another employment within **four months** may make a withdrawal from his RSA."]},{"h4":"Age of contributor and dispute resolution"},{"p":"The authentic age of an employee entering the public service, or any other employment, is that submitted by him on entering service or taking up employment. Any employee or beneficiary dissatisfied with a decision of a PFA or Custodian may request in writing that the Commission review the decision."}]},{"n":"4.7","t":"The National Pension Commission (PenCom)","b":[{"p":"The principal objective of the Commission, per the Pension Reform Act 2014, is “to **regulate, supervise and ensure the effective administration** of pension matters in Nigeria.” Other objectives are:"},{"ol":["Ensure that every person who works in the public service of the Federation, the FCT and the private sector receives his retirement benefits as and when due.","Assist improvident individuals by ensuring that they save in order to cater for their livelihood during old age.","Establish a uniform set of rules, regulations and standards for the administration and payment of retirement benefits for the public service of the Federation, the FCT and the private sector."]},{"h4":"Powers of the Commission"},{"ul":["Formulate, direct and oversee the overall policy on pension matters in Nigeria.","Fix the terms and conditions of service, including remuneration, of Commission employees.","Request or call for information from any employer, pension administrator, custodian or other person or institution on matters relating to retirement benefits.","Charge and collect such fees, levies or penalties as it specifies.","Establish and acquire offices and other premises for its own use.","Establish standards, rules and regulations for the management of pension funds.","Investigate any Pension Fund Administrator, custodian or other party involved in the management of pension funds.","Impose administrative sanctions or fines on erring employers, PFAs or PFCs.","Order the transfer of management or custody of pension funds or assets from a PFA or PFC whose licence has been revoked, or which is subject to insolvency proceedings, to another PFA or PFC.","Do such other things as, in its opinion, are necessary to ensure the efficient performance of its functions."]},{"h4":"Functions of the Commission — section 23"},{"ul":["Regulate and supervise the scheme established under the Act.","Issue guidelines for the investment of pension funds.","Approve, license, regulate and supervise PFAs, Custodians and other institutions relating to pension matters.","Establish standards, rules and guidelines for the management of pension funds.","Ensure the maintenance of a **National Data Bank** on all pension matters.","Carry out public awareness and education on the establishment and management of the scheme.","Promote capacity building and institutional strengthening of PFAs and PFCs.","Receive and investigate complaints of impropriety levelled against any PFA, custodian or employer, or any of their staff or agents.","Perform such other duties as, in its opinion, are necessary or expedient for the discharge of its functions."]},{"h4":"Composition of the Commission"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Member","Qualification"],"rows":[["Part-time Chairman","University degree or its equivalent, and 20 years’ experience"],["Director-General (Chief Executive Officer)","Appropriate professional skills, not less than 20 years’ cognate experience"],["Four full-time Commissioners","Professional skills and not less than 20 years’ cognate experience each, in Finance, Investment, Accounting, Pension Management, Business Administration or Actuarial Science"],["Representative — Head of the Civil Service of the Federation",""],["Representative — Federal Ministry of Finance",""],["Representative — Nigeria Union of Pensioners",""],["Representative — Nigeria Employers’ Consultative Association",""],["Representative — Central Bank of Nigeria",""],["Representative — Nigeria Labour Congress",""],["Representative — Securities and Exchange Commission",""],["Representative — Trade Union Congress of Nigeria",""],["Representative — Nigeria Exchange Group Plc (NGX), formerly the Nigerian Stock Exchange",""],["Representative — National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)",""]]}},{"key":"PenCom regulates and supervises; it does **not** manage or hold any pension money itself. That is left to the PFAs (management) and PFCs (custody) in section 4.8 and 4.9."}]},{"n":"4.8","t":"Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs)","b":[{"p":"A person proposing to operate as a Pension Fund Administrator applies to the Commission for a licence, in the form and with the fees prescribed. The Commission may, if satisfied that the requirements below are met, issue a licence subject to such terms and conditions as it considers necessary."},{"h4":"Requirements for a licence"},{"ul":["A limited liability company incorporated under the Companies and Allied Matters Act, whose object is to manage pension funds.","A **minimum paid-up share capital**, as prescribed from time to time by the Commission.","The professional capacity to manage pension funds and administer retirement benefits.","Never having been a manager or administrator of a fund which was mismanaged or in distress due to any fault of the PFA or any of its subscribers, directors or officers.","An undertaking that it will not engage in any business other than the management of pension funds.","Satisfying any additional requirement or condition prescribed by the Commission."]},{"p":"Companies and institutions already engaged in pension fund management but not licensed by the Commission must, at the commencement of the Act, compute and credit all contributions to the RSAs opened for each contributor, including distributable income, and transfer all pension funds and assets to PFAs and PFCs as the Commission determines. Voting rights of shareholders in a PFA or PFC are proportionate to their contribution to paid-up share capital, per section 116 of the Companies and Allied Matters Act."},{"h4":"Functions of PFAs"},{"p":"Only PFAs licensed by the Commission may manage pension funds. A PFA:"},{"ol":["Opens retirement savings accounts for all employees, with a Personal Identity Number (PIN) attached.","Invests and manages pension funds and assets.","Maintains books of account on all transactions.","Provides regular information on investment strategy, market returns and other performance indicators to the Commission and to RSA holders.","Provides customer service support to employees, including access to account balances and statements on demand.","Processes the calculation and payment of retirement benefits.","Carries out other functions the Commission may assign from time to time."]},{"h4":"Prohibited transactions"},{"p":"A PFA shall not:"},{"ul":["Hold any pension fund or asset.","Keep any pension fund or asset with a PFC in whom the PFA has any business interest, share or other relationship.","Engage in any business transaction or trade in any manner with the PFC as a counterpart, or with a subsidiary, in relation to pension fund or assets.","Divert or convert pension funds and assets, or any income or brokerage or commission arising from their investment, by any means."]},{"h4":"Prescribed structure"},{"p":"Every PFA maintains standing committees to carry out the fund’s functions and ensure compliance:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Committee","Role"],"rows":[["**Risk Management**","Determines the risk profile of investment portfolios; draws up programmes of adjustment for deviations; determines the level of reserves to cover investment risk; advises the PFA on internal control measures; and other risk-related functions the Board directs"],["**Investment Strategy**","Formulates strategies for complying with Commission investment guidelines; determines an optimal investment mix consistent with the agreed risk profile; evaluates the daily mark-to-market value of portfolios and reports to the Board; periodically reviews the performance of major securities in the portfolio; and other investment-strategy functions the Board directs"]]}}]},{"n":"4.9","t":"Pension Fund Custodians (PFCs)","b":[{"p":"A person proposing to act as custodian of pension funds applies to the Commission for a licence, which the Commission may issue subject to the applicant meeting the requirements below, prescribed under section 46 of the Act."},{"h4":"Requirements for a licence"},{"ul":["A limited liability company incorporated under the Companies and Allied Matters Act, by a **licensed financial institution**, with the sole object of keeping custody of pension fund and retirement benefits assets.","A minimum paid-up capital as the Commission prescribes, wholly owned by a licensed financial institution with a net worth of a minimum of **₦25,000,000,000**, or as prescribed from time to time.","Evidence that the parent company has issued a guarantee for the full sum and value of the cash float of pension funds.","An undertaking to hold pension fund assets to the exclusive order of the PFA, on trust for the respective employees, as instructed by the PFA appointed by each employee.","Never having been custodian of a fund which was mismanaged or in distress due to any default of the custodian.","Satisfying any additional requirement the Commission prescribes."]},{"h4":"Functions of a PFC — section 57"},{"ul":["Receives the total contributions remitted by employers on behalf of PFAs.","Notifies the relevant PFA within **24 hours** of receiving a contribution from any employer.","Holds pension funds and assets in safe custody on trust for employees and RSA beneficiaries.","Settles transactions and undertakes activities related to pension fund investments, including the collection of dividends.","Provides data and information on investment to the PFA and the Commission.","Undertakes statistical analysis of investments and returns in its custody, and provides data to the PFA and the Commission.","Executes proxies in favour of the PFA for voting in relation to the investments.","Carries out other functions the Commission prescribes."]},{"h4":"Specific obligations"},{"ul":["A PFC must maintain all pension funds and assets in its custody to the exclusive order of the relevant PFA and the Commission;","It must not utilise any pension fund or asset in its custody to meet its own financial obligations to any person; and","It must not divert or convert pension funds or assets, or any income arising from their investment, by any means."]},{"key":"The separation of the administrator (section 4.8) from the custodian is the central control of the scheme: the PFA decides how the money is invested but never touches it, while the PFC holds the money but has no discretion over it. Neither can misapply the fund alone, and PenCom supervises both."}]},{"n":"4.10","t":"Refusal and revocation of licence","b":[{"h4":"Refusal to issue a licence"},{"p":"The Commission may refuse to issue a licence where it is satisfied that:"},{"ul":["The information in the application is false or untrue in any material particular;","The application does not meet the Act's or the Commission's requirements; or","The applicant's licence had earlier been revoked under section 64 of the Act."]},{"p":"Where it refuses, the Commission notifies the applicant in the prescribed form, specifying reasons."},{"h4":"Grounds for revocation"},{"p":"The Commission may revoke a licence issued to a PFA or PFC where it discovers that:"},{"ul":["A statement made in the application was one the applicant knew, or ought to have known, to be false, untrue or misleading in a material particular.","The licensee is subject to an insolvency proceeding, or is likely to be wound up or otherwise dissolved.","The conduct of its affairs does not conform to the Act or regulations made under it.","An event occurs rendering it unable to manage pension funds or take custody of them.","It is in breach of any condition attached to its licence."]},{"h4":"Process and consequences"},{"ul":["The Commission gives the PFA or PFC at least **seven calendar days’ notice** of its intention to revoke, and considers any written representations made within that period.","The notice is in the prescribed form and specifies the reasons for revocation.","Revocation does not prejudice the entitlements of any RSA beneficiary under the scheme.","Notwithstanding the Companies and Allied Matters Act, the Commission withdraws the powers of the board over the pension funds and assets in its revocation order, and may appoint qualified administrators to superintend the transfer of assets and exercise board powers where necessary.","RSAs managed by a PFA whose licence is revoked are transferred to another PFA; pension funds and assets held by a PFC whose licence is revoked are transferred to another PFC.","The Commission publishes, by notice in the Federal Gazette, the list of PFAs or PFCs whose licences have been revoked."]}]},{"n":"4.11","t":"General obligations, and appointment of chief executives and compliance officers","b":[{"h4":"General obligations of the PFA and PFC"},{"ul":["Ensure the pension fund is at all times managed or held in accordance with the Act, its regulations and any Commission directive.","Take reasonable care that management or custody is carried out in the best interests of RSA holders.","Report to the Commission, as soon as practicable, any unusual occurrence that could adversely affect the rights of an RSA owner.","Report to the Commission, as soon as reasonably practicable, if an employer is in default of remittance for more than **14 days**.","Subject to Commission guidelines, transfer an RSA promptly to another PFA at the employee’s request.","Provide annual **fidelity insurance cover** for staff to the full value of the pension funds and assets managed or held.","Render to the Commission reports of any fraud, forgery or theft occurring in its organisation, in a format the Commission approves.","Not employ any person on the Commission’s section 74(2) list without the Commission’s prior approval."]},{"p":"Except with the Commission's prior written consent, a PFA or PFC may not enter into any agreement or arrangement for:"},{"ol":["The sale or transfer of a significant shareholding capable of changing its shareholding structure;","The restructuring of its share capital;","Amalgamation or merger with another PFA or PFC;","Restructuring of the PFA or PFC itself; or","The employment of a management agent, or transfer of its business to any agent."]},{"warn":"A PFA or PFC that fails to comply with any of these provisions (sections 73, 74 and 75 of the Act) pays a penalty of **₦1,000,000** to the Commission for every violation, and the Commission may additionally remove any top management staff who had knowledge, or ought to have had knowledge, of the offence."},{"h4":"Appointment of chief executive officers"},{"p":"The chief executive officer, directors and management staff of a PFA or PFC must not be appointed without the Commission’s prior written approval, and must execute the code of conduct form the Commission provides. Notwithstanding this, the Commission may suspend, or direct the resignation or sacking of, any chief executive, director or management staff found to have grossly disregarded the Act."},{"h4":"Appointment of a compliance officer"},{"p":"Every PFA and PFC must employ a **compliance officer**, who must have relevant professional and cognate experience, and who is responsible for:"},{"ol":["Ensuring compliance with the Act and internal regulations;","Reporting to the chief executive officer and the Commission on any non-compliance; and","Liaising with the Commission on any matter that will enhance compliance."]}]},{"n":"4.12","t":"Statutory Reserve Fund, Pension Protection Fund and administrative expenses","b":[{"h4":"Statutory Reserve Fund"},{"p":"Every PFA maintains a **Statutory Reserve Fund** as a contingency fund to meet any claim for which it may be liable, as the Commission determines. The fund is credited annually with **12.5% of net profit after tax**, or such other percentage as the Commission stipulates."},{"h4":"Pension Protection Fund"},{"p":"The Commission also establishes and maintains a **Pension Protection Fund**, for the benefit of eligible pensioners covered by any pension scheme established, approved or recognised under the Act. It consists of:"},{"ul":["An annual subvention of **1% of the total monthly wage bill** payable to employees in the Public Service of the Federation, towards funding the minimum guaranteed pension.","An annual pension protection levy paid by the Commission and all licensed pension operators, at a rate the Commission determines.","Income from investment of the Pension Protection Fund."]},{"p":"The Commission utilises the fund for:"},{"ul":["Funding the minimum guaranteed pension under section 84 of the Act;","Compensating eligible pensioners for shortfall or financial losses arising from investment activities; and","Any other protective purpose the Commission determines."]},{"h4":"Pension fund administrative expenses"},{"ul":["All income earned from investment of pension funds is credited to the individual RSAs of beneficiaries.","All fees, charges, costs and expenses on transactions, properly delineated by the PFA, are debited from the pension fund in line with Commission regulations.","The Commission ensures that all information in brochures, advertisements, promotional materials and claims of PFAs is truthful, without omission of any fact that would make it misleading, false or deceptive."]}]},{"n":"4.13","t":"Minimum pension guarantee","b":[{"ul":["All RSA holders who have contributed to a licensed PFA for a number of years specified by the Commission are entitled to a **guaranteed minimum pension**, as the Commission specifies from time to time.","The **Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund** continues to provide every eligible citizen and legal resident with social security insurance services **other than pension**, under the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund Act.","The Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund Act is deemed amended in all particulars to bring it into full compliance with the Pension Reform Act."]}]},{"n":"4.14","t":"Investment of pension funds and restricted investments","b":[{"h4":"Investment of pension funds"},{"p":"All contributions are invested by the PFA with the objectives of **safety and maintenance of fair returns** on the amount invested, in accordance with Commission regulations and guidelines. Pension funds and assets may be invested in:"},{"ul":["Bonds, bills and other securities issued or guaranteed by the Federal Government and the Central Bank of Nigeria.","Bonds, bills and other securities issued by states and local governments.","Bonds, debentures, redeemable preference shares and other debt instruments issued by corporate entities and listed on a Stock Exchange registered under the Investments and Securities Act.","Ordinary shares of public limited companies listed on a securities exchange registered under the Investments and Securities Act.","Bank deposits and bank securities.","Investment certificates of closed-end or hybrid investment funds listed on a registered securities exchange, with good earnings track records.","Units sold by open-end or specialist open-end investment funds registered under the Investments and Securities Act.","Real estate development investments.","Specialist investment funds and other financial instruments the Commission may approve."]},{"h4":"Restricted investments"},{"p":"A PFA shall not invest pension fund or assets in shares or other securities issued by itself or its own PFC, or by a shareholder of either. A PFA shall not:"},{"ol":["Sell pension fund assets to itself, any shareholder, director, affiliate, subsidiary, associate, related party or company of the PFA, any of its employees, affiliates of any of its shareholders, or the PFC holding assets to the order of the PFA (or any of the PFC’s related parties).","Use pension fund to purchase assets from any of the persons above.","Apply pension fund assets under its management as loans and credits, or as collateral for any loan taken by an RSA holder or any other person."]},{"h4":"Supervision and examination"},{"p":"A PFA who fails to comply with any provision of the Act is liable to a penalty of not less than **₦500,000 for each day** the non-compliance continues, and forfeits the profit from that investment to the beneficiaries of the RSAs concerned; where the investment led to a loss, the PFA must make up the loss."}]},{"n":"4.15","t":"Deficiencies of the Pension Reform Act 2014","b":[{"p":"The study text itself lists several practical weaknesses of the 2014 Act, which is useful both for the essay questions this topic attracts and as a critical, rather than purely descriptive, view of the scheme:"},{"ol":["**Scope and coverage.** Mandatory contribution now applies only to organisations with 15 or more employees (previously 5). This effectively reduces the number of employers and employees who benefit, which, given the already low level of contributors under the scheme, is counter-productive.","**Basis of contribution.** “Monthly emoluments” is defined as the total emolument in the employee’s contract of employment, provided it is not less than basic, housing and transport allowance combined. This is vague: read broadly, it could include every item paid monthly, which could more than double some employers’ pension obligations, or reduce employees’ net pay, unless employers raise salaries to compensate.","**Rates of contribution.** The new minimum rates of 10% (employer) and 8% (employee) — up from 7.5% each under the repealed 2004 Act — increase the cost of employment and may push some employers toward rationalising staff strength.","**Commencement date.** The Act was signed into law on 1 July 2014 with the same date as its commencement date, leaving no room for a transition period during which affected employers could plan.","**Gaps in coverage.** Only employers with a minimum of 15 employees must contribute. The Act leaves guidelines to govern participation by organisations with fewer than 3 employees, but is silent on organisations with between 3 and 15 employees, and on what happens to past contributions made by employers with 5 to 14 employees under the old Act.","**Sole contribution by employers.** An employer may take full responsibility for contribution, but in that case must contribute not less than 20% of monthly emoluments — which sits oddly against the combined minimum of 18% for two-party contribution, and may discourage employers from taking on full responsibility."]}]},{"n":"4.16","t":"Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (PTAD)","b":[{"p":"The **Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (PTAD)** is responsible for the pension administration of the **Defined Benefit Scheme (DBS)** — that is, the old, pre-2004 pay-as-you-go arrangement that continues for exempted categories. It was established, empowered by section 30(2a) of the amended Pension Reform Act 2004, to address numerous pensioners’ complaints — non-payment of monthly pension, short payment of pension and gratuity, removal of names from the pension payment voucher, non-payment of harmonised pension arrears, irregular payment of federal pensions and non-receipt of pension after retirement, among others. PTAD took over management of the three offices that previously ran the old scheme: the **Civil Service Pension Department**, the **Police Pension Office**, and the **Customs, Immigration and Prisons Pension Office (CIPPO)**. Sections 42 and 44 of the Pension Reform Act 2014 established PTAD for the public service of the Federation, and a separate PTAD for the Federal Capital Territory."},{"h4":"Functions of PTAD"},{"ul":["Implementation of policies, rules and regulations relating to pension matters under the Defined Benefit Scheme.","Ensuring accurate payments are made to authentic pensioners under Civil Service jurisdiction.","Facilitating the maintenance of an accurate and comprehensive database of pensioners under its jurisdiction.","Management and maintenance of pensioners’ records and files.","Preparing budgetary estimates for existing pensioners, as well as outstanding liabilities and benefits of pensioners with genuine complaints.","Preparing and submitting the monthly payroll of civil service pensioners, and issuing payment instructions to the Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation (OAGF) through the **GIFMIS platform** for direct payment to pensioners.","Responding to pensioners’ complaints, including non-, under- or over-payment of pensions, gratuities and death benefits.","Carrying out monthly payment analysis and payroll review, and ascertaining deficits in pension payments.","Prepayment audit of all payment vouchers.","Compliance audit to ensure government financial rules and regulations are followed in all financial transactions.","Review of financial statements — transcripts, bank reconciliations, pensioners’ statements of claims, and the like.","Safeguarding the assets of the department.","Periodic examination of systems and processes, and certification of payment vouchers and other accounting books and records by use of the Internal Audit stamp.","Production of monthly audit reports.","Collaborating with Pension Support, and ensuring periodic verification of civil service pensioners."]},{"h4":"Powers of the Commission over PTAD"},{"ul":["Regulate and supervise the activities of the Federal Government PTAD and the FCT PTAD to ensure compliance with the Act.","Intervene to administer, and render technical support and advice on, the management of the various Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorates, as directed by the President.","Ensure the Federal Government and FCT PTADs operate under the rules, regulations and directives issued by the Commission."]}]},{"n":"4.17","t":"The Micro Pension Plan","b":[{"p":"Section 2(3) of the Pension Reform Act 2014 provides that employees of organisations with fewer than three employees, as well as self-employed persons, are entitled to participate in the Contributory Pension Scheme, in accordance with guidelines issued by the Commission. This arrangement, for the provision of pensions to the self-employed and to persons operating in the **informal sector**, is the **Micro Pension Plan**."},{"h4":"Key terms"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Informal sector**","Employees in business entities, organisations and/or persons not mandated to implement the Contributory Pension Scheme under section 2(1) of the Act"],["**Self-employed**","A person who earns income by conducting trade or business for himself/herself"],["**Micro Pension Contributor (MPC)**","A person registered under the Micro Pension Plan"],["**Micro Pension Fund (MPF)**","The pool of contributions and other assets under the Micro Pension Plan"],["**Named beneficiary**","A person legally entitled to receive any benefit accruable to a deceased or missing MPC"],["**Automated Fingerprint Identification System (AFIS)**","A biometric identification methodology using digital imaging to obtain, store and analyse fingerprint data"],["**PIN**","A Personal Identification Number issued to an individual upon opening an RSA"]]}},{"p":"The Commission's guidelines are set out to:"},{"ol":["Set eligibility criteria for participation;","Establish the registration process;","Define how contributions are made;","Provide criteria for managing the micro pension fund;","Outline the modes of accessing benefits;","Define the mode of conversion from a micro pension plan to mandatory contribution; and","Set minimum ICT requirements for licensed PFAs and PFCs."]},{"h4":"Eligibility"},{"p":"Persons **not below 18 years** of age with a source of income are eligible, all of whom must be resident in Nigeria:"},{"ul":["Self-employed persons belonging to a trade, profession, cooperative or business association;","Self-employed persons with a business registered as a company, partnership or enterprise;","Employees in the informal sector, with or without a formal written employment contract; and","Other self-employed individuals."]},{"h4":"Registration and contributions"},{"ul":["A prospective MPC opens an RSA by completing a registration form with a PFA of choice, providing a valid means of identification (national ID, permanent voter’s card, driver’s licence or international passport) and supporting documentation (evidence of association membership, business registration, employment letter, BVN, or the like). PFAs capture the applicant’s ten fingerprints against AFIS standards, transmit the registration to the Commission for PIN generation, and forward the PIN to the contributor.","Contributions are made in naira, at whatever frequency suits the contributor (daily, weekly, monthly), by cash, electronic transfer or other approved platform. Every contribution is split **40% to a contingent (short-term) portion and 60% to the retirement benefits portion**.","PFAs and PFCs must inform the Economic and Financial Crimes Commission of any single lodgement of ₦5,000,000 or more.","PFAs may charge a maximum administration fee of **₦80** on contributions of ₦4,000 and above, and **₦20** on contributions below ₦4,000, with no other charges permitted.","The Micro Pension Fund is managed as one fund, invested in line with the same pension fund investment regulations as the main scheme (section 4.14)."]},{"h4":"Accessing benefits"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Rule"],"rows":[["**Contingent withdrawal**","Accessible 3 months after the initial contribution; thereafter, at most once a week; the contributor may withdraw the entire contingent balance including accrued income, or choose to convert it into the retirement-benefits portion at the end of any year. Processing and payment must not exceed **2 working days**, and payment goes only to the contributor’s designated bank account"],["**Retirement benefits withdrawal**","Accessible on retirement and attaining age 50, or on health grounds. The retiree chooses programmed withdrawal or life annuity; MPCs are entitled to the minimum pension guarantee (section 4.13) if they meet the qualifying conditions; where the total contributed is below the amount required, the retiree is paid en bloc"],["**Death or missing person**","Processed in line with the Commission’s regulations for the administration of retirement and terminal benefits (as in section 4.5)"]]}},{"h4":"Conversion between the Micro Pension Plan and mandatory contribution"},{"p":"An MPC who secures employment with an organisation of three or more employees may convert to mandatory contribution under section 2(1) of the Act, retaining the same RSA; the PFA moves the RSA balance to the appropriate fund under the multi-fund structure. Where an eligible contributor fails to request conversion within one month of the first remittance from the new employer, the PFA automatically changes the status on receiving the second remittance. **Conversion the other way — from mandatory contribution back to a micro pension plan — is not permitted.**"}]},{"n":"4.18","t":"Offences, penalties and enforcement","b":[{"h4":"General offences"},{"ul":["A person who contravenes any provision of the Act, where no specific penalty is prescribed, is liable on conviction to a fine of not less than **₦250,000**, or to imprisonment for not less than one year, or both.","A person or body who **attempts** to commit any offence under the Act is liable, on conviction, to the same punishment as for the full offence.","Without prejudice to section 174 of the Constitution, the Commission may **compound** any offence under the Act by accepting an amount less than the prescribed fine.","A PFA or PFC that reimburses or pays, directly or indirectly, a fine imposed on a staff member, officer or director under the Act commits an offence, and is liable on conviction to a fine of not less than **₦5,000,000**, and forfeits the amount repaid."]},{"h4":"Misappropriation of pension fund"},{"ul":["A prison term of **10 years**, plus a fine of **3 times the amount misappropriated**.","Forfeiture of the entire asset, property or fund, with accrued interest, or the proceeds of any unlawful activity under the Act, in the offender’s possession.","It is also an offence for a PFA or PFC to reimburse or pay a staff member, officer or director on whom a fine has been imposed (minimum fine ₦5,000,000).","For a PFC specifically, the minimum fine is **₦10,000,000** on conviction, where it fails to hold funds to the exclusive preserve of the PFA and PenCom, or applies the funds to meet its own obligations (in that specific case, ₦5,000,000 or 5 years’ imprisonment or both).","Any PFA, PFC, person or body who refuses to produce a book, account, document or voucher; refuses to give information or explanation to an examiner; or knowingly or recklessly gives false or misleading information, commits an offence and is liable on conviction to a fine of not less than **₦200,000**, or imprisonment of not less than 3 years, or both, for every false or misleading item given — and, where the offence continues, a further fine of not less than **₦100,000 per day** it continues."]},{"h4":"Liability of a body corporate"},{"p":"Where an offence under the Act is committed by a body corporate, the body corporate, and every director, manager, secretary or other officer who had knowledge, or ought to have had knowledge, of the offence and did not exercise due diligence to ensure compliance, is deemed to have committed the offence. Notwithstanding any other law, the Commission may additionally sanction the board, or any director, manager or officer of a PFA or PFC that violates the Act."}]},{"n":"4.19","t":"Defined benefit and defined contribution schemes","b":[{"warn":"This background comparison is not part of this chapter of the study text — the chapter opens directly with definitions of terms (section 4.1) and assumes the reader already knows why Nigeria moved to a contributory scheme. It is kept here, flagged, because it is standard pension knowledge needed to answer the theory question at the end of this chapter, and because understanding it makes the rest of the chapter’s detail easier to place."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined benefit","Defined contribution"],"rows":[["Promise made","A specified pension, usually a fraction of final salary per year of service","Whatever the accumulated contributions and investment returns will buy"],["Contributions","Whatever is needed to fund the promise; usually the employer alone","A fixed percentage of emoluments from both employer and employee"],["Investment risk","Borne by the **employer**","Borne by the **employee**"],["Longevity risk","Borne by the employer","Borne by the employee"],["Funding","Often unfunded, paid from current revenue (pay-as-you-go)","Fully funded, in an individual account"],["Portability","Poor; the benefit is tied to service with one employer","Full; the account follows the employee"],["Accounting","Requires actuarial valuation; complex","Simple — the expense is the contribution payable"]]}},{"p":"Nigeria operated a **defined benefit** scheme before 2004. It failed largely because it was unfunded: pensions were paid out of current budget provision, arrears accumulated into the hundreds of billions of naira, pensioner records were unreliable and populated with ghost pensioners, and there was no fund from which any claim could be met. The Pension Reform Act 2004, re-enacted with amendments as the Pension Reform Act 2014 (section 4.4), replaced it with a mandatory, fully funded **defined contribution** scheme — though the old scheme continues for the exempted categories in section 4.5, administered by PTAD (section 4.16)."}]},{"n":"4.20","t":"Gratuity","b":[{"p":"This chapter of the study text defines gratuity only in passing, at section 4.1: a **lump sum of money paid once** to a retired officer who has served for a minimum of 5 years. It does not set out a separate gratuity computation for the Contributory Pension Scheme."},{"p":"Under the Contributory Pension Scheme there is no separate gratuity payment: the lump sum a retiree may take is drawn from the balance in his own Retirement Savings Account, subject to the residual-balance test at section 4.5 (enough must be left to fund a programmed withdrawal or annuity). Gratuity as a distinct, separately computed benefit belongs to the **old defined benefit arrangement** (section 4.19) and to the Armed Forces Pension Act (section 4.3), where it is paid alongside monthly pension."},{"warn":"A question asking you to “compute the gratuity” is almost always set under the old defined benefit rules, or under a stated scheme rule, and will give you the formula — typically a percentage of final annual salary for each year of pensionable service. Read the rule given in the question; do not import one."},{"note":"[Every numeric rule in this chapter, and every other chapter, in one place →](guides/ps-numbers.html) — years of service, contribution rates, thresholds and deadlines, gathered from all 23 chapters."}]},{"n":"4.21","t":"Further reading","b":[{"ul":["[Pension Reform Act 2014](https://www.pencom.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/PRA-2014.pdf) — National Pension Commission (PenCom).","[Micro Pension Plan Framework](https://www.pencom.gov.ng/) — National Pension Commission (PenCom).","[Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 1999 (as amended)](https://placng.org/i/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/Constitution-of-the-Federal-Republic-of-Nigeria.pdf)."]}]},{"n":"4.22","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The following, **except one**, are objectives of the Pension Reform Act 2014: (A) to establish a uniform set of rules, regulations and standards for pension administration in Nigeria  (B) to assist improvident individuals by ensuring they save towards their inactive years  (C) to make provision for the smooth operation of the contributory pension scheme  (D) to give a bulk amount to employees at retirement without recourse to contribution from salary  (E) to ensure every public and private sector employee receives retirement benefits as and when due.","The following, **except one**, are categories of persons exempted from the Contributory Pension Scheme: (A) the categories mentioned in section 291 of the Constitution, including the armed forces and the intelligence and secret services  (B) an employee entitled to retirement benefits under a pre-25 June 2004 scheme with less than three years to retire  (C) any person within (a) or (b) who continues under the existing scheme per the Second Schedule  (D) the Chairman of any MDA  (E) the President of the Federal Republic of Nigeria.","The contribution for an employee to whom the Pension Reform Act 2014 applies shall be: (A) minimum 10% employer, minimum 5% employee  (B) minimum 7.5% employer, minimum 7.5% employee  (C) minimum 12.5% employer, minimum 7.5% employee  (D) minimum 10% employer, minimum 8% employee  (E) minimum 10% employer, minimum 10% employer.","Which of the following is **not** a condition governing withdrawal from the Scheme? (A) withdrawal can only occur when staff have attained 35 years in service  (B) withdrawal cannot occur until age 50  (C) where the officer retires through total or permanent disability of mind or body  (D) where an employee disengages before age 50 and cannot secure another job within four months  (E) where a Medical Board certifies that a formerly unfit employee may re-enter the Scheme on securing another job.","The following, **except one**, are exemptions from tax under the PRA 2014: (A) contributions are tax-exempt and are tax-deductible for employer and employee  (B) interest, dividends, profits and other income accruing to pension funds are not taxable  (C) any amount payable as a retirement benefit is not taxable  (D) a voluntary contribution is taxed at withdrawal if withdrawn within 5 years of being made  (E) the income of high-net-worth private individuals in various industries.","The gross salary (basic and allowances) attached to a retiring officer's substantive rank is known as …","The rates of contribution to the pension scheme by the military are … and …","… is responsible for the pension administration of the Defined Benefit Scheme (DBS).","At the death of an employee, the entitlement under the life insurance policy is paid into his …","The qualification and years of experience required of the part-time Chairman of the National Pension Commission are … and …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D — \"to give a bulk amount to employees at retirement without recourse to contribution from salary.\"** The 2014 Act is a *contributory* scheme.","**D — the Chairman of any MDA.** Exemption covers the section 291 categories (armed forces, intelligence, secret services), those with under three years to retire under a pre-2004 scheme, and the President — not MDA chairmen generally.","**D — minimum 10% by the employer and minimum 8% by the employee** (18% in total).","**A — \"withdrawal can only occur when staff have attained 35 years in service.\"** Not a withdrawal condition under the Act.","**E — \"the income of high-net-worth private individuals.\"** Not a PRA 2014 tax exemption.","**Pensionable emoluments.**","**12.5% of basic salary and allowances by the government** and **8% by the military staff**.","**The Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (PTAD).**","**Retirement Savings Account (RSA).**","**A university degree or its equivalent**, and **20 years** of experience."]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["List any **ten functions** of the National Pension Commission.","Enumerate any **ten powers** of the National Pension Commission.","Itemise the **composition** of the National Pension Commission.","List the **requirements for licensing a Pension Fund Administrator**, and the **investment outlets** in which pension funds may be invested under the PRA 2014.","State **eight functions** of the Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (PTAD) and **three powers** of the Commission over PTAD."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Functions of the National Pension Commission (section 23; any ten)"},{"ol":["Regulate and supervise the scheme established under the Act.","Issue guidelines for the investment of pension funds.","Approve, license, regulate and supervise Pension Fund Administrators, Custodians and other pension institutions.","Establish standards, rules and guidelines for the management of the pension funds.","Ensure the maintenance of a National Data Bank on all pension matters.","Carry out public awareness and education on the scheme.","Promote capacity building and institutional strengthening of PFAs and custodians.","Receive and investigate complaints of impropriety against any PFA, custodian, employer or their staff or agents.","Perform such other duties as are necessary or expedient for its functions under the Act."]},{"h4":"2  Powers of the Commission (any ten)"},{"ol":["Formulate, direct and oversee overall policy on pension matters in Nigeria.","Fix the terms and conditions of service, including remuneration, of its employees.","Request or call for information from any employer, PFA, custodian or other person on retirement-benefit matters.","Charge and collect fees, levies or penalties as it may specify.","Establish and acquire offices and premises for its use.","Establish standards, rules and regulations for the management of the pension funds.","Investigate any PFA, custodian or other party managing pension funds.","Impose administrative sanctions or fines on erring employers, PFAs or custodians.","Order the transfer of management or custody of pension funds or assets from a PFA or custodian whose licence is revoked or which is insolvent.","Do such other things as are necessary for the efficient performance of its functions."]},{"h4":"3  Composition of the Commission"},{"ul":["A **part-time Chairman** — a university degree or equivalent and 20 years' experience.","A **Director-General** (Chief Executive) — appropriate professional skills and not less than 20 years' cognate experience.","**Four full-time Commissioners** — each with professional skills and not less than 20 years' cognate experience in finance, investment, accounting, pension management, business administration or actuarial science.","**Representatives** of: the Head of the Civil Service of the Federation; the Federal Ministry of Finance; the Nigeria Union of Pensioners; the Nigeria Employers' Consultative Association; the Central Bank of Nigeria; the Nigeria Labour Congress; the Securities and Exchange Commission; the Trade Union Congress of Nigeria; the Nigerian Exchange Group Plc; and the National Insurance Commission."]},{"h4":"4  Licensing a PFA, and permitted investments"},{"p":"**Requirements to be licensed as a PFA:**"},{"ol":["A limited liability company incorporated under CAMA whose object is to manage pension funds;","Minimum paid-up share capital as prescribed by the Commission;","Proven professional capacity to manage pension funds and administer retirement benefits;","Never having managed a fund that was mismanaged or distressed through its own or its subscribers'/directors'/officers' fault;","An undertaking to engage in no business other than pension fund management; and","Any additional condition the Commission prescribes."]},{"p":"**Permitted investment outlets:**"},{"ol":["Bonds, bills and securities issued or guaranteed by the Federal Government and the CBN;","Bonds, bills and securities issued by States and local governments;","Bonds, debentures, redeemable preference shares and other debt instruments of listed corporates;","Ordinary shares of listed public limited companies;","Bank deposits and bank securities;","Investment certificates of listed closed-end or hybrid investment funds with good earnings records;","Units of open-end or specialist open-end investment funds;","Real estate development investments; and","Specialist investment funds and other instruments the Commission approves."]},{"h4":"5  PTAD — functions and the Commission's powers over it"},{"p":"**Functions of PTAD (any eight):**"},{"ol":["Implement policies, rules and regulations under the Defined Benefit Scheme;","Ensure accurate payments to authentic Civil Service pensioners;","Maintain an accurate, comprehensive database of pensioners;","Manage and maintain pensioners' records and files;","Prepare budgetary estimates for existing pensioners and outstanding liabilities;","Prepare and submit the monthly Civil Service pensioners' payroll and issue payment instructions to the OAGF through GIFMIS;","Respond to pensioners' complaints (non-/under-/over-payment of pensions, gratuities, death benefits);","Carry out monthly payment analysis and payroll review;","Pre-payment audit of all payment vouchers;","Compliance audit;","Review of financial statements (transcripts, bank reconciliations);","Safeguard the department's assets;","Periodic examination and certification of vouchers and records;","Produce monthly audit reports; and","Verify Civil Service pensioners periodically with Pension Support."]},{"p":"**Powers of the Commission over PTAD:**"},{"ol":["Regulate and supervise the activities of the Federal and FCT PTADs to ensure compliance with the Act;","Intervene to administer and render technical support and advice on the management of the various PTADs as directed by the President; and","Ensure the PTADs operate under the rules, regulations and directives issued by the Commission."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Monthly emolument","tex":"\\text{Basic} + \\text{Housing} + \\text{Transport}"},{"lb":"Employer contribution (minimum)","tex":"10\\% \\times \\text{Monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Employee contribution (minimum)","tex":"8\\% \\times \\text{Monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Group life cover (minimum)","tex":"3 \\times \\text{Annual total emolument}"},{"lb":"Statutory Reserve Fund transfer","tex":"12.5\\% \\times \\text{Net profit after tax}"},{"lb":"Lump sum constraint at retirement","tex":"\\text{Residual balance} \\ge \\text{fund a pension of } 50\\% \\text{ of final annual remuneration}"}],"focus":"Reliably examined. Section A commonly tests the contribution rates, the meaning of monthly emolument, the roles of PenCom/PFA/PFC, the composition of PenCom, and the categories exempted from the scheme. Section B commonly asks for the features of the Contributory Pension Scheme, the functions or powers of PenCom, PFA licensing requirements, the deficiencies of the 2014 Act, or the functions of PTAD. A computation of contributions from a salary schedule is easy marks provided the pensionable emolument is identified correctly.","errors":["Applying the contribution percentages to gross pay instead of to basic, housing and transport.","Giving the employer rate as 7.5% and the employee rate as 7.5%; those were the rates under the 2004 Act, superseded in 2014 by 10% and 8%.","Saying the PFA holds the pension assets; the PFC does.","Stating the remittance period as seven calendar days rather than seven working days.","Treating the group life insurance premium as part of the 18% contribution; it is separate and additional.","Forgetting that the lump sum is limited by the requirement to leave enough to fund 50% of final annual remuneration.","Confusing the 12.5%/8% military rates under the Armed Forces Pension Act with the general 10%/8% rates under the Pension Reform Act 2014."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the Pension Reform Act 2014, the minimum contribution rates are","o":["7.5% employer and 7.5% employee","10% employer and 8% employee","8% employer and 10% employee","15% employer and 5% employee","10% employer and 10% employee"],"a":1,"w":"The 2014 Act raised the combined rate to 18%, split 10% employer and 8% employee. 7.5% each were the rates under the repealed 2004 Act.","src":"Chapter 4.4","sec":"4.4"},{"q":"For pension purposes, \"monthly emolument\" means","o":["gross monthly salary","basic salary plus housing and transport allowances","basic salary only","basic salary plus all allowances","net salary after tax and deductions"],"a":1,"w":"The Act defines it as basic salary, housing allowance and transport allowance. Other allowances are excluded unless the employer elects to include them.","src":"Chapter 4.4","sec":"4.4"},{"q":"Pension contributions must be remitted to the Pension Fund Custodian within","o":["seven calendar days of payment of salary","seven working days of payment of salary","thirty days of the end of the month","fourteen working days","the end of the following month"],"a":1,"w":"Section 11(3)(b) requires remittance within seven working days of paying the salary, with a penalty of not less than 2% per month on late remittance.","src":"Chapter 4.6","sec":"4.6"},{"q":"The body that holds pension fund assets in trust is the","o":["Pension Fund Administrator","Pension Fund Custodian","National Pension Commission","Central Bank of Nigeria","employer"],"a":1,"w":"The PFC holds the assets; the PFA manages and invests them but never holds them. That separation is the central control of the scheme.","src":"Chapter 4.9","sec":"4.9"},{"q":"An employer must maintain a group life insurance policy for each employee of not less than","o":["twice the annual total emolument","three times the annual total emolument","five times the annual basic salary","the annual total emolument","ten times the monthly emolument"],"a":1,"w":"Section 4(5) requires cover of a minimum of three times the annual total emolument, in addition to the pension contributions.","src":"Chapter 4.4","sec":"4.4"},{"q":"An employee with an annual basic salary of ₦1,800,000, housing allowance ₦450,000 and transport allowance ₦150,000 will have an annual employee pension contribution of","o":["₦144,000","₦192,000","₦240,000","₦180,000","₦216,000"],"a":1,"w":"Pensionable emolument is $1{,}800{,}000 + 450{,}000 + 150{,}000 = ₦2{,}400{,}000$; the employee contributes 8% of it.","calc":"8\\% \\times 2{,}400{,}000 = ₦192{,}000","src":"Chapter 4.4","sec":"4.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of the Pension Reform Act 2014?","o":["To establish uniform rules and standards for pension administration in Nigeria","To assist improvident individuals to save towards their inactive years","To give a bulk amount to employees at retirement without any contribution from salary","To provide for the smooth operation of the contributory pension scheme","To ensure every employee receives retirement benefits as and when due"],"a":2,"w":"The 2014 Act is a fully contributory scheme; a non-contributory bulk payment is the feature of the old Defined Benefit Scheme it replaced.","src":"Chapter 4.22 (study text Section A Q1)","sec":"4.22"},{"q":"The gross salary (basic plus allowances) attached to a retiring officer's substantive rank is known as","o":["consolidated salary","pensionable emoluments","terminal benefit","gratuity","annuity"],"a":1,"w":"Pensionable emoluments — basic salary plus the regular allowances attached to the substantive rank — is the base on which pension and contributions are computed.","src":"Chapter 4.22 (study text Section A Q6)","sec":"4.22"},{"q":"Pension administration under the Defined Benefit Scheme (the old, pre-2004 pensions) is the responsibility of the","o":["National Pension Commission","Pension Transitional Arrangements Directorate (PTAD)","Office of the Head of the Civil Service","Pension Fund Administrators","Federal Ministry of Finance"],"a":1,"w":"PTAD, established by sections 42 and 44 of the PRA 2014, administers the residual Defined Benefit Scheme for existing pensioners.","src":"Chapter 4.22 (study text Section A Q8)","sec":"4.22"}],"theory":[{"q":"The Pension Reform Act 2014 established a Contributory Pension Scheme in place of the defined benefit arrangement previously operated in the public service. (a) Distinguish between a defined benefit and a defined contribution pension scheme. (b) Explain why the previous defined benefit scheme failed. (c) Describe the roles of the National Pension Commission, the Pension Fund Administrator and the Pension Fund Custodian. (d) State the modes by which a retiree may withdraw benefits from a Retirement Savings Account.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) The two types of scheme"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Feature","Defined benefit","Defined contribution"],"rows":[["The promise","A stated pension, typically a fraction of final salary for each year of service","No stated pension; the benefit is whatever the accumulated fund will provide"],["Contributions","Set at whatever level is needed to fund the promise; usually the employer alone","A fixed percentage of emoluments by both employer and employee"],["Who bears investment risk","The employer, who must make good any shortfall","The employee, whose pension rises or falls with investment returns"],["Who bears longevity risk","The employer, who must pay for as long as the pensioner lives","The employee, unless an annuity is purchased"],["Funding","Frequently unfunded and paid from current revenue","Fully funded in an individual account before benefits fall due"],["Portability","Poor — the benefit is tied to service with the one employer","Complete — the Retirement Savings Account follows the employee"],["Accounting complexity","High; requires actuarial valuation and recognition of a net liability","Low; the expense is simply the contribution payable for the period"]]}},{"h4":"(b) Why the previous scheme failed"},{"ol":["**It was unfunded.** Pensions were paid out of the annual budget as they fell due, with no fund set aside. When revenue was short, pensions were simply not paid, and arrears accumulated into hundreds of billions of naira.","**The liability was never measured.** Because no actuarial valuation was performed, government did not know the size of the obligation it had incurred, and successive budgets under-provided for it.","**Records were unreliable.** There was no reliable database of pensioners. Verification exercises repeatedly disclosed large numbers of ghost pensioners, and genuine pensioners were excluded through poor record-keeping.","**Administration was weak and corrupt.** The pension administration was fragmented across ministries and agencies; several large-scale frauds were subsequently prosecuted.","**Benefits were not portable.** An employee who moved between the public and private sectors, or between agencies, frequently lost accrued entitlement.","**Payment was humiliating and unreliable.** Elderly pensioners were required to attend periodic verification exercises in person, sometimes waiting for days, and payments were months or years in arrears.","**Coverage was narrow.** Most private sector employees had no pension arrangement at all."]},{"h4":"(c) The institutional roles"},{"p":"**National Pension Commission (PenCom)** — the regulator. It does not manage or hold any pension money:"},{"ul":["Licenses and supervises Pension Fund Administrators and Custodians;","Issues guidelines, rules and standards for the administration and investment of pension funds;","Approves the categories of investment and the limits on each;","Maintains a national databank of contributors;","Receives and resolves complaints;","Carries out routine and special examinations of operators; and","Imposes sanctions for breach."]},{"p":"**Pension Fund Administrator (PFA)** — the manager. It gives instructions to the custodian but never holds the assets itself:"},{"ul":["Opens and maintains the Retirement Savings Account for each contributor;","Invests and manages the pension fund within the categories PenCom permits;","Maintains books of account on all transactions;","Provides each contributor with a statement of account at least once a year;","Computes and pays retirement benefits; and","Reports to the Commission."]},{"p":"**Pension Fund Custodian (PFC)** — the trustee of the assets. It has no discretion over the investment of the fund:"},{"ul":["Receives contributions remitted by employers on behalf of the PFA and notifies the PFA within 24 hours;","Holds the pension fund assets in trust for the contributors;","Settles transactions and undertakes activities relating to the assets only on the instruction of the PFA; and","Reports to the Commission."]},{"p":"The separation of the three functions is deliberate and is the principal safeguard in the scheme. The administrator decides but cannot touch the money; the custodian holds the money but cannot decide; and the Commission supervises both. No single party can misapply the fund without the concurrence of another."},{"h4":"(d) Modes of withdrawal"},{"ol":["**Programmed withdrawal** — monthly or quarterly payments computed by the PFA by reference to the retiree’s expected life span, drawn from the balance in the account, which continues to earn investment income.","**Annuity for life** — purchased from a life insurance company licensed by NAICOM under guidelines jointly issued by NAICOM and PenCom, providing monthly or quarterly payments for life.","**A lump sum from the balance** — provided that the amount remaining after the withdrawal is sufficient to fund a programmed withdrawal or an annuity of not less than 50% of the retiree’s annual remuneration as at the date of retirement."]},{"note":"A retiree who disengages before the age of 50 and remains unemployed for four months may withdraw not more than 25% of the balance, and on the death of a contributor the balance is paid to the beneficiaries named under a will or letters of administration, together with the proceeds of the group life policy."}],"src":"Chapter 4.4, 4.6, 4.7–4.9, 4.19","sec":"4.4"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"Employee Benefits and Retirement Benefit Plans","brief":"IPSAS 39 — the four categories of employee benefit, recognition and measurement of short-term benefits, the distinction between defined contribution and defined benefit post-employment plans, and how IPSAS 39 amended the superseded IPSAS 25 — followed by the separate standard for the financial statements of a retirement benefit plan itself, which the source text calls “IPSAS 49”.","outcomes":["State the objective and scope of IPSAS 39","Classify a benefit into one of the four categories","Account for short-term benefits including compensated absences and bonuses","Account for a defined contribution plan","Explain the components of defined benefit cost and where each is recognised","Explain how IPSAS 39 amended IPSAS 25","State the disclosures required in the financial statements of a retirement benefit plan"],"secs":[{"n":"5.1","t":"IPSAS 39 — objective, scope and definitions","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS 39, Employee Benefits**, is the current standard, having amended (in effect replaced) **IPSAS 25**. Its objective is to prescribe the accounting and disclosure for employee benefits, requiring an entity to recognise:"},{"ul":["a **liability** when an employee has rendered service in exchange for benefits to be paid in the future; and","an **expense** when the entity consumes the economic benefit or service potential arising from the service rendered."]},{"p":"Employee benefits include:"},{"ol":["**Short-term employee benefits** — wages, salaries, social security contributions, paid annual and sick leave, profit-sharing and bonuses payable within twelve months, and non-monetary benefits such as medical care, housing, cars and subsidised goods or services, for current employees;","**Post-employment benefits** such as pensions, other retirement benefits, post-employment life insurance and post-employment medical care;","**Other long-term employee benefits**, which may include long-service or sabbatical leave, jubilee or long-service benefits, long-term disability benefits and, if not payable wholly within twelve months, profit-sharing, bonuses and deferred compensation; and","**Termination benefits**."]},{"p":"The standard applies to all employee benefits **except** share-based transactions (dealt with by the relevant standard). Its scope does not cover an employer's own reporting on retirement benefit plans — that is a separate matter, addressed by the standard at section 5.8."},{"h4":"Key definitions"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Actuarial gains and losses**","Comprise experience adjustments (the effects of differences between previous actuarial assumptions and what actually occurred) and the effects of changes in actuarial assumptions"],["**Assets held by a long-term employee benefit fund**","Assets (other than non-transferable financial instruments issued by the reporting entity) held by a fund legally separate from the reporting entity, existing solely to pay employee benefits, and unavailable to the reporting entity’s own creditors even in bankruptcy"],["**Composite social security programmes**","Programmes established by legislation that operate as multi-employer plans to provide both post-employment and other benefits not given in exchange for service"],["**Current service cost**","The increase in the present value of the defined benefit obligation resulting from employee service in the current period"],["**Defined benefit plans**","Post-employment benefit plans other than defined contribution plans"],["**Defined contribution plans**","Post-employment benefit plans under which an entity pays fixed contributions into a separate fund and has no legal or constructive obligation to pay further contributions if the fund cannot pay all benefits relating to current and prior service"],["**Employee benefits**","All forms of consideration given by an entity in exchange for service rendered by employees"],["**Interest cost**","The increase during a period in the present value of a defined benefit obligation, which arises because the benefits are one period closer to settlement"],["**Multi-employer plans**","Defined contribution or defined benefit plans (other than state plans and composite social security programmes) that pool the assets of unrelated entities and use them to provide benefits determined without regard to which entity employs which participant"],["**Other long-term employee benefits**","Employee benefits (other than post-employment and termination benefits) not falling wholly due within twelve months after the end of the period in which the related service is rendered"],["**Past service cost**","The increase in the present value of the defined benefit obligation for prior-period service, arising in the current period from the introduction of or changes to benefits; it may be positive (benefits introduced or improved) or negative (benefits reduced)"],["**Plan assets**","Assets held by a long-term employee benefit fund, plus qualifying insurance policies"],["**Post-employment benefits**","Employee benefits (other than termination benefits) payable after the completion of employment"],["**Post-employment benefit plans**","Formal or informal arrangements under which an entity provides post-employment benefits for one or more employees"],["**Present value of a defined benefit obligation**","The present value, without deducting any plan assets, of expected future payments required to settle the obligation from current and prior service"],["**Qualifying insurance policy**","A policy issued by an insurer that is not a related party, whose proceeds can only be used to pay or fund a defined benefit plan and are not available to the reporting entity’s own creditors"],["**Return on plan assets**","Interest, dividends and other revenue from plan assets, plus realised and unrealised gains or losses, less costs of administering the plan and tax payable by the plan itself"],["**Short-term employee benefits**","Employee benefits (other than termination benefits) falling wholly due within twelve months after the end of the period in which the related service is rendered"],["**State plans**","Plans other than composite social security programmes, established by legislation, that operate as if they are multi-employer plans for all entities in defined economic categories"],["**Termination benefits**","Benefits payable as a result of either an entity’s decision to terminate an employee’s employment before the normal retirement date, or an employee’s decision to accept voluntary redundancy in exchange for those benefits"],["**Vested employee benefits**","Employee benefits that are not conditional on future employment"]]}}]},{"n":"5.2","t":"Recognition and measurement of short-term employee benefits","b":[{"p":"These are the simplest category: no actuarial assumptions are required, the obligation is **not discounted**, and there are no remeasurement gains or losses. When an employee has rendered service during an accounting period, the entity recognises the undiscounted amount of short-term employee benefits expected to be paid in exchange for that service:"},{"ol":["As a **liability (accrued expense)**, after deducting any amount already paid. If the amount already paid exceeds the undiscounted amount of the benefits, the excess is recognised as an **asset (prepaid expense)** to the extent the prepayment will lead to, for example, a reduction in future payments or a cash refund.","As an **expense**, unless another standard requires or permits the benefit to be included in the cost of an asset."]},{"h4":"Compensated absences"},{"ul":["For **accumulating** compensated absences, the expected cost is recognised **as the employees render the service that increases their entitlement** to future compensated absences.","  — *Vesting*: the employee is entitled to a cash payment for unused entitlement on leaving. The whole accumulated entitlement is provided for.","  — *Non-vesting*: no payment on leaving. Provide only for the amount expected to be used, taking account of the possibility that employees may leave.","For **non-accumulating** compensated absences (such as sick leave that lapses, or maternity leave), the cost is recognised **when the absences occur**, not before."]},{"eg":{"t":"Accumulating vesting leave","q":[{"p":"A government agency employs 120 staff, each entitled to 20 working days of paid annual leave. Unused leave may be carried forward for one year, and any balance unused on leaving is paid in cash. At 31 December 2025 the average unused entitlement is 4 days per employee, and the average daily rate of pay is ₦12,500. Determine the amount to be recognised."}],"a":[{"p":"The entitlement is **accumulating and vesting**, so the full accumulated balance must be provided for regardless of whether the employees are expected to use it: it will be paid out in cash if it is not taken as leave."},{"tex":"\\text{Liability} = 120 \\text{ employees} \\times 4 \\text{ days} \\times ₦12{,}500 = ₦6{,}000{,}000"},{"p":"The entries at 31 December 2025 are:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Employee benefits expense","6,000,000",""],["Accrued leave liability","","6,000,000"]]}},{"note":"Had the leave been **non-vesting** — lapsing unpaid on departure — the agency would provide only for the days expected actually to be taken. If, say, 15% of accumulated days were historically forfeited, the liability would be $₦6{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.85 = ₦5{,}100{,}000$."}]}},{"h4":"Bonus and profit-sharing plans"},{"p":"The expected cost is recognised when, and only when, the entity has a **present legal or constructive obligation** to make the payment as a result of past events, and a **reliable estimate** of the obligation can be made. A constructive obligation exists where past practice has created a valid expectation on the part of employees."}]},{"n":"5.3","t":"Post-employment benefits: defined contribution and defined benefit plans","b":[{"p":"Post-employment benefits include retirement benefits such as pensions, and other post-employment benefits such as post-employment life insurance and post-employment medical care. The accounting depends entirely on **where the risk lies**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined contribution plan","Defined benefit plan"],"rows":[["Obligation of the entity","Limited to the amount it agrees to contribute to the fund. Once paid, the entity has no further legal or constructive obligation","To provide the agreed benefits to current and former employees, whatever the fund earns"],["Actuarial risk (benefits less than expected)","Falls on the **employee**","Falls, in substance, on the **entity**"],["Investment risk (assets insufficient to meet benefits)","Falls on the **employee**","Falls on the **entity** — if experience is worse than expected, the entity's obligation may increase"],["Measurement","Undiscounted contributions payable (discounted if not due within twelve months)","Present value of the defined benefit obligation, less the fair value of plan assets"],["Actuary required?","No","Yes — the projected unit credit method"],["Nigerian example","The Contributory Pension Scheme under the Pension Reform Act 2014 (Chapter 4)","The pre-2004 public service pension scheme; some gratuity schemes"]]}},{"h4":"Defined contribution plans"},{"p":"Recognise the contribution payable in exchange for service rendered during the period as a liability, after deducting any contribution already paid, and as an expense. If the contribution already paid exceeds the amount due, recognise the excess as an asset (a prepayment) to the extent that it will lead to a refund or a reduction in future payments."},{"eg":{"t":"Defined contribution entries","q":[{"p":"A ministry's pensionable emoluments for the year are ₦480,000,000. The employer contributes 10% and the employee 8%. By the year end ₦44,000,000 of the employer contribution had been remitted. Show the amounts to be recognised."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Employer contribution} = 10\\% \\times 480{,}000{,}000 = ₦48{,}000{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Employee contribution} = 8\\% \\times 480{,}000{,}000 = ₦38{,}400{,}000"},{"p":"The employer contribution is an expense of the period; the employee contribution is a deduction from gross pay and is not an additional expense — it is already inside the ₦480,000,000 salary cost."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Pension contribution expense","48,000,000",""],["Cash (remitted)","","44,000,000"],["Accrued pension contribution (liability)","","4,000,000"]]}},{"p":"The unremitted ₦4,000,000 is a liability at the reporting date, and if the seven-working-day limit (Chapter 4.6) has passed it also attracts the statutory penalty of not less than 2% per month, which is an additional expense."}]}},{"h4":"Defined benefit plans"},{"p":"These require the **projected unit credit method**: each period of service gives rise to an additional unit of benefit entitlement, and each unit is measured separately and discounted to build up the final obligation. The net liability recognised is:"},{"tex":"\\text{Net defined benefit liability} = \\text{PV of the defined benefit obligation} - \\text{Fair value of plan assets}"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Component of defined benefit cost (IPSAS 39)","What it is","Recognised in"],"rows":[["**Service cost**","The liability arising from the employee providing service during the period (includes current service cost and, immediately, past service cost)","Surplus or deficit"],["**Net interest** on the net defined benefit liability or asset","The interest expense/revenue on the net defined benefit liability (asset), replacing the separate interest cost and expected-return-on-assets components used under IPSAS 25","Surplus or deficit"],["**Remeasurements**","Other changes in the value of the defined benefit obligation, such as changes in estimates, and other changes in the value of plan assets","**Net assets / equity** — not surplus or deficit, and never reclassified"]]}},{"warn":"The single most examinable point on defined benefit plans is that **remeasurements bypass surplus or deficit entirely** and are taken to net assets/equity, where they remain permanently. Service cost and net interest go through surplus or deficit. Getting these two the wrong way round loses the whole of the marks for that part. This is the current, IPSAS 39 position — section 5.6 explains how it differs from the superseded IPSAS 25 corridor approach."},{"h4":"Other long-term employee benefits and termination benefits"},{"p":"**Other long-term employee benefits** are measured on the same basis as a defined benefit obligation, but with one important simplification: **all** of the cost, including remeasurements, is recognised in **surplus or deficit**. There is no separate treatment of actuarial gains and losses, because the uncertainty and the amounts involved are generally smaller."},{"p":"**Termination benefits** are recognised at the **earlier** of: (i) when the entity can **no longer withdraw** the offer of the benefits; and (ii) when the entity recognises **costs for a restructuring** that involves the payment of termination benefits. They arise from the **termination** of employment rather than from service rendered, which is why they are recognised at the point of decision rather than spread over the service period. If not expected to be settled wholly within twelve months, they are discounted."}]},{"n":"5.4","t":"Statement of financial position and reimbursement","b":[{"warn":"This section transcribes the study text’s own **5.1.4 and 5.1.5**, which are headed “paragraph 65 of the standard” and “paragraph 121 of this standard” and describe a **deferred-recognition (corridor) mechanism** — unrecognised actuarial gains/losses and unrecognised past service cost carried outside the statement of financial position. This is the mechanics of the **superseded IPSAS 25**, which section 5.6 below confirms IPSAS 39 **removed**: under IPSAS 39 all remeasurements are recognised immediately, in net assets/equity (section 5.3). The paragraph numbers and formula are kept here exactly as printed, because the chapter presents them as part of its account of the standard, but they should not be read as the current position — use section 5.3 for that."},{"p":"As printed, the amount recognised as a defined benefit liability is the net total of:"},{"ol":["The present value of the defined benefit obligation at the reporting date;","Plus any actuarial gains (less any actuarial losses) not recognised;","Minus any past service cost not yet recognised; and","Minus the fair value at the reporting date of plan assets (if any) out of which the obligations are to be settled directly."]},{"p":"Where the resulting amount is negative (an asset), it is measured at the **lower** of: (a) the amount determined above; and (b) the total of any cumulative unrecognised net actuarial losses and past service cost, plus the present value of any economic benefit available as a refund from the plan or a reduction in future contributions, discounted at a rate reflecting the time value of money."},{"h4":"Reimbursement"},{"p":"When, and only when, it is **virtually certain** that another party will reimburse some or all of the expenditure required to settle a defined benefit obligation, an entity recognises its right to reimbursement as a **separate asset**, measured at fair value. In all other respects the asset is treated in the same way as plan assets. In the statement of financial performance, the expense related to a defined benefit plan may be presented net of the amount recognised for a reimbursement."}]},{"n":"5.5","t":"Financial performance disclosure for defined benefit plans","b":[{"p":"An entity discloses information enabling users to evaluate the nature of its defined benefit plans and the financial effect of changes in those plans during the period. For each defined benefit plan, this includes:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Area","What must be disclosed"],"rows":[["Policy and description","The accounting policy for recognising actuarial gains and losses; a general description of the type of plan"],["Obligation reconciliation","A reconciliation of opening and closing balances of the present value of the defined benefit obligation, showing separately (where applicable) current service cost, interest cost, contributions by plan participants, actuarial gains and losses, foreign exchange effects, benefits paid, past service cost, entity combinations, curtailments and settlements"],["Funding status","An analysis of the obligation into amounts arising from plans that are wholly unfunded and amounts from plans that are wholly or partly funded"],["Plan assets reconciliation","A reconciliation of opening and closing balances of the fair value of plan assets and of any reimbursement right, showing separately (where applicable) expected return on plan assets, actuarial gains and losses, foreign exchange effects, employer and participant contributions, benefits paid, entity combinations and settlements"],["Position reconciliation","A reconciliation of the obligation and the plan assets to the amounts recognised in the statement of financial position, showing net unrecognised actuarial gains/losses, unrecognised past service cost, any amount not recognised as an asset because of the ceiling, the fair value of any reimbursement right, and other amounts recognised"],["Expense analysis","The total expense recognised in surplus or deficit for current service cost, interest cost, expected return on plan assets and on any reimbursement asset, actuarial gains and losses, past service cost, and the effect of any curtailment or settlement"],["Net assets/equity analysis","The total amount recognised in the statement of changes in net assets/equity for actuarial gains and losses, and the cumulative amount recognised there, for entities using that presentation"],["Plan asset detail","For each major category of plan asset — equity instruments, debt instruments, property and all other assets — the percentage each category constitutes of the fair value of total plan assets; amounts included for the entity's own financial instruments and for property occupied or assets used by the entity"],["Assumptions","A narrative description of the basis for the overall expected rate of return on assets; the actual return on plan assets and any reimbursement asset; the principal actuarial assumptions at the reporting date — discount rate and its basis, expected rates of return, expected salary increases, medical cost trend rates, and any other material assumption, each disclosed in absolute terms"],["Sensitivity","The effect of a one-percentage-point increase and decrease in the assumed medical cost trend rate on the aggregate current service and interest cost, and on the accumulated post-employment medical obligation, holding all other assumptions constant"],["History","For the current and previous four annual periods: the present value of the obligation, the fair value of plan assets and the surplus or deficit in the plan; and experience adjustments on plan liabilities and plan assets, expressed as an amount or a percentage"],["Forward look","The employer's best estimate, as soon as it can reasonably be determined, of contributions expected to be paid to the plan in the next annual period"]]}}]},{"n":"5.6","t":"How IPSAS 39 amended IPSAS 25","b":[{"p":"Pension liabilities are highly significant in the public sector, and the requirements for defined benefit pension plans were a key aspect of IPSAS 25. IPSAS 39’s new recognition, presentation and disclosure requirements are intended to make financial statements more useful and transparent about liabilities related to defined benefit plans. In outline, IPSAS 39 amended IPSAS 25 by:"},{"ol":["**Recognition** — removing the deferred-recognition \"corridor approach\";","**Presentation** — eliminating the presentational options that existed for actuarial gains and losses;","**Disclosure** — introducing disclosure objectives built around the characteristics, risks and financial-statement effect of defined benefit plans; and","**Scope** — widening its scope to cover all public sector entities, Government Business Enterprises **and** private sector entities, where IPSAS 25 applied only to public sector entities other than Government Business Enterprises."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["IPSAS 25","IPSAS 39"],"rows":[["**Recognition of actuarial gains and losses.** Three options: surplus or deficit in the period they occur; net assets/equity in the period they occur; or deferral to future periods (the “corridor approach”). A deficit could be recognised as an asset, a surplus as a liability — faithful representation and comparability were difficult to achieve.","**Removes the options.** Re-measurements are recognised in net assets/equity in the period they occur; a surplus is recognised as a net defined benefit **asset** and a deficit as a net defined benefit **liability**. Requiring immediate recognition improves comparability and understandability."],["**Components of defined benefit cost.** Interest cost and expected return on plan assets as separate items; curtailments treated as one form of past service cost.","**New components.** Service cost — the liability from service provided during the period; net interest — replacing interest cost and expected return with a single net interest expense/revenue on the net defined benefit liability (asset); remeasurements — other changes in the obligation (e.g. changes in estimates) and other changes in the value of plan assets."],["**Settlements.** Not classified.","**Clarified.** A settlement is the difference between the present value of the obligation being settled and the settlement price, or a payment of benefits not set out in the terms of the plan; any difference between an estimated and an actual benefit payment is an actuarial gain or loss."],["**Disclosures.** An entity discloses information enabling users to evaluate the nature of its defined benefit plans and the financial effect of changes in those plans during the period.","**Disclosure objectives.** An entity discloses information that explains the characteristics of its defined benefit plans and the risks associated with them; identifies and explains the amounts in its financial statements arising from them; and describes how they may affect the amount, timing and uncertainty of future cash flows."],["**Mortality.** Actuarial assumptions on mortality are the entity’s best estimates of the variables that will determine the ultimate cost of providing post-employment benefits.","**Made explicit.** Mortality assumptions used to determine the defined benefit obligation are the best estimate of the mortality of plan members both during and after employment."],["**Taxes and administration costs.** Not treated.","**Clarified.** Taxes payable by the plan on contributions relating to service before the reporting date, or on benefits from that service, are included in the estimate of the present value of the obligation; other taxes are deducted from the return on plan assets; and administration costs directly related to managing plan assets are deducted from the return on plan assets."],["**Changes to defined benefit cost.** Distinguishes recognition of vested and unvested past service cost.","**Simplified.** Does not distinguish vested from unvested past service cost; includes curtailments as one form of past service cost; and recognises that unvested past service cost results in the same accounting as curtailments."],["**Presentation.** Does not specify whether current service cost, interest cost and the expected return on plan assets should be presented as components of a single item of revenue or expense.","**Clarified.** Presentation of service cost and net interest on the net defined benefit liability (asset) follows IPSAS 1, *Presentation of Financial Statements*."],["**Termination benefits.** Recognised as a liability and expense when, and only when, the entity is demonstrably committed to terminate employment before the normal retirement date, or to provide termination benefits as a result of an offer to encourage voluntary redundancy.","**Simplified trigger.** Recognised when the entity can **no longer withdraw** the offer (for example, when the employee accepts it)."],["**Other long-term employee benefits.** The net total of current service cost, interest cost, expected return on plan/reimbursement assets, actuarial gains and losses (recognised immediately), past service cost (recognised immediately) and the effect of curtailments or settlements is recognised as expense or revenue.","**Restated in the new components.** The net total of service cost, net interest on the net defined benefit liability (asset), and remeasurements of the net defined benefit liability (asset) is recognised in surplus or deficit."]]}}]},{"n":"5.7","t":"Accounting documentation, procedures and treatment","b":[{"h4":"Documentation"},{"ul":["Annual leave roster","Personal emoluments record cards","Post-employment benefit (pension) journal vouchers","Cashbook","Payroll journal vouchers","Payroll deduction journal vouchers","Bank pay-in advice","Approved establishment list"]},{"h4":"Procedures"},{"ol":["Confirm budgetary provisions for employee benefits for the year.","Recognise employee benefits in the various books and ledgers.","Recognise and accrue for employee benefits not yet paid for the year."]},{"h4":"Accounting treatment"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Entry","Remarks"],"rows":[["Dr. Gross salary benefit account &nbsp; Cr. Net salary payable account &nbsp; Cr. Relevant deductions accounts","To recognise salary payable to employees"],["Dr. Net salary payable account &nbsp; Dr. Relevant deductions accounts &nbsp; Cr. Bank account","To recognise net salary payable and remittance of deductions"]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 5.1 — payroll accounting entries","q":[{"p":"Mr. George extracted the following balances from the October 2017 payroll of the Ministry of Culture, which pays its staff one month in arrears: gross salary ₦2,511,000; PAYE deduction ₦350,000; pension deduction ₦250,000; NHIS deduction ₦105,000; cooperative contributions ₦85,000. The Ministry also granted overtime allowance of ₦450,000 to its staff for the month. Show the relevant accounting entries."}],"a":[{"p":"First, the gross salary, deductions and net salary payable for October are recognised (payment falls in November, since the Ministry pays one month in arrears):"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Details","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Gross salary","2,511,000",""],["PAYE deduction","","350,000"],["Pension deduction","","250,000"],["NHIS deduction","","105,000"],["Cooperative contributions","","85,000"],["Net salary payable","","1,721,000"]]}},{"p":"In November, when the deductions are remitted and net salary is paid:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Details","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["PAYE deduction","350,000",""],["Pension deduction","250,000",""],["NHIS deduction","105,000",""],["Cooperative contributions","85,000",""],["Net salary payable","1,721,000",""],["Bank","","2,511,000"]]}},{"p":"The overtime allowance is a separate staff benefit, recognised and then paid in the same pattern:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Details","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Staff benefit expense","450,000",""],["Staff benefit expense payable","","450,000"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Details","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Staff benefit expense payable","450,000",""],["Bank","","450,000"]]}}]}},{"note":"On first adopting IPSAS 25, an entity may choose either to recognise the full transitional liability immediately, or to recognise it as an expense on a straight-line basis over up to **five years**."}]},{"n":"5.8","t":"Retirement benefit plans — objective, scope and definitions","b":[{"warn":"The study text’s own chapter contents list this section as **“5.2 — IPSAS 49, Retirement Benefit Plans”**. This is almost certainly a source error: the IPSASB has not issued a standard numbered IPSAS 49, and the content that follows — a retirement benefit plan treated as its own reporting entity, defined benefit versus defined contribution obligations, the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits, and the specific set of statements required — closely mirrors **IAS 26, Accounting and Reporting by Retirement Benefit Plans** (the IFRS Foundation’s standard for this exact topic). The section is transcribed below exactly as the source presents it; treat the specific standard number with caution and verify against the current IPSASB handbook if it matters for a specific question."},{"p":"The objective of this standard is to prescribe the accounting and reporting requirements for **public sector retirement benefit plans**, which provide retirement benefits to public sector employees and other eligible participants."},{"h4":"Scope"},{"ul":["A retirement benefit plan that prepares and presents financial statements under the **accrual basis of accounting** applies this standard.","Retirement benefit plans are sometimes called pension schemes, superannuation schemes or retirement benefit schemes. The standard treats a retirement benefit plan as a **reporting entity separate from the employers** of the plan's participants. All other standards apply to the plan's financial statements only to the extent not superseded by this one.","The standard deals with reporting to participants **as a group**; it does not cover reports to individual participants about their own retirement benefit rights.","It deals with retirement benefits for public sector employees and other eligible participants. It does **not** deal with other employment benefits such as termination payments, deferred compensation, long-service leave, special early retirement or redundancy plans, health and welfare plans, bonus plans, or government social security arrangements.","Plans are normally either defined benefit or defined contribution, though some contain characteristics of both (**hybrid plans**); for the purposes of this standard, hybrid plans are treated as defined benefit plans.","The standard applies whether or not a separate fund (with or without trustees) is created to receive contributions and pay benefits.","A plan with assets invested with an insurance company is within scope on the same basis as a privately invested plan, unless the insurance contract is in the name of a specified participant (or group) and the retirement benefit obligation is solely the insurance company's responsibility."]},{"h4":"Definitions"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits**","The present value of expected payments by the plan to participants, attributable to service as employees already rendered"],["**Defined benefit plans**","Retirement benefit plans other than defined contribution plans, for the purposes of this standard"],["**Defined contribution obligations**","The amounts owed to participants under the terms of a defined contribution plan"],["**Defined contribution plans**","Plans under which the retirement benefits paid are determined by contributions to a fund together with investment earnings on them"],["**Funding**","The transfer of assets to an entity (the plan) separate from the employer/sponsor, to meet future retirement benefit obligations"],["**Net assets available for benefits**","For a defined benefit plan — plan assets less liabilities other than the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits (and, in a hybrid plan, the defined contribution obligation). For a defined contribution plan — plan assets less liabilities other than the defined contribution obligations"],["**Participants**","The members of a plan and others entitled to benefits under it"],["**Retirement benefit obligations**","For a defined benefit plan — the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits. For a defined contribution plan — the defined contribution obligations"],["**Retirement benefit plans**","Arrangements whereby an employer/sponsor provides benefits for participants on or after termination of service (as an annual income and/or a lump sum) that can be determined or estimated in advance of retirement, from the provisions of a document or from the employer's/sponsor's practices"],["**Vested benefits**","Benefits whose rights, under the plan's conditions, are not conditional on continued employment"]]}},{"h4":"Recognition and measurement"},{"ul":["For **defined benefit plans**, retirement benefit obligations owed to participants are recognised in the statement of financial position as a **provision for the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits**.","For **defined contribution plans**, retirement benefit obligations owed to participants are recognised as **defined contribution obligations**.","Retirement benefit **plan investments** are measured at **fair value**.","The actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits under a defined benefit plan is based on the benefits promised under the plan's terms, on service rendered to date, using **projected salary levels**. If an actuarial valuation has not been prepared as at the date of the financial statements, the most recent valuation is used, updated for any material transactions and material changes in circumstances."]}]},{"n":"5.9","t":"Presentation and disclosure for retirement benefit plans","b":[{"h4":"Presentation of financial statements"},{"p":"A retirement benefit plan, whether defined benefit or defined contribution, presents:"},{"ol":["A statement of financial position;","A statement of changes in net assets available for benefits;","A cash flow statement, prepared using the **direct method**, in accordance with IPSAS 2; and","Notes to the financial statements."]},{"p":"It also explains changes in retirement benefit obligations to participants, either by presenting a **statement of changes in retirement benefit obligations**, or by disclosing a reconciliation between opening and closing obligation balances in the notes."},{"h4":"Financial statement content"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Statement","Line items (if applicable and as appropriate, but not limited to)"],"rows":[["**Statement of financial position**","Plan investments (suitably classified); contributions receivable; other assets; benefits due and payable; other liabilities (excluding retirement benefit obligations to participants); net assets available for benefits; the provision for the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits (defined benefit plan) or the defined contribution obligation (defined contribution plan); and the excess or deficit"],["**Statement of changes in net assets available for benefits**","Employer/sponsor contributions; participant contributions; investment income; other income; benefits paid or payable (analysed, for example, as retirement, death and disability benefits, or lump sums); transfers from and to other plans; administrative expenses; other expenses; and taxes on income"],["**Statement of changes in retirement benefit obligations** (or note reconciliation)","Amendments to the plan; changes in the nature of the plan (e.g. a merger); participant benefits allocated to defined contribution accounts; net changes to defined benefit accrued benefits (e.g. actuarial movements); employer/sponsor contributions; participant contributions; taxes on contributions; benefits paid; and administration expenses"]]}},{"h4":"Disclosure"},{"p":"The notes to the financial statements of a retirement benefit plan, whether defined benefit or defined contribution, disclose:"},{"ul":["A summary of significant accounting policies.","A description of the plan and the effect of any changes in it during the period.","The basis for the valuation of plan assets, including fair value measurement disclosure per class of plan asset as required by the applicable IPSAS.","Details of any single investment exceeding either 5% of net assets available for benefits or 5% of any class or type of security, and details of any investment in the employer/sponsor.","Liabilities other than the provision for the actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits, or the defined contribution obligation.","A description of the funding policy, including any obligation of the employer/sponsor to meet an actuarially determined shortfall in a funded plan."]},{"p":"For **defined benefit plans** specifically, the notes also disclose:"},{"ol":["The actuarial present value of promised retirement benefits, distinguishing vested from non-vested benefits;","A description of the significant actuarial assumptions made and the method used to calculate the actuarial present value;","The effect of any change in actuarial assumptions that has had a significant effect on that present value; and","The date of the actuarial valuation and when the next one will be undertaken."]},{"p":"Every retirement benefit plan's financial statements also contain a **description of the plan**:"},{"ol":["The name(s) of the employer(s)/sponsor(s) and the participant groups covered;","The number of participants receiving benefits and the number of other participants;","The type of plan (defined contribution or defined benefit);","Whether participants contribute;","A description of the retirement benefits promised;","A description of any plan termination terms; and","Changes in any of the above during the period."]}]},{"n":"5.10","t":"Further reading","b":[{"ul":["[IPSAS 39, Employee Benefits](https://www.ifac.org/system/files/publications/files/IPSAS-39-Employee-Benefits.pdf) — IPSASB.","[IPSAS 25, Employee Benefits (superseded by IPSAS 39)](https://www.ifac.org/) — IPSASB, for the corridor-approach history referenced in section 5.6.","[IAS 26, Accounting and Reporting by Retirement Benefit Plans](https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ias-26-accounting-and-reporting-by-retirement-benefit-plans/) — the IFRS Foundation standard the Public Sector retirement-benefit-plan reporting requirements in section 5.8 mirror."]}]},{"n":"5.11","t":"Worked illustration and end-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Illustration 5-1","t":"Ministry of Culture — payroll accounting entries","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr George extracted the following from the October 2017 payroll of the Ministry of Culture. It is the Ministry's policy to pay staff **one month in arrears**."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","₦"],"rows":[["Gross salary","2,511,000"],["PAYE deduction","350,000"],["Pension deduction","250,000"],["NHIS deduction","105,000"],["Cooperative contributions","85,000"]]}},{"p":"The Ministry also granted an overtime allowance of ₦450,000 for the month. Show the relevant accounting entries."}],"a":[{"h4":"Recognising October salary and deductions"},{"ul":["**Dr** Gross salary ₦2,511,000","**Cr** PAYE deduction ₦350,000; Pension deduction ₦250,000; NHIS deduction ₦105,000; Cooperative contributions ₦85,000; **Net salary payable ₦1,721,000**."]},{"h4":"Paying the October salary in November 2017"},{"ul":["**Dr** PAYE ₦350,000; Pension ₦250,000; NHIS ₦105,000; Cooperative ₦85,000; Net salary payable ₦1,721,000","**Cr** Bank ₦2,511,000."]},{"h4":"Overtime (staff benefit)"},{"ul":["**Dr** Staff benefit expense ₦450,000  **Cr** Staff benefit expense payable ₦450,000 — recognise the October benefit.","**Dr** Staff benefit expense payable ₦450,000  **Cr** Bank ₦450,000 — pay it in November 2017."]},{"note":"Because salaries are paid a month in arrears, at 31 October the Ministry recognises the **expense and the payable** (accruals basis); the cash leaves only in November. The deductions (PAYE, pension, NHIS, cooperative) are liabilities to third parties until remitted."}]}},{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set follows, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–8 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["A retirement benefit plan (defined benefit or defined contribution) shall present the following, **except one**: (A) a statement of financial position  (B) a statement of changes in net assets available for benefits  (C) a cash flow statement  (D) a statement of public debt  (E) notes to the financial statements.","A retirement benefit plan's financial statements shall contain the following, **except one**: (A) the names of the employer(s), sponsor(s) and the participant group covered  (B) the number of participants receiving benefits and the number of other participants  (C) the name and address of the Pension Fund Administrator  (D) the type of plan — defined contribution or defined benefit  (E) a note as to whether participants contribute to the plan.","The statement of changes in net assets available for benefits presents opening and closing balances and line items for the following, **except**: (A) employer/sponsor contribution  (B) participants' contribution  (C) investment income  (D) other income  (E) bad debt written off.","The transfer of assets to an entity (the retirement benefit plan) separate from the employer/sponsor, to meet future obligations for retirement benefits, is called …","Under a defined contribution plan the entity's obligation is to provide the agreed benefits to … and … employees.","Employee benefits that are not conditional on future employment are called …","Employee benefits (other than termination benefits) payable after the completion of employment are known as …","The increase during a period in the present value of a defined benefit obligation, because the benefits are one period closer to settlement, is known as …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D — a statement of public debt.** Not a component of a retirement benefit plan's financial statements.","**C — the name and address of the Pension Fund Administrator.** Not a required disclosure of the plan's own statements.","**E — bad debt written off.** Not a line item of the statement of changes in net assets available for benefits.","**Funding.**","**Current and former** employees.","**Vested employee benefits.**","**Post-employment benefits.**","**Interest cost.**"]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory / computation questions with solutions","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**1.** Mr Nuhu Emeka-Balogun joined Faloloi Local Government in 1973. He became Council Treasurer in 2004 on total annual remuneration of ₦4,716,500, was promoted the same year to grade level 14 on ₦5,322,000, and retired on 31 December 2006. In 2004 his pension under the old scheme was computed and transferred to the Central Bank Redemption Bond Account, earning 4% p.a. compound from 31 December 2004. His yearly contribution under the Pension Reform Act 2004 earns 5% p.a. compound from December 2004. The contribution rate is 15% of gross remuneration per annum. Compute (i) the pension due under the old scheme in the Redemption Bond Account at December 2006; (ii) the pension due under the contributory scheme on retirement; (iii) the total pension on retirement."},{"p":"**2.** Write briefly on: (i) the distinction between defined contribution plans and defined benefit plans; (ii) a qualifying insurance policy."},{"p":"**3.** At 31 December 2012, Long-life University's statement of financial position includes a pension liability of ₦150 million. It adopts IPSAS 25 from 1 January 2013. At that date the present value of the obligation is ₦780 million and the fair value of plan assets ₦520 million. Past-service cost for non-vested benefits is ₦24 million, with an average remaining vesting period of eight years. Calculate the transitional liability."}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Mr Nuhu Emeka-Balogun"},{"p":"**(i) Old scheme.** Service 1973–2003 = 31 years. Pension fraction $= a + (n-10)\\times 2 = 30 + (31-10)\\times 2 = 30 + 42 = 72\\%$. Pension $= 72\\% \\times ₦4{,}716{,}500 = ₦3{,}395{,}880$. Compound at 4%: 2004 → ₦3,531,715; 2005 → ₦3,672,984; 2006 → **₦3,819,903** in the Redemption Bond Account."},{"p":"**(ii) Contributory scheme.** Contribution $= 15\\% \\times ₦5{,}322{,}000 = ₦798{,}300$ a year, each year's balance rolled forward at 5%. The study text reaches **≈ ₦2,617,306** by December 2006."},{"p":"**(iii) Total** $= ₦3{,}819{,}903 + ₦2{,}617{,}306 \\approx ₦6{,}437{,}209$."},{"warn":"The study text's printed working for parts (ii) and (iii) is internally inconsistent in the scan — it applies 1.04 in places where 1.05 is meant, and the year-by-year roll-forward does not fully reconcile. Reproduce the **method** (compute the fraction, apply it to remuneration, then compound each stream at its stated rate); do not rely on the printed intermediate figures."},{"h4":"2  Defined contribution vs defined benefit; qualifying insurance policy"},{"ul":["Under a **defined contribution plan**, the entity's obligation is limited to the amount it agrees to contribute; **actuarial risk and investment risk fall on the employee**.","Under a **defined benefit plan**, the entity's obligation is to provide the agreed benefits to current and former employees; **actuarial risk and investment risk fall, in substance, on the entity** — a worse-than-expected experience increases its obligation.","A **qualifying insurance policy** is one issued by an insurer that is not a related party of the reporting entity, where the proceeds can be used only to pay or fund employee benefits under a defined benefit plan and are not available to the entity's own creditors (even in bankruptcy), except as surplus not needed to meet the obligations or as reimbursement of benefits already paid."]},{"h4":"3  Long-life University — transitional liability (₦'000)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Calculation of transitional liability","rows":[["Present value of the obligation",780000],["Fair value of plan assets",-520000],["Past-service cost to be recognised in later periods (24,000 × 3/8)",-9000],["Transitional liability",251000,"@t"],["Liability already recognised",-150000],["Increase in liability",101000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The ₦9,000 is the *unrecognised* past-service cost: of the ₦24,000, five of the eight vesting years have elapsed, so 3/8 remains to be recognised and is excluded from the transitional liability. The ₦101,000 increase may be recognised immediately or spread on a straight-line basis over up to five years (IPSAS 25 transitional provision)."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Net defined benefit liability","tex":"PV(\\text{obligation}) - FV(\\text{plan assets})"},{"lb":"Accumulating vesting leave","tex":"N \\times \\text{days} \\times \\text{daily rate}"},{"lb":"Defined contribution expense","tex":"\\text{Contribution rate} \\times \\text{pensionable emolument}"},{"lb":"Net interest","tex":"\\text{Discount rate} \\times \\text{opening net liability}"}],"focus":"Usually one or two Section A marks on the four categories, on which plan carries the investment risk, or on a specific difference IPSAS 39 introduced against IPSAS 25. In Section B it appears as a discussion of defined contribution against defined benefit, a leave-accrual or contribution-expense computation, or a listing of the statements/disclosures a retirement benefit plan must present. The full projected unit credit computation is beyond Part II; what is examined is the principle and where each component of cost is recognised.","errors":["Classifying a benefit by its name rather than by when it is expected to be settled.","Discounting short-term benefits; they are measured at their undiscounted amount.","Providing for non-accumulating absences before they occur.","Recognising remeasurements of a defined benefit plan in surplus or deficit instead of in net assets/equity — the current IPSAS 39 position (section 5.3), not the deferred corridor mechanics of the superseded IPSAS 25 (section 5.4).","Treating the employee's own pension contribution as an additional expense of the employer; it is already within the salary cost.","Recognising termination benefits over the remaining service period rather than at the point the offer can no longer be withdrawn.","Confusing the standard for an employer's own employee-benefit accounting (IPSAS 39) with the separate standard for a retirement benefit plan's own financial statements (section 5.8), which is a distinct reporting entity."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under IPSAS 39, benefits expected to be settled wholly within twelve months after the end of the reporting period in which the service was rendered are classified as","o":["post-employment benefits","short-term employee benefits","other long-term employee benefits","termination benefits","defined benefit obligations"],"a":1,"w":"The twelve-month test is the dividing line between short-term and other long-term benefits.","src":"Chapter 5.1","sec":"5.1"},{"q":"In a defined contribution plan, actuarial and investment risk falls on the","o":["employer","employee","pension fund custodian","government","actuary"],"a":1,"w":"The employer's obligation ends when the contribution is paid, so any shortfall in investment returns reduces the employee's eventual benefit.","src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"},{"q":"Under IPSAS 39, remeasurements of a net defined benefit liability are recognised in","o":["surplus or deficit","net assets / equity","the statement of cash flows","the notes only","surplus or deficit over the remaining service life"],"a":1,"w":"Remeasurements — actuarial gains and losses and the return on plan assets excluding net interest — go to net assets/equity and are never reclassified to surplus or deficit.","src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"},{"q":"A ministry has 200 staff each with 5 days of accumulating vesting leave unused at the year end. The average daily rate is ₦9,000. The liability to be recognised is","o":["₦1,800,000","₦9,000,000","₦45,000","₦900,000","₦4,500,000"],"a":1,"w":"Vesting entitlement is paid in cash if not taken, so the whole accumulated balance is provided for.","calc":"200 \\times 5 \\times 9{,}000 = ₦9{,}000{,}000","src":"Chapter 5.2","sec":"5.2"},{"q":"Termination benefits are recognised at the earlier of the date the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and the date","o":["the employee accepts the offer","the entity recognises costs for a related restructuring","the payment is made","the employee leaves employment","the reporting period ends"],"a":1,"w":"IPSAS 39 sets these two dates as the recognition trigger, because the obligation arises from the decision to terminate rather than from service rendered.","src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is an \"other long-term employee benefit\"?","o":["Paid annual leave taken within the year","Long-service leave after ten years","A pension payable on retirement","Redundancy pay on restructuring","Monthly salary"],"a":1,"w":"Long-service leave is not expected to be settled within twelve months, is not payable after employment ends, and does not arise from termination — so it falls into the residual \"other long-term\" category.","src":"Chapter 5.1","sec":"5.1"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a component of a retirement benefit plan's financial statements?","o":["Statement of financial position","Statement of changes in net assets available for benefits","Cash flow statement","Statement of public debt","Notes to the financial statements"],"a":3,"w":"A retirement benefit plan presents a statement of financial position, a statement of changes in net assets available for benefits, a cash flow statement and notes. There is no \"statement of public debt\".","src":"Chapter 5.11 (study text Section A Q1)","sec":"5.11"},{"q":"Employee benefits that are not conditional on future employment are described as","o":["vested employee benefits","post-employment benefits","termination benefits","short-term benefits","other long-term benefits"],"a":0,"w":"Vested benefits are those an employee is entitled to regardless of whether they remain in employment.","src":"Chapter 5.11 (study text Section A Q6)","sec":"5.11"},{"q":"The increase in the present value of a defined benefit obligation because the benefits are one period closer to settlement is the","o":["current service cost","past service cost","interest cost","actuarial loss","settlement loss"],"a":2,"w":"Interest cost is the unwinding of the discount — the obligation grows as the payment date approaches.","src":"Chapter 5.11 (study text Section A Q8)","sec":"5.11"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the four categories of employee benefit recognised by IPSAS 39, and distinguish between the accounting treatment of a defined contribution plan and that of a defined benefit plan.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"The objective of IPSAS 39"},{"p":"IPSAS 39 requires an entity to recognise a **liability** when an employee has rendered service in exchange for benefits to be paid in the future, and an **expense** when the entity consumes the economic benefit or service potential arising from that service. The classification of the benefit determines how the liability is measured."},{"h4":"The four categories"},{"ol":["**Short-term employee benefits** — benefits, other than termination benefits, expected to be settled wholly within twelve months after the end of the reporting period in which the employees rendered the related service. They include wages and salaries, social security contributions, paid annual and sick leave, profit-sharing and bonuses payable within twelve months, and non-monetary benefits such as medical care, housing and motor vehicles. They are measured at their **undiscounted** amount, require no actuarial assumptions, and give rise to no remeasurement.","**Post-employment benefits** — benefits, other than termination benefits, payable after the completion of employment. They include pensions, retirement lump sums, post-employment life insurance and post-employment medical care. They are accounted for as either defined contribution or defined benefit plans, discussed below.","**Other long-term employee benefits** — all employee benefits other than short-term, post-employment and termination benefits. They include long-service and sabbatical leave, jubilee awards, long-term disability benefits and deferred bonuses. They are measured in the same way as a defined benefit obligation, but with the important simplification that **all** the cost, including remeasurements, is recognised in surplus or deficit.","**Termination benefits** — benefits payable as a result of either the entity's decision to terminate employment before the normal retirement date, or an employee's decision to accept voluntary redundancy in exchange for those benefits. Because they arise from termination rather than from service, they are recognised at the earlier of the date the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and the date it recognises costs for a related restructuring."]},{"h4":"Defined contribution against defined benefit"},{"p":"The distinction rests on **where the risk lies**. Under a defined contribution plan the entity pays fixed contributions into a separate fund and has **no legal or constructive obligation** to pay further amounts if the fund proves insufficient. Under a defined benefit plan the entity has undertaken to provide agreed benefits, and must make good any shortfall."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined contribution","Defined benefit"],"rows":[["Nature of the obligation","To pay the agreed contribution and nothing more","To provide the agreed benefit, whatever it costs"],["Actuarial and investment risk","Borne by the employee","Borne by the entity"],["Actuarial valuation","Not required","Required, using the projected unit credit method"],["Amount recognised as a liability","The contribution payable for the period, less amounts already paid","Present value of the defined benefit obligation less the fair value of plan assets"],["Expense in surplus or deficit","The contribution payable for the period","Service cost and net interest"],["Remeasurements","None arise","Actuarial gains and losses and the return on plan assets other than net interest, recognised in net assets/equity and never reclassified"],["Disclosure burden","Light — the expense for the period","Extensive — reconciliations, actuarial assumptions and sensitivity analysis"]]}},{"p":"**Defined contribution accounting** is straightforward. The contribution payable in exchange for service in the period is recognised as an expense and as a liability, less any amount already paid. Where the amount paid exceeds the amount due, the excess is recognised as an asset to the extent that it will give rise to a refund or a reduction in future contributions. Contributions not due within twelve months are discounted."},{"p":"**Defined benefit accounting** requires four steps:"},{"ol":["An actuary estimates the benefit attributable to current and prior periods using the projected unit credit method;","That benefit is discounted to a present value;","The fair value of the plan assets is deducted to give the net liability; and","The total defined benefit cost is determined and split between service cost and net interest, both recognised in surplus or deficit, and remeasurements, recognised in net assets/equity."]},{"note":"In the Nigerian public service the Contributory Pension Scheme under the Pension Reform Act 2014 is a **defined contribution** plan, which is precisely why it removed the unfunded liability that destroyed the previous arrangement. The obligation of government now ends when the 10% is remitted."}],"src":"Chapter 5.1, 5.3","sec":"5.1"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Planning and Budgeting in the Public Sector","brief":"The Medium-Term Expenditure Framework, the purposes and uses of a government budget, budgeting with the National Chart of Accounts, the three phases through which a budget is prepared and approved in Nigeria, the competing budgeting techniques (incremental, zero-based, PPBS, performance, rolling), the factors that undermine budget implementation, budgetary control, and IPSAS 24 on presenting budget information in the financial statements.","outcomes":["Discuss the provisions of the Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF)","Define budget and explain the different methods of preparing it","Describe budget preparation, approval and implementation in the public sector","Explain budgetary control measures","State the requirements of IPSAS 24 on presenting budget information in financial statements"],"secs":[{"n":"6.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Budget","d":"A financial and/or quantitative statement prepared and approved prior to a defined period of time, for the purpose of attaining a given objective. A budget is normally prepared for one year, and is therefore a short-term plan."}},{"p":"One of the primary objectives of a budget in the private sector is to measure the **profit** of an organisation. Government has no profit to measure, so in the public sector budgets are instead used:"},{"ul":["As a guide for the present and future;","To plan, control and estimate the amount of receipts and expenditure during a specified period;","To distribute limited resources;","To motivate managers towards the achievement of corporate goals;","As a means of evaluating performance;","To inform managers about the operations and results of their areas of responsibility; and","As a standard of measurement for the purpose of controlling on-going economic endeavours."]},{"note":"Government units that generate revenue through taxes and other sources use the budget to control their operations. A government budget shows authorised appropriations and estimated revenue. Many, however, perceive a budget as a restraining or impeding factor, and so people tend to develop a negative attitude towards budgeting."}]},{"n":"6.2","t":"The Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF)","b":[{"p":"The MTEF is a **medium-term, high-level strategic plan** of government, usually for three years in Nigeria, forming the basis of annual budgeting, and taking into account the legal requirement that spending should not exceed revenue by more than **3% of GDP**. It shifts the psychology of budgeting from “needs” to an “availability of resources”. It is enshrined in **Part II, sections 11–17 of the Fiscal Responsibility Act (FRA) 2007**, which mandates the Federal Government to develop a **Fiscal Strategy Paper (FSP)** within an MTEF for the next three years."},{"p":"Section 11 of the FRA 2007 states that the Federal Government, in consultation with the States, would:"},{"ul":["Prepare and submit to the National Assembly a medium-term expenditure framework for the next three financial years, on which the National Assembly will deliberate — to be done not later than **six months** from the commencement of the Act.","Subsequently, not later than **four months** before the next financial year commences, prepare a fresh MTEF for the next three financial years for the National Assembly’s consideration."]},{"h4":"Objectives of the MTEF"},{"ol":["To improve macroeconomic balance, including fiscal discipline, through good estimates of the available resource envelope, which are then used to make budgets that fit squarely within the envelope.","To improve inter- and intra-sectoral resource allocation, by effectively prioritising all expenditure (on the basis of government’s socio-economic programme) and dedicating resources only to the most important items.","To increase greater budget predictability, as a result of commitment to more credible sectoral budget ceilings.","To increase greater political accountability for expenditure outcomes, through legitimate decision-making.","To make public expenditure more efficient and effective, essentially by allowing line ministries greater flexibility in managing their budgets within hard budget constraints and agreed policies and programmes."]},{"h4":"Content of the MTEF"},{"p":"The MTEF must contain:"},{"ul":["A **macro-economic framework**, setting out the three financial years, the underlying assumptions, and an evaluation and analysis of the macro-economic projection for the preceding three financial years.","A **fiscal strategy document**, setting out: the Federal Government’s medium-term financial objectives; its policies for the medium term relating to taxation, recurrent expenditure, borrowing, lending, investment and other liabilities; the strategies and economic, social and developmental priorities of government for the next three financial years; and an explanation of the financial objectives and fiscal measures.","An **expenditure and revenue framework**, setting out: estimates of aggregate revenue for the Federation for each financial year, based on the pre-determined commodity Reference Price adopted and tax revenue projections; aggregate expenditure for each of the next three financial years; minimum capital expenditure projection for the Federation for each of the next three financial years; and aggregate tax expenditure projection for the Federation for each of the next three financial years.","A **consolidated debt statement**, indicating and describing the fiscal significance of the debt liability and measures to reduce it.","A **statement on contingent liabilities and quasi-fiscal activities**, describing their nature and fiscal significance and measures to offset the crystallisation of such liabilities."]},{"note":"The estimates and expenditure in the revenue and expenditure framework must be based on reliable and consistent data, targeted at achieving the macro-economic projection, and consistent with, and derived from, the underlying assumptions in the fiscal strategy document."},{"h4":"Annual budgets and the MTEF"},{"p":"The MTEF is the basis for the preparation of the estimates of revenue and expenditure presented to the National Assembly. The **annual budget must be accompanied by**:"},{"ul":["A copy of the underlying revenue and expenditure profile for the next two years;","A report setting out actual and budgeted revenue and expenditure, with a detailed analysis of the performance of the budget for the eighteen months up to June of the preceding financial year;","A fiscal target broken down into monthly collection targets;","Measures of cost, cost control and evaluation of the result of programmes financed with budgetary resources;","A **fiscal target document**, derived from the underlying MTEF, setting out the target inflation rate, target fiscal account balances, and any other development targets deemed appropriate for the relevant financial year; and","A **fiscal risk document**, evaluating the fiscal and other related risks to the annual budget, and specifying measures to be taken to offset the occurrence of such risks."]},{"h4":"Preparation of the MTEF"},{"p":"The **Minister** is responsible for preparing the MTEF. In doing this, he may hold public consultations — open to the public, the press, citizens, organisations and groups of citizens — on the macro-economic framework, the fiscal strategy document, the strategic/economic/social/developmental priorities of government, and any other matters he deems necessary. The Minister seeks input from:"},{"ul":["The National Planning Commission;","The Joint Planning Commission;","The National Commission on Development Planning;","The National Economic Commission;","The National Assembly;","The Central Bank of Nigeria;","The National Bureau of Statistics; and","The Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission."]},{"p":"The Minister must, before the end of the **second quarter** of each financial year, present the MTEF to the Federal Executive Council for consideration and endorsement. The MTEF, as endorsed by the Federal Executive Council, takes effect upon approval by the National Assembly. Any adjustment to the MTEF thereafter is limited to the correction of a manifest error, and to changes in fiscal indicators which, in the opinion of the President, are significant."},{"h4":"Format of the MTEF"},{"p":"The FRA 2007 format for the MTEF sets out, for each fiscal item, the approved budget for the current year, the actual figure as at the month of submission to the National Assembly, and forecasts/projections for the following three years. The fiscal items include:"},{"ul":["Oil production volume (million barrels per day), average budget price per barrel (USD) and average exchange rate;","Federally collectible revenue: gross oil revenue, gross non-oil revenue;","Federation account inflows: crude oil sales, gas sales, petroleum profit tax, gas income at 30% CITA, oil royalties, gas royalties;","Net oil and gas revenue less cost, deduction and 13% derivation; net solid mineral revenue after derivation; total non-oil revenue;","Total FGN’s retained revenue; statutory transfers; sinking fund to retire maturing loans;","Recurrent expenditure; special intervention (e.g. conditional cash transfer, home-grown school feeding programme, post-NYSC entrepreneurial development); capital expenditure; total FGN expenditure; fiscal deficit; GDP;","Share of capital as a percentage of non-debt expenditure, and as a percentage of total expenditure; share of recurrent as a percentage of total expenditure;","Financing: sales of government properties, privatisation and FGN’s share of signature bonus; recoveries of misappropriated funds; borrowings; total financing; grand total (revenue plus financing); financing deficit."]},{"h4":"Risks to the MTEF"},{"ul":["**Global development** — fragile economic recovery and the emergence of new political risks.","**Persistence of oil price decline** — low oil prices expected to remain in the medium term.","**Oil production and oil sector management** — oil production bedevilled by crude oil theft and pipeline vandalism.","**Non-oil revenue risks** — due to low remittance by government-owned enterprises into the Treasury, from a lack of transparency, e.g. over operating surplus."]}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"Objectives and uses of a budget","b":[{"p":"In all government units, the executive arm prepares the budget and submits it to the legislative arm for review, modification and approval. The approved budget serves as a basis for the activities of the government unit for the fiscal period under review. There are **four main purposes** a government budget serves:"},{"ol":["A budget is an **economic and financial document**. It highlights government’s policies, which are designed to promote economic growth, full employment and enhance the quality of life of the citizenry.","It is a useful **guide for the allocation** of available resources.","Through the legislature, the executive arm uses the budget as a means of **accountability** for the fund earlier entrusted and the newly approved appropriations.","A budget is a **request** by the executive arm of government to the legislature to collect and disburse funds."]},{"h4":"Uses of a budget"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Use","Explanation"],"rows":[["**Planning**","Budgets are plans to which monetary values are assigned, for what is to be achieved in a determinable future time — for example, a year"],["**Communication**","Budgets assist in communicating horizontally and vertically. When budgets are being prepared, individuals, groups, communities and associations inform government about their areas of interest — “upward communication”. When the budget is approved, government reads it to the public and publishes it in the newspapers — “communicating downwards”"],["**Motivation**","A budget is a target to be achieved. Government motivates staff through promotions and improved conditions of service, for assisting in the full and successful implementation of the budget"],["**Standard for measurement of performance**","Since a budget is a target, it is a measure of performance. What is achieved is recorded and compared with the target set. The process of implementation draws management attention to problem areas"],["**Evaluation of economic and social policy**","Budgets are used to solve the social problems of inflation and unemployment"],["**Cost reduction technique**","Evaluation of operations and procedures during budgeting may result in cost savings"]]}}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"Budgeting with the National Chart of Accounts (NCOA)","b":[{"p":"The classification code forms the basis for budgeting and budgetary control. For a country, state or local government to achieve a reasonable level of success in accountability, transparency, performance evaluation and adherence to the Appropriation Act, the application of a **unified chart of accounts** is paramount."},{"h4":"The six NCOA segments used for budgeting"},{"p":"These are the same six segments introduced for financial reporting in Chapter 3.2, applied here to budget entries:"},{"ul":["**Administrative segment** — identifies the entity responsible for the public funds’ projection, such as the Ministry of Education, Health or Women Affairs, or, at a lower level, schools and hospitals.","**Economic segment** — identifies the type of revenue and expenditure budgeted in a particular period, e.g. salaries, goods and services, transfers and interest due.","**Functional segment** — a detailed classification of the functions or socio-economic objectives that general government units aim to achieve through various outlays, organising government activities according to their broad objectives or purposes.","**Programme segment** — identifies the various sets of activities meant to achieve specific policy objectives of government, e.g. pre-primary education, poverty alleviation and food security.","**Funds segment** — identifies the sources of funding government activities.","**Geographical segment** — identifies the geographical location of an entity (MDA) or a project, so that an analysis of the government budget along various zones, states and local government areas can be done."]},{"warn":"All **six segments** of the chart of accounts must be completed on every budget entry, even where the value for a given segment is inactive. Only numeric values can be budgeted."},{"h4":"Steps for budgeting with the NCOA"},{"ol":["Identify the government institutions (cost and revenue centres) from the hierarchy of administrative fund codes provided in the chart of accounts.","Identify the economic items that would be executed during the fiscal year.","Identify the functions intended to be performed by government institutions (revenue and cost centres).","Identify the programmes intended to be carried out by the government institutions.","Determine the sources of financing the budgeted amount for each budget line.","Identify the planned location for the economic transactions or government institutions."]},{"h4":"Format for annual budgeting with the chart of accounts"},{"p":"A complete set of annual budgeting documents, prepared with the chart of accounts, comprises:"},{"ol":["A revenue budget;","A recurrent budget;","A capital budget;","A summary of total revenue budget;","A summary of recurrent expenditure budget;","A summary of independent revenue budget by MDAs;","A summary of total recurrent expenditure by MDAs;","A summary of total capital receipts budget by MDAs; and","A consolidated budget summary."]}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Procedure for preparation and approval of budgets","b":[{"p":"The budgeting process is a cycle of events which occur sequentially every year and result in the approved budget. In ministries and extra-ministerial departments, budget preparation and approval undergo three levels."},{"h4":"The ministerial or pre-treasury board phase"},{"p":"Before the issuance of the budget preparation guidelines, the Ministry of Finance and Planning (or the Ministry of Finance at State level) receives policy pronouncements from the Presidency (or the State Governor). The guidelines are subsequently issued by the Ministry of Budget and Planning or Finance, in the form of a **call circular**. When the various ministries, extra-ministerial departments and agencies receive the call circular, a committee on “Advance Proposals” is set up. This committee, which acts as the **Pre-Treasury Board**, is headed by the Permanent Secretary, Ministry of Finance and Planning (Federal level) or Ministry of Finance, Budget and Planning (State level), and is charged with appraising the various budget proposals received, in light of the funds available. The requirements, having received provisional approval, are transmitted to the Presidency or Treasury Board (headed by the State Governor)."},{"note":"The Budget Office of the Ministry of Finance issues the call circular to all agencies of government, requesting them to submit their revenue and expenditure estimates for the succeeding year."},{"h4":"Functions of the Budget Office of the Federation"},{"p":"For preparation of the annual estimates and the formulation of the fiscal, monetary and other policies needed to support the economy, the Budget Office of the Federation performs the following functions:"},{"ul":["Developing reasoned economic assumptions and forecasts;","Issuing budget guidelines to the ministries and extra-ministerial departments;","Acting as liaison between the Presidency, ministries, departments and agencies (MDAs) during budget preparation;","Compiling total revenue and expenditure estimates;","Drafting the budget speech;","Supervising and controlling the implementation of the budget;","Monitoring and evaluating the performance of programmes funded through the government budget;","Assessing the impact of the budget on the economy;","Developing formats of returns aimed at ensuring cost effectiveness in the use of government resources; and","Carrying out research on budget utilisation and the attainment of national or state objectives."]},{"h4":"Executive council phase"},{"p":"The draft estimates are presented to the cabinet members, known as the Council of Ministers or the Executive Council, for further consideration and approval. Members of the Executive Council are usually the President and Vice President (Governor and Deputy Governor at State level), the Secretary to the Government, all the Ministers (Commissioners at State level), and the Head of Service. The draft estimates are then sent to the National Assembly or the State Legislature, in the form of an **Appropriation Bill**."},{"h4":"Legislative phase"},{"p":"The President of Nigeria or State Governor presents the budget package and speech to the National Assembly (a joint meeting of the two Houses) or the House of Assembly at State level. This meeting is known as the “Budget Session”. In each House, there is a Standing Committee which considers the budget proposals. Each arm of the National Assembly or the State House of Assembly approves the budget. Where there are discrepancies or divergent opinions on some items, the two Houses appoint a Finance Committee, whose resolution is final. The final stage is consideration of the budget proposals at a joint session of the two Houses of the National Assembly."},{"h4":"Presidential/Governor’s assent"},{"p":"The budget is sent back to the President or State Governor for his assent. It subsequently becomes the **Appropriation Act**. Copies of the approved estimates are printed and distributed to the ministries, extra-ministerial departments and agencies of government."}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"Methods of preparing budgets by governments in Nigeria","b":[{"p":"The budgeting approach commonly used by government to allocate funds for a succeeding year is the **incremental or “line-item” method**. This approach is oriented to expenditure, itemising proposed disbursements under different heads and sub-heads of the various ministries and extra-ministerial departments. The expenditure side of the line-item or incremental budget comprises **personnel emoluments, other charges and capital or developmental items**."},{"h4":"Traditional / line-item / incremental budgeting"},{"p":"This traditional method, also called “incremental budgeting”, involves using last year’s figure as a base and adding a percentage to it to arrive at this year’s budget. The percentage added is based essentially on three factors: the trend of economic events; inflation; and the funds available."},{"p":"Budgeting in government has two main elements. The **procedure of budgeting** consists of the practices, documentation and norms which govern the preparation, approval, implementation and review of the budget. The **budgeting system** has to do with the management process — providing for the purchase, allocation and use of available resources by setting operational criteria in advance, resulting in the achievement of corporate goals."},{"p":"The line-item budgeting system has three features:"},{"ol":["The budget gives prominence to the ministries and extra-ministerial departments for which it is prepared, while little or no attention is given to the end results the funds provide;","The current year's budget is arrived at through routine and incremental reasoning, not scientific analysis; and","The main thrust of the budget is the achievement of control and accountability."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of line-item budgeting","Disadvantages of line-item budgeting"],"rows":[["It is simple to understand and operate","It allows past errors to be carried forward, so it is not efficient in operation"],["It suits the country’s level of development, where there is a paucity of data","Detailed scrutiny is not contained in the budget, so budget preparation is not well researched"],["It is cheaper to produce","It fails to clarify the cost of alternative ways of achieving programme objectives"],["It encourages the continuity of projects","It results in continual growth in budget totals leading to inflation, as opposed to serious economic need"],["The method ensures that the budget is translated into monetary language and relates to the relevant activities/operations","It fails to fund new, high-priority programmes on a sufficiently reasonable scale"],["Allocations into heads and sub-heads facilitate the monitoring of performance","It does not clearly spell out the relationship between capital and recurrent expenditure — the approach is based on organisational set-ups rather than programmes"]]}},{"h4":"Zero-based budgeting (ZBB)"},{"p":"ZBB is a management effort which provides for systematic consideration of all activities and programmes. It is a programme budgeting reform introduced by **Peter Pyhrr of Texas**, and popularised by a past President of the United States, **Jimmy Carter, in 1976**. The technique requires every item of expenditure to be justified as if the particular activity or programme were taking off for the first time — the preparation of operating budgets from a “zero base” of expenditure cost. Resources are not necessarily allocated in accordance with previous patterns; each item of expenditure proposed must be re-justified annually. ZBB seeks to avoid perpetuating obsolete expenditure items."},{"p":"In government, the three key users of ZBB are:"},{"ul":["The Legislature;","The Executive; and","The various ministries, extra-ministerial departments and parastatals."]},{"p":"ZBB involves the use of a **decision-package approach**, based on the identification of activities, classified into five basic events:"},{"ol":["Identification of **decision units** and formulating operational plans. The entire ministry or parastatal is divided into smaller components called decision units.","Analysing the whole budget into **decision packages**, based on the decision units, to which costs are assigned, together with alternative ways of executing the same operation. This also involves assessing the effect of not performing the activity at all — different levels of performance between minimum and maximum points are evaluated to obtain optimality.","**Ranking** the decision packages covering both new and existing activities, in a competitive manner.","Determination of the **cut-off point**, to choose the packages which can be included and those to be rejected.","**Prioritisation** of the packages, to highlight those which fit within available resources."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of ZBB","Disadvantages of ZBB"],"rows":[["It acts as a tool for change, from which benefits are likely to accrue","Lack of, and sometimes unreliable, data may inhibit or undermine its usefulness in a less developed economic environment such as ours"],["It allows for optimum allocation of resources — resources are allocated by need and benefit accruing, rather than political or emotional considerations","It may cause a major shift in resource allocation"],["It creates a questioning attitude instead of assuming that current practice maximises expected money value, thereby reducing wasteful spending","Bureaucrats often do not trust the approach and hence frustrate its effectiveness"],["It provides a better yardstick for the measurement of performance","In determining decision packages, there is sometimes the problem of fixing the minimum level of expenditure"],["It allows for the participation of the various organs of the decision unit","It involves analysing and ranking a lot of data, which a number of civil servants find difficult to manage, complicated by a lack of qualified and competent personnel"],["It focuses attention on the future rather than the past; old and new projects are therefore appraised on the same basis","Accounting structure needs to conform to the zero-based philosophy, which may necessitate a general review, overhauling, adding or scrapping of activities and functions"],["Under ZBB, important projects can continue to receive funds, owing to their viability","It is not so good for recurrent expenditure — it has not been successful in the public sector"],["It is good for profit-oriented projects",""]]}},{"h4":"Planning, Programming and Budgeting System (PPBS)"},{"p":"PPBS is a budgeting approach based on systems theory, output and objective orientation, with substantial emphasis on resource allocation based on the principle of economic analysis. The technique is not based on the traditional organisational structure but on **programmes**, which involve grouping activities with common objectives. Because the resources available to public sector organisations are limited compared with the demands on them, choices must be made to ensure that the meagre resources are distributed fairly to maximise benefits."},{"p":"The main steps in PPBS are:"},{"ol":["Identification and enumeration of the goals and objectives of the organisation.","Defining the total system in detail, including objectives, environment, available resources, the programmes and their objectives.","**Planning and analysis** — a continuous process of developing, comparing and analysing alternative programmes, to evolve the most appropriate package for the organisation.","Development of appropriate measures of performance for the programmes of the organisation.","**Programming and budgeting** — the agreed package of “programmes”, complete with resource requirements and expected results, expressed as “programmed budgets”.","**Reporting and controlling** — PPBS requires a sophisticated information service able to monitor progress towards meeting organisational objectives. Performance evaluation therefore emphasises the attainment or non-attainment of desired objectives, rather than the amount spent (the focus of traditional budgeting).","Development, in each year, of a multi-year programme and financial plan."]},{"p":"**Advantages of PPBS:**"},{"ol":["It provides information on the objectives of the organisation;","It lays emphasis on long-term effects;","It achieves effective use of budgeted resources and anticipated performance;","It ensures rational decision-making and forces those seeking budgetary allocations to consider alternatives; and","It leads to rapid economic development."]},{"h4":"Performance budgeting"},{"p":"Performance budgeting is a technique used for presenting public expenditure in the form of functions or projects to be undertaken, highlighting the cost involved. The anticipated costs are compared with the expected income. The focus of the technique is on **results or output achieved**, rather than how much has been expended. Its essential features are:"},{"ol":["Classification of budgets in terms of functions and activities;","Measurement of the work done or output provided by each activity;","Expression of the budget in a way that allows direct comparison between a project's cost and its anticipated income or benefit; and","Monitoring of actual cost and performance against the budgeted results or expectations."]},{"h4":"Rolling plan / continuous budgets"},{"p":"A continuous budget or rolling plan is the continuous updating of a medium-term plan spanning a specified period of time — for example, “1998 to 2000”, within which special and core capital projects, such as the completion of the Ajaokuta Steel Rolling Mill, are to be accomplished. The time horizon is a target date within which the capital project is expected to be completed; if constraints prevent this, a fresh plan period emerges to accommodate the development. **Nigeria started adopting the Rolling Plan from 1990**, with the “1990 to 1992” Rolling Plan."},{"key":"What \"rolling\" actually means. A **fixed** medium-term plan covers a set window — say 1990 to 1992 — and then simply **stops**: planning has to start completely afresh from zero once 1992 ends, using by-then years-old assumptions right up to the point the new plan is drawn up. A **rolling** plan never stops and is never stale: at the end of **each** year, the year just completed is **dropped off the front**, and a **new year is added at the far end**, so the plan window always slides forward by exactly one year and always still covers the same number of years into the future — continuously re-based on the latest available data, rather than only every three years."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["After year...","The plan window becomes"],"rows":[["1990 (the starting plan)","1990 – 1992"],["1990 ends","1991 – 1993 (1990 dropped, 1993 added)"],["1991 ends","1992 – 1994 (1991 dropped, 1994 added)"]]}},{"p":"This is also why the technique is called a **continuous budget**: because the plan is re-based every single year rather than left to run its full length untouched, it can absorb the reality that a capital project sometimes cannot be finished within its original target window — the Ajaokuta Steel Rolling Mill is the study text's own example of exactly this. Rather than the whole exercise being treated as a failure, a **fresh plan period simply opens up** to accommodate the project's remaining work, and the rolling window carries it forward until completion."},{"note":"Two things distinguish a rolling plan from the ordinary Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF) of §6.2, even though both look forward several years: the MTEF is re-issued wholesale at fixed points (a fresh one before each new financial year), while a rolling plan is a genuinely **continuous** sliding window that shifts by one year at a time, every year, without ever formally \"ending\" and restarting."}]},{"n":"6.7","t":"Factors militating against the budgeting system in Nigeria","b":[{"p":"The key factors that militate against efficient and effective budget implementation in the public sector are:"},{"ol":["**Human element.** Top management members see budgeting as restraining and challenging. They tend to develop apathy towards its adoption and implementation, and the lack of probity and accountability among some operatives affects successful budgeting.","**Uncertainties underlying data inputs.** There are a lot of uncertainties in the data used for budget preparation — projections of revenue accruing from oil may not be forthcoming, given the vagaries of the world market, and a lack of an efficient database also hamstrings reliable forecasts.","**The type of project for which a budget is prepared.** How successful a budget will be depends on the type of project to which it relates. Some projects are popular while others are not; unpopular projects may face stiff implementation problems.","**The problem of inflation.** Inflation tends to reduce purchasing power. When the value of money is falling, budget implementation may run into problems, since available revenue may not cover expenditure.","**Political, social and cultural elements.** Each segment of the nation has its own cultural beliefs and taboos, which may take time to change — introducing innovation may be met with stiff opposition; where there is political instability, budget implementation is also at risk.","**Changing government policies.** Effective budget implementation depends heavily on the policy of government. Government policies must be harmonised and consistent; frequent changes affect budget implementation.","**The problem of debt management and optimal use of limited resources.** There is a challenge in striking a balance between how much of the nation’s resources should be used for servicing debt and how much should be used for economic development.","**Low agricultural output.** Agricultural output is fast dwindling because the method of farming is outdated and the younger population is not attracted to it. Resources that should be used for economic development are therefore diverted to the importation of food items.","**Fiscal indiscipline.** Most government officers are always maximising their budget; under the incremental budgeting system they tend to spend the last naira available in a year’s budget to justify demand for increased allocation the following year, with little or nothing to show for what has happened in the current year."]}]},{"n":"6.8","t":"Budgetary control","b":[{"def":{"t":"Budgetary control","d":"The whole system of controls — financial or otherwise — used to ensure that income and expenditure are in line with the budget, and that wastage is reduced to the barest minimum. Budgetary control is a positive and integral part of a public sector organisation’s planning and appraisal activities, so as to achieve the set objectives. In other words, budgetary control is a process of comparing actual with budgeted activity, resulting in a variance, which could be favourable or adverse."}},{"h4":"Objectives of budgetary control"},{"ul":["To combine the ideas of all levels of management in the preparation of budgets.","To co-ordinate all the activities of a business or organisation.","To centralise control.","To decentralise responsibility to each manager.","To act as a guide for management decisions when unforeseeable conditions affect the budgets.","To plan and control income and expenditure so that maximum benefit is achieved.","To channel capital expenditure in the most profitable manner.","To ensure that sufficient working capital or cash is available for the efficient operation of the business or organisation.","To provide a yardstick against which actual results can be compared.","To show management where action is needed to remedy a situation."]}]},{"n":"6.9","t":"IPSAS 24 — presentation of budget information in financial statements","b":[{"p":"This standard requires a comparison between the budgeted amount and the actual amount arising from execution of the budget to be included in the financial statements of entities that are required to make government accountable to the public. It also requires disclosure and explanation of the reasons for material differences between the budgeted and actual amounts. Compliance ensures that public sector entities discharge their accountability obligations and enhances the transparency of their financial statements, by demonstrating compliance with the approved budget(s) for which they are held publicly accountable and, where the budget(s) and the financial statements are prepared on the same basis, their financial performance in achieving the budgeted results."},{"h4":"Definitions"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Accounting basis**","The accrual or cash basis of accounting, as defined in the accrual-basis IPSASs and the Cash Basis IPSAS"],["**Annual budget**","An approved budget for one year. It does not include published forward estimates or projections for periods beyond the budget period"],["**Appropriation**","An authorisation granted by a legislative body to allocate funds for purposes specified by the legislature or similar authority"],["**Approved budget**","The expenditure authority derived from laws, appropriation bills, government ordinances and other decisions related to the anticipated revenue or receipts for the budgetary period"],["**Budgetary basis**","The accrual, cash or other basis of accounting adopted in the budget that has been approved by the legislative body"],["**Comparable basis**","The actual amounts presented on the same accounting basis, same classification basis, for the same entities and for the same period as the approved budget"],["**Final budget**","The original budget adjusted for all reserves, carry-over amounts, transfers, allocations, supplemental appropriations, and other authorised legislative or similar changes applicable to the budget period"],["**Original budget**","The initial approved budget for the budget period"]]}},{"h4":"Presentation of a comparison of budget and actual amounts"},{"p":"Subject to the presentation and disclosure requirement below, an entity presents a comparison of the budgeted amount on which it is held publicly accountable and the actual amount, either as a **separate additional financial statement** or as **additional budget columns** in the financial statements currently presented in accordance with IPSASs. This comparison of budgeted and actual amounts is presented separately for each level of legislative oversight:"},{"ol":["The original and final budgeted amounts;","The actual amount, on a comparable basis; and","By way of note disclosure, an explanation of material differences between the budget on which the entity is held publicly accountable and the actual amount, unless such an explanation is included in other public documents issued alongside the financial statements, with a cross-reference to those documents made in the notes."]},{"p":"An entity presents a comparison of budgeted and actual amounts as an **additional budget column in the primary financial statements** only where the financial statements and the budget are prepared on a **comparable basis**."},{"h4":"Changes from original to final budget"},{"p":"An entity presents an explanation of whether changes between the original and final budget are a consequence of reallocations within the budget, or of other factors, either: by way of note disclosure in the financial statements; or in a report issued before, at the same time as, or in conjunction with the financial statements, with a cross-reference to that report in the notes."},{"h4":"Note disclosure of budgetary basis, period and scope"},{"ul":["An entity explains, in the notes to the financial statements, the budgetary basis and classification basis adopted in the budget.","An entity discloses, by way of notes, the period of the approved budget.","An entity identifies, by way of notes, the entities included in the approved budget."]},{"h4":"Reconciliation of the actual amounts"},{"p":"Where the financial statements and the budget are **not** prepared on a comparable basis, the actual amount presented on a comparable basis to the budget is reconciled with the actual amount presented in the financial statements, identifying separately any basis, timing and entity differences: total revenue, total expense and net cash flow from operating, investing and financing activities, where the actual basis is adopted for the budget; or net cash flow from operating, investing and financing activities, where a basis other than the accrual basis is adopted for the budget. This reconciliation is disclosed either on the face of the statement of comparison of budget and actual amounts, or in the notes to the financial statements."},{"h4":"Illustrative format"},{"p":"The standard illustrates presentation with an extract statement of financial performance showing budget information for the year, with columns for the previous year, a description, the current year, the final budget for the current year, the supplementary budget, the original budget, and the difference between the original and actual figures. Receipts (taxation, aid agreements, international agencies, other grants and aid, proceeds of borrowing, proceeds of disposal of plant and equipment, trading activities and other receipts) are totalled, followed by payments analysed by COFOG-style function (health, education, public order/safety, social protection, defence, housing and community amenities, recreational/cultural/religion, economic affairs, other), with a net receipts/(payments) total at the foot."}]},{"n":"6.10","t":"Worked example — computing a state government’s revenue budget","b":[{"warn":"The study text names the state **“NEMBE State”** in the question, but its own answer key is headed **“Mende State Government”**. This is preserved as printed — an inconsistency in the source rather than an error in this transcription."},{"eg":{"t":"Nembe State revenue budget for the 2021 fiscal year","q":[{"p":"18,000,000 citizens inhabit Nembe State. For the 2021 fiscal year: (i) 5,000,000 taxable adults reside in the State, paying tax of ₦25,000 per taxable adult per annum on average; (ii) 45,000 industries pay a land use charge of ₦15,000 per industry; (iii) 4,500,000 residential buildings pay a land use charge of ₦7,500 per house per annum; (iv) modern markets generate ₦20,000 per annum per stall (5,000 stalls) and ₦8,000 per annum per seller/hawker (450,000 sellers and hawkers), of which local governments are entitled to 50%; (v) 10,000 mass transit buses each generate ₦8,000 per business day, assuming 28 business days a month; (vi) additional revenue: statutory allocation from the Federation Account ₦45,000,000 and miscellaneous revenue ₦5,000,000. Compute the revenue budget for the fiscal year 2021."}],"a":[{"p":"Workings:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Item","₦'million"],"rows":[["Income tax: $5{,}000{,}000 \\times 25{,}000$","125,000"],["Land use charge (industrial): $45{,}000 \\times 15{,}000$","675"],["Land use charge (residential): $4{,}500{,}000 \\times 7{,}500$","33,750"],["Stalls revenue: $5{,}000 \\times 20{,}000$","100"],["Sellers and hawkers: $450{,}000 \\times 8{,}000$","3,600"],["Mass transit buses: $10{,}000 \\times 28 \\times 12 \\times 8{,}000$","26,880"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Details","₦'million"],"rows":[["Statutory allocation from Federation Account","45,000"],["Income tax","125,000"],["Land use charges (industrial building)","675"],["Land use charges (residential building)","33,750"],["Total revenue from stalls, sellers and hawkers (100 + 3,600)","3,700"],["Less: local government entitlement (50% of 3,700)","(1,850)"],["Net revenue from stalls, sellers and hawkers","1,850"],["Revenue from mass transit buses","26,880"],["Miscellaneous revenue","5,000"],["**Total revenue**","**238,155**"]]}},{"note":"The local government share is deducted only from the market-related revenue (stalls, sellers and hawkers), because the question specifically states that local governments in the areas where the markets are sited are entitled to 50% of that revenue — it is not a general local government share of the whole state budget."}]}}]},{"n":"6.11","t":"Warrants, virement and the vote book","b":[{"warn":"Chapter 7 of the study text (“Financial Responsibilities of Public Sector Officers and Authorisation of Government Expenditure”) treats warrants in full, with the classes of warrant and expenditure authorisation procedure. This section gives only the background needed for the questions below; the fuller treatment belongs in Chapter 7."},{"def":{"t":"Warrant","d":"The authority which confers power on the officer controlling expenditure — or a vote — to incur expenditure already appropriated by the Appropriation Act. All warrants are issued and signed by the Minister of Finance. The Appropriation Act authorises the spending; the warrant releases the cash. Warrants divide broadly into **recurrent expenditure warrants** and **capital expenditure warrants**."}},{"p":"Among recurrent expenditure warrants, the **Provisional General Warrant (PGW)** is issued **before** the Appropriation Act is passed, under section 82 of the Constitution, to keep essential services running — based on the previous year’s provision and limited to six months. The **Annual General Warrant (AGW)** is issued **after** the Appropriation Act, authorising the release of funds for recurrent expenditure generally. Capital expenditure, financed through the **Development Fund** rather than the Consolidated Revenue Fund, is released by a **Development Fund General Warrant**."},{"def":{"t":"Virement","d":"The transfer of savings from one subhead of a vote to another subhead within the same head, authorised by a virement warrant. It does not increase the total sum appropriated — moving money between heads, or increasing the total, requires a further Act (a supplementary appropriation), not a warrant."}},{"p":"The **vote book** (Departmental Vote Expenditure Allocation, or DVEA, book) is the record in which an accounting officer controls his appropriation. It is kept on the **commitment basis**: an amount is charged to the vote when an order is placed, not when payment is made, so the officer always knows the **uncommitted balance** available. An accounting officer who places an order exceeding it commits an over-expenditure, for which he is personally liable to surcharge."}]},{"n":"6.12","t":"Further reading","b":[{"ul":["[Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007](https://www.budgetoffice.gov.ng/) — Budget Office of the Federation, for the Medium-Term Expenditure Framework provisions.","[IPSAS 24, Presentation of Budget Information in Financial Statements](https://www.ifac.org/system/files/publications/files/IPSAS-24-Presentation-of-Budget.pdf) — IPSASB.","[Budget Office of the Federation](https://www.budgetoffice.gov.ng/) — for current MTEF and Fiscal Strategy Paper documents."]}]},{"n":"6.13","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer. Section B question 2 (the NEMBE / Mende State revenue budget) is worked in full at §6.10 above."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is responsible for preparing the Medium-Term Expenditure Framework: (A) Accountant-General of the Federation  (B) Chairman, National Economic Commission  (C) Governor of the Central Bank  (D) Minister of Finance, Budget and National Planning  (E) National Bureau of Statistics.","One of the following methods uses last year's figure as a base and adds a percentage to arrive at this year's budget: (A) Line-item budgeting  (B) Traditional / incremental budgeting  (C) Planning, Programming Budgeting System  (D) Programme Performance Budgeting System  (E) Zero-based budgeting.","One of the following issues the Budget Call Circular at the beginning of the third quarter at the federal level: (A) President  (B) National Assembly  (C) Budget Offices  (D) Ministry of Finance  (E) Accountant-General of the Federation.","The following, **except one**, are included in the format for annual budgeting with the Chart of Accounts: (A) summary of contracts obtained by the reporting entity  (B) recurrent budget  (C) revenue budget  (D) consolidated budget summary  (E) summary of total capital receipts budget by MDAs.","Which of the following is **not** a function of the Budget Office of the Federation? (A) developing reasoned economic assumptions and forecasts  (B) issuing budget guidelines to ministries  (C) compiling total revenue and expenditure estimates  (D) determining the amount to be included in the estimates ratified by the National Assembly  (E) drafting the budget speech.","A medium-term, high-level strategic plan of the Nigerian federal government, usually for three years, is the …","Developing formats of returns aimed at ensuring cost-effectiveness in the use of government resources is a function of the …","A budget in which government intends to spend more than the revenue it will generate is a … budget.","The use of last year's figure as a base and adding a percentage to arrive at the current-year budget is … budgeting.","An unjustified or unnecessary increase in an estimate made by a ministry to escape an anticipated arbitrary cut by the Ministry of Finance is called …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D — the Minister of Finance, Budget and National Planning.**","**B — Traditional / incremental budgeting.**","**D — the Ministry of Finance** (through the Budget Office). *Option C, \"Budget Offices\", is also defensible — the Budget Office of the Federation issues the Call Circular — but the study text's key gives the Ministry of Finance.*","**A — \"summary of contracts obtained by the reporting entity.\"** Not part of the budget format.","**D — \"determining the amount to be included in the estimates ratified by the National Assembly.\"** That is the legislature's role, not the Budget Office's.","**The Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF).**","**Budget Office of the Federation.**","**Deficit** budget.","**Incremental** budgeting.","**Budget padding.**"]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Identify any **four objectives** of cash planning and management.","Compute the revenue budget of NEMBE State Government for the 2021 fiscal year (worked in full at §6.10).","For zero-based budgeting: (a) list the **five basic events** into which ZBB is classified; (b) enumerate **three challenges** of the ZBB system.","Enumerate the **main steps** in the Planning, Programming Budgeting System (PPBS).","List and explain the **factors that militate against** the budgeting system in the Nigerian public sector.","State and explain the **structure for budgeting with the NCOA**, and the steps to ensure completeness when using it."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Objectives of cash planning and management (any four)"},{"ol":["Facilitate budget execution by ensuring funds are available at the right time.","Enable government to anticipate funding gaps so that low-cost borrowing arrangements can be put in place.","Minimise idle cash by investing surplus funds for good returns.","Ensure contracts are awarded only when funds are available (Public Procurement Act 2007).","Ensure effective monitoring and reconciliation of government statutory accounts.","Promote transparent, fraud-resistant government operations."]},{"h4":"3  Zero-based budgeting"},{"p":"**(a) Five basic events:**"},{"ol":["Identify *decision units* and formulate operational plans;","Analyse the budget into *decision packages* (with costs, alternatives and the effect of not doing the activity);","Rank the decision packages competitively (new and existing);","Determine the *cut-off point* to accept or reject packages;","Prioritise the packages to fit available resources."]},{"p":"**(b) Challenges (any three):**"},{"ol":["Unreliable or missing data undermine it in a less developed economy;","It may cause a major shift in resource allocation;","Bureaucrats distrust and frustrate it;","Difficulty fixing the minimum level of expenditure in a decision package;","The volume of data to analyse and rank exceeds available skilled staff;","The accounting structure must be reworked to fit the ZBB philosophy;","It suits capital rather than recurrent expenditure and has not succeeded in the public sector."]},{"h4":"4  Main steps in PPBS"},{"ol":["Identify and enumerate the organisation's goals and objectives.","Define the total system in detail — objectives, environment, resources, programmes.","Planning and analysis — continuously develop, compare and analyse alternative programmes to evolve the best package.","Develop appropriate measures of performance for the programmes.","Programming and budgeting — express the agreed package, with resources and expected results, as programmed budgets.","Reporting and controlling — monitor progress toward objectives; performance evaluation emphasises attainment of objectives, not amounts spent.","Develop, each year, a multi-year programme and financial plan."]},{"h4":"5  Factors militating against the budgeting system in Nigeria"},{"ul":["**Human element** — management sees budgeting as restraining; lack of probity among some operatives.","**Uncertainties in data inputs** — volatile oil-revenue projections; weak databases.","**Type of project** — unpopular projects face stiff implementation problems.","**Inflation** — falling money value means revenue cannot cover planned expenditure.","**Political, social and cultural elements** — cultural resistance to innovation; political instability.","**Changing government policies** — frequent policy changes disrupt implementation.","**Debt management** — the trade-off between debt servicing and development spending.","**Low agricultural output** — resources diverted to food imports.","**Fiscal indiscipline** — spending the last naira to justify a bigger allocation next year."]},{"h4":"6  Budgeting with the NCOA"},{"p":"**Structure — the six segments:**"},{"ol":["*Administrative* (which entity is responsible);","*Economic* (the type of revenue/expenditure);","*Functional* (the socio-economic purpose — COFOG);","*Programme* (the set of activities meeting a policy objective);","*Funds* (the source of financing);","*Geographic* (the location)."]},{"p":"**Steps** — all six segments must be completed on every budget entry, even if a segment is inactive; only numeric values are budgeted:"},{"ol":["Identify the government institutions (cost and revenue centres) from the administrative codes;","Identify the economic items to be executed in the year;","Identify the functions to be performed;","Identify the programmes to be carried out;","Determine the sources of financing for each budget line;","Identify the planned location of the transactions."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Budget balance","tex":"\\text{Estimated revenue} - \\text{Proposed expenditure}"},{"lb":"Deficit limit (FRA 2007 / MTEF)","tex":"\\text{Deficit} \\le 3\\% \\times GDP"},{"lb":"Uncommitted balance","tex":"\\text{Appropriation} - (\\text{Commitments} + \\text{Actual expenditure})"},{"lb":"Budget variance","tex":"\\text{Actual} - \\text{Budget}"}],"focus":"The single most heavily examined chapter in the paper. A Section B question appears in almost every diet: the MTEF and its objectives, a comparison of incremental and zero-based budgeting, the phases of budget preparation and approval, the factors militating against budgeting in Nigeria, or the requirements of IPSAS 24. Section A regularly tests the meaning of terms defined in IPSAS 24, the segments of the NCOA, and the steps of ZBB. Learn zero-based budgeting well enough to give the five steps and several advantages and disadvantages against incremental budgeting — it is asked more often than any other single topic.","errors":["Describing virement as increasing the total appropriation; it only moves money within it.","Confusing the Provisional General Warrant (before the Act, section 82) with the Annual General Warrant (after it).","Listing zero-based budgeting steps without the ranking stage, which is where the technique actually allocates resources.","Saying the vote book is kept on the cash basis; it is kept on the commitment basis.","Omitting the MTEF stage, which begins the annual budget process and constrains it to within 3% of GDP.","Treating capital expenditure as payable from the Consolidated Revenue Fund; it comes from the Development Fund.","Presenting budget and actual figures as an extra column in the primary statements when the budget and the financial statements are not prepared on a comparable basis — IPSAS 24 requires a comparable basis before that presentation is used."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The warrant issued before the Appropriation Act has been passed, to enable essential services to continue, is the","o":["Annual General Warrant","Provisional General Warrant","Virement Warrant","Reserve Expenditure Warrant","Development Fund General Warrant"],"a":1,"w":"The PGW is issued under section 82 of the Constitution and is limited to six months or until the Appropriation Act is passed, whichever is earlier.","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"},{"q":"Virement is the transfer of funds","o":["from one head to another","from one subhead to another within the same head","from the Development Fund to the Consolidated Revenue Fund","from one financial year to the next","from the Contingencies Fund to a ministry"],"a":1,"w":"Virement operates within a head and does not change the total appropriated. Moving money between heads requires a supplementary appropriation.","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"},{"q":"In zero-based budgeting, the lowest level at which an activity can be separately evaluated is called a","o":["decision package","decision unit","cost centre","programme","vote"],"a":1,"w":"Decision units are identified first; decision packages are then prepared for each unit and ranked.","src":"Chapter 6.6","sec":"6.6"},{"q":"The vote book (DVEA) is maintained on the","o":["cash basis","commitment basis","accrual basis","modified cash basis","modified accrual basis"],"a":1,"w":"Charging the vote when the order is placed, rather than when payment is made, is what prevents over-commitment of the appropriation.","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"},{"q":"Capital expenditure of the Federal Government is financed from the","o":["Consolidated Revenue Fund","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Federation Account","Excess Crude Account"],"a":1,"w":"The Development Fund finances capital expenditure; the Consolidated Revenue Fund meets recurrent expenditure.","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"},{"q":"A principal disadvantage of incremental budgeting is that it","o":["is expensive and time-consuming to operate","perpetuates existing inefficiencies because the base is never examined","requires extensive staff training","cannot be used where inflation is high","produces too much paperwork"],"a":1,"w":"Only the increment is scrutinised, so waste embedded in the base carries forward indefinitely. The other options describe zero-based budgeting.","src":"Chapter 6.6","sec":"6.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a purpose of a government budget?","o":["Legal authorisation of expenditure","Determination of the profit of government","Allocation of scarce resources","Control of public expenditure","Management of the economy"],"a":1,"w":"Government does not compute a profit; its objective is service delivery, and the budget serves as an economic and financial document, a guide to resource allocation, a means of accountability and a request to collect and disburse funds.","src":"Chapter 6.1, 6.3","sec":"6.1"},{"q":"Preparation of the Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF) is the responsibility of the","o":["Accountant-General of the Federation","Central Bank of Nigeria","Minister of Finance, Budget and National Planning","National Bureau of Statistics","National Assembly"],"a":2,"w":"Under the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007, the Minister responsible for finance prepares the MTEF for the Executive Council and then the National Assembly.","src":"Chapter 6.13 (study text Section A Q1)","sec":"6.13"},{"q":"An unjustified or unnecessary amount added by a ministry to its estimates to escape an anticipated arbitrary cut by the Ministry of Finance is called","o":["virement","budget padding","a supplementary estimate","zero-basing","incrementalism"],"a":1,"w":"Budget padding — inflating a bid in anticipation of it being cut.","src":"Chapter 6.13 (study text Section A Q10)","sec":"6.13"},{"q":"A budget in which government plans to spend more than the revenue it will generate is a","o":["balanced budget","surplus budget","deficit budget","zero-based budget","supplementary budget"],"a":2,"w":"A deficit budget plans expenditure above expected revenue, to be financed by borrowing.","src":"Chapter 6.13 (study text Section A Q8)","sec":"6.13"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Explain zero-based budgeting and outline the steps involved in its operation. (b) State four advantages and four disadvantages of zero-based budgeting compared with incremental budgeting. (c) Describe the stages of the budget cycle in Nigeria from the preparation of the Medium Term Expenditure Framework to the audit of the accounts.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Zero-based budgeting"},{"p":"Zero-based budgeting is a method of budget preparation in which every activity begins from a base of zero and must be justified in full each year as though it were being proposed for the first time. Nothing is carried forward simply because it existed last year: a manager must show that the activity should be undertaken at all, that it should be undertaken by his unit, and that the amount requested is the right amount. It stands in direct contrast to incremental budgeting, in which the previous year's figure is accepted as the base and only the increase is examined."},{"p":"**Steps in operation:**"},{"ol":["**Identify the decision units.** These are the lowest levels of the organisation at which an activity can be separately identified, costed and evaluated — typically a section, a programme or a service.","**Prepare decision packages.** For each decision unit a package sets out the purpose of the activity, its cost, the benefits expected, alternative ways of achieving the same objective, and the consequences of not performing the activity at all.","**Prepare incremental packages.** For each activity, packages are prepared at different levels of service — commonly a minimum level below which the activity is not worth performing, the current level, and one or more enhanced levels — each showing the additional cost and the additional output.","**Rank the packages.** All packages across the organisation are placed in a single order of priority, from the most essential downwards. Ranking begins at the decision unit and is consolidated upwards through each level of management.","**Determine the cut-off point** and **allocate resources.** The available funds are applied down the ranked list until exhausted. Packages falling below the cut-off receive no funding, and the activities they represent are discontinued or curtailed."]},{"h4":"(b) Advantages and disadvantages"},{"p":"**Advantages over incremental budgeting**"},{"ol":["**It eliminates obsolete and wasteful activities.** Because nothing is assumed, expenditure that has continued out of habit rather than need is exposed. Incremental budgeting can carry waste forward for decades.","**It allocates resources according to current priorities** rather than historical accident, which matters most where resources are tight and policy has changed.","**It forces managers to define objectives and to consider alternatives.** The requirement to state what would happen if the activity ceased is a discipline that incremental budgeting never imposes.","**It identifies the minimum viable level of a service**, which is valuable information when cuts must be made, and improves communication because managers at every level participate in ranking."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages**"},{"ol":["**It is expensive and time-consuming.** Preparing and ranking packages for every activity annually consumes far more management time than adjusting last year's figures, and generates a large volume of paperwork.","**Ranking is subjective.** Comparing a package for school inspection against one for road maintenance requires judgements that no technique can make objective, and the outcome may reflect the persuasiveness of managers rather than the merit of the activities.","**Data is often unreliable in a developing economy.** Where output cannot be measured or the underlying data is weak, the cost-benefit comparison at the heart of a decision package is undermined.","**It is threatening to managers**, whose activities may be discontinued, which invites defensive behaviour: inflated packages, overstated consequences of non-performance, and resistance to the exercise itself, and it has not been successful for recurrent expenditure in the public sector."]},{"h4":"(c) The budget cycle in Nigeria"},{"ol":["**Medium Term Expenditure Framework.** The Minister, in consultation with the National Planning Commission, the Central Bank of Nigeria, the National Bureau of Statistics and other bodies, prepares a three-year MTEF and Fiscal Strategy Paper containing the macro-economic framework, fiscal strategy and the expenditure and revenue framework, presented to the Federal Executive Council before the end of the second quarter and taking effect on National Assembly approval.","**Budget Call Circular.** The Budget Office issues the call circular to all MDAs, conveying each one's expenditure envelope together with the format, the underlying assumptions and the timetable.","**Preparation of estimates by MDAs**, and appraisal by the Pre-Treasury Board headed by the Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Finance, before transmission to the Presidency or Treasury Board.","**Executive Council phase.** The draft estimates are considered and approved by the Council of Ministers/Executive Council, then sent to the legislature as an Appropriation Bill.","**Legislative phase.** The President or Governor presents the budget at the Budget Session; Standing Committees consider the proposals, and a Finance Committee resolves any disagreement between the Houses; the two Houses give final approval at a joint session.","**Presidential/Governor's assent**, at which point the bill becomes the Appropriation Act — the legal authority to spend — and copies are printed and distributed to MDAs.","**Release of funds.** The Minister of Finance issues the Annual General Warrant for recurrent expenditure and the Development Fund General Warrant for capital expenditure.","**Implementation and control.** Accounting officers incur commitments and expenditure within their votes, recording both in the vote book, so the uncommitted balance is known at all times; virement warrants and supplementary appropriations are obtained where necessary.","**Audit and legislative review.** The Auditor-General for the Federation audits the accounts and submits a report to the National Assembly, whose Public Accounts Committees examine it and take evidence from accounting officers — closing the cycle of accountability that the Appropriation Act opened."]},{"note":"Parts of this cycle — the classes of warrant, the vote book, and the detailed audit and accountability machinery — are developed more fully in Chapter 7 and the later chapters on government accounting and audit; this answer draws the threads together into a single sequence."}],"src":"Chapter 6.2, 6.5, 6.6, 6.11","sec":"6.2"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Financial Responsibilities of Public Sector Officers and Authorisation of Government Expenditure","brief":"The officers who control public money — the Minister of Finance, the Accountant-General, the Auditor-General for the Federation and for a State, the accounting officer, the sub-accounting officer, the officer controlling expenditure and the Budget Office — and the warrants, recurrent and capital, by which government expenditure is authorised, with a comparative look at expenditure authorisation in Ghana.","outcomes":["Identify the various finance officers, their powers, functions and responsibilities","Identify and discuss the various financial authorities responsible for the efficient implementation of the budget","Distinguish the classes of warrant, and explain how recurrent and capital expenditure is authorised"],"secs":[{"n":"7.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Government officials saddled with the responsibility of managing government funds and property can be grouped into two categories:"},{"ol":["Officials who have attained their positions by way of experience, hard work and efficiency, and who cannot be arbitrarily removed. These include the Accountant-General of the Federation, the Auditor-General for the Federation, accounting officers, sub-accounting officers, Federal Pay Officers, revenue collectors and the Local Government Secretary.","Officials who occupy their positions by way of political dispensation, who may not necessarily have any experience in the assigned field. These include the Federal Minister of Finance, a State Commissioner for Finance, a Local Government Chairman and Councillors."]},{"p":"All expenditure of government must be properly authorised and approved. The authority which confers power on the officer controlling expenditure — or a vote — to incur expenditure is called a **“warrant”**. All warrants are issued and signed by the Minister of Finance, and divide broadly into **recurrent expenditure warrants** and **capital expenditure warrants** (section 7.10)."}]},{"n":"7.2","t":"The Minister of Finance","b":[{"p":"The Minister of Finance is an officer on **political appointment** who has responsibility for the control and management of the public funds of the Federation. Under the democratic dispensation, the President, after due consultation and with the approval of the Senate, appoints the Minister of Finance."},{"h4":"Functions of the Minister of Finance"},{"ol":["Responsible for the preparation of the **annual estimates** of revenue and expenditure of the Federal Government.","Required to **formulate all policies** related to fiscal and monetary matters.","Ensures the **mobilisation** of both foreign and indigenous financial resources, through external and internal financial institutions, for development purposes.","Ensures a **favourable balance of payments** position, required to maintain adequate foreign exchange reserves.","To **stabilise the value** of the Nigerian currency, both internally and externally.","To supervise all matters connected with the **allocation of revenue** to the three tiers of Federal, State and Local Government.","Relates with relevant international organisations and financial institutions to accelerate the development of Nigerian financial institutions — such as the **Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS)**, the **United Nations** (the source gives this as “UNND”), the **European Union (EU)** and the **African Union (AU)**."]}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"The Accountant-General of the Federation (AGF)","b":[{"p":"In accordance with Government Financial Regulations, the Accountant-General of the Federation is the **Chief Accounting Officer** of the receipts and payments of the Federation. He is saddled with the responsibility of general supervision of the accounts of all ministries and extra-ministerial departments, and of preparing the annual financial statements of the nation, as may be required by the Minister of Finance. He or his representative has access at any reasonable time to all documents, information and records needed for the preparation of the accounts of every ministry and extra-ministerial department."},{"h4":"Powers of the Accountant-General — Financial Regulation 106 (2009 edition)"},{"ul":["Power of access to books and records of all ministries at any reasonable time.","Power to request information and explanation necessary for his duties.","Power to carry out special/ad-hoc investigations in any ministry, department or agency (MDA)."]},{"h4":"Functions of the Accountant-General — Financial Regulation 107 (2009 edition)"},{"ul":["Serve as the Chief Accounting Officer for the receipts and payments of the government of the Federation.","Supervise the accounts of federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government.","Collate, prepare and publish statutory financial statements of the federal government, and any other statements of accounts required by the Minister of Finance.","Manage federal government investments.","Maintain and operate the accounts of the **Consolidated Revenue Fund, Development Fund, Contingencies Fund** and other public funds, and provide cash backing for the operations of the Federal Government.","Maintain and operate the **Federation Account**.","Establish and supervise Federal Pay Offices in each state capital of the Federation.","Conduct routine and in-depth inspection of the books of accounts of federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government, to ensure compliance with rules, regulations and policy decisions.","Approve and ensure compliance with accounting codes, internal audit guides and stock verification manuals of federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government.","Investigate cases of fraud, loss of funds, assets and store items, and other financial malpractices in ministries/extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government.","Provide financial guidelines through the issuance of Treasury Circulars, to ensure strict compliance with existing control systems for the collection, custody and disbursement of public funds and inventories.","Supervise and control the computerisation of the accounting system in federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government.","Carry out revenue monitoring and accounting.","Issue officially approved forms bearing Treasury numbers, for use in all federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government, to ensure uniformity.","Formulate the accounting policy of the federal government.","Service public debt and loans.","Organise training of accounts and internal audit personnel in all federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government."]}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"The Auditor-General for the Federation (AuGF)","b":[{"p":"In accordance with Government Financial Regulations, the Auditor-General for the Federation is the officer responsible, under the **1999 Constitution**, for the audit and reports on the public accounts of the Federation, including all persons and bodies established by law entrusted with the receipt, custody, issue, sale, transfer or delivery of any stamps, securities, inventories or other property of the government of the Federation, and for the certification of the annual accounts of the nation. He is given a free hand to examine the accounts in such manner as he may deem fit. At the end of the audit, he is expected to write a report stating whether, in his opinion:"},{"ol":["The accounts have been properly kept;","All public funds have been fully accounted for, and the rules and procedures applied are sufficient to secure effective check on the assessment, collection and proper allocation of revenue;","Monies have been expended for the purposes for which they were appropriated, and the expenditure has been made as authorised; and","Essential records are maintained, and the rules and procedures applied are sufficient to safeguard public property and funds."]},{"h4":"Appointment and removal"},{"p":"Recognised in **section 86(1)** of the 1999 Constitution:"},{"ul":["Appointed by the President, subject to confirmation by the Senate;","The appointment is based on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission; and","Once appointed, he/she cannot be removed from office except where he/she can no longer perform the functions of the office due to ill-health, death or gross misconduct, or where the term of the office has expired — that is, on serving 35 years, or attaining the age of 60 years, whichever is earlier."]},{"h4":"Powers and constitutional functions"},{"p":"In accordance with government regulations, the Auditor-General has:"},{"ol":["Power of access to books and records of all MDAs at reasonable times;","Power to request information and explanation necessary for his duties; and","Power to carry out special/ad-hoc investigations in any MDA."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section","Constitutional function"],"rows":[["**S.85(2)**","The public accounts of the Federation, and of all offices and courts of the Federation, shall be audited and reported on by the Auditor-General to the National Assembly; for that purpose he shall have access to all the books, records, returns and other documents relating to those accounts"],["**S.85(3)**","The Auditor-General is **not** authorised to audit the accounts of, or appoint auditors for, government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies (including all persons and bodies established by an Act of the National Assembly). He instead: provides such bodies with a list of qualified and experienced auditors from which they shall appoint their external auditors, and guidelines on the level of fees to be paid to them; and comments on their annual accounts and auditors' reports"],["**S.85(4)**","He has power to conduct periodic checks on all government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities, agencies etc., including all persons and bodies established by an Act of the National Assembly"],["**S.85(5)**","He shall, within **ninety days** of receipt of the Accountant-General's financial statements, submit his reports to each House of the National Assembly"]]}},{"h4":"Statutory functions — Financial Regulation 109 (2009 edition)"},{"ul":["**Financial audit**, in accordance with extant laws, to determine whether government accounts have been satisfactorily and faithfully kept.","**Appropriation audit**, to ensure that funds are expended as appropriated by the National Assembly.","**Financial control audit**, to ensure that laid-down procedures are observed in tendering, contracts and storekeeping, with a view to preventing waste, pilferage and extravagance.","Power to carry out the **audit of foreign missions**.","**Value-for-money (performance) audit**, to ascertain the level of economy, efficiency and effectiveness derived from government projects and programmes."]},{"h4":"Scope of work"},{"ul":["Audit of the books, accounts and records of federal ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government.","Vetting, commenting and certifying the audited accounts of all parastatals and government statutory corporations, in accordance with the Constitution of the Federation.","Audit of the accounts of federal government establishments located in all states of the Federation, including all Area Councils in the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja.","Audit of the Accountant-General's annual financial statements.","Auditing and certifying the Federation Account.","Deliberation, verification and reporting on reported cases of loss of funds, inventories, plants and equipment, as stipulated in the Financial Regulations.","Pre- and post-auditing of the payment of pensions and gratuities of retired military and civilian personnel.","Periodic checks of all government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies, including all persons and bodies established by an Act of the National Assembly.","Revenue audit of all government institutions."]},{"h4":"Independence of the Auditor-General"},{"p":"The constitutional provisions which insulate the Auditor-General from being compromised are:"},{"ul":["**S.85(6)** — In the exercise of his constitutional functions, the Auditor-General shall not be subject to the direction or control of any other authority or person.","**S.87(2)** — The Auditor-General shall not be removed from office before his retiring age, as may be prescribed by law, except for inability to perform the functions of his office, or for misconduct.","**S.84(4)** — The remuneration of the Auditor-General shall be drawn from the Consolidated Revenue Fund of the Federation.","**S.84(3)** — His remuneration and salaries, as well as conditions of service other than allowances, shall not be altered to his detriment after his appointment."]},{"h4":"Removal of the Auditor-General for the Federation"},{"p":"**S.87(1)** provides that a person holding the office of Auditor-General for the Federation shall be removed from office by the President, acting on an address supported by two-thirds majority of the Senate, on the ground that: (a) he is unable to discharge his constitutional and managerial functions due to illness or insanity; or (b) he is found to have been involved in a grave act of misconduct."},{"warn":"A common examination trap: the Auditor-General does **not** directly audit the accounts of government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies (S.85(3)). He provides the list of auditors from which they must appoint, comments on their audited accounts, and conducts periodic checks under S.85(4) — but the statutory audit itself is performed by the firm appointed by the corporation."}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"The Auditor-General for a State Government","b":[{"p":"The office of the State Auditor-General is an establishment with statutory and constitutional functions. Its powers and responsibilities derive from the **1999 Constitution** of the Federal Republic of Nigeria. It was created mainly to audit the accounts and underlying records of government at the State level; the Auditor-General for the State Government can call for a special investigation into the books and records of parastatals, ministries and government agencies."},{"h4":"Appointment"},{"p":"According to **section 126(1)** of the Constitution, the Auditor-General for a State shall be appointed by the State Governor, on the recommendation of the State Civil Service Commission, subject to confirmation by the State House of Assembly."},{"h4":"Functions and responsibilities"},{"ul":["To ensure that public establishments are keeping adequate accounts.","To ensure that all public money is properly accounted for, and that the rules and procedures applied are in tandem with the Financial Regulations.","To ensure that essential records are kept, and that the procedures applied safeguard and control public assets and cash.","To audit the public accounts of the State, and of all officers and courts of the State.","Issuance of annual statutory audit reports, in accordance with the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria.","To certify computations of pensions and gratuities of retiring public officers.","To monitor and evaluate all government projects.","To liaise with the **Public Accounts Committee** of the State House of Assembly on matters brought to the notice of the House.","To recommend the remuneration payable to appointed external auditors of government corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies established by law.","To receive and review the reports of the external auditors of government corporations, etc.","To carry out special audit or investigation on the three arms — the executive, the legislature and the judiciary — of the State Government."]}]},{"n":"7.6","t":"Accounting officers","b":[{"p":"In accordance with Government Financial Regulations, accounting officers are the **Permanent Secretaries of the MDAs**. They are saddled with the responsibility of the day-to-day financial affairs of the ministries and extra-ministerial departments. “Accounting officer” means the Permanent Secretary of a ministry, or the head of an extra-ministerial office or other arm of government, who is in full control of, and responsible for, the human, material and financial resources that are critical inputs in the management of an organisation."},{"h4":"General functions"},{"p":"The accounting officer shall:"},{"ul":["Be responsible for safeguarding public funds and the regularity and propriety of expenditure under his control.","Observe and comply fully with the checks and balances spelt out in the existing Financial Regulations, which govern the receipt and disbursement of public funds and other assets entrusted to his care, and shall be liable for any breach of them.","Note that his accountability does not cease on leaving office, and that he may be called upon at any time to account for his tenure as accounting officer.","Ensure that proper budgetary and accounting systems are established and maintained, to enhance internal control, accountability and transparency.","Ensure that essential management control tools are put in place to minimise waste and fraud.","Render monthly and other financial accounting returns and transcripts to the Accountant-General of the Federation, as required by the Financial Regulations.","Ensure the safety and proper maintenance of all government assets under his care.","Ensure personal appearance before the Public Accounts Committee, to answer audit queries relating to his ministry, extra-ministerial department or agency.","Ensure accurate collection and accounting for all public monies received and expended.","Ensure prudence in the expenditure of public funds.","Ensure proper assessments, fees, rates and charges are made where necessary.","Ensure internal guides, rules, regulations and procedures are adequately provided for the security and effective check on the assessment, collection and accounting for revenue.","Ensure that any losses of revenue are promptly reported and investigated.","Ensure that all revenue collected is compared with the budgeted estimates, with a view to highlighting the variances, positive or otherwise, and the reasons for them.","Ensure that any revenue collected is not spent, but remitted to the appropriate authorities promptly."]},{"h4":"Special responsibilities under the Public Procurement Act"},{"p":"In compliance with their special role under the Public Procurement Act, all accounting officers of ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government are charged with the following responsibilities. They shall:"},{"ul":["Preside over the activities of their tenders' boards, for the proper planning and evaluation of tenders and execution of procurements.","Ensure that adequate appropriation is available for procurements in their annual budget.","Integrate their entity's procurement expenditure into its yearly budget.","Ensure the establishment of a procurement planning committee, over whose activities they shall preside.","Constitute a procurement evaluation committee, for the efficient evaluation of tenders.","Constitute a Procurement Committee.","Render annual returns of procurement records to the Bureau of Public Procurement.","Liaise with the Bureau of Public Procurement to ensure implementation of its regulations.","Ensure compliance with the provisions of the Public Procurement Act by their organisations, **failing which they shall be personally liable for any breach or contravention thereof** — whether or not the breach or contravention was caused by them in person, by their subordinates, or by any person to whom they may have delegated their responsibilities."]},{"note":"This chapter does not itself use the word “surcharge”, but the personal liability described above for a breach of the Public Procurement Act is exactly that mechanism: an officer made to answer personally, out of his own responsibility, for a loss or breach that occurred on his watch, whether or not he caused it directly."}]},{"n":"7.7","t":"Sub-accounting officer","b":[{"p":"In accordance with Government Regulations, this officer, who is entrusted with the receipt, custody and disbursement of public funds, is required to maintain one of the recognised cash books, together with such other books as may be required by the Accountant-General. Examples include the **Sub-Treasurer of the Federation, Federal Pay Officer (FPO), Police Pay Officer (PPO), Customs Area Pay Officer (CAPO)** and **Director of Finance and Accounts (DFA)**."},{"h4":"Functions of the sub-accounting officer"},{"ul":["Ensuring that the proper system of accounts, as prescribed by the Accountant-General, is established.","Exercising supervision over the receipt of public revenue, and ensuring prompt collection.","Promptly bringing into account, under the proper heads and sub-heads of the estimates or other approved classifications, all receipts, whether revenue or otherwise.","Ensuring that proper provision is made for the safekeeping of public funds, securities, stamps, receipts, tickets, licences and other valuable documents.","Exercising supervision over all officers under his authority who are entrusted with the receipt and expenditure of public funds, and taking precautions by putting in place efficient checks against the occurrence of fraud, embezzlement and carelessness.","Supervising the expenditure of government, and ensuring that no payment is made without proper authorisation.","Promptly charging in his accounts, under the proper heads and sub-heads, all disbursements.","Checking all cash and stamps in his care, to reconcile the amounts with the balances in the cash book and stamp register.","Promptly bringing to account, as a receipt, any cash or stamp found in excess of the balance shown in the cash book or stamp register.","Making good any minor deficiency, not caused by theft or fraud, in the cash or stamps for which he is responsible, and thereafter reporting in writing to the Minister of Finance.","Promptly preparing such financial statements as are required by law or by the Minister of Finance.","Maintenance of the cash book."]}]},{"n":"7.8","t":"Officer controlling expenditure","b":[{"p":"This is an officer in charge of the various vote-heads of each ministry or extra-ministerial department, saddled with the responsibility of monitoring government expenditure and ensuring that there is no extra-budgetary spending."},{"h4":"Functions of the officer controlling expenditure"},{"ul":["Supervision of government expenditure, and ensuring that no payment is made without proper authority.","Promptly charging in his accounts, under the proper heads and sub-heads, all disbursements.","Ensuring that all books are correctly posted and kept up to date.","Producing, when required by the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General, all cash, stamps etc., in his custody.","Ensuring that funds are available under the appropriate head and sub-heads, to meet payments of specific vouchers.","Effective monitoring of government expenditure.","Ensuring that there is no extra-budgetary spending.","Ensuring that there is adequate security over the custody of public funds.","Maintenance of the **vote book**."]}]},{"n":"7.9","t":"The Budget Office of the Federation","b":[{"warn":"The chapter contents page of the study text lists this section as **“Director of Budget”**, but the body of the chapter treats it as the **“Budget Office of the Federation”** instead. This transcription follows the body text, which is where the substantive content actually appears."},{"p":"The Minister of Finance, through the Budget Office of the Federation, monitors and evaluates the implementation of the annual budget, assesses the attainment of fiscal targets, and reports to the Fiscal Responsibility Commission and the Joint Finance Committee of the National Assembly. In implementing their annual budgets, state and local governments may adopt these provisions. For preparation of the annual estimates and the formulation of the fiscal, monetary and other policies needed to support the economy, the Ministry of Budget and Planning performs the following functions:"},{"ul":["Developing reasoned economic assumptions and forecasts;","Issuing budget guidelines to the ministries and extra-ministerial departments;","Acting as liaison between the Presidency, ministries and extra-ministerial departments during budget preparation;","Compiling total revenue and expenditure estimates;","Drafting the budget speech;","Supervising and controlling the implementation of the budget;","Monitoring and evaluating the performance of programmes funded through the government budget;","Assessing the impact of the budget on the economy;","Developing formats of returns aimed at ensuring cost effectiveness in the use of government resources; and","Carrying out research on budget utilisation and the attainment of national or state objectives."]},{"note":"These ten functions of the Budget Office are identical to those already set out for the ministerial phase of budget preparation in Chapter 6.5 — the study text repeats the list here in the context of implementation and control, rather than preparation."}]},{"n":"7.10","t":"Concept and classes of warrants","b":[{"def":{"t":"Warrant","d":"A document used by the Ministry of Finance to authorise money for spending by government departments and agencies."}},{"p":"The expenditure of government is made under various forms of authorisation. These authorisations exist to ensure that all payments are made:"},{"ol":["From money available for the particular expenditure;","Under strict scrutiny of the responsible official of government; and","As the right type of expenditure, as authorised by Parliament."]},{"p":"There are two broad classes of warrant, matching the two main types of government expenditure: **recurrent** expenditure — incurred regularly in the course of the organisation's annual operations, for items or services used within the year — and **capital** expenditure — not incurred often, for the purchase of fixed assets. Warrants are therefore grouped as **Recurrent Expenditure Warrants** and **Specific/Capital Expenditure Warrants**. Within these two main classes, warrants may be provisional, general or specific. Every warrant is issued in **two copies**: the original is forwarded to the Accountant-General of the Federation, and the duplicate to the Auditor-General for the Federation. A notification that a warrant has been issued is also published in the Federal Office Gazette."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Recurrent expenditure","Capital expenditure"],"rows":[["Frequency","Incurred **regularly**, every year, as part of ordinary operations","Incurred **infrequently** — a one-off acquisition or project"],["What it buys","Items and services **consumed within the year** — salaries, running costs, maintenance","A **fixed (non-current) asset** with a life beyond the year — a road, a building, a vehicle, equipment"],["Benefit to government","Exhausted in the year it is spent","Continues to benefit the government in **future years**"],["NCOA economic segment","**Personnel cost** and **overhead**","**Capital expenditure** — its own distinct code"],["Fund it is charged to","The **Consolidated Revenue Fund**","The **Development Fund** (itself fed by a yearly transfer from the CRF)"],["Warrant that releases it","Annual General Warrant (and its recurrent-side relatives — PGW, SGW, REW)","**Development Fund Annual General Warrant** (with its own provisional/supplementary/virement counterparts below, under \"Capital expenditure warrants\")"],["Typical examples","Salaries and wages; office running costs; debt service; repairs and maintenance","Roads, bridges and dams; buildings and staff housing; hospitals; vehicles; loans made to State Governments for their own development"]]}},{"key":"The two are **never mixed on the same warrant, or the same fund**: a recurrent item is authorised from the CRF by an Annual General Warrant; a capital item is authorised from the Development Fund by a Development Fund General Warrant. This is precisely the \"relationship between capital and recurrent expenditure\" that §6.6 flags **line-item budgeting** as failing to spell out clearly — the budget groups spending by *which ministry* asked for it, not by *which of these two natures* it is, so the two get mixed together within one department's vote."},{"h4":"Recurrent expenditure warrants"},{"p":"Recurrent Expenditure Warrants are authorisations issued by the Minister of Finance to disburse from the **Consolidated Revenue Fund**:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Warrant","What it does"],"rows":[["**Annual General Warrant (AGW)**","Authorises the Accountant-General of the Federation to release funds for the payment of personal emoluments and other services provided for in the approved estimate/budget, and authorises the officers controlling expenditure votes to incur expenditure for these purposes. The Minister may exclude from it any item of expenditure over which he wishes to exercise special control"],["**Provisional General Warrant (PGW)**","Issued at the beginning of the financial year, before the Appropriation Act comes into operation. It provides for the continuation of government services on a scale not exceeding the level of the previous financial year, and remains in operation for a maximum of **six months**, or until the Appropriation Act comes into effect, whichever is shorter. The amount expendable under it must not exceed the sum expended during the same period the previous year, nor the amount specified in the approved budget, and any money utilised is set off against the amounts eventually provided in the Appropriation Act"],["**Supplementary General Warrant (SGW)**","Issued for additional personal emoluments and other services provided for in the approved supplementary estimates. The Minister may similarly exclude items over which he wishes to exercise special control"],["**Reserve Expenditure Warrant (REW)**","Authorises the release of funds included in the approved annual or supplementary estimates, but excluded from the AGW or SGW — the release of funds the Minister had initially withheld to exercise special control"],["**Supplementary (Contingencies) Warrant**","Issued in exceptional cases where virement is not possible, and an application for additional provision reveals such urgency that the issue of funds cannot be postponed until a Supplementary Appropriation Act is passed. A Contingencies Fund Warrant must first be issued by the Minister to authorise the Accountant-General to transfer necessary funds from the Contingencies Fund to the Consolidated Revenue Fund; thereafter a Supplementary (Contingencies) Warrant is issued, authorising expenditure from the Head and Sub-Heads concerned"],["**Virement Warrant (VW)**","Issued where, as a result of unforeseen circumstances during approval of the annual estimates, an additional provision is required under a particular sub-head, and an equivalent amount can be saved under another sub-head of the **same head**. Virement Warrants may not be used to create a new sub-head, or for items disallowed by the Budget or Estimate Committee"],["**Supplementary (Statutory) Expenditure Warrant**","Authorises additional expenditure over and above that in the AGW and SGW, from votes chargeable to the Consolidated Revenue Fund by legislation other than Appropriation Acts. The Ministry of Finance customarily notifies the officers controlling the relevant votes when the supplementary expenditure is made available"],["**Imprest Warrant**","Authorises funds to be released to any senior officer who has to spend the money and account for it periodically"]]}},{"h4":"Requirements for a successful virement application"},{"p":"To be successful, an application for virement should:"},{"ol":["Be in writing;","State that a particular sub-head is in deficit;","State that another sub-head is in surplus;","Indicate that both sub-heads are within the **same economic head**;","State that, after the transfer, the other sub-head will not itself be in deficit; and","State that the virement warrant is not sought to create new sub-heads."]},{"h4":"Capital expenditure warrants"},{"p":"These are issued as authorisations for disbursement from the **Development Fund (DF)**. Capital expenditure may not be incurred except on the authority of one of the following warrants, issued by the Minister of Finance:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Warrant","What it does"],"rows":[["**Development Fund Annual General Warrant (DFAGW)**","Authorises the Accountant-General to issue funds for expenditure on capital projects, as contained in the approved Capital Estimate, and mandates the officers controlling expenditure votes to disburse on the capital projects envisaged. The authority to incur expenditure is conveyed after the National Assembly has approved the Capital Expenditure Budget"],["**Provisional Development Fund General Warrant**","Issued before the National Assembly's approval of the Capital Estimates, at the beginning of the financial year. It authorises payment from the Development Fund of the amount necessary to carry on projects for which expenditure was authorised in the previous financial year, for a period of six months or until National Assembly authority is obtained, whichever is shorter"],["**Development Fund Supplementary General Warrant (DFSGW)**","Authorises the AGF, and the officers controlling votes concerned, to issue funds and incur expenditure on projects sanctioned by the National Assembly in resolutions approving supplementary capital estimates. The Minister may exclude from it any item over which he wishes to exercise special control"],["**Development Fund Reserved Expenditure Warrant (DFREW)**","Authorises the release of funds in the approved Annual or Supplementary Capital Estimates, but excluded from the DFAGW and DFSGW — the release of funds the Minister initially withheld to exercise special control"],["**Development Fund Supplementary Warrant**","Authorises additional expenditure over and above that in the DFAGW or DFSGW, for the purposes of: revoting capital expenditure provided for in the previous financial year but not fully expended in that year; accelerating the provision of funds already formally allocated but not voted for a project; and accelerating the completion of a specific capital project"],["**Development Fund Special Warrant**","Issued in exceptional cases where virement is not possible, and provision for the release of additional funds reveals such urgency that release cannot be postponed until a Supplementary Capital Estimate is approved (where postponement would cause serious injury to the public interest). The amount expended under it must not exceed the balance of the Development Fund remaining after all other expenditure provided for in the Capital Estimate has been incurred"],["**Development Fund Virement Warrant**","Permits the issue of additional funds necessary to complete a capital project for which the amount already allocated in the Estimate is insufficient, provided there are sufficient offsetting savings in the amounts appropriated for other projects in the **same Economic Programme Section**"]]}},{"warn":"The source text abbreviates both the “Development Fund Supplementary Warrant” and the “Development Fund Special Warrant” as **DFSW** — the same abbreviation for two different warrants distinguished only by their full names. This ambiguity is preserved here as printed; always refer to a Development Fund warrant by its full name rather than the abbreviation to avoid confusion."},{"p":"The limitations on the Development Fund Virement Warrant are:"},{"ol":["Re-allocation can be made only within the **same head** of expenditure in the capital estimates;","The re-allocation must not give rise to a new principle or policy; and","It cannot be used to provide funds for new projects."]}]},{"n":"7.11","t":"Expenditure authorisation procedures in Ghana","b":[{"p":"The following is the general procedure for the authorisation of money to be spent by any institution of government in Ghana:"},{"ol":["The **Ministry of Finance and Economic Planning (MOFEP)** prepares a national work plan, based on MDA visions, as stipulated in the budget guidelines.","MOFEP issues a **quarterly cash ceiling** to cost centres, sub-totalled at National, Regional and District levels for each MDA.","MOFEP issues **monthly expenditure warrants** to the controller and Accountant-General, attaching cost centre details submitted by MDAs, which show the sub-totals at National, Regional and District levels.","Based on the monthly warrants from MOFEP, the **Controller and Accountant-General's Department (CAGD)** issues warrants directly to cost centres, but aggregated to show sub-totals at National, Regional and District levels. An amount equal to the warrants for the National, Regional and District levels is transferred into the **sub-Consolidated Fund Bank Account** of the MDA, Regional Co-ordinating Council (RCC) and District Assembly respectively.","The Treasuries have merged with the accounts or finance divisions of MDAs, RCCs and Metropolitan/Municipal/District Assemblies (MMDAs).","Each Ministry in Ghana has its own Accounts/Finance department, which also performs treasury functions.","Each Regional Co-ordinating Council has its Finance Directorate, which also performs treasury functions at the Regional level.","Each District Assembly has its Finance Office, which performs treasury functions at the District level.","Special bank accounts (sub-Consolidated Fund Bank Accounts) are available for the ministries, Regional Co-ordinating Councils and District Assemblies: each Ministry has one Consolidated Fund sub-account, serving the Ministry and all National-level departments and agencies, held with the Bank of Ghana; each Regional Administration has one Consolidated Fund sub-account, serving the Regional Administration and all Region-level departments, agencies and institutions, at the nearest branches of the Bank of Ghana; and each District Assembly has one Consolidated Fund sub-account, serving the District Assembly and all District-level departments, agencies and institutions, at the branches of the Bank of Ghana nearest to the districts."]},{"h4":"Commitment process"},{"p":"On receipt of the quarterly cash ceilings, MDAs review their work plans. On receipt of expenditure warrants and the bank transfer advices:"},{"ol":["The cost centre at the Head Office, Regional or District level prepares an **Activity and Expenditure Initiation Form**, to commence the commitment process.","The cost centre requests quotations from suppliers and selects the best.","The cost centre prepares a **purchase order (PO)** and submits it for approval.","The cost centre submits the approved purchase order to the servicing Treasury for commitment.","The cost centre dispatches the purchase order to the supplier."]},{"key":"The Ghanaian system shares the same underlying logic as the Nigerian one — a central ministry issues ceilings and warrants, which release money to spending units through a Treasury-controlled bank account, and expenditure is committed before it is paid — but organises the machinery around three tiers (National, Regional, District) rather than the federal/state/local structure used in Nigeria, and gives the process an explicit paper trail (the Activity and Expenditure Initiation Form, then the purchase order) before any commitment is made."}]},{"n":"7.12","t":"Further reading","b":[{"ul":["[Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 1999 (as amended)](https://placng.org/i/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/Constitution-of-the-Federal-Republic-of-Nigeria.pdf) — sections 84–87 and 120–126 on the Auditor-General for the Federation and for a State.","[Financial Regulations (2009 edition)](https://www.hmfeg.gov.ng/) — Federal Ministry of Finance, for the powers and functions of the Accountant-General and Auditor-General.","[Public Procurement Act 2007](https://ppa.gov.ng/) — Bureau of Public Procurement, for the accounting officer's procurement responsibilities."]}]},{"n":"7.13","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"p":"The study text's own end-of-chapter set, with its answer key. **Bold** marks the crammable answer."},{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section A","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is **not** among the officials who attain their positions by way of experience, hard work and efficiency and cannot be arbitrarily removed: (A) Accountant-General  (B) Local Government Chairman  (C) Auditor-General for the Federation  (D) Accounting Officer  (E) Federal Pay Officer.","The functions of the officer controlling expenditure include the following **with the exception of one**: (A) supervising government expenditure and ensuring no payment is made without proper authority  (B) ensuring all books are correctly posted and kept up to date  (C) promptly charging all disbursements under proper heads and sub-heads  (D) maintenance of the cashbook  (E) ensuring all books are correctly posted.","One of the following is **not** among the listed functions of the Auditor-General for the Federation under Financial Regulation 109 (2009): (A) appropriation audit  (B) commenting on the annual accounts and auditor's report of corporations established by an Act of the National Assembly  (C) financial control unit  (D) power to carry out the audit of foreign missions  (E) value-for-money (performance) audit.","The following are functions of the Minister of Finance **except**: (A) preparation of the annual estimates of revenue and expenditure of the Federal Government  (B) formulation of all policies relating to fiscal and monetary matters  (C) execution of all capital projects that cut across regions of the country  (D) supervision of all matters connected with the allocation of revenue to the three tiers  (E) stabilisation of the value of the Nigerian currency internally and externally.","The following are functions of the Accountant-General of the Federation **except**: (i) serve as Chief Accounting Officer for the receipts and payments of the government of the Federation  (ii) supervise the accounts of federal ministries and other arms of government  (iii) manage federal government investments  (iv) maintain and operate the Federation Account  (v) comment on the annual accounts and auditors' reports of government statutory corporations.","The public sector officer charged with the control and management of the public funds of the Federation is the …","The Chief Accounting Officer charged with the general supervision of the accounts of all ministries and extra-ministerial departments and the preparation of the annual financial statements of the nation is the …","The remuneration of the Auditor-General for the Federation is drawn from the …","Authorisations issued by the Minister of Finance to disburse from the Consolidated Revenue Fund are known as …","The warrant issued for additional personal emoluments and other services provided for in the approved supplementary estimate is the …"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**B — Local Government Chairman.** A Chairman is an elected political officer, not a career official protected from arbitrary removal.","**D — maintenance of the cashbook.** That is the sub-accounting officer's duty; the officer controlling expenditure maintains the **vote book**.","**C — \"financial control unit.\"** Not a function of the Auditor-General under FR 109.","**C — \"execution of all capital projects that cut across regions.\"** The Minister of Finance formulates policy and prepares estimates; it does not execute projects.","**(v) — commenting on the annual accounts and auditors' reports of statutory corporations.** That is the Auditor-General's function, not the Accountant-General's.","**The Minister of Finance.**","**The Accountant-General of the Federation.**","**The Consolidated Revenue Fund** (a statutory, first-charge item).","**Warrants** — specifically the Recurrent Expenditure Warrant.","**The Supplementary General Warrant (S.G.W.).**"]}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text Section B","t":"Theory questions with suggested solutions","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["List and explain any **five** warrants under Recurrent Expenditure Warrants.","Enumerate the basic **functions of sub-accounting officers**.","List the major **functions of the officer controlling expenditure**.","List the main **functions of the Budget Office** of the Federation."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1  Recurrent Expenditure Warrants (any five)"},{"ul":["**Annual General Warrant (A.G.W.)** — authorises the Accountant-General to release funds, and officers controlling votes to incur expenditure, for the personal emoluments and other services in the approved estimates. The Minister of Finance may exclude items on which he wishes to keep special control. Original to the Accountant-General, duplicate to the Auditor-General.","**Provisional General Warrant (P.G.W.)** — issued at the start of the year before the Appropriation Act comes into force, to continue services at a level not exceeding the previous year, for a maximum of **six months** or until the Act comes into effect, whichever is shorter; amounts spent are set off against the Appropriation Act.","**Supplementary General Warrant (S.G.W.)** — for additional personal emoluments and other services in the approved supplementary estimates.","**Reserve Expenditure Warrant (R.E.W.)** — releases funds in the approved estimates but earlier withheld from the A.G.W. or S.G.W. for the Minister's special control.","**Supplementary (Contingencies) Warrant** — in exceptional cases where virement is impossible and the urgency is such that issue of funds cannot wait for a Supplementary Appropriation Act; a Contingencies Fund Warrant first transfers funds from the Contingencies Fund to the CRF.","**Virement Warrant (V.W.)** — transfers a saving from a surplus sub-head to a deficit sub-head **within the same Head**; may not create a new sub-head or fund items disallowed by the Estimates Committee.","**Supplementary (Statutory) Expenditure Warrant** — authorises expenditure charged on the CRF by legislation other than Appropriation Acts.","**Imprest Warrant** — releases funds to a senior officer who spends and accounts periodically."]},{"h4":"2  Functions of sub-accounting officers"},{"ol":["Ensure the proper system of accounts prescribed by the Accountant-General is established.","Supervise the receipt of public revenue and ensure prompt collection.","Promptly bring all receipts to account under the proper heads and sub-heads.","Ensure safe keeping of public funds, securities, stamps, receipts, tickets, licences and other valuables.","Supervise all officers under his authority who handle receipts and expenditure, and maintain checks against fraud, embezzlement and carelessness.","Supervise government expenditure and ensure no payment is made without proper authorisation.","Promptly charge all disbursements under the proper heads and sub-heads.","Check all cash and stamps and reconcile to the cashbook and stamp register.","Bring to account promptly any cash or stamps found in excess.","Make good any minor deficiency not caused by theft or fraud, and report it in writing to the Minister of Finance.","Prepare promptly such financial statements as the law or the Minister requires.","Maintain the cashbook."]},{"h4":"3  Functions of the officer controlling expenditure"},{"ol":["Supervise government expenditure and ensure no payment is made without proper authority.","Promptly charge all disbursements under the proper heads and sub-heads.","Ensure all books are correctly posted and kept up to date.","Produce, when required by the Accountant-General or Auditor-General, all cash, stamps etc. in his custody.","Ensure funds are available under the appropriate head and sub-heads to meet specific vouchers.","Monitor government expenditure effectively.","Ensure there is no extra-budgetary spending.","Ensure adequate security over the custody of public funds.","Maintain the vote book."]},{"h4":"4  Functions of the Budget Office of the Federation"},{"ol":["Develop reasoned economic assumptions and forecasts.","Issue budget guidelines to ministries and extra-ministerial departments.","Act as liaison between the Presidency, ministries and extra-ministerial departments during budget preparation.","Compile total revenue and expenditure estimates.","Draft the budget speech.","Supervise and control the implementation of the budget.","Monitor and evaluate the performance of budget-funded programmes.","Assess the impact of the budget on the economy.","Develop formats of returns to ensure cost-effectiveness in the use of government resources.","Carry out research on budget utilisation and the attainment of national or state objectives."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Examined in almost every diet. Section A tests the section number establishing the Auditor-General, the reporting line, who the accounting officer of a ministry is, and the name of a specific warrant given its description. Section B commonly asks for the duties of the Accountant-General, the duties of the Auditor-General and the safeguards of his independence, the responsibilities of an accounting officer, or a list-and-explain of the recurrent or capital expenditure warrants. These are recall marks and should be scored in full.","errors":["Saying the Auditor-General reports to the President or the Minister of Finance; he reports to the National Assembly.","Confusing the Accountant-General (prepares the accounts) with the Auditor-General (audits them).","Naming the Minister as the accounting officer of a ministry; it is the Permanent Secretary.","Stating that the Auditor-General audits parastatals directly; he supplies the list of auditors and comments on their reports (S.85(3)).","Omitting the 90-day period for submission of the Auditor-General's report (S.85(5)).","Confusing the Provisional General Warrant (before the Appropriation Act, limited to six months) with the Annual General Warrant (after it).","Describing virement as increasing the total appropriation, or as moving money between different heads; it only moves savings between sub-heads of the same head.","Forgetting that capital expenditure is authorised from the Development Fund, by a distinct family of warrants, not from the Consolidated Revenue Fund."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Auditor-General for the Federation submits his report to the","o":["President","National Assembly","Minister of Finance","Accountant-General of the Federation","Federal Executive Council"],"a":1,"w":"Section 85(5) of the Constitution requires the report to go to the National Assembly — reporting to the legislature rather than to the executive he audits is the core of his independence.","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"The accounting officer of a federal ministry is the","o":["Minister","Permanent Secretary","Director of Finance and Accounts","Accountant-General","Internal Auditor"],"a":1,"w":"The Permanent Secretary is the accounting officer, in full control of and responsible for the human, material and financial resources of the ministry.","src":"Chapter 7.6","sec":"7.6"},{"q":"The Auditor-General must submit his report within how many days of receiving the Accountant-General's financial statements?","o":["60 days","90 days","30 days","180 days","120 days"],"a":1,"w":"Section 85(5) of the Constitution requires submission within 90 days of receipt of the Accountant-General's financial statements.","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"Requiring a public officer to make good a loss of public funds caused by his negligence or breach of duty is known as","o":["interdiction","surcharge","virement","retirement","suspension"],"a":1,"w":"Surcharge is the personal financial liability imposed under the Financial Regulations. Interdiction and suspension are disciplinary measures, not financial ones.","src":"Chapter 7.6","sec":"7.6"},{"q":"Preparation and publication of the statutory financial statements of the Federation is the duty of the","o":["Auditor-General","Accountant-General","Minister of Finance","Budget Office of the Federation","Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Accountant-General collates, prepares and publishes them (Financial Regulation 107); the Auditor-General audits them and reports to the National Assembly.","src":"Chapter 7.3","sec":"7.3"},{"q":"A fixed sum entrusted to an officer to meet minor payments, retired and reimbursed on presentation of vouchers, is authorised by an","o":["Annual General Warrant","Imprest Warrant","Virement Warrant","Reserve Expenditure Warrant","Supplementary General Warrant"],"a":1,"w":"The Imprest Warrant authorises funds to be released to any senior officer who has to spend the money and account for it periodically.","src":"Chapter 7.10","sec":"7.10"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a duty of the officer controlling expenditure?","o":["Supervising expenditure so that no payment is made without proper authority","Promptly charging disbursements under the correct heads and sub-heads","Maintenance of the cashbook","Maintenance of the vote book","Ensuring there is no extra-budgetary spending"],"a":2,"w":"Keeping the cashbook is the sub-accounting officer's duty; the officer controlling expenditure keeps the vote book.","src":"Chapter 7.13 (study text Section A Q2)","sec":"7.13"},{"q":"The provisional authority issued before the Appropriation Act comes into force, allowing services to continue at the previous year's level for up to six months, is the","o":["Annual General Warrant","Provisional General Warrant","Supplementary General Warrant","Reserve Expenditure Warrant","Virement Warrant"],"a":1,"w":"The Provisional General Warrant keeps government running before the Appropriation Act, for a maximum of six months or until the Act takes effect, whichever is shorter.","src":"Chapter 7.13 (study text Section B Q1)","sec":"7.13"},{"q":"The remuneration of the Auditor-General for the Federation is a direct charge on the","o":["Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Consolidated Revenue Fund","Federation Account","budget of the National Assembly"],"a":2,"w":"To protect the Auditor-General's independence, the salary is charged directly on the Consolidated Revenue Fund and is not subject to the annual appropriation.","src":"Chapter 7.13 (study text Section A Q8)","sec":"7.13"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State eight duties of the Accountant-General of the Federation. (b) State the duties of the Auditor-General for the Federation and explain the constitutional provisions that safeguard his independence. (c) Explain the responsibilities of an accounting officer and the sanctions to which he is exposed for breach of them.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Duties of the Accountant-General of the Federation"},{"p":"The Accountant-General is the chief accounting officer of receipts and payments of the Government of the Federation and head of the Federal Treasury. His duties, under Financial Regulation 107, include the following."},{"ol":["**Supervising the accounts** of all federal ministries, extra-ministerial departments and agencies, and ensuring that they conform to the prescribed form.","**Collating, preparing and publishing the statutory financial statements** of the federal government and any other statements required by the Minister of Finance.","**Maintaining and operating the public funds** — the Consolidated Revenue Fund, the Development Fund, the Contingencies Fund and all other funds of the Federation — and providing cash backing for the operations of the Federal Government.","**Maintaining and operating the Federation Account**, and establishing and supervising Federal Pay Offices in each state capital.","**Conducting routine and in-depth inspections** of the books and records of MDAs to ensure compliance with the Financial Regulations and with accounting instructions.","**Managing federal government investments**, and servicing public debt and loans.","**Issuing Treasury Circulars** and providing financial guidelines to ensure strict compliance with control systems for the collection, custody and disbursement of public funds and inventories.","**Investigating cases of fraud, loss of funds and financial irregularity**, and formulating the accounting policy of the federal government."]},{"h4":"(b) The Auditor-General for the Federation"},{"p":"**Duties**"},{"ol":["To audit and report on the **public accounts of the Federation** and of all offices and courts of the Federation (section 85(2)).","To submit his reports to the **National Assembly** within 90 days of receiving the Accountant-General's financial statements (section 85(5)).","To provide the **list of auditors** from which government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies appoint their external auditors, and to comment on their audited accounts (section 85(3)).","To conduct **appropriation and financial control audits**, verifying that expenditure was properly authorised and applied to the purposes appropriated, and to guard against waste, pilferage and extravagance.","To conduct **value-for-money audits** examining economy, efficiency and effectiveness in the use of public resources, and to audit foreign missions.","To carry out **periodic checks** of government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies (section 85(4)), and special investigations at the request of the National Assembly.","To have **unrestricted access** at all reasonable times to all books, records, returns, vouchers, cash and government property, and to require any explanation he considers necessary."]},{"p":"**Safeguards of independence**"},{"ol":["**Security of remuneration.** Under section 84(4) his remuneration is drawn from the Consolidated Revenue Fund, and under section 84(3) it may not be altered to his detriment after his appointment.","**Security of tenure.** Under section 87(2) he may not be removed before his retiring age except for inability to perform his functions or for misconduct; under section 87(1), removal itself requires the President to act on an address supported by two-thirds of the Senate, on the ground of illness/insanity or grave misconduct.","**Reporting line.** He reports to the National Assembly, not to the executive whose accounts he audits — the separation on which the whole value of the audit depends.","**Freedom from direction.** Under section 85(6), in the exercise of his functions he is not subject to the direction or control of any other person or authority.","**Manner of appointment.** Under section 86(1) he is appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission, subject to confirmation by the Senate, and he cannot be removed except for the specific grounds and the term set out above (service of 35 years or attaining age 60, whichever is earlier, for ordinary expiry of term)."]},{"p":"One limitation is worth noting in a full answer: under section 85(3) he does not directly audit government corporations and agencies, which is regularly criticised as a gap in accountability over a large part of public spending."},{"h4":"(c) Responsibilities and liability of an accounting officer"},{"p":"The accounting officer — the Permanent Secretary of a ministry, or the head of an extra-ministerial office — is in full control of, and responsible for, the human, material and financial resources of his organisation. His responsibilities are:"},{"ul":["to be responsible for **safeguarding public funds** and the regularity and propriety of expenditure under his control;","to observe and comply fully with the **checks and balances** in the Financial Regulations, being liable for any breach of them;","to ensure that **proper budgetary and accounting systems** are established and maintained, and that management control tools are in place to minimise waste and fraud;","to render **monthly and other financial returns and transcripts** to the Accountant-General, and to appear personally before the **Public Accounts Committee** to answer audit queries;","to ensure the **safety of government assets**, the accurate collection and accounting of all public monies, and prudence in the expenditure of public funds;","to ensure that any losses of revenue are **promptly reported and investigated**, and that collected revenue is compared against budgeted estimates with variances explained;","under the Public Procurement Act, to preside over the tenders board, constitute a procurement planning committee and a procurement evaluation committee, and ensure the organisation complies with the Act."]},{"p":"**Sanctions.** The distinguishing feature of public sector stewardship is personal liability, which **does not cease on leaving office** — an accounting officer may be called upon at any time to account for his tenure. Under the Public Procurement Act specifically, an accounting officer is **personally liable** for any breach of the Act by his organisation, whether or not he caused it in person, and whether it was caused by a subordinate or by someone to whom he delegated responsibility. More generally, this liability is the mechanism the Financial Regulations call **surcharge**: making the officer good the loss personally, in addition to possible interdiction, suspension, dismissal or criminal prosecution where the conduct amounts to an offence."}],"src":"Chapter 7.3, 7.4, 7.6","sec":"7.3"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Vouchers and Their Classification","brief":"The types of voucher used in government accounting, the contents of a payment voucher, the registers in which vouchers are recorded, the treatment of a lost voucher, and the rules on custody and retention.","outcomes":["Define a voucher and state its purpose","Classify vouchers into payment, receipt, adjustment and journal vouchers","List the contents of a properly completed payment voucher","Describe the flow of a payment voucher through the accounts department","State the registers in which vouchers are recorded","Explain the procedure where a voucher is lost"],"secs":[{"n":"8.1","t":"Nature and purpose","b":[{"def":{"t":"Voucher","d":"A document that evidences a transaction and authorises its entry in the books of account. Every payment out of public funds and every receipt into them must be supported by a voucher, and no entry may be made in the accounts without one."}},{"h4":"Purposes"},{"ul":["To **evidence** that a transaction actually took place and that value was received.","To **authorise** the payment or the accounting entry — the voucher carries the signature of the officer empowered to approve it.","To **classify** the transaction to the correct head, subhead and economic code, so that it is charged to the right vote.","To provide the **audit trail** connecting the ledger entry to the underlying documents.","To fix **responsibility**, since the officers who prepared, checked, authorised and paid are all identified on the face of it."]}]},{"n":"8.2","t":"Classification of vouchers","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Purpose","Effect on the accounts"],"rows":[["**Payment voucher (PV)**","Authorises and records a payment out of public funds — salaries, contractors, suppliers, advances","Credit cash or bank; debit the relevant expenditure or asset head"],["**Receipt voucher (RV)**","Records money received into public funds where no treasury receipt is issued to a payer — for example a transfer from another government office, or a refund","Debit cash or bank; credit the relevant revenue head"],["**Adjustment voucher (AV)**","Corrects an error of classification — a payment charged to the wrong head or subhead","Debit the correct head; credit the head wrongly charged"],["**Journal voucher (JV)**","Records transactions that do not involve the movement of cash — depreciation, accruals, transfers between funds, write-off of losses","As dictated by the entry; no cash movement"]]}},{"p":"Vouchers are further described by the stage they have reached:"},{"ul":["An **unpaid voucher** awaits payment;","A **paid voucher** has been settled and is filed as evidence;","An **outstanding voucher** was raised but not paid by the year end; and","A **duplicate voucher** is a certified copy raised where the original is lost."]},{"warn":"An **adjustment voucher** corrects a classification error only; it does not create or extinguish a liability. Using an adjustment voucher to record a genuine new transaction, or to move an over-expenditure quietly to a head with a balance, is a financial irregularity and is the sort of thing an auditor looks for."}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Contents of a payment voucher","b":[{"p":"The Financial Regulations prescribe the particulars a payment voucher must carry. A voucher missing any of them is not a valid authority to pay."},{"ol":["The **serial number** of the voucher and the date.","The **name of the ministry, department or agency** and the station.","The **head, subhead and economic code** to which the expenditure is chargeable.","The **name and address of the payee**, and the bank account details where payment is by transfer.","The **full particulars of the service or goods** — quantity, rate, description, period covered — sufficient for the charge to be checked without reference to other documents.","The **amount in figures and in words**.","The **supporting documents** attached: local purchase order, invoice, store receipt voucher, certificate of completion, contract agreement, nominal roll.","The **certification** by the officer who checked the arithmetic and the classification.","The **certificate of the accounting officer** or officer authorised on his behalf, that the service has been performed, the rates are correct and the charge is a proper charge on the vote.","The **authority for the payment** — the warrant, contract or approval relied on.","The **payee's receipt or acknowledgement**, and the officer's certificate of payment.","The **stamp \"PAID\"** with the date, applied immediately on payment."]},{"key":"The two certificates matter most. The **certifying officer** vouches that the service was performed and the rate correct; the **authorising officer** vouches that the charge is a proper charge against the vote and that funds remain. Separating those two functions from the officer who makes the payment is the basic internal control over expenditure."}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"The flow of a payment voucher","b":[{"steps":["The **user department** raises a requisition and, where goods or services are required, a local purchase order or contract is issued after the procurement process.","On delivery, the store receives the goods and issues a **store receipt voucher**; or the work is inspected and a **certificate of completion** issued.","The **payment voucher is prepared** by the accounts section, classified to the correct head and subhead, and the supporting documents attached.","The vote book is examined and the amount **charged to the vote**; if the uncommitted balance is insufficient the voucher cannot proceed.","The voucher is **pre-audited** internally: arithmetic, classification, supporting documents, and compliance with the Financial Regulations.","The voucher is **certified and authorised** by the officers empowered to do so.","Payment is made — by mandate to the bank under the Treasury Single Account arrangement — and the voucher is **stamped \"PAID\"** and dated.","The voucher is **entered in the cash book** and posted to the ledger, and recorded in the Register of Payment Vouchers.","The paid voucher is **filed in serial order** and retained for the prescribed period, available for audit."]}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Registers","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Register","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Register of Payment Vouchers**","Records every payment voucher in serial order — number, date, payee, amount, head and subhead. Establishes completeness and locates any voucher"],["**Register of Receipts (Revenue Collectors' Register)**","Records receipt books issued to collectors and their return, so that every receipt form is accounted for"],["**Register of Bill / Cheque Payments (Payment Mandate Register)**","Records mandates issued to the bank, so that payments can be reconciled with the bank statement"],["**Register of Losses**","Records every loss of cash or stores, the officer responsible, the investigation and its outcome"],["**Register of Unclaimed / Outstanding Vouchers**","Records vouchers passed for payment but not yet settled"],["**Vote book (DVEA)**","Records commitments and expenditure against each subhead and shows the uncommitted balance"],["**Register of Advances and Deposits**","Records advances made to officers and deposits received, and their clearance"]]}}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Loss of a voucher and custody","b":[{"h4":"Where a voucher is lost"},{"p":"Loss of a paid voucher is a serious matter, because the evidence supporting a payment out of public funds has disappeared. The Financial Regulations prescribe the following procedure."},{"ol":["The loss is **reported immediately** to the accounting officer, who reports it to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General.","A **thorough search** is made and the circumstances of the loss investigated.","A **duplicate copy** is obtained — from the payee, from the file copy, or reconstructed from the records — and marked clearly as a **certified true copy** of the original.","The duplicate is **certified by the accounting officer**, who states that the original has been lost, that the payment was properly made, that the service was performed, and that to the best of his knowledge no double payment has occurred or will occur.","A **certificate of non-payment** is obtained where appropriate, confirming that the payment has not been made twice.","The circumstances are **recorded in the Register of Losses**, and where negligence is established the officer responsible may be **surcharged** or otherwise disciplined."]},{"h4":"Custody and retention"},{"ul":["Paid vouchers are filed in **serial order** in a secure place under the control of a named officer.","They must be produced to the internal auditor and the Auditor-General on demand and may not be released to any other person without authority.","They are retained for the period prescribed in the Financial Regulations — generally not less than **five years**, and longer where litigation, an unresolved audit query or an investigation is outstanding.","Vouchers may not be destroyed without the **written approval of the Auditor-General**.","Unused voucher and receipt forms are treated as **security documents**, held under lock and issued against signature in a register."]},{"note":"The examinable principle is that a lost voucher is never simply replaced. The duplicate is certified, the loss is reported to both the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General, and it is entered in the Register of Losses — because the possibility of a fraudulent second payment must be positively excluded."}]},{"n":"8.7","t":"Payroll accounting in the public sector","b":[{"def":{"t":"Payroll approval","d":"the **Accountant-General** must approve the procedure and method employed by federal ministries, departments and units for the recording and calculation of personal emoluments. Unless FR 1518 provides otherwise, the standard payroll system maintains three records in a single operation:"}},{"ol":["the **Personal Emoluments Record (PER)**;","the **payroll**, in duplicate, together with a **bank paying-in-advice slip**; and","the **pay statement** issued to the individual officer."]},{"p":"This applies whether the system is manual or computerised. The officer controlling expenditure ensures the **Standard Personal Emoluments Record** shows: standard rate of pay, incremental rate, standard allowance, salary and other advances issued, tax (PAYE), gross emoluments, standard deductions from emoluments, and net emoluments payable."},{"h4":"Custody of Personal Emoluments Record Cards (PERC)"},{"p":"Where the accounting system is **manual**, the **Head, Finance and Accounts** ensures the PERC is kept under strict security — always under lock and key in a fire-resistant cabinet, safe or strong room when not in use. Every officer must complete a Personal Emolument Record form annually, certified by the head of department or other designated officer; the completed form is the basis for opening the group registers, which must be **audited before the officer is included in the payroll**."},{"h4":"Action by the salaries section on payroll"},{"ol":["Effect necessary entries in each employee's Personnel Emolument Record — basic salary for the month, deductions and net salary payable.","Aggregate all such entries and raise **Treasury Form 209** (advice of deduction from salary) for deductions and **TF 1** (other charges payment vouchers) for on-payment vouchers in favour of the relevant beneficiaries.","Classify TF 209 and TF 1 to the appropriate **below-the-line** classification code.","Raise a **summary payment voucher** on a pay-point basis, capturing gross salaries and allowances due, total deductions and net amount payable.","Process the **cash-order form** in accordance with the Financial Regulations.","Effect the corresponding entries in the Personnel Emolument Voucher, register and vote book.","Carry out an **internal check** of payroll entries and vouchers, taking account of staff vacating post during the month, to ensure accurate postings.","Compare and reconcile the salaries-section figures with the **variation control sheet** for the month.","Once the officer controlling expenditure agrees the figures with the variation control sheet, he signs all summary vouchers and TF 209.","Forward the payrolls, with summary vouchers, TF 209 and on-payment vouchers, to the **Internal Audit Unit**."]},{"h4":"Action by the Internal Audit Unit"},{"ol":["Ascertain the **accuracy** of entries in officers' Personnel Emolument Records and the payroll, examining them in detail so that any variation in emoluments is correctly stated.","Examine all summary vouchers and supporting documents for accuracy of deductions and net salaries payable.","Ensure gross salaries in the summary vouchers **agree with the variation control sheets**.","Extract copies of summary vouchers and other supporting documents for record purposes."]},{"h4":"Action by the cash office"},{"ol":["Check the payroll summary voucher and supporting documents against the Internal Audit Unit's forwarding schedule for completeness.","Sort and allocate a **Treasury Payment Voucher number** to summary vouchers, on-payment vouchers and the treasury receipt vouchers for TF 209.","Stamp all summary vouchers and on-payment vouchers **\"PAID\"** for the month of account.","Draw cheques for the net amount payable in the summary payment voucher, in favour of the paying bank.","Draw cheques for the amounts due to the various agencies shown in the on-payment vouchers.","Post all payment vouchers and treasury receipt vouchers separately in a **salary cash book**, balance it and reconcile with the bank statement monthly."]},{"key":"The chain of custody matters more than any single step: salaries prepares and raises the vouchers, Internal Audit independently checks them against the variation control sheet **before** payment, and the cash office pays and reconciles. Collapsing these into one office removes the segregation of duties that is the whole point of the procedure."}]},{"n":"8.8","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["One of the following is responsible to take custody of Personnel Emoluments Records Cards (PERC) where the accounting system is manual (A) Revenue Collector (B) Imprest holder  (C) Accountant-General  (D) Head of Finance and Account  (E) Cashier","One of the following must approve the procedure and method to be employed by federal ministries, departments and units for the recording and the calculation of personal emoluments (A) Minister of Finance  (B) Budget Officer of the Federation  (C) Accounting Officer  (D) Accountant-General  (E) National Assembly","The standard Personal Emoluments Record shall show the following with the exception of one (A) Standard rate of pay of the officer  (B) Incremental rate  (C) Next-of-kin of the officer  (D) Salary and other advances issued  (E) Tax, PAYE","Adjustment voucher is used in any of the following circumstances with the exception of one (A) Payment for inter-ministerial services  (B) Payment for on-going projects  (C) Correction of accounting errors arising from misclassification  (D) Ultimate allocation of unallocated inventories  (E) Carrying out adjustments or transfers between accounts","One of the following is not a component of receipt voucher (A) NCOA code  (B) Commitment balance  (C) Received from  (D) Date  (E) Receipt number","According to Financial Regulation 605 of January 2009, a payment voucher register shall be operated by …","Documentary evidence of payment or receipt of money which is available for future reference, accounting and auditing purposes is called …","A process used for effecting transfers from one account to another without involving physical movement of cash is called …","The office responsible for ascertaining the accuracy of entries in the Personnel Emolument Records of officers and the payroll, by examining them in detail to ensure that variations, if any, in the emolument of staff are correctly stated, is called …","Before any receipt into the Government purse can be regarded as an authentic receipt voucher, the attached Treasury Receipt Book 6 must be supported with …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** D  **2.** D  **3.** C  **4.** B  **5.** B  **6.** Officer Controlling Vote  **7.** Voucher  **8.** Journal entries  **9.** Internal Audit Unit  **10.** Treasury Form 15 (Pay-in form)"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — essential features of a payment voucher","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A valid payment voucher must contain the full particulars of services rendered or goods purchased. List any ten (10) essential features of a well-prepared payment voucher."}],"a":[{"ol":["Date of the voucher, which indicates its life span.","Classification code according to the National Chart of Accounts (NCOA) of expenditure.","Amount in words and figures.","Voucher number.","Description of payment.","Name and address of payee/beneficiary.","Supporting documents — local purchase orders, invoices, store receipt vouchers and contract agreements.","Authority, such as the signature of the officer controlling expenditure and the warrant which released the money.","Signature of the cashier.","Signature of the payee.","Voucher certification — prepared by, checked by, entered into vote book by, paid by and authorised by.","Cashier's stamp \"PAID\"."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — procedure where a payment voucher is lost","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State the procedures that should be followed where a payment voucher is reported lost, and the action to be taken by the cash office."}],"a":[{"ol":["Prompt investigation should be carried out.","It should be established whether payment has been made on it or not.","It should be ascertained whether or not the cash drawn is still on hand.","A report should be made by the accounting officer to the Accountant-General, stating the circumstances of the loss."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — accounting officers' responsibilities under the Public Procurement Act","open":true,"q":[{"p":"In compliance with their special role under the Public Procurement Act, state the responsibilities that accounting officers of ministries, extra-ministerial offices and other arms of government are charged to comply with."}],"a":[{"ol":["Ensure adequate appropriation is available for procurements in the annual budget.","Integrate the entity's procurement expenditure into its yearly budget.","Ensure the establishment of a procurement planning committee over whose activities they preside.","Constitute a Procurement Evaluation Committee for the efficient evaluation of tenders.","Constitute a Procurement Committee.","Render annual returns of procurement records to the Bureau of Public Procurement.","Liaise with the Bureau of Public Procurement to ensure implementation of its regulations.","Ensure compliance with the Public Procurement Act by their organisations — failing which they are personally liable for any breach, whether caused by them, their subordinates, or anyone to whom they delegated the responsibility."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 4–9 — recording discoveries of loss and abandonment","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The following were discoveries made by the Accountant-General of Ogudu State while checking the books of account of the State. Record each using journal entries."},{"ol":["On 31 May 2019, a double payment of N1,950,000 was made to Sango Plc for a contract executed on maintenance of an office complex. The discovery was made in December of the same year.","A fraudulent withdrawal of N950,000 from the account of the State Emergency Relief Agency was made in August 2019 and discovered in December of the same year.","The sum of N1,300,000 initially charged to the Motor Vehicle Advance Account had been abandoned.","The sum of N3,500,000 revenue that should have been collected by the government had been abandoned.","An erroneous overpayment of N76,000 was made in 2018, charged to the CRF, but was discovered in 2019."]}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Journal entries — 31 December 2019","align":"lrr","head":["Account","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Cash account","1,950,000",""],["**Sango Plc** — refund of overpayment made 31 May 2019","","1,950,000"],["Cash account","950,000",""],["**Emergency Relief Agency** — refund of fraudulent withdrawal","","950,000"],["Bad debt account","1,300,000",""],["**Motor vehicle advances account** — abandonment of charge","","1,300,000"],["Bad debt account","3,500,000",""],["**Revenue receivable account** — revenue now abandoned","","3,500,000"],["Cash account","76,000",""],["**CRF account** — erroneous overpayment recovered","","76,000"]]}}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One or two Section A marks most diets on the types of voucher or the contents of a payment voucher. In Section B it appears as \"state the contents of a payment voucher and the procedure where one is lost\", which is straightforward recall. Learn the contents as a numbered list of about ten and the loss procedure as six steps.","errors":["Confusing an adjustment voucher (corrects a misclassification) with a journal voucher (records a non-cash transaction).","Omitting the head, subhead and economic code from the contents of a payment voucher — the classification is the point of the document.","Saying a lost voucher is simply replaced by a photocopy, without certification or report.","Failing to mention that destruction of vouchers requires the Auditor-General's written approval.","Listing only payment and receipt vouchers when four types are examinable."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A voucher used to correct an expenditure charged to the wrong head or subhead is a","o":["payment voucher","adjustment voucher","receipt voucher","journal voucher","duplicate voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The adjustment voucher moves the charge from the head wrongly debited to the correct one. It does not create or discharge a liability.","src":"Chapter 8.2","sec":"8.2"},{"q":"Vouchers may not be destroyed without the written approval of the","o":["Accountant-General","Auditor-General","Minister of Finance","accounting officer","internal auditor"],"a":1,"w":"Destruction would remove audit evidence, so it requires the approval of the officer who must rely on it.","src":"Chapter 8.6","sec":"8.6"},{"q":"A voucher recording a transaction that does not involve any movement of cash — such as depreciation or a transfer between funds — is a","o":["receipt voucher","journal voucher","adjustment voucher","payment voucher","store receipt voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The journal voucher records non-cash entries; the adjustment voucher is the special case of correcting a misclassification.","src":"Chapter 8.2","sec":"8.2"},{"q":"Immediately a payment voucher is settled it should be","o":["filed unmarked","stamped \"PAID\" and dated","returned to the payee","entered in the Register of Losses","sent to the Auditor-General"],"a":1,"w":"Stamping prevents the same voucher being presented a second time for payment.","src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"},{"q":"Where a paid voucher is lost, the duplicate obtained must be certified by the","o":["payee","accounting officer","internal auditor","Auditor-General","Accountant-General"],"a":1,"w":"The accounting officer certifies that the original is lost, that the payment was properly made and that no double payment has occurred, and reports the loss to both the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General.","src":"Chapter 8.6","sec":"8.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Define a voucher and state five purposes it serves in government accounting. (b) List ten items that a properly completed payment voucher should contain. (c) Describe the procedure to be followed where a paid voucher is lost.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Definition and purposes"},{"p":"A voucher is a document evidencing a transaction and authorising its entry in the books of account. Every payment out of public funds and every receipt into them must be supported by a voucher, and no entry may be made in the accounts without one."},{"p":"**Purposes**"},{"ol":["**Evidence.** It proves that the transaction took place and that value was received for the money paid.","**Authorisation.** It carries the signature of the officer empowered to approve the payment, so that no money leaves the treasury without proper authority.","**Classification.** It states the head, subhead and economic code to which the transaction is chargeable, ensuring the correct vote is charged and the accounts correctly analysed.","**Audit trail.** It links the entry in the cash book and ledger to the underlying invoice, order, store receipt and certificate of completion, so that an auditor can trace a figure to its source.","**Fixing responsibility.** The officers who prepared, checked, certified, authorised and paid are each identified on the face of the voucher, so that liability can be established if the payment proves improper.","**Budgetary control.** The voucher is the instrument by which the charge reaches the vote book, where it reduces the uncommitted balance."]},{"h4":"(b) Contents of a payment voucher"},{"ol":["Serial number and date of the voucher.","Name of the ministry, department or agency, and the station.","The head, subhead and economic code to which the expenditure is chargeable.","Name and address of the payee, with bank account details where payment is by transfer.","Full particulars of the goods or services — description, quantity, rate and period covered — sufficient for the charge to be verified without reference to other documents.","The amount, in both figures and words.","A list of the supporting documents attached: local purchase order, invoice, store receipt voucher, certificate of job completion, contract agreement or nominal roll.","The certificate of the checking officer as to arithmetical accuracy and correct classification.","The certificate of the accounting officer, or an officer authorised on his behalf, that the service has been performed, the rates are correct and the charge is a proper charge on the vote.","The authority for the payment — warrant, contract, approval or regulation relied upon.","The payee's receipt or acknowledgement of the money.","The \"PAID\" stamp with the date of payment."]},{"h4":"(c) Procedure on loss of a paid voucher"},{"p":"The loss of a paid voucher removes the evidence supporting a payment already made out of public funds, and creates the risk that the same claim may be presented again. The Financial Regulations therefore prescribe a formal procedure."},{"ol":["**Immediate report.** The officer discovering the loss reports it at once to the accounting officer, who in turn reports it to the Accountant-General of the Federation and to the Auditor-General for the Federation.","**Search and investigation.** A thorough search is conducted and the circumstances of the loss investigated, to establish whether it arose from negligence, from a defect in the filing system, or from an attempt to conceal an irregularity.","**Obtain a duplicate.** A duplicate is obtained from the payee, from the departmental file copy, or reconstructed from the cash book, vote book and supporting documents. It is marked clearly as a certified true copy of the original.","**Certification.** The accounting officer certifies on the duplicate that the original has been lost, that the payment was properly made and the service performed, that the charge was correctly classified, and that to the best of his knowledge and belief no double payment has been or will be made.","**Certificate of non-payment.** Where appropriate, confirmation is obtained from the payee and from the bank that the sum has been received once only.","**Record and sanction.** The loss is recorded in the Register of Losses with full particulars. Where negligence on the part of an officer is established, he may be surcharged with any resulting loss and is liable to disciplinary action; where fraud is suspected the matter is referred for investigation and possible prosecution."]},{"note":"The reason for this weight of procedure is that a missing voucher is the classic cover for a fraudulent duplicate payment. The requirement to report to the Auditor-General ensures the matter is examined by someone outside the department in which the loss occurred."}],"src":"Chapter 8.3, 8.6","sec":"8.3"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Sources of Government Revenue","brief":"Tax and non-tax revenue, the Federation Account and the principles on which it is shared, the derivation principle, the distribution of value added tax, and internally generated revenue at state and local level.","outcomes":["Distinguish tax from non-tax revenue and give examples of each","Distinguish statutory allocation from internally generated revenue","Explain the operation of the Federation Account","State the vertical and horizontal principles of revenue allocation","Explain the 13% derivation principle and the distribution of VAT","Suggest ways of improving internally generated revenue"],"secs":[{"n":"9.1","t":"Classification of government revenue","b":[{"h4":"Tax revenue"},{"p":"A **tax** is a compulsory levy imposed by government on individuals and organisations, for which no direct or proportionate benefit is received in return."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Tax","Collected by","Basis"],"rows":[["**Petroleum Profits Tax / Hydrocarbon Tax**","FIRS","Profits of companies engaged in petroleum operations"],["**Companies Income Tax**","FIRS","Profits of registered companies"],["**Personal Income Tax**","State Boards of Internal Revenue (FIRS for the Armed Forces, police, Foreign Affairs and FCT residents)","Income of individuals, by residence"],["**Value Added Tax**","FIRS","Consumption of goods and services"],["**Customs and Excise Duties**","Nigeria Customs Service","Imports, exports and excisable goods manufactured locally"],["**Capital Gains Tax**","FIRS / State Boards","Gains on disposal of chargeable assets"],["**Stamp Duties**","FIRS / State Boards","Instruments and certain transactions"],["**Education Tax (TETFund levy)**","FIRS","Assessable profits of companies"]]}},{"h4":"Non-tax revenue"},{"ul":["**Royalties** on crude oil, gas and solid minerals.","**Licences and fees** — drivers' licences, business premises registration, radio and television licences, court fees.","**Fines and penalties** imposed by courts and regulators.","**Earnings and sales** — proceeds of government commercial activities, hospital and school fees, sale of government publications and scrap.","**Rent** on government property and land.","**Interest and repayments** on loans granted by government.","**Dividends and operating surpluses** from government investments and from scheduled corporations under the Fiscal Responsibility Act.","**Grants and aid** from foreign governments and international agencies.","**Borrowing** — internal (treasury bills, bonds) and external — which is a source of **financing** rather than revenue proper, since it must be repaid."]},{"warn":"Borrowing is often listed by candidates as a source of revenue. It is more precisely a source of **financing**: it creates a liability and does not increase net assets. Saying so explicitly is worth a mark in a question on classification."},{"h4":"By tier of government"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Nature"],"rows":[["**Statutory allocation**","The share of the Federation Account distributed to a tier of government under the Allocation of Revenue Act"],["**Internally generated revenue (IGR)**","Revenue a state or local government raises within its own jurisdiction — personal income tax, licences, fees, fines, rates, rents, earnings"],["**Value added tax allocation**","The share of the VAT pool distributed to the tier"],["**Grants**","From a higher tier or from a donor, often for a specified purpose (matching grants for capital projects)"],["**Excess crude / augmentation**","Distributions from savings of revenue above the budget benchmark"]]}}]},{"n":"9.2","t":"The Federation Account","b":[{"p":"Section 162(1) of the 1999 Constitution provides that the Federation shall maintain a special account called **the Federation Account** into which shall be paid all revenue collected by the Government of the Federation, with the exception of the proceeds of personal income tax of the personnel of the Armed Forces, the Nigeria Police Force, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and residents of the Federal Capital Territory."},{"h4":"Operation"},{"ol":["Revenue-collecting agencies — the Federal Inland Revenue Service, the Nigeria Customs Service, the Nigerian Upstream Petroleum Regulatory Commission and NNPC — pay collections into the Federation Account.","First-line charges are deducted: the cost of collection of the collecting agencies, and certain other statutory deductions.","The **Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)** meets monthly, reviews the inflows, and determines the amount available for distribution.","The balance is distributed among the three tiers on the **vertical** formula, and among the states and among the local governments on the **horizontal** formula.","The Federal share is paid into the **Consolidated Revenue Fund**; each state's share into its own consolidated revenue fund; and the local government share into the **State Joint Local Government Account** for distribution to the councils."]},{"h4":"Vertical allocation (sharing between tiers)"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tier","Share of the Federation Account"],"rows":[["Federal Government","52.68%"],["State Governments","26.72%"],["Local Governments","20.60%"],["Total","100.00%"]]}},{"p":"These are the percentages in operation following the Supreme Court judgment of 2002 and the subsequent modification orders. The formula is subject to periodic review by the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC), whose recommendations require an Act of the National Assembly."},{"h4":"Horizontal allocation (sharing among states, and among local governments)"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Principle","Weight"],"rows":[["Equality of states","40%"],["Population","30%"],["Internal revenue generation effort","10%"],["Land mass and terrain","10%"],["Social development factor (education, health, water)","10%"],["Total","100%"]]}},{"key":"The **derivation principle** operates separately and before the horizontal formula. Section 162(2) requires that not less than **13% of the revenue accruing to the Federation Account directly from any natural resource** be paid to the state from which it was derived, as a first charge. Only the balance goes into the pool shared on the formula above."}]},{"n":"9.3","t":"Value added tax","b":[{"p":"VAT proceeds are not paid into the Federation Account but into a **separate VAT Pool Account**, and are distributed on a different formula."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tier","Share of the VAT pool"],"rows":[["Federal Government","15%"],["State Governments","50%"],["Local Governments","35%"],["Total","100%"]]}},{"p":"The state and local government shares are then distributed among them on the basis of equality (50%), population (30%) and derivation (20%)."},{"note":"The distribution of VAT has been the subject of litigation between the Federal Government and several states, which have argued that consumption tax is a residual matter within the legislative competence of the states. Candidates need only know that the dispute exists and that the formula above is the one currently operated."}]},{"n":"9.4","t":"Internally generated revenue","b":[{"p":"Heavy dependence on statutory allocation, and therefore on the oil price, is the principal structural weakness of state and local government finance in Nigeria. Improving internally generated revenue is a standard Section B question."},{"h4":"Problems of internally generated revenue"},{"ul":["A **narrow revenue base**, with much economic activity in the informal sector and outside any register.","**Poor records** — no reliable database of taxpayers, properties, vehicles or businesses.","**Leakage and diversion**, where collections are received in cash by officials and never reach the treasury.","**Multiple taxation** by overlapping authorities, which discourages compliance and drives businesses out of the formal sector.","**Weak enforcement** and reluctance to prosecute defaulters, particularly where they are politically connected.","**Public resistance**, arising from the perception that taxes are not translated into services.","**Untrained and poorly motivated staff**, and inadequate revenue offices and equipment."]},{"h4":"Ways of improving internally generated revenue"},{"ol":["**Broaden the base** by identifying and registering informal-sector operators, presumptive taxation of small businesses, and enumeration of properties for tenement rating.","**Automate collection** — electronic payment platforms, a single revenue account, and the elimination of cash handling by collectors. This is the single most effective measure, because it removes the opportunity rather than relying on integrity.","**Build a taxpayer database** and integrate it with other registers — the corporate registry, land registry, vehicle registration and the national identity number.","**Harmonise levies** to eliminate multiple taxation, publish an approved list of taxes and levies, and prohibit collection outside it.","**Strengthen enforcement** with adequate legal powers, tax tribunals, and the publication of the names of persistent defaulters.","**Train and motivate revenue staff**, with performance-based incentives tied to collection rather than to assessment.","**Improve taxpayer education and service delivery**, since compliance rises when taxpayers can see what the money purchases.","**Reform the revenue authority** into an autonomous, professionally managed board, as the states with the highest IGR have done.","**Block leakages** through internal audit, revenue monitoring and reconciliation between assessments raised and amounts collected."]}]},{"n":"9.5","t":"Fund accounting: classification and types of fund","b":[{"def":{"t":"Fund","d":"a separate fiscal and accounting entity in which resources are held, governed by special regulations, kept apart from other funds, and established for a specific purpose. For stewardship, government income is categorised into a series of such funds, each catering for a specific activity."}},{"h4":"Classification of funds"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Government funds**","Accrue resources derived from the general tax and revenue powers of government — e.g. debt service fund, special fund, revolving fund"],["**Proprietary funds**","Account for resources derived from the business activities of government and its agencies, such as parastatals"],["**Fiduciary funds**","Account for resources held and managed by government as custodian or trustee — e.g. Petroleum Technology Development Fund (PTDF), Trust and Agency Fund, Pension Trust Fund"]]}},{"h4":"Types of fund (Finance (Control and Management) Act, 1958)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Fund","What it is"],"rows":[["**General fund**","For general administration and services — this **is** the Consolidated Revenue Fund (s.5 of the Act)"],["**Capital project fund**","For acquisition of capital assets/facilities — this is the **Development Fund** (s.18)"],["**Special fund**","Created for a specific purpose, e.g. South African Relief Fund, African Staff Housing Scheme Fund"],["**Trust fund**","Resources held as trustee, used only for the purpose in the trust deed — e.g. PTDF, Research Foundation Fund"],["**Contingency fund**","For expenditure of uncertain amount and timing — e.g. natural disaster relief (s.15)"],["**Inter-governmental service fund**","Provides a service to other funds — e.g. the Government Clearance Fund, which nets transactions between the Federal and State Governments"],["**Revolving (working capital) fund**","Finances a service one unit provides to other departments — e.g. a Revolving Loan Fund"],["**Self-liquidating fund**","Resources transferred in periodically; any balance left is transferred to a current fund — e.g. a Deposit Fund holding money for third parties"]]}}]},{"n":"9.6","t":"Revenue collection agencies in Nigeria","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Agency","Regulates / collects","Key point"],"rows":[["**NNPC Limited (NNPCL)**","Foreign and domestic sales of crude oil and gas, PSC profit oil/gas, sale of refined products","Incorporated under CAMA per the Petroleum Industry Act (PIA) 2021, shares held by Ministry of Finance Inc. and Ministry of Petroleum Inc. on behalf of the FGN; operates on a commercial basis"],["**Nigerian Upstream Petroleum Regulatory Commission (NUPRC)**","Royalties, gas-flare penalties, rents on oil fields, signature bonuses","Regulator for all upstream operations (technical, operational, commercial); inherited the assets/functions of the old DPR upstream side"],["**Nigerian Midstream and Downstream Petroleum Regulatory Authority (NMDPRA)**","Licensing and pricing in midstream/downstream operations","Absorbed DPR (downstream), PPPRA and the Petroleum Equalisation Fund; sets the Domestic Supply Obligation (DSO)"],["**Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)** — formerly FIRS","Companies Income Tax, Hydrocarbon/Petroleum Profits Tax, VAT, and PAYE/Capital Gains/Stamp Duty of Armed Forces, Police, Foreign Affairs staff and FCT residents","Renamed and re-established under the 2025 tax reform Acts"],["**State Board of Internal Revenue Service (SBIRS)**","Personal income tax of individuals in the state (other than the FIRS/NRS-collected categories), plus the state's share of Capital Gains Tax and Stamp Duty","Established under the Personal Income Tax Act 1993; liaises with the Joint Tax Board on policy and reform"],["**Nigeria Customs Service (NCS)**","Import/export/excise duties","Core functions: revenue collection and suppression of smuggling. Also supports combating illicit trade, IP infringement, arms/drug trafficking and money laundering at the border"]]}},{"note":"The **Nigeria Customs Service Act 2023** changed how Customs itself is funded: rather than a share of the Federation Account, it now retains a **4% free-on-board (FOB) levy** on the value of imports collected at the ports for its own operations, and FAAC stopped the old 7% cost-of-collection deduction from gross Federation Account revenue in January 2026."}]},{"n":"9.7","t":"Bodies governing Federation Account distribution","b":[{"def":{"t":"Federation Accounts Allocation Committee (FAAC)","d":"set up under the Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account etc.) Act to deliberate on and allocate funds from the Federation Account to the three tiers of government. It meets in two sessions:"}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Session","Chair / key members / role"],"rows":[["**Technical session**","Chaired by the **Accountant-General of the Federation**; States' Accountants-General and representatives of NNPC, NRS, NCS, DPR, RMAFC, Federal Ministry of Finance, CBN, National Planning Commission and others. *Role:* considers the collecting agencies' returns, deliberates on the revenue available, and recommends the distribution to the plenary session"],["**Plenary session**","Chaired by the **Minister of Finance**; States' Commissioners of Finance, the Accountants-General (Federal and State), and the same agency representatives. *Role:* ensures allocations are promptly and fully paid into each tier's treasury, and reports annually to the National Assembly"]]}},{"def":{"t":"State Joint Local Government Account Allocation Committee (SJLGAAC)","d":"ensures the local government share of the Federation Account, together with 10% of the state's own internally generated revenue, is paid promptly into the **State Joint Local Government Account** and shared to the councils on criteria of equality, population, primary school enrolment and internally generated revenue. Chaired by the state Commissioner for Local Government, with council chairmen, two representatives of the Accountant-General of the Federation, a representative of the state Accountant-General and two persons appointed by the Governor."}},{"warn":"Following the **Supreme Court judgment of 11 July 2024**, local government allocations from the Federation Account are now paid **directly** into each local government's own account rather than routed through the SJLGAAC — a recent, examinable change to what was previously the standard procedure."},{"def":{"t":"Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (JRB)","d":"a new body under the **Joint Revenue Board (Establishment) Act 2025** (part of the 2025 tax reform), coordinating tax policy and administration across federal, state and local government. Chaired by the Executive Chairman of the NRS, with the Finance and Planning Ministers, the Attorney-General, the CBN Governor, State IRS chairmen, a local-government representative and two private-sector members; a Federal Ministry of Finance representative is Secretary. Members serve part-time for a renewable 4-year term."}},{"ul":["**Standard-setting** — model guidelines for assessment, collection and audit that states and LGAs adopt, so taxpayers face uniform rules nationwide.","**Dispute resolution** — binding decisions where two states claim the same taxpayer or disagree on a revenue share, replacing litigation.","**Taxpayer database** — runs the unified national TIN system, blocking duplicate registrations and tracking cross-border taxpayers.","**Policy review** — fiscal-impact assessment of any new levy or tax waiver before it is introduced.","**Redress** — routes taxpayer grievances through the Tax Appeal Tribunal and an Ombudsman."]},{"def":{"t":"Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC)","d":"established by the RMAFC Act 1989; composed of a chairman and one member from each state and the FCT, appointed by the President."}},{"ol":["Monitors accruals to, and disbursement from, the Federation Account.","Reviews the **revenue allocation formula** from time to time to keep it in line with changing realities.","Advises the three tiers on fiscal efficiency and how to raise more revenue.","Determines the remuneration of holders of political and public offices.","Recommends the formula for distributing the Federation Account and Local Government Accounts.","Is a statutory member of FAAC, the Local Government Joint Account Allocation Committee, the JRB, the Niger-Delta Development Commission and the Ecological Fund Commission.","Has power to demand data from NNPC, NCS, NRS, the CBN and the Federal Ministry of Finance."]}]},{"n":"9.8","t":"The Consolidated Revenue Fund, Development Fund and Contingency Fund","b":[{"note":"[Read the full study guide on the fund system →](guides/crf.html) — sources of CRF income, what it finances, the full warrant system, how the Development and Contingency Funds fit in, Ghana's parallel system with its own Contingency Fund / Contingency **Reserve** Fund trap, and every past exam question on the topic with answers, all in one page."},{"def":{"t":"Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF)","d":"established by section 80 of the 1999 Constitution. Every revenue item not specifically designated to another fund is paid into the CRF."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["CRF receipts",""],"rows":[["Direct allocation from the Federation Account","At the Federal share of the prevailing vertical formula"],["Direct taxes","PAYE of Armed Forces, Police and Foreign Service personnel"],["Licences and internal revenue","Arms/ammunition, goldsmith, gold-dealer, radio/TV licence fees"],["Mining","Mining fees, rent of crown lands, royalties on gold, tin, iron ore, coal"],["Fees","Court fees, court fines, medical fees"],["Earnings and sales","Sale of inventories/publications/stamps, money-order commission"],["Rent of government property","Government quarters, land and buildings"],["Interest and repayments (general/State)","On loans the Federal Government granted to individuals, corporations, government companies and State Governments"],["Armed Forces sales","Old vehicles and stores"],["Reimbursements","Refunds for services rendered by Federal officers to states, local governments and statutory bodies — e.g. audit fees, overpayment refunds"],["Miscellaneous","Overpayments refunded, lapsed deposits"]]}},{"p":"Charges against the CRF fall into three groups: **(1)** every recurrent expenditure head in the approved estimates (personnel cost, overhead cost, debt service); **(2)** salaries and consolidated allowances of **statutory officers**, chargeable directly regardless of budget approval — the Police Service Commission, Public Complaints Commission, Public Service Commission, Auditor-General for the Federation, Nigerian Law Reform Commission, INEC, the President and Justices of the Court of Appeal, and the Chief Judge/Justices of the Federal High Court and Supreme Court; and **(3)** pension and gratuity of statutory and non-statutory officers, including the Armed Forces."},{"def":{"t":"Development Fund (DF)","d":"created by section 25 of the Finance (Control and Management) Act 1958 and entrenched in the 1999 Constitution, to finance **capital development projects**."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Sources","Charges"],"rows":[["Yearly contribution from the CRF","Capital expenditure — roads, bridges, dams"],["External grants","General administration capital items — barracks, staff houses, vehicles, hospitals"],["External loans (e.g. IMF)","External financial obligations — aid to other countries"],["Internal loans — development stocks (long-term), Treasury Certificates (2-year), Treasury Bills (91-day)","Loans made to State Governments for development"]]}},{"def":{"t":"Contingency Fund (CF)","d":"authorised by section 83 of the 1999 Constitution (s.81 of the 1979/1989 Constitutions), fed from the CRF, to meet **unforeseen, urgent expenditure** — e.g. a natural disaster — where virement is impossible and the need cannot wait for a Supplementary Appropriation Act. Any advance from it is replaced by a supplementary estimate as soon as possible."}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 9.1 — Federation Account and Consolidated Revenue Fund","q":[{"p":"For a month, inflows were: import duties ₦400,000; export duties ₦300,000; excise duties ₦200,000; petroleum profits tax ₦80,000,000; companies income tax ₦71,000,000; PAYE — Armed Forces ₦400,000, Police ₦30,000, Abuja residents ₦20,000; dividend from FGN investments ₦120,000. Outflows: remuneration of statutory officers ₦13,800,000; recurrent expenditure ₦1,500,000; transfer to Development Fund ₦2,500,000; transfer to Contingency Fund ₦20,000. The vertical formula is Federal 52.68%, State 26.72%, Local 20.60%. Prepare the Federation Account and the Consolidated Revenue Fund."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Federation Account for the month","align":"lr","head":["","₦"],"rows":[["Import duties","400,000"],["Export duties","300,000"],["Excise duties","200,000"],["Petroleum profits tax","80,000,000"],["Companies income tax","71,000,000"],["**Total income**","**151,900,000**"],["Federal Govt. 52.68%","80,020,920"],["State Govts. 26.72%","40,587,680"],["Local Govts. 20.60%","31,291,400"],["**Total distributed**","**151,900,000**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"Consolidated Revenue Fund for the month","align":"lr","head":["","₦"],"rows":[["PAYE — Armed Forces","400,000"],["PAYE — Police","30,000"],["Dividends from FGN investments","120,000"],["Share from Federation Account","80,020,920"],["**Total income**","**80,570,920**"],["Less: Remuneration of statutory officers","(13,800,000)"],["Less: Recurrent expenditure","(1,500,000)"],["Less: Transfer to Development Fund","(2,500,000)"],["Less: Transfer to Contingency Fund","(20,000)"],["**Balance carried forward**","**62,750,920**"]]}}]}}]},{"n":"9.9","t":"Sources of central, regional and local government finance (Ghana)","b":[{"p":"Ghana operates a **unitary**, not federal, system, so government accounts there are unitary accounts, with accountability run through the **public accounts**."},{"h4":"Public money"},{"ul":["**Tax revenue** — direct tax (income tax, corporate tax, paid directly by the person who bears it) and indirect tax (petroleum tax, VAT, import/export duties, borne by the final buyer).","**Non-tax revenue** — everything else government generates, including fines and penalties.","**Internally generated revenue (IGR / \"user fees\")** — passport fees, vehicle examination/licensing fees, product-testing fees, university facility-user fees."]},{"h4":"Special funds account"},{"p":"Money earmarked by law or the Constitution (Article 174(2)) for a specific purpose, kept outside the Consolidated Fund and reported separately under the **Public Financial Management Act (Act 921)** — e.g. the **Road Fund** (a fuel cess), the **Ghana Education Trust Fund (GETFund)**, and the **National Health Insurance Levy (NHIL)** managed by the NHIA."},{"h4":"District Assembly revenue"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Examples"],"rows":[["**Central government revenue** (to districts)","Grants-in-aid (foreign donations routed via central government); recurrent transfers (staff running costs); ceded revenue (casino, betting/advertisement tax, entertainment duty)"],["**Locally generated revenue**","Rates/levies on animals; taxes on the self-employed; investment interest; trading profits; vehicle-licence collections; loans (up to ¢20m, with Ministerial approval); local/foreign grants"],["**Loans and grants**","Local loans via the central bank (Treasury bills/bonds); foreign loans (IMF, World Bank). Grants are **specific** (a named project) or **general/\"budget support\"** (discretionary; pooled donor funding is called \"multi-donor budget support\", MDBS)"],["**Other public money**","Government loans/advances repaid, dividends from profit-making state organisations, interest earnings, sale of securities, equity or property"],["**Trust money**","Held by government for others, e.g. a superannuation fund held in trust for employees until retirement"]]}},{"h4":"Revenue estimation (FAR s.159)"},{"p":"A head of department estimating the year's revenue must: identify all activities already generating revenue; identify those with the *potential* to; estimate their frequency and the revenue arising; and produce a monthly forecast of when the revenue will flow in — checked against how past estimates compared to actual collections."},{"h4":"Public and departmental accounts"},{"p":"The **public accounts** (FAA ss.40–41, FAR reg.191), prepared and kept by the **Controller and Accountant-General**, comprise: the Consolidated Fund's assets and liabilities at year end; a summary of receipts and payments against budget; revenue and expenditure against the approved estimate; and an analysis of public debt, deposits/trust money, government securities, advances, loans, equity investments and a cash flow statement. Each MDA (a \"department\" under the FAA) separately prepares monthly/annual **departmental accounts** — balance sheet, revenue and expenditure statement, cash flow statement and performance notes."},{"h4":"Ghana's public funds (Article 175, Constitution; s.5, FAA)"},{"p":"Public funds comprise the **Consolidated Fund**, the **Contingency Fund**, and any other fund Parliament establishes by Act — e.g. the Road Fund (1997), GETFund (2000), District Assemblies Common Fund (1993), Ghana Investment Fund (2002), Venture Capital Trust Fund (2004), the Business Assistance Fund, the HIPC Fund, and the Social Investment Fund. \"Public funds\" is the generic, umbrella term for all of these together — every fund below is a *subset* of it, not a rival to it."},{"warn":"Two of the funds below share almost the same name and are easy to mix up on an exam: the **Contingency Fund** is the constitutional fund (Article 177 in Ghana; s.83/s.123 in Nigeria) that Parliament/the National Assembly funds and controls, replenished by a **supplementary estimate** after each advance. The **Contingency *Reserve* Fund** is a completely different thing — a slice of the **Executive's own already-approved budget**, set aside internally as a first line of relief, with no Parliamentary supplementary estimate involved at all."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Fund","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Consolidated Fund**","All central-government money except that legally payable into another fund or retained by a department/agency"],["**Contingency Fund**","Unbudgeted, urgent, unforeseen expenditure (Article 177); advances are replaced by a supplementary estimate to Parliament"],["**Contingency Reserve Fund**","A set-aside share of approved appropriation, held by the Executive as a first line of relief before a supplementary request or virement"],["**District Assemblies' Common Fund (DACF)**","At least **5%** of Ghana's total annual revenue, paid quarterly, for district development projects; overseen by a DACF Administrator; each district holds a separate Common Fund Account"],["**National loans fund / Debt service (sinking) fund**","Receives contracted loans and services their principal/interest — an alternative to holding loans in the Consolidated Fund"],["**Trust and Agency Fund**","Money held as trustee/agent for other bodies — e.g. peacekeeping rewards for the armed forces"],["**Counterpart fund**","Supports projects financed by a foreign donation, matched with a government contribution"],["**Intra-governmental service fund**","A unit supplying a service (e.g. stationery) to other departments"],["**Revolving (working capital) fund**","Proceeds of what it finances are paid back in to fund the next cycle — e.g. Ghana Education Service textbook sales to students"],["**Local government fund / DACF**","For the social and economic development of metropolises, municipalities and districts"],["**Asset renewal fund**","Replaces local-government equipment — harvesters, tractors, bulldozers"],["**Stabilisation (revenue) fund**","Set aside against a future fall in revenue"],["**Special fund**","For a named purpose — Education Fund, National Housing Fund, SMEF, Agricultural Development Fund"],["**Self-liquidating fund**","Balance left after periodic transfers moves to a current fund — e.g. a Deposit Fund"]]}},{"h4":"Advantages and disadvantages of fund accounting"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Ensures financial control — a fund states expressly what its money is for","No information on debtors/creditors; it is cash accounting"],["Highlights government policy","Assets are not capitalised — written off in the year of purchase"],["Facilitates coordination and planning of operations","Effective control across many funds is difficult, and consolidation of government accounts is harder; often managed by under-skilled staff"]]}},{"h4":"Entries of fund accounting"},{"ol":["Dr Bank; Cr Fund — proceeds realised on creating the fund.","Dr Fund Investment; Cr Bank — value of an investment purchased with the fund.","Dr Fund Investment Disposal; Cr Fund Investment — value of an investment disposed of.","Dr Bank; Cr Fund Investment Disposal — proceeds on disposal.","Dr Fund Investment Disposal; Cr Fund — profit realised on disposal.","Dr Fund; Cr Fund Investment Disposal — loss sustained on disposal."]},{"h4":"Revenue control"},{"p":"The checks that ensure moneys due are received and accounted for: periodic monitoring; policing the revenue system so no service is rendered unbilled; timely demand notices and follow-up on debts; timely issue of revenue documents; prompt banking of collections where e-collection is not used; authority limits for revenue handling; and a functioning system of internal control, constantly reviewed."}]},{"n":"9.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–12 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Charges/outlets of funds from the Consolidated Revenue Fund could be summarised as: I. all recurrent expenditure heads in the approved estimates; II. salaries and allowances of statutory officers; III. pension and gratuity. (i) I only  (ii) II only  (iii) III only  (iv) I, II and III  (v) I and II only","The general sources of government revenue include the following EXCEPT (i) Taxation  (ii) Fines  (iii) Foreign investment  (iv) Ordinary shares  (v) Public debt","The revenue collection agencies of the federal government are the following EXCEPT: Nigerian Customs Service; State Internal Revenue Service; Nigeria Revenue Service; Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation; Department of Petroleum Resources","Membership of the FAAC technical session includes the following except ONE (A) Federal Ministry of Finance  (B) Department of Petroleum Resources  (C) Central Bank of Nigeria  (D) Office of the Auditor-General for the Federation  (E) Office of Head of the Service of the Federation","Functions of the technical session include the following except ONE (A) To consider the accounting returns of revenue collecting agencies  (B) To submit annual report of its performance/activities to the National Assembly  (C) To deliberate and consider the revenue available for distribution  (D) To make recommendation to the plenary session for adoption of the revenue to be shared to the three tiers of government  (E) To consider any other issues sent from the plenary session","Functions of the Joint Revenue Board (JRB) include the following except ONE (A) Harmonising tax administration  (B) Resolving inter-governmental jurisdictional dispute  (C) Focused on personal-income-tax coordination between the NRS and state boards  (D) Maintaining the national taxpayer-ID platform  (E) Advising on double-taxation and reform","Funds used to account for the resources derived from the business activities of government and its agencies such as parastatals is called …","A compulsory levy imposed by the government for which citizens receive no direct benefit is called …","The State Joint Local Government Account Allocation Committee is set up to distribute allocation to local governments from the Federation Account and …(%) of the internally generated revenue of the appropriate state government.","The expenditure incurred for the provision and maintenance of infrastructural amenities such as road and bridge construction is contained in …","The fund set up to meet unforeseen expenditure in urgent situations occasioned by natural disasters is called …","The Nigeria Customs Service Act 2023 (and the 2025 tax reforms) scrapped the 7% cost-of-collection deduction from gross Federation Account revenue; it is instead funded by a …(%) FOB levy on the value of imports collected at the ports."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** A  **2.** D  **3.** B  **4.** D  **5.** B  **6.** C  **7.** Proprietary  **8.** Taxation  **9.** 10%  **10.** Summary of capital expenditure payments  **11.** Contingency Fund  **12.** 4%"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund, JOJO Federal Republic","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Prepare the Statement of the Consolidated Revenue Fund as at 31 December 2021 from: balance at 1 Jan 2021 ₦6,292,000; treasury bills issued during the year ₦7,114,000; treasury bills repaid ₦6,518,000; transfer to Contingencies Fund ₦3,400,000; issues from Contingencies Fund ₦3,000,000; total revenue received ₦35,554,000; recurrent expenditure ₦28,897,000; transfer to Development Fund ₦3,400,000; appreciation on valuation of securities ₦44,000."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"JOJO Federal Republic — Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund as at 31 December 2021 (₦'000)","align":"lrr","head":["","",""],"rows":[["Balance b/f","","6,292"],["Treasury bills issued","7,114",""],["Treasury bills repaid","(6,518)","596"],["Issues from Contingencies Fund","","3,000"],["Transfer to Contingencies Fund","(3,400)","(400)"],["Total revenue received","","35,554"],["Appreciation on securities","","44"],["**Sub-total**","","**42,086**"],["Recurrent expenditure","","(28,897)"],["Transfer to Development Fund","","(3,400)"],["**Balance carried forward**","","**9,789**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — RMAFC composition and powers","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) State the composition of the Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC). (b) State the powers the Commission can exercise."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** A chairman, and one member from each state and the FCT, all persons of unquestionable integrity with the requisite qualifications and experience, appointed by the President."},{"p":"**(b)** Monitor accruals/disbursement from the Federation Account; periodically review the revenue allocation formula; advise the three tiers on fiscal efficiency; determine remuneration of political/public office holders; recommend the Federation Account and Local Government Account distribution formula; discharge any other function conferred by the Constitution or an Act; sit as a statutory member of FAAC, the Local Government Joint Account Allocation Committee, the JRB, the Niger-Delta Development Commission and the Ecological Fund Commission; and demand data from NNPC, NCS, NRS, the CBN and the Federal Ministry of Finance."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — SJLGAAC","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(i) State the stipulated criteria for sharing revenue allocations to local governments under the 1999 Constitution. (ii) State the composition of the State Joint Local Government Account Allocation Committee (SJLGAAC). (iii) State the functions of the Committee."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i)** Equality, population, primary school enrolment and internally generated revenue."},{"p":"**(ii)** The state Commissioner for Local Government (chairman); every local government council chairman; two representatives of the Accountant-General of the Federation; a representative of the state Accountant-General; two persons appointed by the Governor; and the Permanent Secretary of the relevant state ministry (or an officer the Commissioner designates) as secretary."},{"p":"**(iii)** Ensures allocations from the Federation Account and from the state are promptly paid into the State Joint Local Government Account, and distributed to the councils under the House of Assembly's law."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — the Consolidated Revenue Fund","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF) was created by section 80 of the 1999 Constitution. (i) List and explain the various revenue payable to the CRF. (ii) What are the charges against the CRF?"}],"a":[{"p":"**(i)** Direct allocation from the Federation Account; direct taxes (PAYE of Armed Forces, Police and Foreign Service personnel); licence and internal revenue (arms, goldsmith, radio/TV, gold-dealer licences); mining (fees, rent of crown lands, royalties); fees (court fees/fines, medical fees); earnings and sales (inventories, publications, stamps, money-order commission); rent of government property; interest and repayments, general and State; Armed Forces property sales; reimbursements (audit fees, overpayment refunds from other tiers); and miscellaneous (refunded overpayments, lapsed deposits)."},{"p":"**(ii)** All recurrent expenditure heads in the approved estimates; salaries and consolidated allowances of statutory officers (Police Service Commission, Public Complaints Commission, Public Service Commission, Auditor-General for the Federation, Nigerian Law Reform Commission, INEC, the President and Justices of the Court of Appeal, the Chief Judge/Justices of the Federal High Court, and the Chief Justice/Justices of the Supreme Court); and pension and gratuity of statutory and non-statutory officers, including the Armed Forces."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 5 — the Joint Revenue Board","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(i) Explain the term \"the Joint Revenue Board\". (ii) State its composition. (iii) State how members are appointed and how they may be removed."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i)** A body established by the Joint Revenue Board (Establishment) Act 2025 to harmonise and coordinate revenue administration across federal, state and local government."},{"p":"**(ii)** The Executive Chairman of the NRS (chairman); chairmen of all State IRS and the FCT-IRS; representatives of the Ministry of Finance, the National Identity Management Commission, RMAFC, the Nigerian Immigration Service, the Federal Road Safety Corps and the Nigeria Customs Service."},{"p":"**(iii)** Members serve part-time, for a renewable 4-year term, and may be removed for resignation, incapacity or misconduct."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Derivation (first charge)","tex":"\\ge 13\\% \\times \\text{natural resource revenue}"},{"lb":"Amount distributable","tex":"\\text{Gross} - \\text{cost of collection} - \\text{derivation}"},{"lb":"A state's statutory allocation","tex":"\\text{Distributable} \\times 26.72\\% \\times \\text{state horizontal factor}"}],"focus":"Two or three Section A marks every diet on the vertical formula, the 13% derivation principle, the VAT distribution, or the classification of a particular receipt. Section B commonly asks for the sources of revenue of a state or local government, or for ways of improving internally generated revenue. Learn the three vertical percentages and the five horizontal principles with their weights; they are asked directly.","errors":["Saying all revenue of the Federation goes into the Consolidated Revenue Fund; revenue collected by the Government of the Federation goes into the Federation Account, and only the Federal share reaches the CRF.","Giving the derivation principle as 13% of the Federation Account; it is 13% of revenue from natural resources, and it is a first charge.","Applying the Federation Account vertical formula to VAT; VAT has its own pool and its own formula.","Listing borrowing as revenue without noting that it is financing and creates a liability.","Confusing statutory allocation (from the Federation Account) with internally generated revenue (raised by the tier itself)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under section 162(2) of the 1999 Constitution, the derivation principle provides that not less than what percentage of revenue from natural resources goes to the state of derivation?","o":["10%","13%","20%","25%","50%"],"a":1,"w":"Not less than 13% of revenue accruing directly from any natural resource is paid to the state of derivation as a first charge, before the balance is shared.","src":"Chapter 9.2","sec":"9.2"},{"q":"The current vertical allocation of the Federation Account to the Federal Government is","o":["48.50%","52.68%","56.00%","26.72%","20.60%"],"a":1,"w":"The shares are Federal 52.68%, States 26.72% and Local Governments 20.60%.","src":"Chapter 9.2","sec":"9.2"},{"q":"Value added tax proceeds are shared among the tiers of government in the ratio","o":["Federal 52.68%, States 26.72%, Local 20.60%","Federal 15%, States 50%, Local 35%","Federal 50%, States 35%, Local 15%","Federal 35%, States 50%, Local 15%","Federal 20%, States 40%, Local 40%"],"a":1,"w":"VAT is not part of the Federation Account and has its own pool and formula: 15% Federal, 50% States, 35% Local Governments.","src":"Chapter 9.3","sec":"9.3"},{"q":"In the horizontal allocation formula for sharing among states, the principle carrying the greatest weight is","o":["population","equality of states","internal revenue effort","land mass","social development"],"a":1,"w":"Equality of states carries 40%, population 30%, and internal revenue effort, land mass and social development 10% each.","src":"Chapter 9.2","sec":"9.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is a non-tax revenue of government?","o":["Companies income tax","Royalties on crude oil","Value added tax","Customs duty","Education tax"],"a":1,"w":"Royalties are a payment for the extraction of a natural resource, not a compulsory levy on income or consumption.","src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"Personal income tax of members of the Armed Forces and residents of the Federal Capital Territory is collected by","o":["the State Boards of Internal Revenue","the Federal Inland Revenue Service","the Nigeria Customs Service","the Accountant-General","the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission"],"a":1,"w":"Section 162(1) excepts this category from the Federation Account, and the FIRS collects it.","src":"Chapter 9.2","sec":"9.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Many state and local governments in Nigeria depend heavily on statutory allocation from the Federation Account. (a) Distinguish between statutory allocation and internally generated revenue, and state five sources of internally generated revenue available to a state government. (b) Explain the problems that limit internally generated revenue in Nigeria. (c) Recommend measures by which a state government could improve its internally generated revenue.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) The distinction"},{"p":"**Statutory allocation** is the share of the Federation Account distributed to a tier of government under section 162 of the Constitution and the Allocation of Revenue Act. It is received rather than earned: the state exerts no effort to obtain it and has no control over its size, which depends on federally collected revenue and therefore largely on the price and volume of crude oil."},{"p":"**Internally generated revenue** is revenue a state raises within its own jurisdiction from its own tax and non-tax sources. It depends directly on the state's administrative effort, its economic base and the quality of its revenue machinery, and it is therefore both more stable and more within the state's control."},{"p":"**Sources of internally generated revenue available to a state:**"},{"ol":["**Personal income tax** on residents of the state, under the Pay As You Earn system and by direct assessment of the self-employed — normally the largest single source.","**Road taxes and vehicle-related charges** — vehicle licences, drivers' licences, number plates, roadworthiness certificates and haulage permits.","**Business premises registration and renewal fees**, and development levies.","**Fees and licences** — market and abattoir fees, liquor licences, court fees, registration of births, deaths and marriages, hospital and school fees.","**Earnings and sales** from state commercial undertakings, and dividends from state investments.","**Rent** on state-owned land and property, ground rent and certificate of occupancy charges.","**Fines and penalties** imposed by state courts and regulatory agencies.","**Capital gains tax and stamp duties** within the state's competence."]},{"h4":"(b) Problems limiting internally generated revenue"},{"ol":["**A narrow and largely informal revenue base.** A very large proportion of economic activity takes place outside any register — traders, artisans, transporters, small landlords — and is neither assessed nor collected from.","**Absence of reliable records.** Most states lack a complete database of taxpayers, properties, vehicles and businesses, so assessments cannot be raised systematically and defaulters cannot be identified.","**Leakage and diversion.** Where collections are received in cash by individual officers, a proportion never reaches the treasury. Fake receipts and unauthorised collection points are common.","**Multiple taxation.** Overlapping demands by state agencies, local governments and unauthorised bodies impose a heavy compliance burden, discourage formalisation and invite evasion.","**Weak enforcement.** Legal powers are underused, prosecution of defaulters is rare, and revenue agencies are reluctant to pursue politically connected taxpayers.","**Taxpayer resistance.** Where citizens see no relationship between the taxes they pay and the services they receive, compliance is treated as optional and evasion attracts no social disapproval.","**Institutional weakness.** Revenue staff are frequently untrained, poorly paid and poorly equipped, and revenue administration is fragmented across ministries rather than concentrated in a professional authority.","**Over-reliance on allocation.** Because the monthly allocation arrives whatever the state does, there has historically been little political incentive to undertake the unpopular work of taxing residents."]},{"h4":"(c) Recommended measures"},{"ol":["**Automate assessment and collection.** Move all collections onto electronic platforms paying into a single state revenue account, and abolish cash handling by collectors. This is the most effective single measure available, because it removes the opportunity for diversion rather than relying on the honesty of individual officers.","**Build and integrate a taxpayer database.** Enumerate residents, properties, vehicles and businesses, and link the register to the national identity number, the corporate registry, the land registry and utility records so that non-filers are identified automatically.","**Broaden the base.** Bring the informal sector in through simplified presumptive taxation at modest rates, which is administratively cheap and establishes a compliance habit; and complete the enumeration and valuation of properties for tenement rating, which is a large and largely untapped source.","**Harmonise and publish the approved list of taxes and levies**, prohibit collection outside it, and dismantle unauthorised roadblocks and collection points. Reducing multiple taxation raises revenue by encouraging formalisation.","**Establish an autonomous, professionally managed state internal revenue service**, with its own board, power to recruit and remunerate competitively, and a defined percentage of collections for its running costs. The states with the highest IGR are those that have done this.","**Strengthen enforcement.** Use the tax appeal machinery, prosecute persistent defaulters, publish their names, and apply the statutory sanctions consistently and without regard to political connection.","**Train and motivate revenue staff**, with performance measured on amounts actually collected and reconciled rather than on assessments raised.","**Improve taxpayer education and, above all, visible service delivery.** Compliance rises where citizens can see roads, schools and clinics attributable to the taxes they pay. Publishing what was collected and what it purchased is itself a revenue measure.","**Institute internal audit and revenue monitoring**, reconciling assessments raised with amounts collected and banked, to block leakage."]},{"note":"The underlying point, worth stating in a conclusion, is that internally generated revenue is not merely a fiscal matter. A government financed by its own citizens is accountable to them in a way that a government financed by an unearned monthly allocation is not. Raising IGR improves governance as well as cash flow."}],"src":"Chapter 9.1, 9.4","sec":"9.1"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"Public Procurement","brief":"The Public Procurement Act 2007: the institutions it creates, the fundamental principles of public procurement, the methods of procurement and when each may be used, approval thresholds, and the offences and penalties.","outcomes":["State the objectives of the Public Procurement Act 2007","Describe the roles of the National Council on Public Procurement and the Bureau of Public Procurement","State the fundamental principles of public procurement","Distinguish open competitive bidding from restricted tendering, request for quotations, direct procurement and emergency procurement","Explain the procurement planning and approval process, including the certificate of no objection","Identify offences under the Act and the penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"10.1","t":"Purpose and institutions","b":[{"p":"Before 2007, public procurement in Nigeria was governed only by the Financial Regulations and by circular. Contracts were routinely awarded without competition, at inflated prices, to companies connected with the awarding officers, and frequently for projects that were never completed. The **Public Procurement Act 2007** was enacted to establish a legal framework, following the \"due process\" reform begun in 2001."},{"h4":"Objectives of the Act"},{"ul":["To harmonise existing government policies and practices on public procurement.","To establish pricing standards and benchmarks.","To ensure **probity, accountability and transparency** in the procurement process.","To achieve **value for money** and fitness for purpose in public expenditure.","To promote competition, economy and efficiency.","To ensure that public procurement is applied as a tool of national development."]},{"def":{"t":"National Council on Public Procurement (NCPP)","d":"the policy-making body established by the Act. Chaired by the **Minister of Finance**, with the Attorney-General and Minister of Justice, the Secretary to the Government of the Federation, the Head of Service, the Economic Adviser to the President, **six part-time members** representing the Nigerian Institute of Purchasing and Supply Management, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigeria Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture, the Nigerian Society of Engineers, civil society, and the media, and the **Director-General of the Bureau**, who serves as Secretary to the Council. It considers and approves policies, approves the appointment of the Bureau's directors, receives the Bureau's audited accounts, and approves changes in procurement thresholds."}},{"note":"A recurring criticism, and a good point to make in a discussion question, is that the National Council on Public Procurement was not constituted for many years after the Act, its functions being exercised in practice by the Federal Executive Council. Since the Council approves thresholds and appointments, its absence weakened the independence the Act intended."},{"def":{"t":"Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP)","d":"the regulatory and supervisory body established by the Act, headed by a Director-General."}},{"h4":"Objectives of the Bureau"},{"ul":["Harmonising existing government policies and practices on public procurement, and ensuring **probity, accountability and transparency** in the procurement process.","Establishing **pricing standards and benchmarks**.","Ensuring fair, competitive, transparent and value-for-money standards/practices for the procurement and disposal of public assets and services.","Attaining transparency, competitiveness, cost-effectiveness and professionalism in the public-sector procurement system."]},{"h4":"Functions of the Bureau"},{"ul":["Formulates general procurement policies/guidelines for NCPP approval, and publicises and explains the Act's provisions.","Certifies federal procurement **before award** (the certificate of no objection) and supervises implementation of procurement policies.","Monitors tendered-item prices and keeps a **national database of standard prices**, and of contractors' particulars, classification and categorisation.","Publishes major-contract details, and paper/electronic editions, in the **procurement journal**, and maintains an archive of federal procurement plans and information.","Undertakes procurement research/surveys; organises training for procurement professionals; periodically reviews the socio-economic effect of procurement policy and advises the NCPP.","Prepares and updates standard bidding and contract documents.","Prevents fraudulent/unfair procurement, applying administrative sanctions where necessary, and reviews the procurement/contract-award procedures of every entity the Act applies to."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Other institution","Role"],"rows":[["**Accounting officer of the procuring entity**","Personally responsible for the entity's procurement: for planning, for constituting the tenders board, for ensuring compliance with the Act, and for the records"],["**Tenders Board / Procurement Planning Committee**","The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the plan; the Tenders Board conducts the process and makes the award within its threshold"]]}}]},{"n":"10.2","t":"Fundamental principles","b":[{"ol":["**Open competitive bidding** is the default method for all procurement. Any other method requires justification and, above the threshold, the approval of the Bureau.","**Economy and efficiency** — the procurement must represent value for money, which is not necessarily the lowest price but the lowest **evaluated responsive** bid.","**Transparency** — bids are opened in public, immediately after the deadline, in the presence of bidders and observers from professional bodies and civil society.","**Equal opportunity and non-discrimination** — all interested suppliers must have the same information, the same deadline and the same criteria applied.","**Objective evaluation on criteria published in advance.** No criterion may be applied that was not in the bid documents.","**Procurement planning.** No procurement may proceed unless it is in the approved procurement plan and funds are available and appropriated.","**Accountability and record keeping** — a complete record of the process must be kept and is subject to procurement audit.","**Professionalism** — procurement must be conducted by trained officers of a procurement cadre."]},{"key":"The Act requires the award to go to the **lowest evaluated responsive bid**, not simply to the lowest bid. A bid that does not meet the specification, or comes from a bidder who fails the prequalification, is not responsive however cheap it is — and a bid that is abnormally low may be rejected as unrealistic."}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"Methods of procurement","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","When it may be used","Procedure"],"rows":[["**Open competitive bidding**","The default for all procurement above the threshold for quotations","Advertisement in at least two national newspapers, the procuring entity's website and the government tenders journal, allowing a minimum of six weeks; public bid opening; evaluation against published criteria; award to the lowest evaluated responsive bid"],["**Restricted (selective) tendering**","Where the goods or services are available only from a limited number of suppliers; where the cost of open bidding would be disproportionate to the value; or where the goods are of a specialised nature","Invitation of a shortlist of qualified suppliers, with the Bureau's approval, but otherwise following the open bidding procedure"],["**Request for quotations**","For procurement of readily available goods or small works below the prescribed threshold","Written quotations obtained from at least **three** unrelated suppliers; award to the lowest responsive quotation"],["**Direct (single-source) procurement**","Where the goods are available from only one supplier and no reasonable alternative exists; for additional supplies from the original supplier for compatibility reasons; or where there is an unforeseeable emergency","Negotiation with the single supplier, with full documentation of the justification and the approval of the Bureau"],["**Emergency procurement**","Where there is an imminent or actual threat to life, property or the environment, or where the country is seriously threatened by a disaster or war","Expedited procedure limited to what is strictly necessary; a report must be made to the Bureau within the prescribed period and the procurement ratified"],["**Two-stage tendering**","Where it is not feasible to formulate detailed specifications at the outset — complex works or information technology systems","First stage: technical proposals without prices, leading to a refined specification. Second stage: priced bids on the agreed specification"],["**Request for proposals**","Procurement of **consultancy and other services** where quality is paramount","Evaluation on quality and cost combined, in the weightings stated in the request"]]}}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"The procurement process","b":[{"steps":["**Procurement planning.** The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the annual procurement plan, based on the approved budget, and no procurement outside the plan may proceed.","**Confirmation of funds.** The accounting officer certifies that funds are appropriated and available. Commencing a procurement without funds is an offence.","**Advertisement and prequalification.** The invitation is published; where the works are complex, bidders are prequalified on experience, financial capacity, plant and personnel.","**Issue of bidding documents**, containing the specification, the conditions of contract, the evaluation criteria and the deadline.","**Receipt and public opening of bids.** Bids are opened immediately after the deadline, in public, in the presence of bidders and of observers from professional bodies and civil society organisations. Names and prices are read out and recorded.","**Evaluation.** A technical evaluation against the specification, then a financial evaluation of the technically responsive bids, applying only the published criteria.","**Recommendation and approval by the Tenders Board** within its threshold, or referral upward where the value exceeds it.","**Certificate of no objection.** Where the value exceeds the threshold for the entity, the Bureau reviews the process and issues a **Certificate of \"No Objection\" to Contract Award**. Without it the award is invalid.","**Award and contract execution.** The letter of award is issued, the contract signed, and performance bonds and advance payment guarantees obtained.","**Contract administration and payment**, against certificates of work done and after inspection.","**Record keeping and procurement audit.** The complete record is retained and is subject to review by the Bureau and by the Auditor-General."]},{"h4":"Approval thresholds"},{"p":"The Act provides for approving authorities by value, with the naira limits set by circular and periodically revised. The structure — accounting officer, then Tenders Board, then Bureau certificate, then FEC at the top — is fixed; only the figures move. The tables below reproduce the **revised guidelines and thresholds on public procurement** (Circular Ref. No. 59780/S.2/B/532 of 27 May 2025), the figures current at the time of writing and the ones the examiner is expected to test."},{"table":{"align":"lllll","head":["Approving authority","Goods","Works","Non-consultant services","Consultancy"],"rows":[["**BPP certificate + FEC/NASS** (Tenders Board)","₦5bn and above","₦10bn and above","₦5bn and above","₦5bn and above"],["**BPP certificate** — Ministerial / NASS-Parastatal / Judicial TB","₦1bn – ₦5bn","₦5bn – ₦10bn","₦1bn – ₦5bn","₦1bn – ₦5bn"],["**Ministerial TB / NASS-Parastatal TB / Judicial TB** approval","₦50m – ₦1bn","₦75m – ₦5bn","₦50m – ₦1bn","₦50m – ₦1bn"],["**Parastatals TB** — MDAs / NASS / Court bodies","₦25m – ₦500m","₦50m – ₦1bn","₦25m – ₦500m","₦25m – ₦500m"],["**Accounting Officer** — Permanent Secretary / Head of Ministerial body","below ₦50m","below ₦75m","below ₦50m","below ₦50m"],["**Accounting Officer** — DG / CEO","below ₦25m","below ₦50m","below ₦25m","below ₦25m"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lllll","head":["Procurement method","Goods","Works","Non-consultant services","Consultancy"],"rows":[["**International/National Competitive Bidding**","₦1bn and above","₦5bn and above","₦1bn and above","—"],["**National Competitive Bidding**","₦30m – ₦1bn","₦30m – ₦5bn","₦30m – ₦1bn","—"],["**Request for quotation**","below ₦30m","below ₦50m","below ₦30m","—"],["**Single source / direct contracting**","below ₦5m","below ₦5m","below ₦5m","as indicated in the PPA 2007"],["**Prequalification required**","₦500m and above","₦1bn and above","₦500m and above","—"],["**Quality & Cost Based Selection / Consultant Qualifications / Least Cost**","—","—","—","below ₦100m (QCBS/CQS), below ₦1bn (QCBS), below ₦100m (Least Cost)"]]}},{"note":"The **Nigerian National Petroleum Company Limited (NNPCL)** operates under its own, dollar-denominated thresholds (Circular Ref. PROC/OSGF/BPP/709/85 of 19 January 2022): BPP + FEC from **US$20m**; Ministerial TB (NNPC) US$10m–US$20m; Group HQ Tenders Board US$4m–US$10m; Parastatal TB (Refinery/E&P/Supply-Chain) US$2m–US$4m; Parastatal TB (Minor) US$0.5m–US$2m; Parastatal TB (Business Unit) US$0.10m–US$0.5m; Accounting Officer (PS/GMD, at Corporate HQ) below US$0.3m; Accounting Officer (DG/CEO, at a Strategic Business Unit) below US$0.10m."},{"h4":"Procurement processing timeline"},{"p":"The prescribed number of working days at each stage, from advertisement to contract signing:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Stage","NCB","ICB"],"rows":[["Advertisement / prequalification documents","14 days","21 days"],["Evaluation and shortlisting","14 days","14 days"],["Tender documents issuance and submission","21 days","30 days"],["Tender evaluation, approval and publication","21 days","21 days"],["Standstill period","14 days","14 days"],["Award","7 days","7 days"],["**Total**","**91 days**","**107 days**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Consultancy services — stage","Days"],"rows":[["Invitation and submission of expressions of interest (EOI)","14 days or more"],["EOI evaluation and shortlisting","14 days"],["Proposal submission","30 days or more"],["Technical evaluation","30 days or more"],["Notification","5 days"],["Financial-proposal opening, evaluation, approval and negotiation notice","21 days"],["Negotiation and approval","7 days"],["Contract signing","7 days"],["**Total**","**128 days**"]]}},{"h4":"Federal Executive Council"},{"p":"The Federal Executive Council approves, on the Bureau's certificate of no objection, procurements of **₦5bn and above for goods, ₦10bn and above for works, and ₦5bn and above for non-consultancy and for consultancy services** — the same figures as the top tier of the approving-authority table above."},{"warn":"These figures are revised periodically by circular, so a question may be set on an older or a newer scale — but the **structure never changes**: accounting officer, then Tenders Board, then Bureau certificate for the higher band, then FEC at the very top, with works always attracting a higher band than goods or services at the same tier. If a question's figures do not match the table above, answer on the structure and the relative ordering, which is what is actually being tested."}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Offences and remedies","b":[{"h4":"Offences under the Act"},{"ul":["Entering or attempting to enter into a **collusive agreement** with bidders, or **bid-rigging**.","Directing the procuring entity to **split a contract** in order to bring it below a threshold and avoid the required approval.","Altering any procurement document with intent to influence the outcome.","Using a **fake document or encouraging its use**.","Wilful refusal to allow the Bureau access to any procurement record.","**Bid-rigging, giving or receiving bribes**, or influencing the process for personal gain.","Awarding a contract **without budgetary provision, approval or a cash-backed plan**.","Failing to obtain the certificate of no objection where required."]},{"h4":"Penalties"},{"ul":["For a **natural person**: imprisonment for a term of not less than five calendar years without the option of a fine.","For a **legal person** (a body corporate): a fine of not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, and the officers of the company are liable to prosecution; the company may be **debarred** from participating in public procurement for a stated period.","A public officer additionally faces **summary dismissal** from service.","Conviction may be accompanied by an order for **restitution** of any loss to government."]},{"h4":"Complaints and review"},{"p":"A bidder who considers that he has suffered loss through a breach of the Act must first complain in writing to the **accounting officer** of the procuring entity, who must respond within 15 working days. If dissatisfied, the bidder may complain to the **Bureau**, which must decide within 21 working days and may nullify the procurement, order a re-tender, or declare a rule or practice inconsistent with the Act. A bidder still dissatisfied may seek **judicial review** in the Federal High Court."}]},{"n":"10.6","t":"Tenders' Boards and contract administration","b":[{"def":{"t":"Construction contract","d":"the execution of a building or civil engineering project, mechanical and electrical engineering installations and other fabrications, normally evidenced by an agreement between two or more parties. It is a **capital project**, normally financed from the Capital Development Fund."}},{"h4":"History and types of Tenders' Board"},{"p":"Before the Public Procurement Act, the Federal Ministry of Finance (circular F15775 of 27 June 2001) **abrogated the Departmental Tenders Board and the Federal Tenders Board**. Their functions passed to the Permanent Secretary of the ministry and to the Ministerial Tenders Board."},{"key":"A Permanent Secretary or a parastatal's Chief Executive may, without going to open tendering, approve contracts up to **₦5,000,000**, provided at least **three written quotations** are obtained and a quarterly return is made to the Minister."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tenders Board","Composition"],"rows":[["**Ministerial Tenders Board**","Ministry: Chairman = Permanent Secretary, Members = Heads of Department. Parastatal: Chairman = Chief Executive Officer, Members = Heads of Department"],["**Armed Forces / Ministry of Defence Tenders Board**","Chairman = Permanent Secretary, Ministry of Defence; Members = representatives of the Army, Navy and Air Force, and the Director of Finance and Accounts"],["**Nigeria Police Tenders and Purchasing Board** (Ministerial)","Chairman = Permanent Secretary, Ministry of Police Affairs; Members = Deputy Inspector-General (Finance and Administration), all Heads of Department, and the Head of Finance and Accounts"]]}},{"h4":"How a Tenders Board operates"},{"steps":["**Notification.** Once a contract is approved and funded, the Secretariat is informed of its magnitude; the Secretary informs the Chairman so a meeting can be convened — periodic for routine business, or emergency where urgent.","**Notice of invitation.** The Board orders a notice giving the full details of the job or service, published in the official gazette and/or national newspapers/magazines and on the Ministry's notice board, always stating a closing date.","**Deposit for tender**, where required: a Treasury receipt for the deposit is obtained and confirmed before the tender form is issued.","**Tender procedure.** Tenders are submitted in sealed envelopes to the Board Secretariat and opened, under the Chairman's (or a deputy's) supervision, at the close of the notice; each is numbered serially and initialled and dated by the Secretary, then listed in duplicate and kept in safe custody.","**Selection.** The Board meets to select the best tender, considering the contractor's track record, quality of service and experience — **the lowest tender is not necessarily the best**. If every tender is rejected, fresh applications are called for; if one is recommended, all bids go forward to the approving authority with the duplicate list and the Board's comments.","**Award.** The approving authority communicates its decision to the Board; the Secretary writes the letter of award and invites the contractor to sign, with bonds or sureties where necessary. The award is published in the newspapers/gazette and unsuccessful tenderers are informed. Certified copies of the contract are sent to the **Auditor-General and the Accountant-General**. Government contracts may not be **sub-let or assigned** except where the agreement permits it; disposal of government property follows a similar tender process."]},{"h4":"Audit inspection"},{"p":"Documents forwarded to the Auditor-General for inspection include: **(a)** certified true copies of all contract agreements, **(b)** minutes of Tenders Board meetings, and **(c)** the full records of the tendering process. These are available for inspection by the Auditor-General or the Accountant-General at short notice or none, and must be **retained for seven years** from the completion or takeover of the project."},{"h4":"Terms of the contract"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Effect"],"rows":[["**Contingencies clause**","If the contractor exercised reasonable care but meets an unexpected situation, the contractee/owner bails the contractor out — more money, or an upward review of the contract sum. Otherwise the contractor bears the cost"],["**Retention fee**","Government withholds about **5%** of the contract sum for **six months** after completion. If the project is properly executed with no construction fault found, the retained amount is released; if a fault is found (e.g. a wall crack from a construction error), the retained sum is used to fix it and the contractor must pay any shortfall or risk **blacklisting**"]]}},{"h4":"Contract payment vouchers"},{"p":"A voucher raised to pay a contractor must contain: **(a)** the contractor's name and address, **(b)** the contract number, **(c)** the vote of charge, **(d)** a description of the project, **(e)** the number of the certificate being paid, **(f)** the gross amount and any retention fee, **(g)** the authority for payment, and **(h)**, where it is a part-payment, the full contract amount and the outstanding balance."},{"p":"Before it is processed, the voucher for the **Tenders Board information** must carry a certified copy of the Board's minutes, a certified copy of the contract agreement, a copy of the approving authority's approval, and a copy of each prior payment voucher on the same contract; the minutes and records are also available to the Accounting Officer on request and to the Auditor-General on demand. Attachments required before payment: **(a)** the Tenders Board award minutes (verifying the award was within the Board's power), **(b)** a completion certificate signed by a competent authority (an engineer, surveyor or architect), **(c)** the letter of award and the contract agreement, **(d)** for supplies, the original delivery notes and store receipt vouchers, and **(e)** the firm's bill or invoice."},{"h4":"Electronic payment of contractors"},{"p":"From January 2009, Federal Government policy required **all payments to contractors to be electronic**, to eliminate payment delays and reduce corruption and undue contact between government officials and contractors. The implementation guidelines (Treasury Circular Ref. TYR/A8&B8/2008) require: all payments made through the bank; cheque payments to contractors stopped; contractors' bank accounts cease to be cheque accounts; contractors disclose their bank particulars on every invoice; mandates authorise the bank to pay the contractor's account directly; copies of mandates go to the OAGF with the monthly returns; every Federal Government employee must hold a commercial bank account; and cash pay officers must never collect cash from a bank for disbursement."},{"h4":"Contract register"},{"p":"Copies of every contract agreement are forwarded to the Accounts Division and entered in a **Contract Register**, showing: **(a)** the contractor's name and address, **(b)** the contract number, **(c)** the contract sum, **(d)** any contingency or variation, **(e)** the payment terms, **(f)** the completion period, **(g)** the file number, **(h)** payment particulars and the balance outstanding, and **(i)** the signature of the officer controlling the expenditure. Large multi-contract projects may also keep a summary **Project Register**."}]},{"n":"10.7","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The Permanent Secretary/Chief Executive can approve contracts of works, services and purchase up to a certain sum of … without open competitive tendering (A) N2,500,000  (B) N3,000,000  (C) N5,000,000  (D) N10,000,000  (E) N20,000,000","By the current approved revised threshold for service-wide application, the approval limit for the Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP) and Federal Executive Council for Goods is one of the following (A) N500,000,000 and above  (B) N750,000,000 and above  (C) N100,000,000 and above  (D) N250,000,000 and above  (E) N200,000,000 and above","With the exception of one method, which may not require the approval of the Bureau, all others attract the \"Certificate of 'No Objection' to Award Contracts\" — which method is exempt? (A) Two-stage bidding  (B) Restrictive bidding  (C) Direct procurement  (D) Emergency procurement  (E) Request for quotations","The following are the reasons for introducing due process to the award of contracts, except (A) To stem the tide of corruption of government officials conniving with contractors to dupe government  (B) To allocate contracts to the key management staff of government MDAs  (C) To eradicate embezzlement of public funds in the name of contract awards  (D) To arrest flagrant abuse of contract award procedures  (E) To disabuse inflation of contract and lack of transparency in contract award","Which of the following is NOT one of the benefits of Due Process? (A) To safeguard public funds and assets  (B) To enhance transparency and accountability in governance  (C) To see that documentation is effected only where government officials are involved in contract execution  (D) To rebuild public confidence in government financial activities  (E) To ensure that government receives value for money expended","The mechanism for ensuring strict compliance with rules and procedures guiding the award of contract with openness, competition and cost accuracy is known as …","The proposal for the supply of goods or services, usually made and presented as a result of an invitation, is known as …","The offence committed when a public officer deliberately splits tenders, contracts of works, purchases or services so as to circumvent the provisions of the Act and the relevant circular is known as …","In the membership of the National Council on Public Procurement (NCPP), the chairman of the Council is …","The invitation for bids must be advertised on the notice board of the procuring entity, on its official website, and in at least … national newspapers."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** C  **2.** C  **3.** E  **4.** B  **5.** C  **6.** Due Process  **7.** Tender  **8.** Tender splitting  **9.** Minister of Finance  **10.** Two"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — Due Process and its benefits","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What is Due Process? (b) Enumerate five benefits of Due Process."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Due Process is the mechanism for ensuring strict compliance with the openness, competition, cost accuracy, rules and procedures that should guide contract awards within the three tiers of government in Nigeria. It is the instrument used by the Budget Monitoring and Price Intelligence Unit (BMPIU), tasked with implementing Nigeria's Public Procurement Reform Programme, and was introduced to arrest flagrant abuse of procedures, inflation of contract costs and lack of transparency."},{"p":"**(b) Benefits:**"},{"ol":["Safeguards public money and assets.","Improves planning and diligent project analysis, leading to accurate costing and prioritisation of investments.","Improves fiscal management through more efficient and effective expenditure.","Improves technical efficiency through unimpaired, enhanced information flow.","Enhances transparency and accountability in governance.","Rebuilds public confidence in government financial activities.","Ensures government receives value for money spent.","Applies the principle of competition and right costing, saving money for use elsewhere.","Reassures foreign investors who previously doubted that genuine business could survive in the country."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — post-contract-award activities","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Under post-contract-award activities, discuss the processes involved, classifying them into (i) Tenders Board information on vouchers, and (ii) Tenders Board information availability."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i) Tenders Board information on vouchers.** A payment voucher in respect of a contract awarded through tender must contain, among other things: a certified true copy of the minutes of the Tenders Board meetings relating to the award; a certified true copy of the contract agreement; a copy of the approving authority; and a copy of each voucher for payments already made on the contract."},{"p":"**(ii) Tenders Board information availability.** The minutes of Tenders Board meetings and the full records are to be made available to the accounting officer on request, and for inspection by the Auditor-General on demand."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — the e-payment procedure","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State the guidelines for implementation of the \"e-payment\" procedures as contained in Treasury Circular Ref. No. TYR/A8 and BB/2008, reference OAGT/CAD/026/vol.II/465 of 22 October 2008."}],"a":[{"ol":["All payments from government funds are made through the banks — commercial banks or the Central Bank of Nigeria.","All MDAs stop using cheques to pay contractors.","All government-fund bank accounts cease to be cheque accounts.","Contractors must state their current-account particulars with a commercial bank on invoices submitted for payment, under their corporate seal.","Mandates detailing payments are issued to banks, authorising payment into the contractors' designated accounts.","Copies of mandates issued to banks are forwarded to the Accountant-General of the Federation, in addition to the existing monthly financial returns.","All Federal Government employees must open commercial-bank accounts into which all payments due to them are made.","Central Pay Officers must on no account collect cash from the bank for disbursement to any government official."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — circumstances for direct procurement","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State the circumstances in which an entity may carry out direct procurement."}],"a":[{"ol":["The goods, works or services are available only from a particular supplier, or that supplier has exclusive rights over them, and no reasonable alternative exists.","There is an urgent need and using tender proceedings or any other method is impractical because of unforeseeable circumstances not caused by the procuring entity's own delay.","A catastrophic event creates an urgent need, making other methods impractical given the time they would take.","The entity has already procured goods, equipment, technology or services from a supplier and needs additional supplies from the same supplier for **standardisation** or **compatibility** with the existing procurement, the additional quantity is small relative to the original, the price is reasonable and no suitable alternative exists.","The contract is for research, study, equipment or development, unless it involves producing goods in quantities to establish commercial viability or recover research/development cost.","The procurement concerns **national security**, and the entity determines that single-source procurement is the most appropriate method."]},{"note":"In every case, the entity must record the grounds for its decision and the circumstances justifying single-source procurement in the procurement proceedings file."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"A Section B question in most diets, usually on the methods of procurement and when each may be used, the fundamental principles, or the roles of the Council and the Bureau. Section A tests the year of the Act, the minimum number of quotations, and the meaning of the certificate of no objection. Learn the seven methods with the circumstances for each — that table alone answers half the questions set on this chapter.","errors":["Saying the contract goes to the lowest bid; it goes to the lowest **evaluated responsive** bid.","Confusing the National Council on Public Procurement (policy) with the Bureau of Public Procurement (regulation and certification).","Stating that two quotations suffice for a request for quotations; the Act requires at least three from unrelated suppliers.","Describing restricted tendering as available at the entity's discretion; it requires justification and the Bureau's approval.","Omitting the certificate of no objection from the process, which is where the Bureau's control actually bites.","Giving the penalty for a body corporate as imprisonment; a company is fined not less than 25% of the value and may be debarred."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The default method of procurement under the Public Procurement Act 2007 is","o":["restricted tendering","open competitive bidding","request for quotations","direct procurement","two-stage tendering"],"a":1,"w":"Every other method requires justification, and above the threshold the approval of the Bureau of Public Procurement.","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"Under a request for quotations, quotations must be obtained from at least","o":["two unrelated suppliers","three unrelated suppliers","five suppliers","one supplier","seven suppliers"],"a":1,"w":"The Act requires a minimum of three quotations from unrelated contractors or suppliers.","src":"Chapter 10.3","sec":"10.3"},{"q":"The document issued by the Bureau of Public Procurement before a high-value contract may be awarded is the","o":["procurement plan","certificate of no objection","letter of award","performance bond","bid security"],"a":1,"w":"The Certificate of \"No Objection\" to Contract Award confirms that the Bureau has reviewed the process. Without it the award is invalid.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"A contract under the Act should be awarded to the","o":["lowest bidder","lowest evaluated responsive bidder","bidder with the best technical proposal","most experienced bidder","bidder recommended by the Minister"],"a":1,"w":"The bid must first be responsive — meeting the specification and the qualification requirements — before price is compared. A cheap but non-responsive bid is rejected.","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"A body corporate convicted of an offence under the Public Procurement Act is liable to a fine of not less than","o":["10% of the procurement value","25% of the procurement value","50% of the procurement value","5% of the procurement value","₦1,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"The company is fined not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, its officers may be prosecuted, and it may be debarred from public procurement.","src":"Chapter 10.5","sec":"10.5"},{"q":"The policy-making body established by the Public Procurement Act 2007 is the","o":["Bureau of Public Procurement","National Council on Public Procurement","Federal Executive Council","Ministerial Tenders Board","Procurement Planning Committee"],"a":1,"w":"The Council makes policy and approves thresholds; the Bureau is the regulatory and supervisory body that certifies individual procurements.","src":"Chapter 10.1","sec":"10.1"},{"q":"By the revised guidelines and thresholds on public procurement, a contract with a value exceeding ₦50,000,000 for goods would ordinarily require the approval of","o":["the Accounting Officer alone","the Ministerial Tenders Board","the National Council on Public Procurement directly","the Nigeria Police Tenders and Purchasing Board only","no approval, since it is below every threshold"],"a":1,"w":"For goods, the Accounting Officer's limit is below ₦50m; ₦50m–₦1bn falls to the Ministerial (or NASS-Parastatal/Judicial) Tenders Board. A figure exceeding ₦50,000,000 therefore sits above the Accounting Officer's own limit and is a Tenders Board matter.","src":"Chapter 10.6","sec":"10.6"},{"q":"Under the retention-fee term of a public sector construction contract, government ordinarily withholds about","o":["5% of the contract sum for six months after completion","25% of the contract sum indefinitely","10% of the contract sum for one month","the full contract sum until an audit is completed","5% of the contract sum for six months before commencement"],"a":0,"w":"About 5% of the contract sum is withheld for six months after completion; it is released if no construction fault is found, or used to fix one if found, with any shortfall recovered from the contractor.","src":"Chapter 10.6","sec":"10.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State six objectives of the Public Procurement Act 2007. (b) Describe five methods of procurement recognised by the Act, stating the circumstances in which each may be used. (c) Outline the stages of an open competitive bidding process from planning to award. (d) State four offences under the Act and the penalties on conviction.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Objectives of the Act"},{"ol":["To **harmonise** existing government policies and practices on public procurement, which before 2007 were scattered across the Financial Regulations and successive circulars.","To **establish pricing standards and benchmarks**, so that the reasonableness of a quoted price can be tested against a national database.","To ensure **probity, accountability and transparency** in the procurement process, by requiring advertisement, public bid opening and published evaluation criteria.","To achieve **value for money and fitness for purpose** in the application of public funds.","To promote **competition, economy and efficiency** among suppliers and contractors.","To ensure that public procurement is applied as a **tool of national development**, including support for local content where appropriate.","To **professionalise** procurement by establishing a trained procurement cadre."]},{"h4":"(b) Methods of procurement"},{"ol":["**Open competitive bidding.** The default method for all procurement above the quotation threshold. The invitation is advertised in at least two national newspapers, on the entity's website and in the tenders journal, with a minimum period for the preparation of bids. It applies unless the circumstances justify one of the alternatives below.","**Restricted (selective) tendering.** Permitted where the goods, works or services are available only from a limited number of suppliers; where the time and cost of the open procedure would be disproportionate to the value of the procurement; or where the subject matter is of a highly specialised nature. It requires the approval of the Bureau, and bids are invited from a shortlist of qualified suppliers.","**Request for quotations.** Used for readily available goods or minor works whose value falls below the prescribed threshold. Written quotations must be obtained from at least three unrelated suppliers, and the award goes to the lowest responsive quotation.","**Direct (single-source) procurement.** Permitted where the goods or services are available from only one supplier and no reasonable alternative exists; where additional supplies are required from the original supplier for reasons of compatibility or standardisation; or where an unforeseeable emergency makes competitive methods impracticable. Full documentation of the justification and the Bureau's approval are required.","**Emergency procurement.** Available where there is an imminent or actual threat to life, property or the environment, or where the country is seriously threatened by disaster or war. It is limited strictly to what is necessary to meet the emergency, and a report must be made to the Bureau, which ratifies or otherwise the action taken.","**Two-stage tendering.** Used where it is not feasible to formulate detailed specifications at the outset, as with complex works or information technology systems. Technical proposals without prices are invited first and used to settle the specification; priced bids on that specification follow.","**Request for proposals.** Used for consultancy and other services, where quality rather than price is the dominant consideration. Evaluation combines quality and cost in weightings stated in advance."]},{"h4":"(c) Stages of an open competitive bidding process"},{"ol":["**Procurement planning.** The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the annual procurement plan from the approved budget. Nothing outside the plan may be procured.","**Confirmation of funding.** The accounting officer certifies that the expenditure is appropriated and that funds are available. Commencing a procurement without funds is itself an offence.","**Advertisement.** The invitation to bid is published in at least two national newspapers, on the entity's website and in the government tenders journal, allowing the minimum period prescribed.","**Prequalification**, where the works are complex, assessing bidders on experience, financial capacity, plant and personnel.","**Issue of bidding documents** setting out the specification, conditions of contract, evaluation criteria and deadline. No criterion may later be applied that is not in these documents.","**Receipt and public opening of bids** immediately after the deadline, in the presence of bidders and of observers from professional bodies and civil society organisations, with names and prices read out and recorded.","**Technical evaluation** against the specification to determine responsiveness, followed by **financial evaluation** of the responsive bids.","**Recommendation and approval** by the Tenders Board within its threshold, or referral to the higher approving authority.","**Certificate of no objection** obtained from the Bureau of Public Procurement where the value exceeds the entity's threshold.","**Award and contract execution**, with performance bonds and advance payment guarantees where applicable.","**Contract administration**, payment against certified work done, and retention of the complete record for procurement audit."]},{"h4":"(d) Offences and penalties"},{"p":"**Offences** include:"},{"ul":["Entering into a collusive agreement with bidders or engaging in bid-rigging;","Splitting a contract to bring it below a threshold and avoid the required approval;","Altering a procurement document or using a fake document with intent to influence the outcome;","Giving or receiving a bribe or otherwise influencing the process for personal gain;","Wilfully refusing the Bureau access to procurement records; and","Awarding a contract without budgetary provision, without approval, or without the certificate of no objection."]},{"p":"**Penalties.** A natural person convicted is liable to imprisonment for a term of not less than five calendar years without the option of a fine, and a public officer is additionally liable to summary dismissal from service. A body corporate is liable to a fine of not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, its directors and officers may themselves be prosecuted, and it may be debarred from participating in public procurement for a stated period. The court may in addition order restitution of any loss caused to government."},{"note":"The practical significance of the Act lies less in the penalties, which have rarely been applied, than in the procedural requirements — advertisement, public opening, published criteria and the certificate of no objection. These work by removing discretion and creating a record, which is a more reliable control than the threat of a prosecution that may never come."}],"src":"Chapter 10.1–10.5","sec":"10.1"}]}},{"n":11,"t":"Stores, Losses and the Board of Survey","brief":"The classification and control of government stores, the ledgers and cards used to record them, the treatment of losses of cash and stores, and the composition and duties of Boards of Survey and Boards of Enquiry.","outcomes":["Classify government stores as allocated, unallocated or expendable","Describe the documents used to control receipt and issue of stores","Explain stock verification and the treatment of surpluses and shortages","Describe the procedure on discovery of a loss of cash or stores","Distinguish a Board of Survey from a Board of Enquiry","Explain surcharge and the write-off of losses"],"secs":[{"n":"11.1","t":"Classification of stores","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Class","Description","Accounting treatment"],"rows":[["**Allocated stores**","Purchased for and charged directly to a particular vote or job at the time of purchase","Charged to the vote immediately; no store ledger value is carried"],["**Unallocated stores**","Purchased in bulk and held in store for future issue to various votes as required — stationery, fuel, spare parts, drugs","Held at cost in the store ledger; charged to the receiving vote on issue"],["**Expendable stores**","Consumed in use and not returnable — stationery, cleaning materials, drugs, fuel","Written off on issue"],["**Non-expendable stores**","Durable items returnable after use — tools, furniture, equipment, vehicles","Recorded on an inventory or asset register and accounted for until disposal"]]}},{"key":"The distinction between **allocated** and **unallocated** stores decides *when* the vote is charged. Allocated stores hit the vote on purchase; unallocated stores sit in a stores account and hit the vote on issue. Confusing the two produces a wrong charge to the vote and a wrong closing stores balance."}]},{"n":"11.2","t":"Records and documents","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Document","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Local Purchase Order (LPO)**","Authorises a supplier to deliver goods"],["**Store Receipt Voucher (SRV)**","Evidences receipt of goods into store after inspection; the authority for entry on the receipt side of the ledger"],["**Store Issue Voucher (SIV)**","Authorises and evidences the issue of stores from store to a using department; the authority for the issue side"],["**Bin card**","Kept by the storekeeper at the bin. Records **quantities only** — receipts, issues and balance — item by item"],["**Store ledger (stock card)**","Kept in the accounts section. Records **quantity and value**, and is the basis of the stores account"],["**Requisition**","The using department's request for stores"],["**Inventory / asset register**","Lists non-expendable items, their location and the officer responsible"],["**Store Adjustment Voucher**","Records approved adjustments — surpluses, shortages, revaluation, conversion"],["**Certificate of unserviceability / Board of Survey report**","Supports the write-off or disposal of stores no longer usable"]]}},{"note":"The **bin card is kept by the storekeeper and shows quantity only; the store ledger is kept in the accounts section and shows quantity and value.** The separation is deliberate: two independent records of the same movement, kept by different officers, which must agree. It is examined almost every diet."}]},{"n":"11.3","t":"Stock verification","b":[{"p":"Stock verification is the physical counting of stores and comparison with the records. It is carried out by **stock verifiers** independent of the storekeeper, periodically and without notice, and comprehensively at the year end."},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["To confirm the **existence and quantity** of stores shown in the records.","To detect **shortages, pilferage and fraud**.","To identify **obsolete, damaged or unserviceable** items for write-off or disposal.","To test the **accuracy of the ledgers and bin cards** and the adequacy of store procedures.","To confirm **safe custody and proper storage** conditions.","To provide assurance for the accounts and for the auditor."]},{"h4":"Treatment of differences"},{"ul":["**Surplus** — physical quantity exceeds the record. The cause is investigated; it usually reflects an unrecorded receipt or an issue recorded twice. Once explained, the ledger is adjusted by a Store Adjustment Voucher.","**Shortage** — record exceeds physical quantity. Investigated as a **loss**, following the procedure in §11.4. A shortage within a normal allowance for evaporation, breakage or wastage may be written off on the certificate of the accounting officer; anything beyond it requires a Board of Enquiry."]}]},{"n":"11.4","t":"Losses of cash and stores","b":[{"h4":"Causes of loss"},{"ul":["Theft, burglary, armed robbery or fraud.","Fire, flood or other accident.","Negligence in custody or in the keeping of records.","Deterioration, obsolescence or expiry.","Errors of accounting or overpayment not recovered.","Loss in transit or on delivery."]},{"h4":"Procedure on discovery of a loss"},{"ol":["The officer discovering the loss reports it **immediately in writing** to his supervising officer and to the **accounting officer**.","The accounting officer reports it at once to the **Accountant-General** and the **Auditor-General**, and to the **Police** where theft or fraud is suspected.","The loss is entered in the **Register of Losses** with full particulars.","The officer suspected of responsibility may be **interdicted** pending investigation.","A **Board of Enquiry** is constituted to establish the facts: the amount, the cause, whether the Financial Regulations were complied with, whether negligence contributed, and who is responsible.","The Board reports its findings and recommendations to the accounting officer.","Where **negligence or misconduct** is established, the officer is **surcharged** with the whole or part of the loss, and is liable to disciplinary action and, where the conduct is criminal, to prosecution.","The irrecoverable balance is **written off** by the authority competent to do so — the accounting officer, the Minister of Finance or the National Assembly, according to the amount involved.","The **internal control weakness** that permitted the loss is identified and remedied."]},{"def":{"t":"Surcharge","d":"The imposition on a public officer of personal liability to make good a loss of public funds or stores caused by his negligence, misconduct or failure to comply with the Financial Regulations. The amount may be recovered from his salary, allowances or terminal benefits."}},{"def":{"t":"Write-off","d":"The formal removal of an irrecoverable loss from the books. It requires the approval of the competent authority and does not absolve any officer from liability: an officer may be surcharged even though the loss itself has been written off."}},{"warn":"Write-off and surcharge are **different decisions**. Writing off clears the accounting record; surcharging fixes personal liability. A question that asks about the treatment of a loss expects both, and the point that one does not discharge the other."}]},{"n":"11.5","t":"Boards of Survey and Boards of Enquiry","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry"],"rows":[["Purpose","To verify cash, bank balances and stores, and to examine and report on unserviceable or obsolete stores","To investigate a **specific loss** of cash or stores and determine its cause and who is responsible"],["When constituted","Routinely — at the year end, and on the handing over of a cash office or store","On the occurrence of a loss"],["Composition","Officers appointed by the accounting officer, none of whom is connected with the custody of the items surveyed","Senior officers independent of the office where the loss occurred, sometimes with external or professional members"],["Output","A survey report certifying balances, and recommendations on disposal of unserviceable items","A report of findings on cause and responsibility, with recommendations on surcharge, discipline and prevention"]]}},{"p":"A Board of Survey is **convened by the Accountant-General of the Federation**, mostly at the close of each financial year. Where an entity has adopted e-payment, a cash survey is not relevant, since physical cash is not expected to be held in the safe."},{"key":"A Board consists of **three officers**: a President and not less than **two members**. The President must hold a post of **Grade Level 08 or higher**; other members must not be below **Grade Level 06**. Where this is not possible, a single-member board may be appointed, but the reasons for this must be clearly stated on the survey report."},{"h4":"Duties of a Board of Survey"},{"ol":["To count the **cash and stamps** on hand and agree them to the cash book balance.","To confirm **bank balances** by direct certificate from the bank and to examine the bank reconciliation.","To verify **stores** physically against the ledgers and bin cards.","To examine items reported **unserviceable, obsolete or surplus to requirements** and recommend their disposal by sale, transfer or destruction.","To confirm **safe custody arrangements** — keys, safes, security of the store.","To report **discrepancies** and comment on the adequacy of the records and procedures.","To sign and submit the report to the accounting officer, with copies to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General."]},{"p":"The **annual Board of Survey** is convened at the close of the financial year and its certificate supports the cash and stores figures in the financial statements. A **special Board of Survey** is convened on a change of custody — when a cashier or storekeeper hands over — so that the incoming officer takes over a verified balance and neither officer can be blamed for the other's shortfall."},{"key":"The single sentence that best distinguishes them: a **Board of Survey verifies what is there**; a **Board of Enquiry investigates what is missing**."},{"note":"[Every document, both Boards and the full loss procedure, in one reference →](guides/stores-losses.html) — this is the most-repeated topic in the entire PS past-question bank."}]},{"n":"11.6","t":"The storekeeper, procurement and handing over","b":[{"h4":"Functions of the storekeeper"},{"ul":["Maintain proper accounting records that timely reflect transactions.","Arrange the store diligently and keep it clean.","Invite purchase requisitions from the departments that need stores.","Collect items from the supplier, checking they agree with the specification and price on the Local Purchase Order.","Update the bin/tally cards, and issue items only against a properly authenticated requisition.","Prepare inventory receipt and issue vouchers.","Ensure the store is adequately secured."]},{"h4":"Vouchers for the movement of stores"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Voucher","Use"],"rows":[["**Inventory transfer voucher**","Raised in duplicate by the requisitioning store where stores move between warehouses; the original goes to the issuing store, and an inventory issue voucher (one copy receipted and returned as the receipt voucher) accompanies the goods"],["**Inventory issue voucher**","Prepared in duplicate to support every issue against a properly signed requisition; the storekeeper posts the tally card at the time of issue"],["**Conversion voucher**","Evidences stores/materials issued **within the same store** for manufacture or conversion. The original copy doubles as the issue voucher for the finished article, which is returned to store and taken back on charge; both the receipt and the issue sides show quantities and values"]]}},{"h4":"Procedure for procuring stores"},{"steps":["The purchasing department obtains **approval from the officer controlling the vote**.","It carries out a **market survey** or obtains quotations/tenders from prospective suppliers, and fixes a **closing date** for submission.","A **Contract Tenders' Board** is constituted after the closing date to determine the lowest bidder and recommend the award.","The **Head of Department approves** the Board's recommendation.","A **Local Purchase Order** is issued to the successful contractor for supply within the agreed time."]},{"h4":"Sale of government property"},{"key":"**Government property must not be sold**, except where specifically authorised by the **Minister of Finance**. The selling price is the gross cost (for allocated stores) or the current issue price (for unallocated stores), plus a rateable store charge the Minister approves. It may be issued on payment only when the **head of the MDA is satisfied the sale is in the public interest**, and the inventory issue voucher supporting the release is not passed until the purchaser presents proof of payment to the schedule officer."},{"h4":"Handing over of stores"},{"p":"When custody passes to another officer, the incoming officer confirms the physical count agrees with the bin cards/store ledger; both officers then jointly sign **Store Form 10**. Where the outgoing officer is unavailable, an Inventory Verifier or Board of Survey does the check instead. The **outgoing officer answers for any discrepancy found at handover**; the **incoming officer answers for anything discovered later** that was not found at that time."}]},{"n":"11.7","t":"Cost of inventories and valuation methods","b":[{"p":"For unallocated stores, cost for accounting purposes is: for **imported** items, the invoice (FOB) price plus freight, inspection fees, marine insurance and customs duty; for **locally purchased** items, the full purchase price less any discount."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Valuation method","How it works"],"rows":[["**Fixed price method**","A hypothetical unit price is set (the arithmetic mean of stock values and known/estimated replacement price) and held fixed until the replacement cost varies"],["**Last known price method**","Items are taken on charge at the last known price when the full landed cost is not yet available"]]}},{"note":"Minimum records for stores accounting: a Purchases Journal (Inventory Cost Book), an Issues Journal (Inventory Issues Summary), and an Inventory Ledger with separate accounts for shortfalls/excesses (price adjustments) and for claims (settlements for damaged imported goods). The storekeeper keeps only the bin/tally card — never the ledger, which stays in the accounts section."}]},{"n":"11.8","t":"IPSAS 12 — Inventories","b":[{"def":{"t":"Inventories (IPSAS 12.9)","d":"assets held for sale in the ordinary course of operations, in production for such sale, to be consumed in producing goods or services, or held as materials/supplies awaiting use. In the **public sector** this extends to goods distributed to third parties free or for a nominal charge (e.g. free textbooks), and specifically includes ammunition, maintenance materials, spare parts, strategic stockpiles (energy reserves, hospital medicine), unissued currency and stamps, work-in-progress and property held for sale."}},{"h4":"What IPSAS 12 does not cover"},{"ul":["Work-in-progress under construction contracts.","Financial instruments.","Biological assets and agricultural produce at the point of harvest.","Work-in-progress on services to be rendered for no or nominal consideration.","**Measurement** (though not recognition/disclosure) of agricultural/forest/mineral inventories held at net realisable value, and of commodity broker-traders' inventories held at fair value less costs to sell, under established industry practice."]},{"key":"Inventories are measured at the **lower of cost and net realisable value** — except inventories held for distribution at no or nominal charge, or to be consumed in producing such goods, which are measured at the **lower of cost and current replacement cost** (there being no \"selling price\" for a free good)."},{"def":{"t":"Net realisable value","d":"the estimated selling price in the ordinary course of operations, less the estimated costs of completion and the estimated costs necessary to make the sale, exchange or distribution."}},{"h4":"Cost of inventories"},{"p":"Cost = **cost of purchase** + **cost of conversion** + other costs of bringing the item to its present location and condition."},{"ul":["**Cost of purchase** — purchase price, import duties and other non-recoverable taxes, and directly attributable transport/handling, less trade discounts and rebates.","**Cost of conversion** — direct costs (e.g. direct labour) plus a systematic allocation of **fixed** production overheads (e.g. factory depreciation and management — relatively constant regardless of volume) and **variable** production overheads (e.g. indirect materials/labour — vary with volume).","**Excluded and expensed instead**: abnormal waste; storage costs (unless needed before a further production stage); administrative overheads not contributing to bringing the item to its location/condition; and selling costs.","**Service provider's inventory cost** — mainly the labour and attributable overheads of personnel directly providing the service (including supervision); sales and general administration staff costs are expensed, not included."]},{"h4":"Cost formulas — assigning cost to items issued"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Formula","How it works"],"rows":[["**FIFO**","Assumes items are used/sold in the order received, so what remains at period end is the most recently purchased"],["**Weighted average**","The cost of each item is the purchase cost at the start of the period plus the purchase cost at the end, divided by two — calculated periodically or as each new shipment arrives"]]}},{"note":"The same cost formula must be used for all inventories of similar nature and use to the entity; a mere difference in geographic location is **not**, by itself, grounds for using a different formula."},{"h4":"Recognition and disclosure"},{"ul":["The carrying amount is expensed when the inventory is sold, exchanged or distributed (matched to the period the related revenue, if any, is recognised) — or, with no related revenue, when the goods are distributed or the service rendered.","Any **write-down** to net realisable/replacement value, and any inventory loss, is expensed in the period it occurs; a later **reversal** of a write-down reduces the expense in the period of reversal.","**Disclose:** the accounting policy and cost formula used; the total carrying amount and its classification; the carrying amount held at fair value less costs to sell; and any write-down reversal recognised in the period."]}]},{"n":"11.9","t":"Loss of government fund and the Federal Losses Committee","b":[{"h4":"Types of loss"},{"ul":["Misappropriation of funds.","Falsification of records.","Conversion of funds to personal use.","Fraudulent payments.","Theft.","Negligence.","Abandonment of revenue receivable.","Abandonment of an advance granted from recurrent expenditure.","Loss of cash."]},{"h4":"The accounting officer's own surcharge power"},{"p":"Where a **cash loss of ₦50,000 or less** occurs with no fraud involved, the accounting officer is personally empowered to **surcharge the responsible officer up to the full amount**, and is personally responsible for seeing that this is done. Losses involving an officer above Grade Level 10 must still be reported to the Accountant-General. Whichever way it is treated, the accounting officer sends a brief report to the **Chairman of the Federal Civil Service Commission, the Auditor-General, the Accountant-General and the Federal Ministry of Finance**."},{"p":"The officer in whose office the loss occurs must: report immediately to the head of unit/division (by the fastest means, if away from headquarters); report to the Police if fraud or theft is suspected; complete **Treasury Form 146** (\"Report on Loss of Funds or Inventories\") Part II, in quintuplicate, and forward it up; fix any control weakness found; and ensure the accounting entries are made."},{"def":{"t":"Federal Losses Committee","d":"a **standing committee** considering every case of loss of cash, stores and vehicles. Chaired by a representative of the **Auditor-General**, with representatives of the **Accountant-General**, the administration department of the ministry/agency concerned, the **Inspector-General of Police**, and the **EFCC**; the Inspectorate Department of the Accountant-General's office provides the secretariat."}},{"p":"It meets with officers of the Treasury, the Civil Service Commission and NIPOST to investigate reported losses of cash, stores, plant and equipment from theft, fraud or negligence (and on the death of a government debtor), examining the Board of Enquiry report and the relevant files, in order to **apportion blame** and recommend **surcharge, write-off or preventive measures**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Event","Debit","Credit"],"rows":[["Loss of cash","Non-Personal Advance A/c","Cash A/c"],["Fraudulent payment/overpayment, discovered same year","Non-Personal Advance A/c","Relevant sub-head"],["Same, but charged to CRF/Development Fund in a prior year","Non-Personal Advance A/c","Relevant sub-head"],["Same, but charged to another public fund (not CRF/DF) in a prior year","Non-Personal Advance A/c","Account originally debited"],["Abandoning recovery of an advance","Loss of Fund A/c","Advance A/c"],["Recovery of an amount earlier written off to a Non-Personal Advance","Cash/Bank A/c","Non-Personal Advance A/c"]]}},{"note":"A prior-year overpayment **not** involving fraud, charged to the CRF or DF, an abandoned bicycle advance from recurrent expenditure, and an abandoned unpaid revenue receivable, all need **no adjusting entry** — the Accountant-General simply recognises the loss."}]},{"n":"11.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Where a cash loss of ₦50,000 or less has occurred without fraud, the accounting officer's report of the loss must be sent to all of the following EXCEPT one: (A) The Chairman, Federal Civil Service Commission  (B) The Inspector-General of Police  (C) The Auditor-General for the Federation  (D) The Accountant-General of the Federation  (E) The Federal Ministry of Finance","The composition of the Federal Losses Committee includes the following, with the exception of one: (A) A representative of the Auditor-General for the Federation  (B) A representative of the Accountant-General of the Federation  (C) A representative of the Inspector-General of Police  (D) A representative of the Governor of the Central Bank  (E) A representative of the Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC)","The functions of the storekeeper include the following, with the exception of one: (A) Maintenance of proper accounting records that timely reflect transactions  (B) Diligent arrangement of the store  (C) Ensuring cleanliness of the store  (D) Invitation of purchase requisitions from the needy department  (E) Carrying out a market survey or obtaining quotations/tenders from a prospective supplier","Which of the following is NOT covered by the exceptions from IPSAS 12 for inventories? (1) Work-in-progress under a construction contract  (2) Assets to be consumed in the production of goods or services for sale  (3) Financial instruments  (4) Biological assets related to agricultural activity and produce at the point of harvest  (5) Work-in-progress on services to be provided for no or nominal consideration","The following, except one, are items on the debit side of the shortfall-and-excess account when treating unallocated inventories: (i) Claims written-off  (ii) Inventories written-off  (iii) Inventories found surplus and taken on charge  (iv) Revaluation of inventories  (v) Transfer to Unallocated Inventories Deficiencies sub-head","The estimated selling price in the ordinary course of operations, less the estimated costs of completion and of making the sale, exchange or distribution, is called …","The cost formula that adds the purchase cost of an item at the start of a period to its purchase cost at the end and divides by two is called …","The method of arriving at the value at which inventories are taken on charge, when the full landed cost is not yet available, is called …","A method used to refine inventory control to balance holding too much against too little inventory is called …","Inventories whose cost is chargeable to, and remains a charge on, the NCOA code for which funds were provided in the budget estimates are called …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** B  **2.** D  **3.** E  **4.** B  **5.** C  **6.** Net realisable value  **7.** Weighted average cost  **8.** Last known price method  **9.** Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)  **10.** Allocated inventories"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — actions on loss of government inventory","open":true,"q":[{"p":"In the event of a loss of government inventory, explain the actions to be taken by (i) the storekeeper, (ii) the head of department, and (iii) the accounting officer."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i) The storekeeper"},{"ol":["Report to the head of department.","Report to the nearest police station if fraud or theft is possible.","Complete Part I of Treasury Form 146 and forward it to the head of department.","Ensure any internal-control weakness found is fixed at once, to prevent recurrence."]},{"h4":"(ii) The head of department"},{"ol":["Forward brief details of the loss to the accounting officer.","Investigate the loss and complete Parts II and III of Treasury Form 146.","Recommend a Board of Enquiry to the accounting officer where warranted.","Strengthen any internal-control weakness identified.","Obtain and transmit copies of the police report or court proceedings to the accounting officer."]},{"h4":"(iii) The accounting officer"},{"p":"If the loss is **not significant**: complete Part IV of TF 146 and copy it to the Accountant-General, the Auditor-General, and the Head of the Accounts Department."},{"p":"If the loss is **material**: forward details to the Accountant-General and Auditor-General for follow-up; convene a Board of Survey if warranted; recommend suspension of the officer where disciplinary action is called for; write to the Accountant-General, the Auditor-General and the Federal Civil Service Commission with the police report and TF 146 attached; review and tighten the internal control system; and recoup the loss as the procedures require."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — a Board of Enquiry","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What circumstances would warrant setting up a Board of Enquiry? (b) State the contents of a Board of Enquiry's report."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** If fraud is probable; if the loss is substantial; if several officers are involved; if responsibility is not clearly defined; or if the loss took place over a period of time."},{"p":"**(b)** A statement of the exact amount lost; an assessment of whether the accounting system was faulty and any remedy to institute; recommendations to improve physical security where it was inadequate; and a recommendation on the extent of negligence of the officers responsible."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — loss of government inventory/fund","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What is loss of government inventory/fund? (b) List the types of losses that can arise in the public sector. (c) State the actions to be taken by the Head of Division where the officer concerned serves."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** A depletion of government fund at a given time."},{"p":"**(b)** Misappropriation of funds; falsification of records; conversion of funds to personal use; theft; negligence; abandonment of revenue receivable; abandonment of an advance from recurrent expenditure; and loss of cash."},{"p":"**(c)** The same actions as the head of department: forward details to the accounting officer, investigate and complete TF 146 Parts II and III, recommend a Board of Enquiry where warranted, correct any control weakness, and forward any police report or court proceedings."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — IPSAS 12 recognition and disclosure","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Explain the recognition of inventories as an expense. (b) State what IPSAS 12 requires the financial statements to disclose."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** When inventories are sold, exchanged or distributed, their carrying amount is recognised as an expense in the period the related revenue is recognised (or, if there is none, when the goods are distributed or the service rendered); a write-down or loss is expensed when it occurs, and a reversal of a write-down reduces the expense in the period of reversal."},{"p":"**(b)** The accounting policy and cost formula used; the total carrying amount of inventories and its classification; the carrying amount held at fair value less costs to sell; and the amount of any write-down reversal recognised in the period."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Closing stores balance","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Receipts} - \\text{Issues} \\pm \\text{Adjustments}"},{"lb":"Stock discrepancy","tex":"\\text{Physical count} - \\text{Ledger balance}"},{"lb":"Amount surchargeable","tex":"\\text{Loss} - \\text{amount recovered} - \\text{allowable wastage}"}],"focus":"A Section B question in perhaps half the diets, most often on the duties of a Board of Survey, the procedure on discovery of a loss, or the difference between a Board of Survey and a Board of Enquiry. Section A tests the bin card / store ledger distinction and the classification of stores. The procedure on a loss should be learnt as a numbered sequence of about eight steps.","errors":["Saying the bin card shows quantity and value; it shows quantity only, and is kept by the storekeeper.","Treating write-off as discharging the officer from liability; surcharge and write-off are separate decisions.","Confusing a Board of Survey (routine verification) with a Board of Enquiry (investigation of a specific loss).","Charging unallocated stores to the vote on purchase; they are charged on issue.","Omitting the report to the Auditor-General and the Police from the loss procedure.","Appointing to a Board of Survey an officer connected with the custody of the items being surveyed."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Stores purchased in bulk and held for issue to various votes as required are described as","o":["allocated stores","unallocated stores","expendable stores","non-expendable stores","obsolete stores"],"a":1,"w":"Unallocated stores are charged to the receiving vote on issue, not on purchase. Allocated stores are charged directly to a vote when bought.","src":"Chapter 11.1","sec":"11.1"},{"q":"The record kept by the storekeeper showing quantities only is the","o":["store ledger","bin card","store receipt voucher","inventory register","store issue voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The bin card records quantities at the bin. The store ledger, kept in the accounts section, records both quantity and value.","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"A body constituted to investigate a specific loss of cash or stores and determine responsibility for it is a","o":["Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry","Tenders Board","Procurement Planning Committee","Public Accounts Committee"],"a":1,"w":"A Board of Survey verifies what is present; a Board of Enquiry investigates what is missing.","src":"Chapter 11.5","sec":"11.5"},{"q":"A Board of Survey is convened by the","o":["Auditor-General for the Federation","Accountant-General of the Federation","Minister of Finance","Head of Civil Service of the Federation","Federal Civil Service Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Accountant-General of the Federation convenes a Board of Survey, mostly at the close of each financial year.","src":"Chapter 11.5","sec":"11.5"},{"q":"A Board of Survey consists of a President and not less than","o":["one other member","two other members","three other members","four other members","five other members"],"a":1,"w":"Three officers in all: a President (Grade Level 08 or higher) and not less than two members (not below Grade Level 06). A single-member board is exceptional and must be justified on the report.","src":"Chapter 11.5","sec":"11.5"},{"q":"Stores and materials issued within the same store for manufacturing or conversion must be supported by a","o":["inventory transfer voucher","conversion voucher","local purchase order","store form 10","bin card"],"a":1,"w":"A conversion voucher evidences the issue; its original copy also serves as the issue voucher for the finished article when it is returned to store.","src":"Chapter 11.6","sec":"11.6"},{"q":"Requiring a public officer to make good a loss caused by his negligence is known as","o":["write-off","surcharge","interdiction","virement","adjustment"],"a":1,"w":"Surcharge imposes personal financial liability. Write-off merely removes the irrecoverable amount from the books and does not absolve the officer.","src":"Chapter 11.4","sec":"11.4"},{"q":"A Board of Survey should be constituted","o":["only when a loss occurs","at the end of the financial year and on a change of custody of cash or stores","only when the Auditor-General so directs","every month","only when stores are declared unserviceable"],"a":1,"w":"The annual board supports the cash and stores figures in the financial statements; a special board on handing over protects both the outgoing and the incoming officer.","src":"Chapter 11.5","sec":"11.5"},{"q":"On discovery of a loss of public funds through suspected theft, the accounting officer must report to the Accountant-General, the Police and the","o":["Minister of Finance only","Auditor-General","National Assembly","Bureau of Public Procurement","Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Auditor-General must be informed so that the loss is examined independently of the department in which it occurred.","src":"Chapter 11.4","sec":"11.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Distinguish between a Board of Survey and a Board of Enquiry, stating the composition and duties of each. (b) Describe the procedure to be followed on the discovery of a loss of government stores. (c) Explain the terms \"surcharge\" and \"write-off\" and state the relationship between them.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Board of Survey and Board of Enquiry"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry"],"rows":[["Purpose","Routine verification of cash, bank balances and stores, and examination of items reported unserviceable or obsolete","Investigation of a particular loss of cash or stores, to establish its cause, extent and responsibility"],["Trigger","The end of the financial year, or a handing over of a cash office or store","The occurrence or discovery of a loss"],["Composition","Officers appointed by the accounting officer, none of whom has any connection with the custody of the items being surveyed","Senior officers independent of the office where the loss occurred, and where the matter is serious, officers from outside the entity"],["Nature","Verificatory and preventive","Investigative and, in effect, quasi-judicial"],["Output","A certificate of the balances found and recommendations on disposal","Findings on cause and responsibility, with recommendations on surcharge, discipline and prevention"]]}},{"p":"**Duties of a Board of Survey**"},{"ol":["Count the cash and postage stamps on hand and agree the total to the cash book balance.","Obtain bank certificates directly from the banks and examine the bank reconciliation statements.","Verify stores physically and compare the quantities found with the store ledgers and bin cards.","Examine items reported unserviceable, obsolete, damaged or surplus to requirements, and recommend disposal by public auction, sale by tender, transfer or destruction.","Satisfy itself as to the safe custody arrangements — safes, keys, security of the store and access controls.","Report any discrepancy found, with an explanation where one can be obtained, and comment on the adequacy of records and procedures.","Sign and submit the report to the accounting officer, with copies to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General."]},{"p":"**Duties of a Board of Enquiry**"},{"ol":["Establish the exact nature and amount of the loss.","Determine how and when it occurred, taking evidence from the officers concerned.","Establish whether the Financial Regulations and store procedures were complied with.","Determine whether negligence, misconduct or fraud on the part of any officer contributed to the loss, and identify that officer.","Assess whether any part of the loss is recoverable.","Recommend the amount, if any, to be surcharged, and any disciplinary or criminal action.","Identify the weakness in internal control that permitted the loss and recommend remedial measures."]},{"h4":"(b) Procedure on discovery of a loss of stores"},{"ol":["The officer discovering the loss reports it **immediately and in writing** to his supervising officer and to the accounting officer, stating the items and quantities involved and the circumstances.","The accounting officer reports the loss **at once to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General**, and to the **Police** where theft, burglary or fraud is suspected. Reporting outside the department is essential: it prevents the matter being concealed by those responsible for it.","The loss is entered in the **Register of Losses**, with the date, description, quantity, value and circumstances.","The scene and the records are **preserved**, and the officer suspected of responsibility may be **interdicted** so that he does not remain in control of the items or records concerned.","A **Board of Enquiry** is constituted to investigate, taking evidence and examining the store ledgers, bin cards, receipt and issue vouchers and stock verification reports.","The Board **reports** its findings on the cause of the loss, the extent of any negligence and the officer responsible, with recommendations.","Where negligence or misconduct is established, the officer is **surcharged** with the whole or part of the loss and is subject to disciplinary action; where the conduct is criminal, the matter is referred for prosecution.","Any irrecoverable balance is **written off** by the authority competent to do so according to the amount — the accounting officer, the Minister of Finance, or the National Assembly.","The **control weakness** disclosed is remedied, and the stores records are adjusted by a Store Adjustment Voucher so that the ledger agrees with the physical position."]},{"h4":"(c) Surcharge and write-off"},{"p":"**Surcharge** is the imposition on a public officer of personal liability to make good a loss of public funds or stores which arose from his negligence, misconduct or failure to comply with the Financial Regulations. The amount may be recovered from his salary, his allowances or ultimately his terminal benefits. It is a measure directed at the **officer**."},{"p":"**Write-off** is the formal removal of an irrecoverable loss from the books, authorised by the officer or body competent to approve it for the amount involved. It is an accounting measure directed at the **records**, and is necessary so that the ledgers reflect the true position."},{"p":"**The relationship between them.** They are independent decisions and neither implies the other. A loss may be written off because it cannot be recovered from anyone — as with a fire — without any officer being surcharged. Conversely an officer may be surcharged and the loss still written off, because writing off clears the record while the surcharge pursues the officer. It is precisely because write-off does **not** discharge liability that the two are considered separately: if it did, an officer's easiest defence would be to procure a write-off."}],"src":"Chapter 11.4–11.5","sec":"11.4"}]}},{"n":12,"t":"Local Government Accounting","brief":"The constitutional position of local government, its functions and sources of revenue, the State Joint Local Government Account, the Model Financial Memoranda, the departmental structure and the officers responsible for its finances.","outcomes":["State the constitutional basis of local government and its functions","Identify the sources of local government revenue","Explain the State Joint Local Government Account and the Joint Account Allocation Committee","Describe the Model Financial Memoranda and their status","Describe the departmental structure of a local government and the duties of the Treasurer","Discuss the problems of local government finance"],"secs":[{"n":"12.1","t":"Constitutional position and functions","b":[{"p":"Section 7(1) of the 1999 Constitution guarantees a **system of local government by democratically elected local government councils**, and requires each State to ensure their existence, structure, composition, finance and functions by law. There are **774** local government areas listed in the First Schedule."},{"h4":"Main functions (Fourth Schedule, paragraph 1)"},{"ol":["Consideration and making of recommendations to the State commission on economic planning on the economic development of the State.","Collection of **rates** and of radio and television licences.","Establishment and maintenance of **cemeteries, burial grounds** and homes for the destitute or infirm.","Licensing of **bicycles, trucks** (other than mechanically propelled trucks), **canoes, wheelbarrows and carts**.","Establishment, maintenance and regulation of **slaughter houses and slabs, markets, motor parks and public conveniences**.","Construction and maintenance of **roads, streets, street lighting, drains, parks, gardens and open spaces**.","**Naming of roads and streets and numbering of houses**.","Provision and maintenance of **public conveniences, sewage and refuse disposal**.","**Registration of births, deaths and marriages**.","**Assessment of privately owned houses or tenements** for the purpose of levying rates.","Control and regulation of **out-door advertising and hoardings**, movement and keeping of pets, shops and kiosks, restaurants and other places for the sale of food, laundries, and the licensing and control of the sale of liquor."]},{"h4":"Participatory functions (Fourth Schedule, paragraph 2)"},{"p":"Performed jointly with the State Government:"},{"ul":["The provision and maintenance of **primary, adult and vocational education**;","The development of **agriculture and natural resources** other than the exploitation of minerals;","The provision and maintenance of **health services**; and","Such other functions as the State House of Assembly may confer."]}]},{"n":"12.2","t":"Sources of revenue","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Basis"],"rows":[["**Statutory allocation from the Federation Account**","20.60% of the distributable Federation Account, shared among the 774 councils on the horizontal formula. Normally the largest single source, often exceeding 80% of total revenue"],["**Share of value added tax**","35% of the VAT pool, shared among the councils"],["**Ten per cent of State internally generated revenue**","Section 162(7) of the Constitution requires each State to pay to its local government councils such proportion of its total revenue as may be prescribed; the figure commonly applied is 10% of the State's internally generated revenue"],["**Tenement rates**","Levied on privately owned houses and tenements assessed by the council. Potentially the largest local source and almost everywhere the most under-exploited"],["**Licences and fees**","Bicycle, truck, canoe, wheelbarrow and cart licences; liquor licences; shops and kiosks rates; slaughter slab fees; market and motor park fees; radio and television licences; registration of births, deaths and marriages; naming of streets; advertising and signboard permits; customary burial ground permits"],["**Earnings from commercial undertakings**","Rent from council property, income from transport services, guest houses and other ventures"],["**Grants**","From the Federal or State Government, often matching grants tied to a capital project"],["**Loans**","With the approval of the State Government, and subject to the Fiscal Responsibility framework"],["**Fines and penalties**","Imposed by customary courts and by council regulation"]]}},{"key":"The distinguishing feature of local government revenue is **dependence**. Where a council draws 80% or more of its income from a monthly allocation over which it has no influence, it cannot plan, its finances move with the oil price, and the political incentive to develop the tenement rate — the one substantial source genuinely within its control — is very weak."}]},{"n":"12.3","t":"The State Joint Local Government Account","b":[{"p":"Section 162(6) requires each State to maintain a special account called the **State Joint Local Government Account**, into which are paid all allocations to the local government councils of the State from the Federation Account and from the Government of the State."},{"ul":["Section 162(5): the amount standing to the credit of local government councils in the Federation Account is allocated to the States **for the benefit of** their local government councils.","Section 162(8): the amount standing to the credit of local government councils of a State is distributed among them **on such terms and in such manner as may be prescribed by the House of Assembly of the State**.","The **Joint Account Allocation Committee (JAAC)**, comprising representatives of the State and of the councils, meets monthly to determine and effect the distribution."]},{"warn":"The Joint Account is the most criticised provision in local government finance. Because the money passes through the State, deductions are frequently made before distribution — for primary school teachers' salaries, for state-wide projects, and in some cases for purposes never accounted for. Several councils have litigated the point. Candidates should know both the constitutional basis and the criticism, since discussion questions almost always ask for both."}]},{"n":"12.4","t":"Financial regulation and structure","b":[{"def":{"t":"Model Financial Memoranda","d":"The comprehensive code of financial and accounting procedure for local government, issued by the States and modelled on a national template. It performs for local government the function the Financial Regulations perform for the Federal Government, covering budgeting, revenue collection, expenditure control, stores, losses, the cash book, the treatment of vouchers and the preparation of the final accounts."}},{"h4":"Political and administrative structure"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Organ or officer","Role"],"rows":[["**Chairman**","Chief executive and accounting officer of the council"],["**Vice-Chairman**","Deputises for the Chairman"],["**Supervisory Councillors**","Political heads of the departments, appointed by the Chairman"],["**Legislative Council (Councillors)**","Approves the annual estimates and the by-laws, and exercises oversight over the executive"],["**Secretary to the Local Government**","Co-ordinates the administration and the departments"],["**Treasurer / Director of Finance**","The professional head of the finance department; responsible for the accounts"],["**Head of Personnel Management**","Human resources and general administration"],["**Internal Auditor**","Reports to the Chairman; appraises the internal control system continuously"],["**Local Government Service Commission**","A State body responsible for appointment, promotion and discipline of local government staff"]]}},{"h4":"Departments of a local government"},{"ul":["**Administration and General Services** — personnel, records, legislative support.","**Finance and Supplies** — budget, accounts, revenue, stores.","**Works, Housing and Transport** — roads, drains, street lighting, buildings.","**Education, Social Welfare and Community Development**.","**Agriculture and Natural Resources**.","**Primary Health Care**."]},{"h4":"Duties of the Local Government Treasurer"},{"ol":["Maintains the books of account of the council and ensures they comply with the Model Financial Memoranda.","Prepares the annual **estimates** in consultation with the departments and the Chairman.","Prepares the **monthly and annual financial statements** and the transcripts required by the State.","Controls the **collection of revenue** and ensures it is banked promptly and intact.","Ensures all **payments are properly authorised**, vouched and within the vote.","Maintains the **vote book** and advises the Chairman when a vote is nearing exhaustion.","Ensures the **safe custody** of council funds and the security of receipt books and other accountable documents.","Advises the Chairman and the council on **all financial matters**.","Liaises with the **auditors** — the State Auditor-General for Local Governments — and responds to queries."]},{"note":"Local government accounts are audited by the **Auditor-General for Local Governments** of the State, an office distinct from the State Auditor-General, who reports to the State House of Assembly through the Local Government Service Commission or as the State law prescribes."}]},{"n":"12.5","t":"Problems of local government finance","b":[{"ol":["**Over-dependence on statutory allocation.** Most councils raise less than 10% of their income themselves, so their finances rise and fall with federally collected revenue and they cannot plan with confidence.","**Deductions from the Joint Account.** First-line charges for teachers' salaries and state projects frequently leave little of the allocation for the council's own functions, and the deductions are not always transparent.","**Weak internally generated revenue.** The tenement rate, the largest local source, is undeveloped almost everywhere because properties are not enumerated or valued.","**Shortage of qualified staff.** Councils, particularly rural ones, struggle to attract and retain accountants, engineers and revenue officers.","**Poor record keeping**, so that revenue due cannot be identified and expenditure cannot be properly accounted for.","**Political interference** in the appointment of officials and in the award of contracts, and in several States the replacement of elected councils with appointed caretaker committees, contrary to section 7(1).","**Recurrent expenditure dominance.** Personnel costs consume the greater part of most councils' revenue, leaving little for the capital projects that justify the tier's existence.","**Weak audit and oversight.** Audit reports are frequently in arrears and rarely acted on, and legislative councils often lack the capacity to scrutinise the accounts.","**Corruption and leakage**, particularly in revenue collected in cash at markets and motor parks."]},{"p":"**Remedies** commonly proposed:"},{"ul":["Direct payment of the allocation to councils, abolishing or reforming the Joint Account;","Enumeration and valuation of properties for tenement rating;","Automation of revenue collection to eliminate cash handling;","Financial autonomy backed by the constitutional amendment guaranteeing tenure for elected councils;","Strengthening of the internal audit function; and","Capacity building for finance staff."]}]},{"n":"12.6","t":"The Model Financial Memoranda: objectives and contents","b":[{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["Serve as **administrative guidelines** facilitating the day-to-day running of local governments.","Spell out the implications of **disbursing government funds or property without proper authority**, approval or justification.","Facilitate recording local government financial transactions in the appropriate accounting method.","Serve as a **learning tool** for officers newly appointed or transferred to a new section."]},{"h4":"Contents"},{"ul":["The format of the budget and of budgetary control.","The financial responsibilities of the Chairman and other accounting officers.","The responsibilities of the Secretary, Treasurer and Heads of Department.","The Internal Auditor's responsibilities, including the **Audit Alarm** function.","The powers and functions of the Auditor-General for Local Government.","The functions and operation of the **Audit Alarm Committee**.","Financial offences and their sanctions.","The means of revenue collection and control.","The main books of account kept.","The custody, accounting and control of stores."]}]},{"n":"12.7","t":"Expenditure and its accounting treatment","b":[{"p":"Council expenditure is **recurrent** (personnel costs; maintenance, repairs, fuel, rent, electricity and water — the day-to-day running of its affairs) or **capital** (constructing roads, motor parks, toilet facilities), each classified to the main heads and the appropriate NCOA code, departmentally."},{"ul":["Amounts disbursed under an NCOA code within a Head are aggregated at year end; the number of sub-heads under a Head varies and can exceed twenty.","Capital expenditure is **written off in the year incurred** — only a memorandum entry is kept for tangible assets, so the statement of financial position discloses **no** information on the tangible assets a council has acquired.","There is no capital/proprietorship interest shown, as there would be in a private organisation.","The excess of receipts over payments is the **general revenue balance** (not \"profit\"), transferred to the statement of financial position.","The statement of financial position accordingly shows only current assets and liabilities."]}]},{"n":"12.8","t":"Local government final accounts (IPSAS cash basis)","b":[{"p":"Local government uses the **cash basis**, as the Federal and State tiers do: income is recognised when cash is received, expenditure when the liability is paid. Each council prepares and publishes:"},{"ol":["A **declaration of responsibility** by the Treasurer, that the statements follow the Finance (Control and Management) Act Cap 144 LFN 1990 and generally accepted accounting practice.","The **Auditor-General's certificate** on whether the statements give a true and fair view.","**Statement No. 1 — Statement of Cash Flow**, in the three usual activities (operating, investing, financing), with operating receipts split into IGR, grants/subventions, VAT, statutory revenue allocation and miscellaneous, and payments into personal emoluments, pensions/gratuities, CRF charges, overheads, public debt charges and subsidies.","**Statement No. 2 — Statement of Financial Position** (Assets and Liabilities): liquid assets (cash and bank), investments/advances/other cash assets, against public funds (the general revenue balance), external/internal loans and deposits.","**Statement No. 3 — Statement of Financial Performance** (Revenue and Expenditure): revenue by source (rates, fines/fees/licences, earnings and sales, rent, interest, taxes, statutory allocation) less expenditure by department (general administration, health, works, education, agriculture, grants, capital projects), each column showing budget, actual and variance, down to an **operating balance**."]}]},{"n":"12.9","t":"Financial control, spending limits, grants-in-aid and fee policy","b":[{"h4":"Financial control"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Internal controls","External controls"],"rows":[["Issuance of financial authorities (e.g. supplementary warrants)","Legislative control — National and State Assemblies"],["Committees for different services","Federal and State Executive control"],["Centralisation of all payments","Public comment and scrutiny"],["Standing orders on cheque-signing and payments on account","External audit — Auditors-General for Local Government, for the State, and for the Federation"],["Internal audit",""],["Preparation of income/expenditure estimates",""],["Budgetary control and feedback",""]]}},{"h4":"Other limitations facing councils"},{"ul":["Cannot raise a tax or introduce a new one without the **State Government's express permission**.","Cannot raise or maintain loans without permission — which limits capital projects.","Delayed or non-payment of Federal/State grants or oil-revenue shares.","Non-viability of some councils, especially those with small populations.","Rising costs and demand for services, against ineffective financial and management control."]},{"h4":"Spending limits (expenditure approval ceilings, by IGR band)"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["IGR band","Chairman","Vice-Chairman","Head of Personnel Mgt","Head of Department"],"rows":[["Above ₦2m","₦250,000","₦50,000","₦10,000","₦5,000"],["₦1m – ₦2m","₦100,000","₦20,000","₦5,000","₦3,000"],["Below ₦1m","₦50,000","₦10,000","₦3,000","₦2,000"]]}},{"ul":["Every approval must be **reported within a week** to a higher officer.","The approving officer is **personally liable** for what he approves.","Approval is always subject to normal **budgetary appropriation**.","A contract above the council's limit needs the **Ministry of Local Government's** approval."]},{"h4":"Types of grant"},{"def":{"t":"Specific grant","d":"a discretionary financial assistance given to finance a **particular, named project** — e.g. building a community school. Also called **programme or project support**."}},{"def":{"t":"General grant","d":"a grant not tied to any identified activity, used at the recipient's own discretion — also called **\"budget support\"**. Where several donors pool their resources this way, the fund is called **\"multi-donor budget support\" (MDBS)**."}},{"note":"The study text's own wording of these two definitions is internally inconsistent — it describes a \"specific grant\" as **not** for any particular purpose, immediately followed by an example of a named, particular project. The definitions above follow the examples given (and the examiner's own answer key), which is the only reading consistent with the illustrations: **specific = tied to one named project; general = untied, discretionary**."},{"h4":"Objectives of the grants-in-aid system"},{"ol":["Augment the resources of local government councils.","Meet the exceptional needs, or the limited means, of a particular area.","Assist central control over particular services.","Influence aggregate local government expenditure as part of the control process."]},{"h4":"Fee-charging policy"},{"p":"The Treasurer advises on service charges, considering: the **level of development** of the area; the **status of the people** in the community; the **nature of the service** rendered; and the **skill and technical competence** needed to perform it."},{"h4":"Budgeting and budgetary control"},{"p":"The Executive prepares the budget for Legislative approval, assented to by the Council Chairman. Control procedures: **approval** before spending; **monthly reports** comparing actual to budget and extracting variances; **corrective action** on the variances found; **internal audit** verifying the integrity of the accounts; **external audit** by the Auditor-General for Local Government; and individual **expenditure limits** that officers must not exceed."}]},{"n":"12.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is NOT a considerable influence of States over local governments as contained in the Fourth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution? (A) Issuance of licences in respect of motorcycles, cars, bicycles and keeping of pets  (B) Registration of births, marriages and deaths  (C) Naming of streets, roads and crescents and numbering of houses  (D) Provision and maintenance of basic facilities for refuse disposal and public conveniences  (E) Collection of personal income taxes and withholding taxes","One of the following is NOT in the Executive arm of a local government (A) The Executive Chairman  (B) The Secretary  (C) The Cashier  (D) Head of Personnel Management  (E) The Legislature","The following, except one, are duties of the Secretary of a local government council now performed by the Head of Personnel Management: (A) Co-ordinates the operations of the various departments and represents the Chairman as directed at high-level meetings  (B) Liaises with the Secretary to the State Government and other important dignitaries on matters of interest to local government councils  (C) Handles the disbursement of funds on various contracts awarded by the council  (D) Maintains the record of proceedings of meetings  (E) Performs other assignments delegated by the Chairman","The Accounting Officer of the Local Government is ONE of the following: (i) Secretary to the Local Government  (ii) Treasurer  (iii) The Chairman  (iv) Leader of the House  (v) Head of Personnel Management","Election of leaders in the council is performed by ONE of the following: (i) Chairman  (ii) State Governor  (iii) Electorate at the poll  (iv) Council Clerk  (v) The Councillors","The publication by the Federal Government containing administrative guidelines, the existing systems of checks and balances, and the role of all officers in a local government is called …","Budgetary control and feedback process in local government finance is one of the measures of …","Proceeds from loans and other borrowings, in Statement No. 1 (Cash Flow Statement), is an item under …","The share of the Statutory Allocation going to local government councils under the Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act, Cap A15 LFN 2005, is …","Vetting and monitoring the implementation of projects and programmes in the local government council's annual budget is the prerogative of …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** C  **3.** C  **4.** C (the Chairman)  **5.** E (electorate at the poll)  **6.** Financial Memoranda for Local Government  **7.** Internal controls  **8.** Cash flows from financing activities  **9.** 20.60%  **10.** The Council Legislature"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — the Financial Memoranda","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Local Government Financial Memoranda guides all financial activities in local government. (i) Enumerate the objectives of the Memoranda. (ii) List the contents of the Local Government Financial Memoranda."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i) Objectives** — see §12.6 above: administrative guidelines for day-to-day running; highlighting the implications of unauthorised disbursement; facilitating correct recording of transactions; and serving as a learning tool for new or transferred officers."},{"p":"**(ii) Contents** — see §12.6 above: budget/budgetary-control format; financial responsibilities of the Chairman and accounting officers; responsibilities of the Secretary, Treasurer and Heads of Department; the Internal Auditor's responsibilities and the Audit Alarm function; the Auditor-General for Local Government's powers; the Audit Alarm Committee; financial offences and sanctions; revenue collection and control; the main books of account; and the custody, accounting and control of stores."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — functions of the Local Government Treasurer (1988 Reform)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Local Government Treasurer oversees all financial matters of a local government. Enumerate his functions as specified in the Civil Service and Local Government Reform of 1988."}],"a":[{"ol":["Rendering financial advice to the council.","Serving as secretary to the Budget Committee.","Receiving and disbursing money for authorised ends.","Keeping proper accounting records of money collected or utilised.","Verifying the accuracy and integrity of all accounting records.","Ensuring compliance with financial instructions/laws for safe custody of council money.","Ensuring vouchers are correctly made out and funds are available in the appropriate vote of charge.","Rendering the statutory returns required by the State and Federal Governments.","Ensuring all revenue belonging to the council is collected when due.","Ensuring fiscal policies are executed and expenditure incurred with due diligence.","Maintaining effective and well-staffed financial operations.","Keeping up-to-date statistical information for prompt, accurate reporting.","Submitting recommendations to the council as financial adviser.","Serving as a signatory to the council's bank account and other disbursements.","Offering expert opinion on short-, medium- and long-term bases."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — statutory and permissive revenue sources","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Local governments in Nigeria run on revenue from both statutory and permissive sources. Enumerate (i) the statutory sources, and (ii) the permissive sources."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i) Statutory sources:** statutory allocation from the Federation Account (20.60% of federally collected revenue, paid directly to councils); 10% of the State's internally generated revenue; and fees/charges imposed by the council under its instrument of creation and applicable legislation."},{"p":"**(ii) Permissive sources:** shop and kiosk rates; tenement rates; on- and off-liquor licence fees; slaughter slab fees; birth/marriage/death registration fees; street-naming registration fees (outside the State capital); rural right-of-occupancy fees; market taxes and levies; motor park levies; domestic animal licence fees; bicycle/truck/canoe/wheelbarrow/cart fees; cattle tax; merriment and road-closure levies; radio/TV licence fees; vehicle radio licence fees; wrong-parking charges; public-convenience and refuse-disposal fees; customary burial-ground permit fees; religious-place establishment permit fees; and signboard/advertisement permit fees."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — the new statutory financial statements","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Enumerate the new statutory financial statements to be prepared and published by each local government."}],"a":[{"ol":["The Treasurer's declaration of responsibility for the financial statements.","The Auditor-General's certificate on whether they give a true and fair view.","Statement No. 1 — Statement of Cash Flow.","Statement No. 2 — Statement of Financial Position (Assets and Liabilities).","Statement No. 3 — Statement of Financial Performance (Revenue and Expenditure)."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Local government share of Federation Account","tex":"20.60\\% \\times \\text{distributable pool}"},{"lb":"Local government share of VAT","tex":"35\\% \\times \\text{VAT pool}"},{"lb":"Share of State revenue (s.162(7))","tex":"10\\% \\times \\text{State internally generated revenue}"}],"focus":"A Section B question in perhaps a third of diets, usually on the sources of local government revenue, the functions under the Fourth Schedule, the duties of the Treasurer, or the problems of local government finance. Section A tests the number of local governments, the Joint Account, and which section of the Constitution guarantees elected councils. Learn the Fourth Schedule functions as a list of at least eight.","errors":["Saying local government allocations are paid directly to the councils; they pass through the State Joint Local Government Account.","Confusing the Financial Regulations (federal) with the Model Financial Memoranda (local government).","Naming the Secretary to the Local Government as the accounting officer; it is the Chairman.","Giving the local government share of VAT as 20.60%; that is the Federation Account share, while VAT is 35%.","Listing participatory functions (education, health, agriculture) as though they were exclusive functions of the council; they are shared with the State."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The number of local government areas listed in the First Schedule to the 1999 Constitution is","o":["768","774","776","750","780"],"a":1,"w":"There are 774 local government areas, together with the six area councils of the Federal Capital Territory.","src":"Chapter 12.1","sec":"12.1"},{"q":"Allocations to local government councils from the Federation Account are paid into the","o":["council's own account","State Joint Local Government Account","Consolidated Revenue Fund of the State","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund"],"a":1,"w":"Section 162(6) requires each State to maintain the Joint Account, from which the Joint Account Allocation Committee distributes to the councils.","src":"Chapter 12.3","sec":"12.3"},{"q":"A discretionary financial assistance given to finance a particular project is known as","o":["a grant","non-matching grant","a specific grant","a general grant","an aid"],"a":2,"w":"A specific grant (also called programme or project support) is tied to a named project. A general grant — \"budget support\" — is untied and used at the recipient's own discretion.","src":"Chapter 12.9","sec":"12.9"},{"q":"The accounting officer of a local government council is the","o":["Treasurer","Chairman","Secretary to the Local Government","Head of Personnel Management","Supervisory Councillor for Finance"],"a":1,"w":"The Chairman is the chief executive and accounting officer; the Treasurer is the professional head of the finance department.","src":"Chapter 12.4","sec":"12.4"},{"q":"The code of financial and accounting procedure applicable to local government is the","o":["Financial Regulations","Model Financial Memoranda","Treasury Circulars","Store Regulations","Establishment Circulars"],"a":1,"w":"The Model Financial Memoranda perform for local government the function the Financial Regulations perform for the Federal Government.","src":"Chapter 12.4","sec":"12.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is an exclusive function of a local government council under the Fourth Schedule?","o":["Provision of secondary education","Naming of roads and numbering of houses","Provision of tertiary health care","Exploitation of minerals","Registration of companies"],"a":1,"w":"Naming of roads and numbering of houses is listed in paragraph 1. Primary education and health services are participatory functions shared with the State.","src":"Chapter 12.1","sec":"12.1"},{"q":"Section 7(1) of the 1999 Constitution guarantees","o":["the Joint Account Allocation Committee","a system of local government by democratically elected councils","the 20.60% share of the Federation Account","the Model Financial Memoranda","the office of the Treasurer"],"a":1,"w":"Section 7(1) guarantees democratically elected councils and requires each State to provide for their existence, structure, composition, finance and functions by law.","src":"Chapter 12.1","sec":"12.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State eight functions of a local government council under the Fourth Schedule to the 1999 Constitution. (b) Identify the sources of revenue available to a local government council. (c) State eight duties of the Local Government Treasurer. (d) Discuss the principal problems of local government finance in Nigeria and suggest remedies.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Functions under the Fourth Schedule"},{"ol":["Consideration and making of recommendations to the State commission on economic planning on the economic development of the State.","Collection of rates and of radio and television licences.","Establishment and maintenance of cemeteries, burial grounds and homes for the destitute or infirm.","Licensing of bicycles, trucks other than mechanically propelled trucks, canoes, wheelbarrows and carts.","Establishment, maintenance and regulation of slaughter houses and slabs, markets, motor parks and public conveniences.","Construction and maintenance of roads, streets, street lighting, drains, parks, gardens and open spaces.","Naming of roads and streets and numbering of houses.","Provision and maintenance of public conveniences, sewage and refuse disposal.","Registration of all births, deaths and marriages.","Assessment of privately owned houses or tenements for the purpose of levying rates.","Control and regulation of out-door advertising, shops and kiosks, restaurants and other places for the sale of food, laundries, and the sale of liquor."]},{"p":"In addition, councils perform **participatory functions** jointly with the State:"},{"ul":["Primary, adult and vocational education;","The development of agriculture and natural resources other than minerals; and","The provision and maintenance of health services."]},{"h4":"(b) Sources of revenue"},{"ol":["**Statutory allocation from the Federation Account** — 20.60% of the distributable pool, shared among the councils and normally the largest source by a wide margin.","**Share of value added tax** — 35% of the VAT pool.","**Share of State internally generated revenue** — under section 162(7), commonly 10% of the State's IGR.","**Tenement rates** on privately owned houses and tenements assessed by the council.","**Licences and fees** — bicycle, truck, canoe, wheelbarrow and cart licences; liquor licences; shops and kiosks rates; market and motor park fees; slaughter slab fees; registration of births, deaths and marriages; advertising permits.","**Earnings from commercial undertakings** — rent from council property, transport services, guest houses.","**Grants** from the Federal or State Government, frequently tied to a specified capital project.","**Loans**, with the approval of the State Government.","**Fines and penalties** imposed under council by-laws and by customary courts."]},{"h4":"(c) Duties of the Treasurer"},{"ol":["Maintaining the books of account of the council in the form prescribed by the Model Financial Memoranda.","Preparing the annual estimates in consultation with the departments and the Chairman, for approval by the legislative council.","Preparing the monthly and annual financial statements and the transcripts required by the State.","Controlling the collection of revenue and ensuring that it is receipted and banked promptly and intact.","Ensuring that every payment is properly authorised, supported by a voucher, and within the vote.","Maintaining the vote book and advising the Chairman when a vote is nearing exhaustion.","Ensuring the safe custody of council funds and the security of receipt books and other accountable documents.","Advising the Chairman and the council on all financial matters, including the financial implications of proposed decisions.","Liaising with the Auditor-General for Local Governments and responding to audit queries."]},{"h4":"(d) Problems and remedies"},{"p":"**Problems**"},{"ol":["**Over-dependence on statutory allocation.** Most councils generate less than a tenth of their revenue internally, so their finances follow federally collected revenue and ultimately the oil price. A council cannot plan a capital programme on an income it cannot forecast.","**Deductions from the Joint Account.** Because allocations pass through the State, first-line deductions for primary school teachers' salaries and for state-wide projects often absorb most of the allocation before it reaches the council, and the deductions are frequently not transparent.","**Undeveloped internally generated revenue.** Tenement rating, potentially the largest local source, requires enumeration and valuation of properties that most councils have never carried out.","**Shortage of qualified personnel.** Rural councils in particular cannot attract accountants, engineers and valuers, so neither the revenue nor the expenditure side is competently managed.","**Poor records and weak internal control**, so that revenue due is not identified and cash collections at markets and motor parks leak substantially.","**Political interference**, including the appointment of caretaker committees in place of elected councils contrary to section 7(1), which removes the accountability of councils to their own electorates.","**Dominance of recurrent expenditure.** Personnel costs consume the greater part of revenue in most councils, leaving little for the services that justify the tier.","**Weak audit and oversight.** Audit reports are often years in arrears and rarely acted upon, and legislative councils frequently lack the capacity to scrutinise them."]},{"p":"**Remedies**"},{"ol":["**Reform the Joint Account** so that allocations reach councils directly, or at least so that every deduction is agreed at the Joint Account Allocation Committee and published.","**Enumerate and value properties** and develop the tenement rate, which is the only substantial revenue source genuinely within a council's control.","**Automate revenue collection**, paying into a single council account through electronic channels and abolishing cash handling by collectors — the measure that closes leakage most effectively.","**Guarantee the autonomy and tenure of elected councils**, so that councils are answerable to their electorates rather than to the State executive.","**Strengthen the finance function** — recruit and train qualified accountants and revenue officers, and resource the internal audit unit properly.","**Enforce timely audit and legislative scrutiny**, with sanctions where accounts are not submitted.","**Control the recurrent burden** by verifying the payroll and eliminating ghost workers, so that a larger share of revenue reaches capital projects."]}],"src":"Chapter 12.1–12.5","sec":"12.1"}]}},{"n":13,"t":"Cash Book, Transcripts and Subsidiary Accounts","brief":"The treasury cash book and the distinction between above-the-line and below-the-line transactions, the subsidiary accounts for advances, deposits and remittances, the monthly transcript rendered by a sub-accounting officer, and bank reconciliation.","outcomes":["Distinguish above-the-line from below-the-line transactions","Record transactions in a treasury cash book and extract the balance","Explain and account for advances, deposits and remittances","State the contents of a monthly transcript and its supporting schedules","Prepare a bank reconciliation statement for a government office"],"secs":[{"n":"13.1","t":"Above the line and below the line","b":[{"p":"Every transaction passing through a government cash book is either **above the line** or **below the line**. The \"line\" is the boundary of the revenue and expenditure account: transactions above it change the surplus or deficit for the year, and transactions below it do not."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Above the line","Below the line"],"rows":[["Nature","Revenue and expenditure of the year","Movements on personal and impersonal accounts that create or discharge an asset or a liability"],["Effect on the accounts","Enters the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments as revenue or expenditure","Enters the Statement of Assets and Liabilities; does not affect revenue or expenditure"],["Examples","Taxes, fees, fines, rents; personnel cost, overhead cost, capital expenditure","Advances, deposits, remittances, investments, unallocated stores"],["Recorded in","Revenue and expenditure heads and subheads","Subsidiary (below-the-line) accounts"]]}},{"key":"The test is whether the transaction is **the government's own income or expense**, or merely **money held for or owed by someone else**. A deposit received as security for a contract is not revenue: it must be returned. An advance to an officer is not expenditure: it must be repaid."}]},{"n":"13.2","t":"The subsidiary accounts","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Account","Nature","Balance represents"],"rows":[["**Advances**","Money paid out that will be repaid or accounted for — salary advances, touring advances, advances to contractors, imprest advances","A **debit** balance: an **asset**, being amounts recoverable"],["**Deposits**","Money received that will be repaid or applied for a specified purpose — tender and performance deposits, retention money, court deposits, unclaimed salaries","A **credit** balance: a **liability**, being amounts repayable"],["**Remittances**","Transfers of funds between government offices — from the Treasury to a sub-treasury and back, or between sub-treasuries","A **contra** item that must clear on consolidation; a balance means a transfer is in transit or unrecorded at one end"]]}},{"h4":"Advances"},{"ul":["An advance may be granted only where the Financial Regulations authorise it, and within the limits prescribed.","Every advance must be **recorded in the Advances Ledger** in the name of the officer or body concerned.","It must be **retired or repaid** within the period stipulated — a touring advance within a set number of days of return, a salary advance by monthly deduction.","An advance not retired when due is recovered from salary, and an officer who fails to retire an advance may not be granted another.","At the year end, outstanding advances appear as an **asset** in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities, and irrecoverable advances are written off only with the authority competent to approve the amount."]},{"h4":"Deposits"},{"ul":["Deposits are recorded in the **Deposits Ledger** in the name of the depositor.","They may be applied only for the purpose for which they were received, and are otherwise refundable.","A deposit **unclaimed for the period prescribed** in the Financial Regulations is transferred to revenue, but remains repayable if the depositor subsequently establishes the claim.","Deposits outstanding at the year end appear as a **liability**."]},{"warn":"A deposit is **never** treated as revenue merely because the money is in hand, and an advance is never treated as expenditure merely because the money has gone out. Doing so overstates both the revenue and the expenditure of the year and misstates the assets and liabilities."}]},{"n":"13.3","t":"The treasury cash book","b":[{"p":"The cash book is the book of prime entry for all receipts and payments at a treasury or sub-treasury. It is columnar, with separate columns for cash and bank on each side, and analysis columns for revenue heads, expenditure heads and each of the below-the-line accounts. It is **balanced daily** and the balance agreed with the cash actually held."},{"eg":{"t":"Summarising a sub-treasury cash book","q":[{"p":"The following transactions passed through the cash book of the Ilorin Sub-Treasury during the month of March 2026:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","₦"],"rows":[["Balance at 1 March — cash on hand","150,000"],["Balance at 1 March — bank","4,250,000"],["Fees collected","1,800,000"],["Fines collected","420,000"],["Rents collected","630,000"],["Deposits received from contractors","350,000"],["Advances repaid by officers","180,000"],["Remittances received from Headquarters Treasury","12,000,000"],["Personnel cost paid","8,400,000"],["Overhead cost paid","2,650,000"],["Advances granted to officers","500,000"],["Deposits refunded","120,000"],["Remittances to Headquarters Treasury","2,850,000"]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 31 March was ₦150,000. Prepare a summary of the cash book distinguishing above-the-line from below-the-line items, and determine the closing bank balance."}],"a":[{"h4":"Summary of receipts and payments"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash book summary — March 2026","rows":[["Balance brought forward (cash 150,000 + bank 4,250,000)",4400000],["**Receipts**@t",null],["Fees",1800000],["Fines",420000],["Rents",630000],["Deposits received",350000],["Advances repaid",180000],["Remittances from Headquarters",12000000],["Total receipts@sub",15380000],["**Payments**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-8400000],["Overhead cost",-2650000],["Advances granted",-500000],["Deposits refunded",-120000],["Remittances to Headquarters",-2850000],["Total payments@sub",-14520000],["Balance carried forward@tot",5260000]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 31 March is ₦150,000, so the **closing bank balance** is $5{,}260{,}000 - 150{,}000 = ₦5{,}110{,}000$."},{"h4":"Classification"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Receipts (₦)","Payments (₦)"],"rows":[["**Above the line**","",""],["Revenue — fees, fines and rents","2,850,000",""],["Expenditure — personnel and overhead cost","","11,050,000"],["**Below the line**","",""],["Advances","180,000","500,000"],["Deposits","350,000","120,000"],["Remittances","12,000,000","2,850,000"],["**Total**","**15,380,000**","**14,520,000**"]]}},{"p":"The net movements below the line for the month are: advances **₦320,000 net granted** (an increase in the asset); deposits **₦230,000 net received** (an increase in the liability); and remittances **₦9,150,000 net received** from headquarters."},{"note":"A useful check: the net increase in cash of ₦860,000 must equal revenue less expenditure, adjusted for the below-the-line movements: $2{,}850{,}000 - 11{,}050{,}000 - 320{,}000 + 230{,}000 + 9{,}150{,}000 = ₦860{,}000$. ✓"}]}}]},{"n":"13.4","t":"The monthly transcript","b":[{"def":{"t":"Transcript","d":"The monthly return rendered by a sub-accounting officer to the Accountant-General (or by a self-accounting unit to its headquarters), consisting of a copy of the cash book for the month together with all supporting schedules and documents, by which the transactions of the outstation are brought into the central accounts."}},{"h4":"Contents of a transcript"},{"ol":["The **cash book** for the month, cast, balanced and certified.","A **schedule of revenue collected**, analysed by head and subhead, supported by the receipt vouchers and treasury receipts.","A **schedule of expenditure**, analysed by head and subhead, supported by the paid payment vouchers in serial order.","A **schedule of advances** — opening balance, advances granted, advances repaid, closing balance, with the names of the officers concerned.","A **schedule of deposits** — opening balance, deposits received, deposits refunded, closing balance, with the names of the depositors.","A **schedule of remittances** in and out, with the numbers of the transfer advices.","A **bank reconciliation statement** with the bank statement and, where required, a bank certificate of balance.","A **certificate of cash and stamps** on hand at the close of the month.","A statement of **unpaid and outstanding vouchers**.","The **certificate of the sub-accounting officer** that the transcript is correct and complete."]},{"h4":"Purposes of the transcript"},{"ul":["To bring the outstation's transactions into the **central accounts** of the Federation or of the ministry.","To enable the Treasury to **check and verify** the transactions and the balances of the outstation.","To provide the basis of the **consolidated financial statements**.","To provide **control** over sub-accounting officers, since the transcript must be rendered by a fixed date each month and delay is a disciplinary matter.","To supply the **audit evidence** on which the Auditor-General relies for the outstation."]},{"note":"The transcript is the mechanism by which the accounts of a geographically dispersed government are consolidated. Where transcripts are rendered late — a perennial complaint in the Auditor-General's reports — the annual accounts of the Federation cannot be closed on time, which is why the Financial Regulations treat late rendition as a serious offence."}]},{"n":"13.5","t":"Bank reconciliation","b":[{"p":"The balance in the cash book will rarely agree with the balance on the bank statement at the same date. The reconciliation identifies the reasons and establishes the correct balance."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cause of difference","Treatment"],"rows":[["Cheques or mandates issued but not yet presented","Deduct from the bank statement balance"],["Lodgements made but not yet credited by the bank","Add to the bank statement balance"],["Bank charges, commission and interest debited by the bank","Deduct in the **adjusted cash book**"],["Interest or direct credits received by the bank","Add in the **adjusted cash book**"],["Dishonoured cheques","Deduct in the adjusted cash book"],["Errors in the cash book","Correct in the adjusted cash book"],["Errors by the bank","Adjust against the bank statement balance"]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Bank reconciliation for the sub-treasury","q":[{"p":"Continuing the Ilorin example, the cash book bank balance at 31 March 2026 was ₦5,110,000 and the bank statement showed ₦5,640,000. Investigation revealed: mandates issued but not presented ₦680,000; lodgements not yet credited ₦140,000; bank charges not recorded in the cash book ₦18,000; and interest credited by the bank and not recorded ₦8,000. Prepare the adjusted cash book and the bank reconciliation statement."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Adjusted cash book (bank column)","rows":[["Balance per cash book",5110000],["Add: Interest credited by the bank",8000],["Less: Bank charges not recorded",-18000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",5100000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Bank reconciliation statement as at 31 March 2026","rows":[["Balance per bank statement",5640000],["Less: Mandates issued but not presented",-680000],["Add: Lodgements not yet credited",140000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",5100000]]}},{"p":"The two statements agree at **₦5,100,000**, which is the figure to be carried into the transcript and into the Statement of Assets and Liabilities. The unadjusted cash book figure of ₦5,110,000 is not the correct balance, because the bank charges and interest were genuine transactions of the month that the office had not yet recorded."},{"key":"Two rules govern every reconciliation. **Items the office knows about but the bank has not yet processed** — unpresented mandates and uncredited lodgements — are adjusted against the **bank statement**. **Items the bank has processed but the office has not yet recorded** — charges, interest, direct credits, dishonoured cheques — are adjusted in the **cash book**, because they are real entries that were simply omitted."}]}}]},{"n":"13.6","t":"The cash office and cash control","b":[{"p":"The cash office is a **sensitive department** — only diligent, trustworthy, experienced, security-conscious staff should work there."},{"h4":"Essential features"},{"p":"A paying cage/cubicle; a notice of working hours; a cash tank or safe under **dual control**; a notice restricting entry to authorised staff; security personnel; CCTV; a security alarm; a counting machine; a UV/mercury light (for checking currency); a computer system; and a fire alarm."},{"h4":"Functions"},{"ul":["Receiving and paying out liquid cash, and its safe custody.","Maintaining a conventional cash book for all cash transactions, balanced **daily**.","Operating the ministry's current account on which cheques are drawn.","Reconciling the bank statement with the cash book, monthly or as required.","Submitting the original cash book and voucher copies to the accounts department for the final accounts."]},{"note":"A **contra entry** records cash moving between the office and the bank — cash withdrawn (by cheque, without a payment voucher) or surplus cash banked above the authorised maximum. The **cheque/mandate summary register** cross-checks every cheque, lodgement, bank advice and teller against the cash book, and must be **posted by someone other than the cashbook keeper**, as an internal check."},{"h4":"Security documents and retention"},{"p":"Cheque books, Treasury Receipt Books 6/6A, the cheque summary register, cash books, payment vouchers, local purchase orders, postal and money orders are all **security documents** — kept in a safe/strong room under dual-key control. Retention: financial warrants, cash books and PE records — **permanently**; revenue collectors' cash books and original payment vouchers — **7 years**; LPOs and transport-warrant book copies — **2 years**. Unclaimed cheques are written back after **six months**."},{"h4":"Cash control measures"},{"ul":["Cash limits; daily banking of all takings; periodic surprise cash counts; a safe under dual control; a raid alarm; counting/sorting machines and mercury light.","Adequate insurance over the cash limit; investment of idle funds; an authority limit; daily balancing of the cash book; and regular bank reconciliation."]}]},{"n":"13.7","t":"Imprest and the imprest holder","b":[{"def":{"t":"Imprest holder","d":"an officer, other than a sub-accounting officer, entrusted with disbursing public money for which a voucher cannot be presented immediately to a sub-accounting officer — and who must keep an Imprest Holder's Cash Book."}},{"def":{"t":"Imprest","d":"cash advanced to an officer to meet urgent, budgeted expenditure whose vouchers cannot be prepared and presented immediately for payment."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Nature"],"rows":[["**Standing imprest**","A general imprest running the whole fiscal year, reimbursed as needed; must be **retired by 31 December**"],["**Special imprest**","Raised for a specific purpose; terminated and retired **immediately the purpose is achieved**"]]}},{"h4":"Checks and balances"},{"ul":["The imprest holder must be **Grade Level 04 or above**, of proven integrity.","Imprest money is used for **no other purpose**, and there is **no lending** to employees from it.","The imprest cash book is balanced regularly, and the sub-accounting officer checks it regularly for anomalies.","Any balance must be **retired at the fiscal year end**.","₦50,000 or more must be **banked** by the holder in an account opened in his official capacity.","Every disbursement needs proper authorisation/approval and a **payment voucher** — vouchers are classified straight to the expenditure heads affected, **not** kept under an \"imprest\" head as in the private sector."]},{"p":"On **reimbursement**, the imprest holder presents all payment vouchers for the money spent to the sub-accounting officer before the imprest is topped up."}]},{"n":"13.8","t":"The revenue collector","b":[{"def":{"t":"Revenue collector","d":"a government officer holding an official receipt book (TF6, TF6A, or similar) to collect specified budgeted revenue items. He **may not spend from his collections** — he accounts for them intact."}},{"h4":"Functions"},{"ul":["Supervises the receipt of public revenue, ensuring prompt banking.","Promptly reflects all monies collected under the proper heads and subheads.","Ensures proper custody of public funds and securities, and supervises staff entrusted with receipts, custody or disbursement.","Maintains internal checks against malpractice.","Checks all cash and stamps in his care against the cash book and stamps register.","Makes good any minor deficit not caused by theft/fraud, and reports it in writing to the appropriate officer."]},{"p":"His cash book's debit side records each receipt (date, receipt number, NCOA code, payer, amount); the credit side records remittances of total collections to the sub-accounting officer (daily, weekly, monthly or as directed), each acknowledged with a **treasury receipt** and logged in the **cash remittance register**. Anything not yet remitted at month end is carried forward."}]},{"n":"13.9","t":"Advances: personal and non-personal","b":[{"p":"Advances granted and authorised by the Minister of Finance are also used to write off a loss of government fund. **Chapter 14 of the Financial Regulations** (2009) governs their grant."},{"def":{"t":"Non-personal advance","d":"granted to an officer to carry out a task for the organisation (e.g. off-site staff training) — authorised by the Minister of Finance through the Accountant-General, though the Accounting Officer may approve up to ₦50,000. Must be retired within a reasonable time, or the whole sum is deducted from salary en bloc; a fresh non-personal advance may not be granted while an earlier one is unretired."}},{"h4":"Personal advances (for the officer's own benefit)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","When granted"],"rows":[["**Salary/rent advance**","Returning from leave of 21+ days and proceeding on transfer; assuming first appointment outside official quarters; returning to Nigeria by sea to a station other than Lagos; or posted to an overseas Foreign Affairs office"],["**Correspondence-course advance**","The officer's ability warrants the course; it relates to his work and will raise his efficiency; the institution is reputable; completing it isn't itself grounds for promotion; repayable interest-free over 24 instalments; receipts must prove the money was properly used"],["**Estacode advance (overseas tour)**","Drawn as traveller's cheques from the CBN against a Government mandate; the officer accounts for it afterwards with receipts and air tickets"]]}}]},{"n":"13.10","t":"Self-accounting, sub-self-accounting and non-self-accounting units","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Unit","Control over its own records","Example"],"rows":[["**Self-accounting unit**","Full control; relates to the Treasury only by rendering transcripts","Ministries of Finance, Works, Education"],["**Sub-self-accounting unit**","Same functions, but sends the Treasury the original cash book, duplicate vouchers, a certificate of cash/bank balances, a pre-listed voucher schedule and the bank reconciliation","The Federal Pay Office in each State"],["**Non-self-accounting unit**","No control at all — prepares vouchers but pays through the Treasury; keeps no Treasury Cash Book and renders no transcript","A State Code of Conduct Bureau"]]}},{"p":"A Ministry becomes **self-accounting** only once it has adequate qualified personnel, a functioning internal control system, and an internal audit department."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of self-accounting","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Relieves top management of workload; speeds up operational decisions","Co-ordination and consistency can be difficult to achieve"],["Increases flexibility, reduces communication problems","Extended communication lines can cause information overload"],["Motivates staff, encourages initiative; trains junior management","Risk of duplicated services and sub-optimality (ministerial goals over government goals); assumes managers of a quality that may not be available; inter-departmental friction"]]}},{"h4":"Transcripts: three types"},{"ul":["**Main (cash) transcript** — the monthly transcript a self-accounting unit submits to the Accountant-General.","**Supplementary transcript** — the double-entry adjustment to the main transcript.","**Subsidiary transcript** — corrects errors or omissions in the main transcript."]},{"p":"Preparing a transcript: obtain the cash book and all vouchers posted for the month and check them; **pre-list** vouchers by head/subhead; **post the totals** into an analysis book, reconciling with the cash book; **schedule** vouchers by serial number and gross amount; then **generate the transcript** itself, opening with the prior month's balance carried forward and closing with the balance carried down, which must agree with the cash book."},{"def":{"t":"Main ledger","d":"kept by a self-accounting MDA for below-the-line and other Accountant-General-controlled accounts — cash, imprest, internal bank adjustment, deposit, personal advances, non-personal advances and cash-transfer accounts — posted and balanced monthly, and **reconciled with the Treasury general ledger**, any difference being flagged for follow-up."}}]},{"n":"13.11","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The following, except one, are essential features of a cash office: (i) Paying cage or cubicle  (ii) Close-circuit monitor  (iii) Cash tank or safe with dual control key  (iv) Means of convenience for toileting  (v) Counting machine","Which of the following cannot be classified as a security document in government? (i) Cheque book  (ii) Cash flow statement  (iii) Cash book  (iv) Payment voucher  (v) Cheque summary register","The following items, except one, should be posted into the Cheque/Mandate Summary Register: all cheques issued; money lodged into the bank account; vouchers raised to cover bank advices; total payroll bills of the local government council's staffers; teller particulars","Which of the following documents is NOT required for preparing a bank reconciliation statement? Cash book and cheque summary register; bank statements; cheque stubs; debit and credit advices; list of contractors","Salary and rent advances are granted to officers under the following conditions, except ONE: (A) An officer on official duty lost either of his parents  (B) An officer is returning from leave of not less than 21 days and/or proceeding on transfer bearing his own transport cost  (C) An officer is assuming first appointment and is not living in government residential quarters  (D) An officer is returning to Nigeria by sea to be stationed elsewhere other than Lagos  (E) An officer is posted to an overseas office of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs","A register that serves as a useful record for verifying bank transactions in the cash book is called …","Cash credits granted to individual officers in their ministries/parastatals/departments, or given to carry out a specified task and to be retired later, are called …","An MDA that has no control whatsoever over any of its accounting records is called …","A summary of the total receipts and payments as posted in the cash book is called …","The ledger book used to record all receipts and payments of any nature is called …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** D  **2.** B  **3.** D  **4.** E (list of contractors)  **5.** A  **6.** Cheque/Mandate Summary Register  **7.** Advances  **8.** Non-self-accounting unit  **9.** Transcript  **10.** Treasury cash book"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — cash control measures","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Control of cash is a major activity required of MDAs. List the various cash control measures they adopt."}],"a":[{"ol":["Establishment of cash limits.","Daily banking of all takings.","Periodic surprise cash counts (cash survey).","A safe under dual control.","A raid alarm.","Counting/sorting machines and mercury light.","Adequate insurance cover over the cash limit.","Investment of idle funds.","Establishment of an authority limit.","Balancing of the cash book.","Preparation of bank reconciliation statements."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — procedures for bank reconciliation","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Enumerate the key procedures for preparing a bank reconciliation statement."}],"a":[{"ol":["Extract the cash book balance at the end of the month.","Have the previous month's reconciliation to hand, for the outstanding unpresented cheques and uncredited lodgements.","Tick cash-book debit entries against bank-statement credit entries, and cash-book credit entries against bank-statement debit entries.","Extract the un-ticked items into schedules: A — unpresented cheques; B — credits in the bank not in the cash book; C — uncredited lodgements; D — debits in the bank not in the cash book.","Prepare the reconciliation statement from the schedules."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — controlling imprest and the imprest holder","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Keeping an imprest is a sensitive role that may lead to fraudulent practice. Enumerate the checks and balances that may be instituted to control imprest and the imprest holder."}],"a":[{"ol":["The holder must be Grade Level 04 or above, of proven integrity.","Imprest money is used for no other purpose.","The imprest cash book is balanced regularly.","The sub-accounting officer checks it regularly for anomalies.","Any balance is retired at the end of the fiscal year.","₦50,000 or more is banked in an account opened in the holder's official status.","Every disbursement is properly authorised and approved.","Payment vouchers are raised for all disbursements.","There is no lending to employees from imprest money."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — self-accounting units","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) State the conditions for a ministry to become a self-accounting unit. (b) List the advantages and disadvantages of a self-accounting unit."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Adequate qualified personnel; an adequate, functional internal control system; and an internal audit department."},{"p":"**(b) Advantages:** relieves top management of workload; speeds operational decision-making; increases flexibility and reduces communication problems; motivates staff and encourages initiative; and provides better junior-management training."},{"p":"**Disadvantages:** co-ordination can be hard to achieve; extended communication lines cause information overload; consistency is harder; services may be duplicated; ministerial sub-optimality (a ministry's goals overriding overall government objectives); it assumes well-qualified managers who may not be available; and inter-departmental friction where functions depend on each other."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Closing cash balance","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Receipts} - \\text{Payments}"},{"lb":"Net advance movement","tex":"\\text{Advances granted} - \\text{Advances repaid}"},{"lb":"Net deposit movement","tex":"\\text{Deposits received} - \\text{Deposits refunded}"},{"lb":"Adjusted cash book","tex":"\\text{Cash book} + \\text{credits omitted} - \\text{debits omitted}"},{"lb":"Reconciliation","tex":"\\text{Bank statement} - \\text{unpresented} + \\text{uncredited} = \\text{adjusted cash book}"}],"focus":"One of the two or three most reliable Section B preparation questions in the paper. The usual form is a list of transactions from which you must prepare a cash book summary or a transcript, classify items above and below the line, and produce a bank reconciliation. Marks are given line by line, so present the work in proper statement form with headings and a total, even where you are unsure of one or two classifications.","errors":["Treating deposits received as revenue, or advances granted as expenditure.","Adjusting bank charges against the bank statement instead of in the cash book.","Netting remittances in and out instead of showing both, which loses the audit trail.","Omitting the certificate of cash and stamps and the bank reconciliation from the contents of a transcript.","Failing to deduct cash on hand when deriving the bank balance from a combined cash book total.","Leaving the reconciliation to end at the unadjusted cash book figure rather than the adjusted one."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is a below-the-line transaction?","o":["Payment of salaries","Receipt of a contractor's performance deposit","Collection of market fees","Payment for stationery consumed","Collection of tenement rates"],"a":1,"w":"A deposit is repayable, so it creates a liability rather than revenue and does not affect the surplus or deficit for the year.","src":"Chapter 13.1","sec":"13.1"},{"q":"An advance granted to an officer and outstanding at the year end appears in the financial statements as","o":["expenditure","an asset","a liability","revenue","a contingent liability"],"a":1,"w":"The advance is recoverable, so it is an asset until it is retired or repaid.","src":"Chapter 13.2","sec":"13.2"},{"q":"In a bank reconciliation, bank charges not recorded in the cash book are","o":["added to the bank statement balance","deducted in the adjusted cash book","deducted from the bank statement balance","added in the adjusted cash book","ignored"],"a":1,"w":"The bank has already processed the charge; it is the office that has not recorded it, so the cash book must be adjusted.","src":"Chapter 13.5","sec":"13.5"},{"q":"The monthly return by which a sub-accounting officer brings his transactions into the central accounts is called a","o":["warrant","transcript","voucher","mandate","schedule of losses"],"a":1,"w":"The transcript comprises the cash book for the month together with all supporting schedules and certificates.","src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"A cash book shows a bank balance of ₦2,400,000. Unpresented cheques are ₦350,000 and uncredited lodgements ₦120,000. There are no other differences. The bank statement balance is","o":["₦2,170,000","₦2,630,000","₦2,870,000","₦1,930,000","₦2,400,000"],"a":1,"w":"Work backwards: the bank statement balance less unpresented cheques plus uncredited lodgements equals the cash book balance.","calc":"\\text{Bank} = 2{,}400{,}000 + 350{,}000 - 120{,}000 = ₦2{,}630{,}000","src":"Chapter 13.5","sec":"13.5"},{"q":"Remittances between government offices are recorded","o":["as revenue at the receiving office and expenditure at the sending office","below the line at both offices, clearing on consolidation","as expenditure at both offices","only at the Headquarters Treasury","in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments as revenue"],"a":1,"w":"A remittance is an internal transfer of the government's own money. Treating it as revenue and expenditure would inflate both figures on consolidation.","src":"Chapter 13.2","sec":"13.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following transactions passed through the cash book of the Bauchi Sub-Treasury for the month of June 2026:\n\nBalance at 1 June: cash ₦200,000, bank ₦3,600,000. Receipts: court fees ₦940,000; motor licence fees ₦1,260,000; fines ₦180,000; deposits received ₦420,000; advances repaid ₦150,000; remittances from Headquarters Treasury ₦9,000,000. Payments: personnel cost ₦6,800,000; overhead cost ₦1,940,000; advances granted ₦360,000; deposits refunded ₦95,000; remittances to Headquarters ₦1,500,000.\n\nCash on hand at 30 June was ₦200,000. The bank statement at 30 June showed ₦5,080,000. Mandates issued but not presented amounted to ₦480,000, lodgements not yet credited ₦230,000, and bank charges of ₦25,000 had not been recorded in the cash book.\n\n(a) Prepare a summary of the cash book for the month. (b) Classify the transactions into above-the-line and below-the-line items. (c) Prepare the adjusted cash book and the bank reconciliation statement at 30 June 2026. (d) State six items that must accompany the transcript for the month.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Cash book summary"},{"stmt":{"t":"Bauchi Sub-Treasury — cash book summary for June 2026","rows":[["Balance brought forward (200,000 + 3,600,000)",3800000],["**Receipts**@t",null],["Court fees",940000],["Motor licence fees",1260000],["Fines",180000],["Deposits received",420000],["Advances repaid",150000],["Remittances from Headquarters Treasury",9000000],["Total receipts@sub",11950000],["**Payments**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-6800000],["Overhead cost",-1940000],["Advances granted",-360000],["Deposits refunded",-95000],["Remittances to Headquarters Treasury",-1500000],["Total payments@sub",-10695000],["Balance carried forward@tot",5055000]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 30 June is ₦200,000, so the cash book **bank balance** is $5{,}055{,}000 - 200{,}000 = ₦4{,}855{,}000$."},{"h4":"(b) Classification"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Receipts (₦)","Payments (₦)"],"rows":[["**Above the line — revenue**","",""],["Court fees","940,000",""],["Motor licence fees","1,260,000",""],["Fines","180,000",""],["","**2,380,000**",""],["**Above the line — expenditure**","",""],["Personnel cost","","6,800,000"],["Overhead cost","","1,940,000"],["","","**8,740,000**"],["**Below the line**","",""],["Advances","150,000","360,000"],["Deposits","420,000","95,000"],["Remittances","9,000,000","1,500,000"],["","**9,570,000**","**1,955,000**"],["**Total**","**11,950,000**","**10,695,000**"]]}},{"p":"The revenue of the month is therefore **₦2,380,000** and the expenditure **₦8,740,000**, giving a deficit for the month of ₦6,360,000 which was financed by the net remittance of ₦7,500,000 from headquarters. Below the line, advances increased by ₦210,000 (an asset) and deposits by ₦325,000 (a liability)."},{"p":"Check: $2{,}380{,}000 - 8{,}740{,}000 - 210{,}000 + 325{,}000 + 7{,}500{,}000 = ₦1{,}255{,}000$, which equals the increase in cash from ₦3,800,000 to ₦5,055,000. ✓"},{"h4":"(c) Adjusted cash book and reconciliation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Adjusted cash book (bank column)","rows":[["Balance per cash book",4855000],["Less: Bank charges not recorded",-25000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",4830000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Bank reconciliation statement as at 30 June 2026","rows":[["Balance per bank statement",5080000],["Less: Mandates issued but not presented",-480000],["Add: Lodgements not yet credited",230000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",4830000]]}},{"p":"Both statements arrive at **₦4,830,000**, which is the bank figure to be carried into the transcript and into the Statement of Assets and Liabilities. Together with cash on hand of ₦200,000, total cash and bank at 30 June is ₦5,030,000."},{"warn":"If the two sides of a reconciliation do not agree, the difference is never to be forced or written off. It means a further item exists that has not been identified — an unrecorded lodgement, a second unpresented mandate, or an arithmetical error — and the sub-accounting officer must find it before the transcript is rendered."},{"h4":"(d) Items accompanying the transcript"},{"ol":["The **cash book** for the month, cast, balanced and certified by the sub-accounting officer.","A **schedule of revenue collected**, analysed by head and subhead, with the treasury receipts and receipt vouchers.","A **schedule of expenditure**, analysed by head and subhead, with the paid payment vouchers in serial order.","A **schedule of advances** showing the opening balance, advances granted, advances repaid and the closing balance, with the names of the officers concerned.","A **schedule of deposits** showing the opening balance, deposits received, deposits refunded and the closing balance, with the names of the depositors.","A **schedule of remittances** in and out, with the transfer advice numbers.","The **bank statement, bank certificate and bank reconciliation statement**.","A **certificate of cash and postage stamps** on hand at the close of the month.","A statement of **unpaid and outstanding vouchers**."]}],"src":"Chapter 13.3–13.5","sec":"13.3"}]}},{"n":14,"t":"Statutory Financial Statements — Cash Basis IPSAS","brief":"The financial statements required under the cash basis IPSAS and the Nigerian templates: the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments, the Statement of Assets and Liabilities, the fund statements, the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts, and the notes.","outcomes":["List the general purpose financial statements required under cash basis IPSAS","Prepare a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","Prepare a Statement of Assets and Liabilities","Prepare a Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts and interpret the variances","State the notes and disclosures required","Explain the limitations of cash basis reporting"],"secs":[{"n":"14.1","t":"The required statements","b":[{"note":"[Cash vs accrual, every statement side by side →](guides/statements.html) — what each statement reports under each basis, the modified-basis combinations, and every past exam question on the topic with answers."},{"p":"The **Cash Basis IPSAS**, *Financial Reporting Under the Cash Basis of Accounting*, requires an entity to present a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments together with accounting policies and explanatory notes, and encourages comparison with the budget. The Nigerian templates issued under the National Chart of Accounts prescribe the following set."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Statement","Content"],"rows":[["**Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments** (Statement of Cash Flow)","All cash received and paid during the period, classified by nature, showing the opening and closing cash and cash equivalents"],["**Statement of Assets and Liabilities**","Cash, investments, advances and other assets against deposits and other liabilities, and the net assets represented by the fund balances"],["**Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund**","Opening balance, revenue, recurrent expenditure and transfers, giving the closing balance on the CRF"],["**Statement of Capital Development Fund**","Opening balance, transfers from the CRF, capital receipts, capital expenditure and the closing balance"],["**Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts**","Original budget, final budget, actual amounts on a comparable basis, and the variance, with explanations of material differences"],["**Notes to the financial statements**","Statement of accounting policies and supporting analyses of every principal figure"]]}},{"key":"The **Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts** is peculiar to the public sector and is required under both cash and accrual IPSAS. Because the budget is a legal authority rather than a management plan, reporting against it is an obligation, not a convenience. Candidates who omit it from a list of required statements lose an easy mark."}]},{"n":"14.2","t":"The Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","b":[{"p":"This is the principal statement under the cash basis. It reports **only cash movements**: revenue is recognised when received and expenditure when paid, whatever period the transaction relates to."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","q":[{"p":"The following balances were extracted from the records of the Government of Kwara State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (all figures in ₦'000):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","₦'000"],"rows":[["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account","48,600,000"],["Value added tax allocation","14,200,000"],["Internally generated revenue — taxes","9,800,000"],["Internally generated revenue — fees, fines and licences","3,400,000"],["Grants received","1,200,000"],["Other receipts","640,000"],["Personnel cost — salaries, wages and allowances","28,500,000"],["Social contributions — pension and gratuity","3,100,000"],["Overhead cost","12,700,000"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges — public debt service and statutory transfers","6,900,000"],["Capital expenditure","21,400,000"],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026","3,150,000"]]}},{"p":"Prepare the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Kwara State — Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**RECEIPTS**@t",null],["Statutory allocation",48600000],["Value added tax allocation",14200000],["Internally generated revenue — taxes",9800000],["Internally generated revenue — fees, fines and licences",3400000],["Grants received",1200000],["Other receipts",640000],["Total receipts@sub",77840000],["**PAYMENTS**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-28500000],["Social contributions",-3100000],["Overhead cost",-12700000],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges",-6900000],["Capital expenditure",-21400000],["Total payments@sub",-72600000],["Net cash flow for the year@t",5240000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",3150000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",8390000]]}},{"note":"Note the discipline of the statement: it begins with receipts, deducts payments, and reconciles to the movement in cash. The closing figure of ₦8,390,000 thousand must equal the cash and bank balances in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities — the articulation check to perform before submitting any answer."}]}}]},{"n":"14.3","t":"The Statement of Assets and Liabilities","b":[{"p":"Under the cash basis this statement is limited: it shows the **financial** assets and liabilities arising from cash transactions — cash, investments, advances, deposits — but **no property, plant and equipment, no receivables and no payables**, because those are not recognised on a cash basis."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Assets and Liabilities","q":[{"p":"Continuing the Kwara State example, the following further balances stood at 31 December 2026 (₦'000): advances outstanding ₦1,240,000; investments ₦2,600,000; deposits held ₦980,000. The Consolidated Revenue Fund balance carried forward was ₦6,850,000. Prepare the Statement of Assets and Liabilities."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Kwara State — Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**ASSETS**@t",null],["Cash and cash equivalents",8390000],["Investments",2600000],["Advances",1240000],["Total assets@sub",12230000],["**LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Deposits",-980000],["Total liabilities@sub",-980000],["NET ASSETS@tot",11250000],["**REPRESENTED BY**@t",null],["Consolidated Revenue Fund",6850000],["Capital Development Fund",4400000],["Total funds@tot",11250000]]}},{"p":"The Capital Development Fund balance of ₦4,400,000 thousand is the balancing figure: net assets of ₦11,250,000 less the CRF balance of ₦6,850,000."},{"warn":"A candidate who includes buildings, roads, vehicles or trade payables in a **cash basis** Statement of Assets and Liabilities has misunderstood the basis. Those items appear only under accrual IPSAS, in the Statement of Financial Position (Chapter 16). This is the single most common error in the question."}]}}]},{"n":"14.4","t":"The Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","b":[{"p":"The statement compares the **final budget** with the **actual amounts on a comparable basis** and discloses the variance. Where the original and final budgets differ — because of a supplementary appropriation or virement — both are shown. Material variances must be explained, either on the face of the statement or in the notes."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","q":[{"p":"The final budget of Kwara State for 2026 provided for receipts of ₦81,500,000 thousand and payments of the same amount, made up as follows (₦'000): statutory allocation 52,000,000; VAT 13,000,000; IGR taxes 11,000,000; IGR other 3,000,000; grants 2,000,000; other receipts 500,000. Payments: personnel cost 29,000,000; social contributions 3,200,000; overhead cost 13,500,000; CRF charges 7,000,000; capital expenditure 28,800,000. Prepare the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts and comment on the performance."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Final budget ₦'000","Actual ₦'000","Variance ₦'000"],"rows":[["**RECEIPTS**","","",""],["Statutory allocation","52,000,000","48,600,000","(3,400,000)"],["Value added tax allocation","13,000,000","14,200,000","1,200,000"],["IGR — taxes","11,000,000","9,800,000","(1,200,000)"],["IGR — fees, fines and licences","3,000,000","3,400,000","400,000"],["Grants received","2,000,000","1,200,000","(800,000)"],["Other receipts","500,000","640,000","140,000"],["**Total receipts**","**81,500,000**","**77,840,000**","**(3,660,000)**"],["**PAYMENTS**","","",""],["Personnel cost","29,000,000","28,500,000","500,000"],["Social contributions","3,200,000","3,100,000","100,000"],["Overhead cost","13,500,000","12,700,000","800,000"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","7,000,000","6,900,000","100,000"],["Capital expenditure","28,800,000","21,400,000","7,400,000"],["**Total payments**","**81,500,000**","**72,600,000**","**8,900,000**"],["**Net**","**—**","**5,240,000**","**5,240,000**"]]}},{"p":"A variance on **receipts** is favourable when actual exceeds budget; a variance on **payments** is favourable when actual is below budget. Both are shown here as budget less actual for payments and actual less budget for receipts, with adverse amounts in brackets."},{"h4":"Comment on performance"},{"ul":["**Revenue underperformed by ₦3.66 billion (4.5%).** The shortfall is concentrated in statutory allocation, which came in ₦3.4 billion below budget, reflecting lower federally collected revenue than the benchmark assumed. VAT, in contrast, exceeded budget by ₦1.2 billion.","**Internally generated revenue reached 94.3% of budget** ($13{,}200 \\div 14{,}000$), with the shortfall in taxes partly offset by better performance on fees and licences. This is respectable but leaves the State drawing about 81% of its revenue from federation sources.","**Capital expenditure achieved only 74.3% of budget** ($21{,}400 \\div 28{,}800$), an underspend of ₦7.4 billion. This is by far the largest variance and is the one requiring explanation: it is the classic pattern in which the revenue shortfall is absorbed entirely by the capital budget, because recurrent commitments — salaries, pensions and debt service — cannot be deferred.","**Recurrent expenditure was contained within budget** on every line, with an aggregate saving of ₦1.5 billion (3.0%), so the underspend was not the result of general austerity but of deliberate protection of recurrent items.","**The reported surplus of ₦5.24 billion is therefore misleading** if read as good performance. It arises from failing to execute a quarter of the capital programme, not from revenue outperformance. A user should read the capital variance and the revenue variance together before drawing any conclusion."]},{"key":"Examiners award most of the marks in this question for the **commentary**, not the table. The commentary that scores is the one that connects two variances — here, that the revenue shortfall was met entirely out of capital — rather than describing each line in turn."}]}}]},{"n":"14.5","t":"Notes and disclosures","b":[{"ol":["A **statement of accounting policies**, including that the statements are prepared on the cash basis in accordance with the Cash Basis IPSAS, the reporting currency, and the basis of consolidation.","The **composition of cash and cash equivalents**, and a reconciliation with the amounts in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments.","Any **restrictions** on cash balances and access to borrowing facilities.","The **basis of the budget** and the period it covers, and an explanation of material variances.","Amounts of **payments made by third parties on behalf of the entity** — for example a donor paying a contractor directly.","**Undrawn borrowing facilities** and the terms attached.","Detailed **analyses supporting each principal line** of the statements — revenue by source, expenditure by ministry, advances and deposits by category.","The **entities consolidated** where the statements cover a group of entities.","**Related party disclosures** and the emoluments of key management personnel."]},{"h4":"Limitations of cash basis reporting"},{"ul":["It **ignores receivables and payables**, so unpaid contractors' certificates and uncollected taxes are invisible and the true financial position is not disclosed.","It **omits non-current assets and depreciation**, so the cost of using government assets never enters the accounts and the asset base is unknown.","It permits **manipulation by timing** — deferring payments past the year end improves the reported surplus without any change in substance.","It **does not disclose the full cost of a service**, so economy and efficiency cannot be assessed.","It shows only **cash liabilities**, understating public debt where obligations have been incurred but not paid.","It is **not comparable** with accrual-based reporting elsewhere."]},{"p":"These limitations are the reason for the move to accrual basis IPSAS, dealt with in Chapters 15 and 16."}]},{"n":"14.6","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which one of the following should NOT appear under investments in the statement of financial position of government under the cash basis? (A) Imprest  (B) Revolving loans granted  (C) Cash balances of trust and other funds of the government  (D) Advances  (E) Intangible assets","Which one of the following is NOT one of the operating activities reported under the statement of cash flow? (A) Statutory allocation  (B) Value added tax allocation  (C) Royalties  (D) Subvention to parastatals  (E) Proceeds from aids and grants","The following outflows are from the statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund, except (A) Recurrent expenditure  (B) Transfers to Contingency Fund  (C) Redemption of Treasury Bills  (D) Direct taxes  (E) Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF) charges","Which of the following is NOT one of the liquid assets of government under the cash basis? (A) Cash balances with federal pay offices  (B) Cash held by MDAs  (C) Pension account (CBN/Bank)  (D) Cash balances of trust and other funds of the state government  (E) Police Reward Fund balance","The format of the statement of financial position of government prepared under the cash basis must give information on the following, except (A) Liquid assets  (B) Investments and other cash assets  (C) Final budget  (D) Public funds  (E) External and internal loans","… are made up of Treasury Bills, Treasury Certificates and Loan Stocks raised and retired within the country.","The office charged with the responsibility of preparing and publishing the accounts of the Federal Government of Nigeria is ….","The item collected from the government's day-to-day activities is known as ….","… relate to the day-to-day disbursements to run the administration of local government councils, and state and federal governments.","The fund meant to finance general capital projects such as the construction of government hospitals is called …."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** D  **3.** D  **4.** E  **5.** C  **6.** Internal debt  **7.** Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation  **8.** Recurrent revenue  **9.** Recurrent expenditure  **10.** Development Fund"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — the Egu-Awori Memorial Loan Fund","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Iludun Polytechnic held the following balances for the Egu-Awori Memorial Loan Fund as at 31 December 2020 (₦'000): Cash 8,400 Dr; Loan receivable 316,000 Dr; Investments 202,000 Dr; Fund balance 526,400 Cr. During 2021: (i) investments costing 61,600 were sold for 63,800; (ii) 61,400 cash was received from loan repayments; (iii) 5,000 was received from a former student's family in full payment of a loan earlier written off; (iv) 83,600 was issued out as new loans; (v) a loan of 1,500 was written off as uncollectable; (vi) a gift of 6,000 cash was received from a former borrower. (a) Open the ledger accounts and record the 2021 transactions. (b) Extract a trial balance and prepare the balance sheet of the Fund as at 31/12/2021. (c) Prepare a statement of changes in the fund balance."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"(a)(i) Cash account (₦'000)","align":"lrlr","head":["Dr","","Cr",""],"rows":[["Balance b/f","8,400","Loan receivable a/c","83,600"],["Investment disposal a/c","63,800","Balance c/d","61,000"],["Loan receivable a/c","61,400","",""],["Bad debt recovered a/c","5,000","",""],["Fund balance (gift)","6,000","",""],["**Total**","**144,600**","","**144,600**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"(a)(ii) Loan receivable account (₦'000)","align":"lrlr","head":["Dr","","Cr",""],"rows":[["Balance b/f","316,000","Cash account","61,400"],["Cash a/c","83,600","Bad debt recovered","1,500"],["","","Balance c/d","336,700"],["**Total**","**399,600**","","**399,600**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"(a)(iii) Investment account (₦'000)","align":"lrlr","head":["Dr","","Cr",""],"rows":[["Balance b/f","202,000","Investment disposal a/c","61,600"],["","","Balance c/d","140,400"],["**Total**","**202,000**","","**202,000**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"(a)(iv) Fund balance account (₦'000)","align":"lrlr","head":["Dr","","Cr",""],"rows":[["Balance c/d","538,100","Balance b/f","526,400"],["","","Cash account (gift)","6,000"],["","","Investment disposal a/c (profit)","2,200"],["","","Bad debt recovered a/c","3,500"],["**Total**","**538,100**","","**538,100**"]]}},{"note":"Workings: **Investment disposal** — cost 61,600 Dr, cash 63,800 Cr, profit 2,200 to fund balance. **Bad debt recovered** — 1,500 credited back to loan receivable (the original write-off), 3,500 transferred to fund balance (the excess recovered above the amount written off)."},{"table":{"cap":"(b) Trial balance as at 31/12/2021 (₦'000)","align":"lrr","head":["","Dr","Cr"],"rows":[["Cash","61,000",""],["Loan receivable","336,700",""],["Investment","140,400",""],["Fund balance","","538,100"],["**Total**","**538,100**","**538,100**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"(c) Statement of changes in the fund balance (₦'000)","align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["Balance b/f","526,400"],["Add: Gift received","6,000"],["Add: Bad debt recovered","5,000"],["Add: Profit on disposal of investment","2,200"],["Sub-total","539,600"],["Less: Bad debt written off","(1,500)"],["**Balance carried forward**","**538,100**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — trial balance, Akinyele Local Government","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The following balances were extracted from the books of Akinyele Local Government Treasury for the month ended 31 March 200X (₦'000): Reserve Fund 33,500; Cash on hand 53,000; Bank overdraft 5,700; Revenue (recurrent/capital) 158,500; Expenditure (recurrent/capital) 125,390; Loans 200,000; Deposits into the LGC Treasury 23,450; Advances granted by the LGC 25,560; Investments 15,200; Suspense account 202,000. Prepare a trial balance for the month."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Akinyele Local Government — trial balance as at 31 March 200X (₦'000)","align":"lrr","head":["","Dr","Cr"],"rows":[["Reserve Fund","","33,500"],["Cash on hand","53,000",""],["Bank overdraft","","5,700"],["Revenue (recurrent/capital)","","158,500"],["Expenditure (recurrent/capital)","125,390",""],["Loans","","200,000"],["Deposits into the LGC Treasury","","23,450"],["Advances granted by the LGC","25,560",""],["Investments","15,200",""],["Suspense account","202,000",""],["**Total**","**421,150**","**421,150**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — recurrent and capital accounts, Denge Pose State","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Denge Pose State Government of Manna reported (₦'000, 2020 / 2021): statutory allocation 644,997 / 4,841,017; VAT 119,102 / 160,133; IGR 403,020 / 498,843; other revenue 58,256 / 79,397; personnel cost 490,110 / 1,170,666; overhead cost 280,095 / 739,646; CRF charges 137,081 / 382,936 (of which unfunded internal debt servicing was 36,970 / 45,364); other capital receipts 246,400 / 379,237; capital expenditure 394,969 / 2,753,553. Any surplus/deficit on the recurrent account transfers to the capital account. Prepare the recurrent and capital accounts for the year ended 31 December 2021 (with 2020 comparatives)."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Denge Pose State — consolidated recurrent and capital accounts (₦'000)","align":"lrr","head":["","2020","2021"],"rows":[["**Recurrent revenue**","",""],["Statutory allocation","644,997","4,841,017"],["Value added tax","119,102","160,133"],["Internally generated revenue","403,020","498,843"],["Other revenue","58,256","79,397"],["**Total (A)**","**1,225,375**","**5,579,390**"],["**Recurrent expenditure**","",""],["Personnel cost","490,110","1,170,666"],["Overhead cost","280,095","739,646"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","137,081","382,936"],["**Total (B)**","**907,286**","**2,293,248**"],["**Surplus to capital account (A − B)**","**318,089**","**3,286,142**"],["**Capital account**","",""],["Transfer from recurrent account","318,089","3,286,142"],["Other capital receipts","246,400","379,237"],["Sub-total","564,489","3,665,379"],["Less: Capital expenditure","(394,969)","(2,753,553)"],["**Capital Development Fund c/f**","**169,520**","**911,826**"],["*(of which internal debt servicing, within CRF charges)*","36,970","45,364"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — statement of cash flow (direct method, with comparatives)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Office of the Accountant-General of the Federal Republic of Legacy reported (₦m, 2021 / 2020): VAT receipts 2,295 / 2,196; miscellaneous revenue 375 / 425; PIT (direct tax) 10,500 / 7,200; allocation for collection costs (FIRS/Customs) 3,075 / 3,305; personnel costs 7,200 / 9,600; share of statutory allocation 13,500 / 8,450; CRF charges 2,250 / 3,750; grants/subvention from foreign donors 75 / 87; rent of federal properties 285 / 295; overhead charges 1,500 / 1,200; subvention to parastatals 1,800 / 1,900; sales of federal properties 375 / 485 (plus a further 1,050 / — from properties in Yenagoa); repayment of loans 870 / 243; purchases/construction of non-current assets 2,500 / 2,700; purchases of marketable securities 500 / 300; proceeds from loans and other borrowings 3,000 / 1,000; cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 3,750 / —, and at 31 December 21,660 / 3,750. Prepare a statement of cash flow for the year ended 31 December 2021 using the direct method, showing comparatives."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Federal Republic of Legacy — statement of cash flow for the year ended 31 December 2021 (₦m)","align":"lrr","head":["","2021","2020"],"rows":[["**Operating activities — receipts**","",""],["Share of statutory allocation","13,500","8,450"],["Value added tax","2,295","2,196"],["Personal income tax (direct tax)","10,500","7,200"],["Allocation for collection cost","3,075","3,305"],["Grants and subvention received","75","87"],["Miscellaneous revenue","375","425"],["**Total receipts**","**29,820**","**21,663**"],["**Operating activities — payments**","",""],["Personnel costs","7,200","9,600"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","2,250","3,750"],["Overhead charges","1,500","1,200"],["Subvention to parastatals","1,800","1,900"],["**Total payments**","**12,750**","**16,450**"],["**Net cash from operating activities**","**17,070**","**5,213**"],["**Investing activities**","",""],["Sale of federal properties (Yenagoa)","1,050","—"],["Purchases/construction of non-current assets","(2,500)","(2,700)"],["Purchases of marketable securities","(500)","(300)"],["Sale of government property","375","485"],["Rent of government property","285","295"],["**Net cash from investing activities**","**(1,290)**","**(2,220)**"],["**Financing activities**","",""],["Proceeds from loans and other borrowings","3,000","1,000"],["Repayment of loans","(870)","(243)"],["**Net cash from financing activities**","**2,130**","**757**"],["**Net increase in cash and cash equivalents**","**17,910**","**3,750**"],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January","3,750","—"],["**Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December**","**21,660**","**3,750**"]]}}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Net cash flow","tex":"\\text{Total receipts} - \\text{Total payments}"},{"lb":"Closing cash","tex":"\\text{Opening cash} + \\text{Net cash flow}"},{"lb":"Net assets","tex":"\\text{Total assets} - \\text{Total liabilities}"},{"lb":"Budget variance — receipts","tex":"\\text{Actual} - \\text{Budget}"},{"lb":"Budget variance — payments","tex":"\\text{Budget} - \\text{Actual}"},{"lb":"Budget performance","tex":"\\frac{\\text{Actual}}{\\text{Budget}} \\times 100"}],"focus":"The most reliable preparation question in the paper. A Section B question in most diets asks for a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments, a Statement of Assets and Liabilities, or both, from a trial balance or a list of balances, often with a budget comparison and a commentary. Marks follow the lines of the statement, so use the proper headings and reach a total even where one or two items are uncertain — and always check that closing cash in the cash statement equals cash in the assets statement.","errors":["Including property, plant and equipment, receivables or payables in a cash basis Statement of Assets and Liabilities.","Failing to reconcile closing cash in the cash statement to cash in the assets statement.","Treating advances as expenditure and deposits as revenue instead of as below-the-line items.","Computing payment variances in the same direction as receipt variances, so that an underspend appears adverse.","Omitting the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts from the list of required statements.","Describing an underspend on capital as a favourable outcome without noting that projects were not delivered."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the cash basis IPSAS, which of the following would NOT appear in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities?","o":["Cash and cash equivalents","Property, plant and equipment","Advances","Deposits","Investments"],"a":1,"w":"Non-current assets are recognised only under accrual IPSAS. A cash basis statement shows financial assets and liabilities arising from cash transactions.","src":"Chapter 14.3","sec":"14.3"},{"q":"Total receipts were ₦92,400,000 and total payments ₦86,150,000. Opening cash was ₦4,300,000. Closing cash is","o":["₦6,250,000","₦10,550,000","₦1,950,000","₦14,850,000","₦2,150,000"],"a":1,"w":"Net cash flow is ₦6,250,000; add the opening balance.","calc":"4{,}300{,}000 + (92{,}400{,}000 - 86{,}150{,}000) = ₦10{,}550{,}000","src":"Chapter 14.2","sec":"14.2"},{"q":"The statement peculiar to the public sector and required under both cash and accrual IPSAS is the","o":["Statement of Cash Flows","Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","Statement of Financial Position","Statement of Financial Performance","Statement of Changes in Net Assets"],"a":1,"w":"The budget is a legal authority to spend, so reporting against it is a statutory obligation rather than a management convenience.","src":"Chapter 14.1","sec":"14.1"},{"q":"Actual capital expenditure of ₦18,000,000 against a budget of ₦24,000,000 represents a budget performance of","o":["133%","75%","25%","60%","80%"],"a":1,"w":"Divide actual by budget. The 25% underspend means a quarter of the capital programme was not executed.","calc":"\\frac{18{,}000{,}000}{24{,}000{,}000} \\times 100 = 75\\%","src":"Chapter 14.4","sec":"14.4"},{"q":"A principal limitation of cash basis reporting is that it","o":["is difficult to audit","does not disclose receivables and payables","requires actuarial valuation","cannot be compared with the budget","is expensive to prepare"],"a":1,"w":"Recognising only cash means unpaid obligations and uncollected revenue never appear, so the true financial position is not shown.","src":"Chapter 14.5","sec":"14.5"},{"q":"Closing cash in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments must agree with","o":["net assets in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities","cash and cash equivalents in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities","the Consolidated Revenue Fund balance","total receipts for the year","the budgeted surplus"],"a":1,"w":"This articulation check should be performed on every answer before it is submitted.","src":"Chapter 14.2","sec":"14.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following balances were extracted from the records of the Government of Ondo State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000):\n\nReceipts: statutory allocation 39,200,000; VAT allocation 11,600,000; internally generated revenue 8,750,000; grants 900,000; other receipts 350,000.\n\nPayments: personnel cost 24,100,000; social contributions 2,650,000; overhead cost 10,300,000; Consolidated Revenue Fund charges 5,400,000; capital expenditure 16,800,000.\n\nOther balances at 31 December 2026: advances outstanding 1,050,000; investments 3,200,000; deposits held 760,000. Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026 were 2,480,000, and the Consolidated Revenue Fund balance carried forward is 5,900,000.\n\n(a) Prepare the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year. (b) Prepare the Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026. (c) State four limitations of financial statements prepared on the cash basis.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**RECEIPTS**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",39200000],["Value added tax allocation",11600000],["Internally generated revenue",8750000],["Grants received",900000],["Other receipts",350000],["Total receipts@sub",60800000],["**PAYMENTS**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-24100000],["Social contributions",-2650000],["Overhead cost",-10300000],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges",-5400000],["Capital expenditure",-16800000],["Total payments@sub",-59250000],["Net cash flow for the year@t",1550000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",2480000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",4030000]]}},{"h4":"(b) Statement of Assets and Liabilities"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**ASSETS**@t",null],["Cash and cash equivalents",4030000],["Investments",3200000],["Advances",1050000],["Total assets@sub",8280000],["**LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Deposits",-760000],["Total liabilities@sub",-760000],["NET ASSETS@tot",7520000],["**REPRESENTED BY**@t",null],["Consolidated Revenue Fund",5900000],["Capital Development Fund (balancing figure)",1620000],["Total funds@tot",7520000]]}},{"p":"Two checks confirm the answer. Closing cash of ₦4,030,000 thousand appears identically in both statements; and net assets of ₦7,520,000 thousand equal the sum of the fund balances, the Capital Development Fund being the balancing figure of $7{,}520{,}000 - 5{,}900{,}000 = ₦1{,}620{,}000$ thousand."},{"p":"It is worth noting for the reader that the State spent ₦59.25 billion against receipts of ₦60.8 billion, a surplus of only 2.5% of receipts, and that recurrent expenditure of ₦42.45 billion consumed 70% of receipts before a single capital project was funded. Internally generated revenue of ₦8.75 billion represents just 14.4% of total receipts, so the State depends on federation sources for more than four-fifths of its income."},{"h4":"(c) Limitations of cash basis financial statements"},{"ol":["**Receivables and payables are not recognised.** Taxes assessed but not collected, and contractors' certificates approved but not paid, do not appear anywhere in the statements. A government with substantial unpaid liabilities can therefore report a healthy cash surplus while being in serious financial difficulty.","**Non-current assets and depreciation are omitted.** Roads, buildings, plant and vehicles are treated as expenditure of the year of purchase and then disappear from the records. Government does not know what assets it owns, no charge is made for their consumption, and the full cost of delivering a service is never known — which makes any assessment of economy or efficiency impossible.","**Results can be manipulated by timing.** Deferring payments until after the year end, or accelerating collections into it, improves the reported position without any change in the underlying substance. The cash basis rewards precisely the behaviour that creates arrears.","**Public debt is understated.** Only cash movements on borrowing appear; obligations incurred and not yet paid, guarantees, and long-term commitments such as pension liabilities are outside the statements altogether.","**Comparability is poor.** The statements cannot be compared with those of private-sector entities, of international organisations, or of governments reporting on the accrual basis."]},{"note":"These limitations are the reason Nigeria adopted accrual basis IPSAS with effect from 1 January 2016, with the transitional relief provided by IPSAS 33."}],"src":"Chapter 14.2–14.5","sec":"14.2"}]}},{"n":15,"t":"IPSAS 33 — First-Time Adoption of Accrual Basis IPSAS","brief":"The transitional standard: the date of adoption and the opening statement of financial position, the three-year relief period, deemed cost, the exemptions that affect fair presentation and those that do not, and the disclosures required.","outcomes":["State the objective and scope of IPSAS 33","Define first-time adopter, date of adoption and transitional IPSAS financial statements","Prepare the opening statement of financial position","Distinguish exemptions that affect fair presentation from those that do not","Explain the three-year transitional relief period and the use of deemed cost","State when an entity may assert compliance with accrual basis IPSAS"],"secs":[{"n":"15.1","t":"Objective and key terms","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS 33, First-time Adoption of Accrual Basis IPSAS**, was issued to ease the transition from another basis of accounting — typically the cash basis — to full accrual IPSAS. Its objective is to provide **transitional exemptions** so that an entity can adopt accrual accounting without being required to do everything at once, while ensuring that users are told exactly what has and has not yet been done."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**First-time adopter**","An entity that presents its first transitional IPSAS financial statements or its first IPSAS financial statements for the reporting period"],["**Date of adoption of IPSAS**","The date on which the entity adopts accrual basis IPSAS for the first time — the beginning of the period for which the first statements are prepared"],["**Transitional IPSAS financial statements**","The first statements prepared under accrual IPSAS in which the entity takes one or more of the exemptions that **affect fair presentation and compliance**"],["**First IPSAS financial statements**","The first statements in which the entity is able to make an **explicit and unreserved statement of full compliance** with accrual basis IPSAS"],["**Opening statement of financial position**","The statement of financial position prepared at the date of adoption, being the starting point for accrual accounting"],["**Deemed cost**","An amount used as a surrogate for cost or depreciated cost at a given date, where the actual cost cannot be determined reliably"]]}},{"key":"The distinction between **transitional** IPSAS financial statements and **first** IPSAS financial statements is the heart of the standard. An entity may not claim compliance with accrual IPSAS while it is still relying on an exemption that affects fair presentation — and the exemption may not be relied on for more than three years."}]},{"n":"15.2","t":"The opening statement of financial position","b":[{"def":{"t":"PSE","d":"the acronym/mnemonic for a **Public Sector Entity** — the term IPSAS 33 uses for the reporting entity moving from the cash basis (or from accrual accounting outside IPSAS) to accrual-basis IPSAS."}},{"p":"At the date of adoption, the entity prepares an opening statement of financial position. In doing so it must:"},{"ol":["**Recognise** all assets and liabilities whose recognition is required by IPSAS, subject to the exemptions taken.","**Derecognise** items previously recognised that IPSAS does not permit as assets or liabilities.","**Reclassify** items into the categories IPSAS requires — for example separating investment property from property, plant and equipment.","**Measure** all recognised assets and liabilities in accordance with IPSAS, subject to the exemptions."]},{"p":"The resulting adjustments are recognised directly in **opening accumulated surplus or deficit** (or another category of net assets/equity where appropriate), not in the surplus or deficit for the period."},{"warn":"Adjustments on transition go to **opening accumulated surplus or deficit**, not through the Statement of Financial Performance. Running them through performance would make the year of transition look catastrophic or spectacular for reasons that have nothing to do with the year's operations."}]},{"n":"15.3","t":"The exemptions","b":[{"p":"IPSAS 33 divides the reliefs into two classes, and the distinction determines whether the entity may claim compliance."},{"h4":"A. Exemptions that AFFECT fair presentation and compliance"},{"p":"These allow the entity a **three-year transitional relief period** from the date of adoption in which it need not recognise, or need not measure in accordance with IPSAS, certain items. While any of them is relied on, the entity presents **transitional IPSAS financial statements** and may **not** assert compliance with accrual IPSAS."},{"ul":["**Property, plant and equipment** (IPSAS 17) — three-year relief from recognition and/or measurement. This is the exemption Nigerian entities have relied on most heavily, because identifying and valuing legacy infrastructure acquired over decades, often without records, is the single largest practical obstacle to accrual adoption.","**Investment property** (IPSAS 16).","**Intangible assets** (IPSAS 31).","**Financial instruments** (IPSAS 41) — recognition and measurement.","**Inventories** (IPSAS 12).","**Service concession assets and the related liabilities** (IPSAS 32).","**Biological assets and agricultural produce** (IPSAS 27).","**Employee benefits — the defined benefit obligation** (IPSAS 39), where actuarial valuation cannot be obtained immediately.","**Interests in controlled entities, associates and joint ventures**, and the preparation of consolidated financial statements.","**Provisions, contingent liabilities and contingent assets** (IPSAS 19)."]},{"h4":"B. Exemptions that DO NOT affect fair presentation and compliance"},{"p":"These are practical simplifications that do not prevent an entity from asserting full compliance."},{"ul":["Not restating **comparative information** for the periods before adoption, where it is not available.","Using **deemed cost** for property, plant and equipment, investment property or intangible assets where the actual cost is not determinable — fair value at the date of adoption, or a reliable revaluation, becomes the cost from that date.","Not applying **IPSAS 5** to capitalise borrowing costs on qualifying assets in progress at the date of adoption.","Relief in respect of **related party disclosures** for periods before adoption.","Relief in respect of the **disclosure of key management personnel** emoluments for prior periods.","Not applying **IPSAS 34–38** retrospectively to business combinations occurring before the date of adoption."]},{"def":{"t":"Deemed cost","d":"Where an entity cannot determine the historical cost of an asset — very common for government property acquired long ago, or transferred without consideration — it may use fair value at the date of adoption as the deemed cost. That amount then becomes the basis for depreciation and for any subsequent carrying amount, exactly as if it were cost."}}]},{"n":"15.4","t":"The three-year relief period","b":[{"ol":["The relief period runs for **three years from the date of adoption**.","During it the entity presents **transitional IPSAS financial statements** and must state that it has adopted accrual basis IPSAS **subject to the transitional exemptions taken**.","The entity must disclose **which exemptions it has taken**, the **progress made** towards recognition and measurement, and the **date by which it expects to comply in full**.","Where an item is recognised **during** the relief period, the adjustment is made against opening accumulated surplus or deficit in the period of recognition.","At the end of the three years the entity must have recognised and measured everything, and its statements become **first IPSAS financial statements** carrying an explicit and unreserved statement of compliance.","An entity that adopts accrual IPSAS without relying on any exemption affecting fair presentation may make that statement of compliance **immediately**."]},{"p":"In Nigeria the accrual basis was adopted with effect from **1 January 2016**, so a federal entity taking the property, plant and equipment exemption from that date had until the end of **2018** to complete recognition and measurement. In practice many entities have taken longer, principally because the identification and valuation of legacy assets has proved slower than the roadmap assumed."}]},{"n":"15.5","t":"Presentation and disclosure","b":[{"ol":["A statement that the financial statements are the entity's **transitional IPSAS financial statements** or its **first IPSAS financial statements**, as the case may be.","The **date of adoption** of accrual basis IPSAS.","The **exemptions taken** and, for each, the reason and the effect on the statements.","For items not yet recognised, the **progress towards recognition** and the date by which the entity expects to recognise them.","Where **deemed cost** has been used, the fact, the amounts involved and the basis on which fair value was determined.","A **reconciliation** of the amounts reported under the previous basis of accounting to the amounts under accrual IPSAS, for both net assets and surplus or deficit, at the date of adoption and at the end of the comparative period.","Where comparative information has **not been restated**, that fact and the reason.","The **accounting policies** adopted, in full."]},{"h4":"Challenges of adoption in practice"},{"ul":["**Identification and valuation of legacy assets**, many of them acquired decades ago, without title documents, records of cost or reliable registers.","**Shortage of valuers and of accountants trained in accrual concepts**, particularly at state and local government level.","**Weak information systems** unable to capture accruals, and the cost of upgrading them.","**The persistence of a cash budget.** Appropriation and control remain on a cash basis while reporting moves to accrual, so two parallel sets of figures must be maintained and reconciled.","**Institutional resistance**, since accrual reporting discloses liabilities and asset shortfalls previously invisible.","**Cost**, which for a small entity may be difficult to justify against the benefit."]},{"key":"The purpose of IPSAS 33 is not to relax the standards but to make the transition honest. An entity may take longer to recognise its assets, but it must say so, say which assets, and say when it will finish — and it may not describe its statements as IPSAS-compliant until it has."}]},{"n":"15.6","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A — multiple choice"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["What is the primary objective of IPSAS 33? (A) To provide guidance on financial reporting  (B) To establish accounting standards for public sector entities  (C) To facilitate the transition to accrual basis IPSASs  (D) To harmonize accounting standards with IFRSs  (E) To audit financial statements","What is the definition of \"first-time adoption\" under IPSAS? (A) Adopting IPSASs for the first time  (B) Changing from cash basis to accrual basis IPSASs  (C) Adopting a new accounting policy  (D) Preparing financial statements for the first time  (E) Both A and B","What is the purpose of the \"deemed cost\" approach in IPSAS? (A) To determine fair value  (B) To provide relief from retrospective application  (C) To calculate carrying amount  (D) To recognize transitional adjustments  (E) To impair assets","What is the accounting treatment for assets and liabilities under IPSAS 33? (A) Recognize at fair value  (B) Recognize at historical cost  (C) Apply relevant IPSASs  (D) Do not recognize until transition date  (E) Use deemed cost for all assets and liabilities","What is the role of the \"date of transition\" in IPSAS 33? (A) Marks the beginning of the first reporting period  (B) Determines the opening balance sheet  (C) First financial statements under IPSASs  (D) All of the above  (E) None of the above","What is a benefit of adopting IPSAS 33? (A) Improved financial reporting  (B) Reduced compliance costs  (C) Increased complexity  (D) Decreased accountability  (E) Enhanced transparency and accountability","What is the primary principle in IPSAS 33? (A) Full retrospective application  (B) Prospective application  (C) Fair value measurement  (D) Comparative information not required  (E) Modified retrospective application","What is the purpose of IPSAS 33? (A) To provide guidance on financial reporting  (B) To facilitate transition to accrual basis IPSASs  (C) To establish accounting standards  (D) To audit financial statements  (E) To provide training on IPSASs","What is the accounting treatment for transitional adjustments under IPSAS 33? (A) Recognize in opening balance sheet  (B) Recognize in income statement  (C) Recognize in equity  (D) Do not recognize in opening balance sheet  (E) Account for as a change in accounting policy","What is the effective date of IPSAS 33? (A) Immediate adoption  (B) Annual financial statements after a specific date  (C) Optional adoption  (D) Prospective application  (E) Retrospective application"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** C  **2.** E  **3.** B  **4.** C  **5.** D  **6.** E  **7.** E  **8.** B  **9.** B  **10.** B"}]}},{"h3":"Section A — short answer"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–6 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The transition to accrual basis International Public Sector Accounting Standards for public entities is treated in …","The approach in IPSAS 33 that provides relief from retrospective application by allowing entities to use a deemed cost as a surrogate for historical cost or fair value is known as …","Transitional adjustments are recognised in the opening statement of financial position of an entity's …","IPSAS 33 ensures that an entity's first IPSAS financial statements, and its interim financial reports for the period, provide a … of its financial position, performance and cash flows.","The application of IPSAS for the first time — including recognition, measurement and disclosure principles, and transitional provisions and reliefs — is treated in …","The beginning of the earliest period for which an entity presents full comparative information under IPSAS in its first IPSAS financial statements is known as …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** IPSAS 33  **2.** Deemed-cost approach  **3.** First IPSAS financial statements  **4.** Faithful representation  **5.** IPSAS 33  **6.** Date of transition"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — recognising revenue from 1 January 2016","open":true,"q":[{"p":"IPSAS 33 gives up to three years' transitional relief within which to develop models for transiting to accrual IPSAS; Nigeria adopted accrual IPSAS for revenue effective 1 January 2016. State how each of the following would be recognised: (a) government share of FAAC (statutory revenue); (b) aid/grants receivable as at 31 December 2015, and those approved on or after 1 January 2016; (c) debt forgiveness approved on or after 1 January 2016; (d) other transfer revenue; (e) personal income tax on or after 1 January 2016; (f) licences, fees and fines on or after 1 January 2016."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) FAAC allocation** is recognised by the head of the respective Treasury (AGF/AGS/LGA Treasurer) at the end of each monthly FAAC/JAAC meeting, using the distribution schedule as the source document."},{"p":"**(b) Aid/grants.** A grant an entity was unconditionally entitled to by 31 December 2015 but had not yet received is recognised as a **receivable** in the opening statement of financial position. A grant approved on or after 1 January 2016 is recognised (accrued) immediately once both parties have signed an agreement/MoU with **no conditions**; a **conditional** grant is recognised only as its conditions are met, with the unmet portion held as a **liability**."},{"p":"**(c) Debt forgiveness** approved on or after 1 January 2016 is recognised as revenue immediately once the agreement is approved by both parties with **no conditions**; where conditions attach and failure to meet them is possible, the forgiven amount (or the conditional portion) remains a **liability** until met."},{"p":"**(d) Other transfer revenue** is recognised where it meets the IPSAS 23 (Revenue from Non-Exchange Transactions) recognition criteria; a conditional portion at risk of failing its conditions is held as a liability until the conditions are met."},{"p":"**(e) Personal income tax** is recognised in two stages: at the **Relevant Tax Authority**, when the taxpayer/employer submits returns; and in the books of the **whole of government** (Federal/State Treasury), only on **remittance** by the RTA to the Consolidated Revenue Fund."},{"p":"**(f) Licences, fees and fines** are recognised when the application is **approved**, and again on approval of each renewal."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — preparing the opening statement of financial position (1 January 2016)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State the practical steps for preparing the Opening Statement of Financial Position under each condition: (i) whole of government on the cash basis; (ii) a PSE already on accrual accounting but not IPSAS-compliant; (iii) a PSE on the cash basis preparing only an incomplete set of financial statements (transcript of accounts) as at 31 December 2015."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i) Cash basis, whole of government:** prepare the draft annual financial statements to 31 December 2015 under cash-basis IPSAS; have them audited by the respective Auditor-General; submit the audited statements to Parliament; ascertain the value of all assets (e.g. via a stock report) and confirmed liabilities as at 31 December 2015; restate the existing Statement of Assets and Liabilities into the Opening Statement of Financial Position at 1 January 2016; and convert it accordingly."},{"p":"**(ii) Already on accrual, not IPSAS-compliant:** prepare the draft annual financial statements to 31 December 2015 on the accrual basis; have them audited by an approved external auditor; submit them to the Board/Governing Council; ascertain the value of confirmed assets/liabilities missing from the statements; restate the existing balance sheet into the Opening Statement of Financial Position at 1 January 2016; and convert it accordingly."},{"p":"**(iii) Cash basis, incomplete transcript of accounts:** submit the December 2015 transcript to the respective Treasury; carry out a stock-take of all stores per the Treasury circular as at 31 December 2015; value the stock-take using FIFO or another method the FAAC Sub-Committee on IPSAS Implementation (or the Treasury) prescribes; extract cash assets/liabilities from the Statement of Assets and Liabilities and ascertain other assets/liabilities under the PSE's control at that date; restate present balances into the Opening Statement of Financial Position; and convert accordingly."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — recognising and measuring existing assets","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State (a) the criteria for recognition of assets, and (b) the basic steps for recognising and measuring existing assets as at 31 December 2015."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) Recognition criteria:** it is probable that future economic benefits or service potential will flow to the entity; the cost/deemed cost (fair value) can be measured reliably; and the entity **controls** the asset."},{"ol":["Verify all the entity's assets and confirm they satisfy the recognition requirements.","Prepare a schedule of the verified assets, correctly classified.","Obtain source documents to establish details such as historical cost.","Where cost cannot be established, engage professional valuers/estimators to determine fair value (deemed cost).","Bring the values into the books — debit the relevant asset account, credit accumulated reserves."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Regularly examined since Nigeria adopted accrual IPSAS. Section A tests the length of the transitional relief period, the meaning of deemed cost, and the date of adoption. Section B asks for the exemptions available, the distinction between transitional and first IPSAS financial statements, or the challenges of implementation. Learn the three-year period, the two classes of exemption, and at least five items in each class.","errors":["Giving the transitional relief period as five years; it is three.","Treating all exemptions alike; only those in class A prevent an assertion of compliance.","Putting transition adjustments through the Statement of Financial Performance instead of opening accumulated surplus or deficit.","Saying deemed cost means fair value must be used every year; it fixes an amount at the date of adoption which is then treated as cost.","Asserting compliance with accrual IPSAS while still relying on a class A exemption."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The transitional relief period allowed by IPSAS 33 for the recognition and measurement of certain assets and liabilities is","o":["one year","three years","five years","two years","ten years"],"a":1,"w":"Three years from the date of adoption, after which everything must be recognised and measured in accordance with IPSAS.","src":"Chapter 15.4","sec":"15.4"},{"q":"In line with IPSAS 33, the acronym/mnemonic PSE means","o":["Public Sector Entity","Public Sector Expenditure","Public Service Establishment","Provisional Statement of Estimates","Public Sector Examination"],"a":0,"w":"PSE is IPSAS 33's shorthand for the reporting entity — the Public Sector Entity — moving from cash basis (or from accrual accounting outside IPSAS) to accrual basis IPSAS.","src":"Chapter 15.2","sec":"15.2"},{"q":"An amount used as a surrogate for cost where the actual cost of an asset cannot be determined reliably is called","o":["fair value","deemed cost","residual value","recoverable amount","depreciated replacement cost"],"a":1,"w":"Deemed cost is fixed at the date of adoption and thereafter treated exactly as if it were cost, for depreciation and carrying amount.","src":"Chapter 15.3","sec":"15.3"},{"q":"Adjustments arising on transition to accrual basis IPSAS are recognised in","o":["surplus or deficit for the period","opening accumulated surplus or deficit","the statement of cash flows","a revaluation reserve only","the notes only"],"a":1,"w":"Taking them through performance would distort the results of the year of transition for reasons unconnected with its operations.","src":"Chapter 15.2","sec":"15.2"},{"q":"Financial statements in which an entity relies on an exemption that affects fair presentation are described as","o":["first IPSAS financial statements","transitional IPSAS financial statements","modified IPSAS financial statements","qualified financial statements","cash basis financial statements"],"a":1,"w":"Only when no such exemption remains may the entity present first IPSAS financial statements with an explicit and unreserved statement of compliance.","src":"Chapter 15.1","sec":"15.1"},{"q":"Which of the following exemptions does NOT affect fair presentation and compliance with accrual basis IPSAS?","o":["Non-recognition of property, plant and equipment","Non-restatement of comparative information","Non-recognition of the defined benefit obligation","Non-recognition of inventories","Non-recognition of service concession assets"],"a":1,"w":"Not restating comparatives is a practical simplification. The other four withhold recognition of items IPSAS requires, and so prevent an assertion of full compliance.","src":"Chapter 15.3","sec":"15.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from 1 January 2016. (a) Explain the objective of IPSAS 33 and define \"first-time adopter\", \"date of adoption\" and \"deemed cost\". (b) Distinguish between transitional IPSAS financial statements and first IPSAS financial statements. (c) State five exemptions that affect fair presentation and three that do not. (d) Discuss the challenges facing public sector entities in Nigeria in adopting the accrual basis.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Objective and definitions"},{"p":"**Objective.** IPSAS 33 provides transitional exemptions to entities adopting accrual basis IPSAS for the first time, so that the change of basis can be made in an orderly way without requiring every asset and liability to be identified, valued and recognised on a single date. At the same time it requires the entity to disclose exactly which exemptions it has taken, so that users are not misled about the completeness of the statements."},{"ul":["**First-time adopter** — an entity presenting its first transitional IPSAS financial statements or its first IPSAS financial statements for the reporting period.","**Date of adoption of IPSAS** — the date on which the entity adopts accrual basis IPSAS for the first time, being the beginning of the period for which the first such statements are prepared. For Nigerian federal entities, 1 January 2016.","**Deemed cost** — an amount used as a surrogate for cost, or for depreciated cost, at a given date, where the actual cost cannot be determined reliably. Fair value at the date of adoption is then treated exactly as though it were cost for all subsequent purposes."]},{"h4":"(b) Transitional against first IPSAS financial statements"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Transitional IPSAS financial statements","First IPSAS financial statements"],"rows":[["Exemptions relied on","One or more that affect fair presentation and compliance","None that affect fair presentation and compliance"],["Statement of compliance","Cannot assert compliance; states that accrual IPSAS has been adopted subject to the exemptions taken","Explicit and unreserved statement of full compliance with accrual basis IPSAS"],["Completeness","Some assets or liabilities are not yet recognised or measured","All assets and liabilities recognised and measured in accordance with IPSAS"],["Duration","Permitted for up to three years from the date of adoption","Thereafter, and permanently"],["Additional disclosure","Which exemptions were taken, progress made, and the date by which full compliance is expected","The normal IPSAS disclosures"]]}},{"p":"The relationship is sequential: an entity that takes a class A exemption presents transitional statements and moves to first IPSAS financial statements only when the last such exemption has been discharged, and in any event by the end of the three-year relief period."},{"h4":"(c) The exemptions"},{"p":"**Exemptions that affect fair presentation and compliance** (three-year relief):"},{"ol":["Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **property, plant and equipment** under IPSAS 17.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **investment property** under IPSAS 16.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **intangible assets** under IPSAS 31.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **financial instruments**.","Non-recognition of **inventories** under IPSAS 12.","Non-recognition of **service concession assets and the related liabilities** under IPSAS 32.","Non-recognition of the **defined benefit obligation for employee benefits** under IPSAS 39.","Non-recognition of **provisions and contingent liabilities** under IPSAS 19.","Relief from **consolidation** of controlled entities and from equity accounting for associates and joint ventures."]},{"p":"**Exemptions that do not affect fair presentation and compliance:**"},{"ol":["**Non-restatement of comparative information** for periods before the date of adoption, where that information is not available.","Use of **deemed cost** for property, plant and equipment, investment property or intangible assets whose cost cannot be determined reliably.","Relief from **capitalising borrowing costs** on qualifying assets in progress at the date of adoption.","Relief from **related party disclosures** and from the disclosure of **key management personnel emoluments** for periods before adoption.","Relief from applying the **business combination standards retrospectively** to combinations occurring before the date of adoption."]},{"h4":"(d) Challenges of adoption in Nigeria"},{"ol":["**Identification and valuation of legacy assets.** This is by a wide margin the largest obstacle. Government owns roads, bridges, schools, hospitals, land and buildings acquired over many decades, frequently without title documents, purchase records or asset registers. Establishing what exists, who controls it and what it is worth is a physical exercise of enormous scale, which is precisely why the property, plant and equipment exemption has been the most widely used.","**Shortage of skills.** Accrual accounting requires judgement about recognition, measurement, impairment and depreciation that a cash-based service never had to exercise. There are too few accountants trained in accrual IPSAS, and too few professional valuers, especially at state and local government level.","**Weak information systems.** Many entities operate manual or partially computerised records incapable of maintaining an asset register, tracking receivables and payables, or supporting consolidation. GIFMIS addresses this at federal level but coverage is incomplete and states vary widely.","**A cash budget against accrual reporting.** Appropriation, warrants and vote control remain firmly on the cash basis, while reporting has moved to accrual. Two parallel measurement bases must therefore be maintained and reconciled, and the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts must bridge them.","**Institutional resistance.** Accrual reporting discloses unpaid liabilities, pension obligations and the deterioration of assets that cash reporting concealed. Officials whose performance will be judged against those figures have limited incentive to accelerate the process.","**Cost and competing priorities.** Valuation exercises, system upgrades and training are expensive and compete against service delivery for scarce funds, and the benefits accrue slowly and diffusely.","**Uneven adoption across tiers.** Federal progress has outrun that of many states and most local governments, which limits the comparability and consolidation that were among the principal objectives of adopting IPSAS in the first place."]},{"p":"The proper conclusion is that IPSAS 33 is doing what it was designed to do. It does not relax the standards; it makes the transition honest, by allowing an entity more time while requiring it to disclose what remains outstanding and when it will be completed. The risk is not the exemption itself but its indefinite extension, and the answer to that is enforcement of the three-year limit."}],"src":"Chapter 15.1–15.5","sec":"15.1"}]}},{"n":16,"t":"Accrual Basis Financial Statements","brief":"The general purpose financial statements required under accrual basis IPSAS: the Statement of Financial Performance, the Statement of Financial Position, the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, the Statement of Cash Flows and the notes — with a fully worked set.","outcomes":["List the general purpose financial statements required under accrual basis IPSAS","Prepare a Statement of Financial Performance","Prepare a Statement of Financial Position","Prepare a Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","Prepare a Statement of Cash Flows by the indirect method","Explain the advantages of accrual over cash reporting"],"secs":[{"n":"16.1","t":"The required statements","b":[{"note":"[Cash vs accrual, every statement side by side →](guides/statements.html) — what each statement reports under each basis, the modified-basis combinations, and every past exam question on the topic with answers."},{"p":"**IPSAS 1, Presentation of Financial Statements**, requires a complete set of financial statements to comprise the following."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Statement","What it reports"],"rows":[["**Statement of Financial Performance**","Revenue earned and expenses incurred during the period, whether or not cash has moved, giving the surplus or deficit for the year"],["**Statement of Financial Position**","Assets, liabilities and net assets/equity at the reporting date, including property, plant and equipment, receivables and payables"],["**Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity**","The movement in each component of net assets — accumulated surplus, revaluation reserve, other reserves — between the opening and closing dates"],["**Statement of Cash Flows** (IPSAS 2)","Cash flows classified into operating, investing and financing activities, reconciling to the movement in cash and cash equivalents"],["**Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts** (IPSAS 24)","Original budget, final budget, actual amounts on a comparable basis and the variance, with explanations of material differences"],["**Notes**","A summary of significant accounting policies and other explanatory information, including analyses of every principal figure"]]}},{"key":"The essential difference from the cash basis is **recognition**. Revenue is recognised when it is **earned** and expenses when they are **incurred**, so the accounts show what the government owns and owes rather than merely what passed through its bank account."}]},{"n":"16.2","t":"The Statement of Financial Performance","b":[{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Financial Performance","q":[{"p":"The following balances were extracted from the accrual-based records of the Government of Ondo State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000): statutory allocation 39,200,000; VAT allocation 11,600,000; tax revenue 6,300,000; fees, fines and licences 2,900,000; grants 900,000; investment income 420,000; other revenue 350,000; salaries, wages and allowances 24,100,000; social benefits and pension contributions 2,650,000; depreciation and amortisation 4,850,000; use of goods and services 10,300,000; finance cost 1,900,000; grants and subsidies 1,400,000; impairment loss 260,000. Prepare the Statement of Financial Performance."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**REVENUE**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",39200000],["Value added tax allocation",11600000],["Tax revenue",6300000],["Fees, fines and licences",2900000],["Grants",900000],["Investment income",420000],["Other revenue",350000],["Total revenue@sub",61670000],["**EXPENSES**@t",null],["Salaries, wages and allowances",-24100000],["Social benefits and pension contributions",-2650000],["Use of goods and services",-10300000],["Depreciation and amortisation",-4850000],["Grants and subsidies",-1400000],["Finance cost",-1900000],["Impairment loss",-260000],["Total expenses@sub",-45460000],["SURPLUS FOR THE YEAR@tot",16210000]]}},{"note":"Compare this with the cash basis statement for the same government in Chapter 14.5, which reported a net cash inflow of only ₦1,550,000 thousand. The difference is not error but basis: the accrual statement includes revenue earned but not yet received and excludes the ₦16,800,000 thousand spent on capital assets, which is not an expense but the acquisition of an asset — charged instead through the ₦4,850,000 thousand depreciation."}]}}]},{"n":"16.3","t":"The Statement of Financial Position","b":[{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Financial Position","q":[{"p":"At 31 December 2026 the following balances stood in the books of the Government of Ondo State (₦'000): property, plant and equipment 128,400,000; investment property 6,200,000; intangible assets 1,150,000; long-term investments 3,200,000; inventories 2,340,000; receivables 5,680,000; prepayments 480,000; cash and cash equivalents 4,030,000; payables 6,720,000; deposits 760,000; current portion of borrowings 3,500,000; long-term borrowings 28,600,000; employee benefit obligations 9,400,000; revaluation reserve 14,200,000. Prepare the Statement of Financial Position."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**NON-CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Property, plant and equipment",128400000],["Investment property",6200000],["Intangible assets",1150000],["Long-term investments",3200000],["@sub",138950000],["**CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Inventories",2340000],["Receivables",5680000],["Prepayments",480000],["Cash and cash equivalents",4030000],["@sub",12530000],["TOTAL ASSETS@t",151480000],["**CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Payables",-6720000],["Deposits",-760000],["Current portion of borrowings",-3500000],["@sub",-10980000],["**NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Long-term borrowings",-28600000],["Employee benefit obligations",-9400000],["@sub",-38000000],["TOTAL LIABILITIES@t",-48980000],["NET ASSETS@tot",102500000],["**NET ASSETS / EQUITY**@t",null],["Accumulated surplus",88300000],["Revaluation reserve",14200000],["TOTAL NET ASSETS / EQUITY@tot",102500000]]}},{"p":"The accumulated surplus of ₦88,300,000 thousand is the balancing figure: net assets of ₦102,500,000 less the revaluation reserve of ₦14,200,000."},{"key":"Everything in this statement that would be absent from a cash basis statement is the point of accrual accounting. **₦128.4 billion of property, plant and equipment**, **₦5.68 billion of receivables**, **₦6.72 billion of payables** and **₦38 billion of long-term obligations** — including a ₦9.4 billion pension liability — are all invisible under the cash basis."}]}}]},{"n":"16.4","t":"The Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","b":[{"p":"This statement explains the movement in each component of net assets between the opening and closing dates. It captures the surplus or deficit for the year together with items recognised directly in net assets, such as revaluation gains and the remeasurements of defined benefit plans."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","q":[{"p":"At 1 January 2026 the accumulated surplus was ₦72,090,000 thousand and the revaluation reserve ₦11,900,000 thousand. During the year a revaluation gain of ₦2,300,000 thousand was recognised. Prepare the statement."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Accumulated surplus ₦'000","Revaluation reserve ₦'000","Total ₦'000"],"rows":[["Balance at 1 January 2026","72,090,000","11,900,000","83,990,000"],["Surplus for the year","16,210,000","—","16,210,000"],["Gain on revaluation of property","—","2,300,000","2,300,000"],["**Balance at 31 December 2026**","**88,300,000**","**14,200,000**","**102,500,000**"]]}},{"p":"The closing total of ₦102,500,000 thousand agrees with net assets in the Statement of Financial Position, and the closing accumulated surplus of ₦88,300,000 thousand with the figure shown there. **Perform both checks before submitting an answer.**"},{"warn":"A revaluation gain goes to the **revaluation reserve**, not to the surplus for the year. So do the remeasurements of a defined benefit obligation (IPSAS 39). Routing either through the Statement of Financial Performance is a standard error and misstates both statements."}]}}]},{"n":"16.5","t":"The Statement of Cash Flows","b":[{"p":"IPSAS 2 requires cash flows to be classified into **operating**, **investing** and **financing** activities. Operating cash flows may be presented by the **direct** method (gross receipts and payments) or the **indirect** method (surplus adjusted for non-cash items and working capital movements). IPSAS encourages the direct method; the indirect method is the one usually set in examinations because it tests understanding of the difference between the two bases."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Cash Flows — indirect method","q":[{"p":"During 2026 the Government of Ondo State: purchased property, plant and equipment for ₦16,800,000 thousand and intangible assets for ₦350,000 thousand; received investment income of ₦420,000 thousand; raised new borrowings of ₦2,000,000 thousand and repaid ₦5,770,000 thousand; and paid finance costs of ₦1,900,000 thousand. Inventories rose by ₦340,000 thousand, receivables by ₦1,180,000 thousand, payables by ₦890,000 thousand, deposits by ₦120,000 thousand and employee benefit obligations by ₦1,600,000 thousand, while prepayments fell by ₦60,000 thousand. Cash at 1 January 2026 was ₦2,480,000 thousand. Prepare the Statement of Cash Flows."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Cash Flows for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**CASH FLOWS FROM OPERATING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Surplus for the year",16210000],["Adjustments for non-cash items:@t",null],["Depreciation and amortisation",4850000],["Impairment loss",260000],["Finance cost",1900000],["Investment income",-420000],["Operating surplus before working capital changes@sub",22800000],["Increase in inventories",-340000],["Increase in receivables",-1180000],["Decrease in prepayments",60000],["Increase in payables",890000],["Increase in deposits",120000],["Increase in employee benefit obligations",1600000],["Cash generated from operations@sub",23950000],["Finance cost paid",-1900000],["Net cash from operating activities@t",22050000],["**CASH FLOWS FROM INVESTING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Purchase of property, plant and equipment",-16800000],["Purchase of intangible assets",-350000],["Investment income received",420000],["Net cash used in investing activities@t",-16730000],["**CASH FLOWS FROM FINANCING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Proceeds from borrowings",2000000],["Repayment of borrowings",-5770000],["Net cash used in financing activities@t",-3770000],["NET INCREASE IN CASH AND CASH EQUIVALENTS@t",1550000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",2480000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",4030000]]}},{"key":"The net increase of ₦1,550,000 thousand is **exactly the net cash flow reported under the cash basis in Chapter 14.5**, and the closing figure of ₦4,030,000 thousand is the cash in the Statement of Financial Position. Cash is cash whatever the basis of accounting: what accrual reporting changes is everything *around* the cash, not the cash itself. Demonstrating that articulation is worth stating explicitly in an answer."},{"note":"Note the two-step treatment of finance cost and investment income. Both are removed from the surplus in the adjustments — finance cost added back because it is not an operating item, investment income deducted for the same reason — and then shown at the point where the cash actually moved: finance cost paid within operating (IPSAS permits either operating or financing), investment income received within investing."}]}}]},{"n":"16.6","t":"Advantages of accrual reporting","b":[{"ol":["**It discloses the full financial position.** Assets, receivables, payables and long-term obligations all appear, so the reader can see what government owns and owes rather than only what passed through the bank.","**It shows the true cost of services.** Depreciation charges the consumption of assets to the period that benefited, so the cost per pupil, per patient or per kilometre can be computed and value for money assessed.","**It improves asset management.** Recognising and depreciating assets forces the maintenance of asset registers, which most governments previously lacked, and makes deterioration visible.","**It discloses the real level of public debt**, including obligations incurred but not yet paid and pension liabilities.","**It removes the incentive to manipulate by timing.** Deferring a payment past the year end no longer improves the reported result, because the expense was recognised when incurred.","**It improves comparability** with other governments, with international organisations and with the private sector.","**It supports better decisions** on whether to buy or lease, to maintain or replace, and to contract out or provide directly — none of which can be evaluated properly on cash information."]},{"p":"Against these must be set the cost and complexity of accrual accounting, the judgement it requires and the scope for manipulation that judgement creates, and the difficulty of valuing government assets such as heritage assets and infrastructure for which no market exists. These are the arguments a balanced answer should acknowledge before concluding in favour of accrual."}]},{"n":"16.7","t":"IPSAS 3 — accounting policies, changes in estimates and errors","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Accounting policies**","The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices an entity applies in preparing and presenting financial statements"],["**Change in accounting estimate**","An adjustment to the carrying amount of an asset or liability (or to periodic consumption of an asset) from reassessing its present status and expected future benefits/obligations — arises from **new information**, so it is **not** a correction of an error"],["**Prior period error**","An omission from, or misstatement in, prior-period financial statements from failing to use, or misusing, reliable information that was available and could reasonably have been obtained at the time — includes arithmetic mistakes, misapplied policies, oversight, misinterpretation and fraud"],["**Material** omission/misstatement","One that could, individually or collectively, influence users' decisions — materiality is a threshold, not a primary qualitative characteristic in itself"]]}},{"p":"A change **from one basis of accounting to another** is a change in accounting policy, as is a change in how a transaction, event or condition is recognised or measured within a basis. It is **not** a change in policy to apply a policy to transactions that genuinely differ in substance from before, or to a new/previously immaterial kind of transaction."},{"note":"Disclosure on initial application of a new IPSAS covers: the standard's title; any transitional provision and its effect on future periods; the nature of the change; the amount of adjustment by line item; and, if retrospective application is impracticable, why, and how/from when the change was applied instead. A **voluntary** change additionally requires disclosing why the new policy gives more reliable and relevant information."}]},{"n":"16.8","t":"Notes to the financial statements and qualitative characteristics","b":[{"p":"Every component of the financial statements must be clearly identified, and the following prominently displayed (repeated wherever needed for understanding): the entity's name, domicile and legal form; the nature of its operations; the extent to which transitional provisions were used; whether the statements cover the individual or the economic entity; the reporting date/period; the reporting currency and level of rounding; a reference to the governing legislation; the measurement bases and accounting policies applied; a statement of IPSAS compliance; supporting information for each line item, in presentation order; and other disclosures including contingencies, commitments and non-financial matters."},{"h4":"Qualitative characteristics of financial reporting"},{"p":"The four **principal** characteristics are **understandability, relevance, reliability and comparability**; IPSAS 3 §37 requires accounting policies to be developed so the statements meet these and the related characteristics below."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Characteristic","What it means"],"rows":[["**Understandability**","Comprehensible to a user with reasonable knowledge of the entity who studies the information — complexity alone is no reason to omit something"],["**Relevance**","Helps evaluate past/present/future events or confirm/correct earlier evaluations; must also be timely"],["**Materiality**","A threshold, not a characteristic in itself — omission/misstatement that could influence a user's decision"],["**Reliability**","Free from material error and bias; can be depended on"],["**Faithful representation**","Presented per the substance of events, not merely their legal form"],["**Substance over form**","Substance and economic reality govern presentation, since these are not always the same as legal form"],["**Neutrality**","Free from bias — not selected/presented to engineer a predetermined outcome"],["**Prudence**","Caution under uncertainty — but never hidden reserves, excessive provisions, or deliberate over/understatement, since that would itself be non-neutral"],["**Completeness**","Complete within the bounds of materiality and cost"],["**Comparability**","Users can spot similarities/differences — between entities, and for the same entity over time; requires disclosing policies, policy changes and their effects, and showing corresponding prior-period figures"],["**Timeliness**","Undue delay loses relevance; reporting too early can weaken reliability — balance struck by what best serves users' decisions"],["**Balance of cost and benefit**","A pervasive constraint — information's benefit should exceed the cost of providing it, though this is a matter of judgement since costs and beneficiaries often differ"]]}}]},{"n":"16.9","t":"IPSAS 45 — Property, Plant and Equipment (replaces IPSAS 17)","b":[{"p":"Applies to every accrual-basis entity's PP&E except where another standard applies — and **not** to agricultural biological assets (other than bearer plants), mineral rights/reserves, assets held for sale (IPSAS 44) or exploration/evaluation assets. It **does** apply to heritage assets, infrastructure assets, weapons systems, and to service concession assets once initially recognised under IPSAS 32."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Special asset class","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["**Heritage assets**","Rare/significant archaeologically, culturally, historically, etc. — restricted use/disposal, irreplaceable, long or indefinite useful lives (e.g. historic buildings, monuments, museum collections, art)"],["**Infrastructure assets**","Networks/systems serving the community at large, long lives, continually maintained/replaced/refurbished — removing components can impair the whole network's service potential"],["**Weapons systems**","Warships, submarines, military aircraft, tanks, missile carriers/launchers, even single-use ordnance providing an ongoing deterrence service"]]}},{"h4":"Recognition, measurement and revaluation"},{"ul":["PP&E cost is recognised as an asset only if future economic benefits/service potential will probably flow to the entity **and** the cost/fair value is reliably measurable. Recognition stops once the asset is ready for its intended use — later relocation costs or initial operating losses while demand builds up are **not** capitalised.","**Cost** comprises the purchase price (less discounts, plus import duties/non-refundable taxes), directly attributable costs of bringing it to working condition (site preparation, delivery, installation), and the initial estimate of dismantling/restoration costs the entity is obliged to incur.","An asset acquired through a **non-exchange transaction** (e.g. donated) is measured at its **deemed cost**.","After recognition, an entity chooses, by class: the **cost model** (cost less accumulated depreciation/impairment) or the **revaluation model** (fair/operational value at revaluation date, less subsequent depreciation/impairment).","A **revaluation increase** is credited to the **revaluation surplus** (through surplus or deficit only to the extent it reverses a previous decrease of the same class); a **revaluation decrease** goes through **surplus or deficit** (charged to any existing revaluation surplus of that class first). Increases/decreases are offset **within** a class but never **across** classes.","Depreciation: charged on the depreciable amount (cost less residual value) over useful life, by a method reflecting the pattern of consumption of benefits; each significant part is depreciated separately; useful life, residual value and method are reviewed at least annually and any change is a **change in accounting estimate** under IPSAS 3.","Derecognised on disposal, or when no further benefit is expected; the gain/loss (net disposal proceeds less carrying amount) goes to surplus or deficit."]},{"note":"Disclose, per class: the measurement basis; depreciation method and useful life/rate; gross carrying amount and accumulated depreciation/impairment at both period ends; and a full reconciliation of the carrying amount — additions, disposals, acquisitions through combinations, revaluation movements, impairment losses and reversals, depreciation, and exchange differences."}]},{"n":"16.10","t":"IPSAS 22 (general government sector) and Treasury circular on accrual books","b":[{"def":{"t":"General Government Sector (GGS)","d":"all resident central, state and local government units and social security funds at every level, plus non-market non-profit institutions they control (departments, courts, public schools/hospitals, other agencies) — funded mainly from taxes, corporation dividends and borrowing. It excludes the PFC and PNFC sectors."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Sector","Nature"],"rows":[["**Public Financial Corporation (PFC) sector**","Government-controlled financial corporations/institutions (including central banks) engaged in market financial intermediation"],["**Public Non-Financial Corporation (PNFC) sector**","Government-controlled entities producing goods/non-financial services for the market — e.g. publicly owned utilities"],["**Government Business Enterprise (GBE)**","Contracts in its own name; has financial/operational authority to run a business; sells at a profit or full cost recovery; not reliant on continuing government funding to remain a going concern; controlled by a public sector entity"]]}},{"p":"A government electing to disclose GGS information discloses, at minimum: assets and liabilities by major class (showing investment in other sectors separately); net assets/equity; revaluation and other items recognised directly in net assets/equity; revenue and expenses by major class; surplus or deficit; and cash flows from operating, investing and financing activities by major class — plus the significant controlled entities within the GGS and any changes to that list, with reasons."},{"p":"The **Federal Treasury Circular Ref. TRY/A7 & B7 and OAGF/CAD/026/Vol.III/188** (3 July 2018) standardises accrual-basis IPSAS books of account across MDAs. It makes the **Director/Head of Finance and Accounts** responsible for compliance with the Financial Regulations and the Accounting Code/Manual; for organising finance and accounting to secure financial control, due diligence and due process; for advising the Accounting Officer on financial matters; for maintaining the main and subsidiary ledgers; and for the control and management of property, plant and equipment."}]},{"n":"16.11","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A — multiple choice"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which one of the following is NOT one of the characteristics of a Government Business Enterprise (GBE)? (A) It is an entity with the power to contract in its own name  (B) It has been assigned the financial and operational authority to carry on a business  (C) It is controlled by a public sector entity  (D) It is not reliant on continuing government funding to be a going concern  (E) A legal entity created for the purpose of producing goods and services for the market","Which one of the following is NOT one of the definitions stated under IPSAS 3? (A) Accounting policies  (B) Changes in accounting estimates  (C) Prior period errors  (D) Recoverable service amount  (E) Material omissions or misstatements","The following items are classified as investing activities, except (A) Cash repayment of amounts borrowed  (B) Cash receipts from sale of property, plant and equipment, intangibles and other long-term assets  (C) Cash receipts from sale of equity or debt instruments of other entities  (D) Cash advances and loans made to other parties  (E) Cash payments to acquire equity or debt instruments of other entities","The following are to be presented on the face of the statement of financial performance, except (A) Revenue  (B) Finance costs  (C) Cash payments to and on behalf of employees  (D) Surplus or deficit  (E) Minority interest share of surplus/(deficit)","The following terms are used in IPSAS 22, except (A) General Government Sector (GGS)  (B) Entity-specific value  (C) Public Financial Corporation sector (PFC)  (D) Public Non-Financial Corporation sector (PNFC)  (E) Government Business Enterprises (GBEs)","What is the purpose of the statement of financial position under accrual basis IPSAS? (A) To report on cash flows  (B) To report on financial performance  (C) To report on financial position  (D) To report on budgetary information  (E) To report on changes in equity","What type of entities can use accrual-basis IPSAS? (A) Public sector entities  (B) Private sector entities  (C) Non-profit organisations  (D) All of the above  (E) None of the above","What is a benefit of using accrual-basis IPSAS? (A) Simplified accounting reporting  (B) Improved financial reporting and transparency  (C) Enhanced transparency and accountability  (D) All of the above  (E) None of the above","How often should financial statements be prepared under cash-basis IPSAS? (A) Annually  (B) Quarterly  (C) Monthly  (D) Bi-annually  (E) As required by law or regulation","What is the objective of the cash-basis IPSAS framework? (A) To provide a comprehensive framework for financial reporting  (B) To simplify financial reporting (C) To enhance transparency  (D) All of the above  (E) None of the above"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** D  **3.** A  **4.** C  **5.** B  **6.** C  **7.** A  **8.** D  **9.** A  **10.** A"}]}},{"h3":"Section A — short answer"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to cash and subject to insignificant risk of a change in value are called …","… are the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices an entity applies in preparing and presenting financial statements.","… items are material if they could, individually or collectively, influence the decisions of users of the financial statements.","An … of a cash-generating asset is the amount by which the carrying amount exceeds its recoverable amount.","An entity that sells goods and services, in the normal course of its business, to other entities at a profit or full cost recovery is called a …","The framework for financial reporting that focuses on cash receipts and payments, ensuring transparency and accountability in the public sector, is found in …","The accounting basis under which revenues and expenses are recognised when earned or incurred is …","Besides annual financial statements, what other financial reports are prepared?","The disclosure of accounting policies, cash flows and budgetary information under the cash basis is contained in …","The report on the cash flows of a public entity, showing cash receipts, payments and the net change in cash, is the …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** Cash equivalents  **2.** Accounting policies  **3.** Material omissions or misstatements  **4.** Impairment loss  **5.** Government Business Enterprise (GBE)  **6.** Cash-basis IPSAS  **7.** Accrual basis  **8.** Interim financial reports  **9.** Notes to the financial statements  **10.** Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments (Statement of Cash Flows)"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — statement of cash flow (direct method), Welfare State of Nigeria","open":true,"q":[{"p":"For the year ended 31 December 2021, Welfare State of Nigeria reported (₦'000): personal emolument 2,000,000; CRF charges 1,000,000; statutory revenue allocation 20,000,000; proceeds from sale of tangible assets 100,000; purchase of marketable securities 50,000; purchase/construction of tangible assets 500,000; share of VAT 200,000; share of excess crude oil 100,000; internally generated revenue 10,000,000; gratuities and pensions 15,000,000; miscellaneous income 50,000; overhead expenses 36,000; recurrent grants made 20,000; miscellaneous expenses 10,000; servicing/repayment of public debt 100,000; grants/subventions from NGOs 200,000; proceeds from loans and other borrowings 300,000; dividends received 100,000. Prepare the statement of cash flow using the direct method."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Welfare State of Nigeria — statement of cash flows for the year ended 31 December 2021 (₦'000)","align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["**Operating activities**",""],["Statutory revenue allocation","20,000,000"],["Share of excess crude oil","100,000"],["Internally generated revenue","10,000,000"],["Share of value added tax","200,000"],["Grants and subventions received","200,000"],["Miscellaneous income","50,000"],["Personal emoluments","(2,000,000)"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","(1,000,000)"],["Gratuities and pensions","(15,000,000)"],["Overhead expenses","(36,000)"],["Recurrent grants made","(20,000)"],["Miscellaneous expenses","(10,000)"],["Servicing and repayment of public debt","(100,000)"],["**Net cash flow from operating activities**","**12,384,000**"],["**Investing activities**",""],["Proceeds from sale of tangible assets","100,000"],["Purchase of marketable securities","(50,000)"],["Purchase and construction of tangible assets","(500,000)"],["**Net cash flow from investing activities**","**(450,000)**"],["**Financing activities**",""],["Proceeds from loans and other borrowings","300,000"],["Dividends received","100,000"],["**Net cash flow from financing activities**","**400,000**"],["**Net increase in cash and cash equivalents**","**12,334,000**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — qualitative characteristics","open":true,"q":[{"p":"IPSAS 3 §37 requires accounting policies to ensure the financial statements meet a number of qualitative characteristics. Discuss the main characteristics of financial reporting."}],"a":[{"p":"See §16.8 above for the full table: understandability, relevance, materiality, reliability, faithful representation, substance over form, neutrality, prudence, completeness, comparability, timeliness and the balance between cost and benefit."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — clear identification of financial statements, and reliability","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What information must be clearly displayed in the financial statements? (b) Name any four persons charged with responsibility for preparing and presenting financial statements. (c) Enumerate the factors that make financial statements reliable documents."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** The name of the reporting entity; the reporting date and the period covered; the presentation currency; and the level of rounding used."},{"p":"**(b)** The Accountant-General of the Federation (Federal accounts); the Accountant-General of a State (State accounts); the Local Government Treasurer/Head of Finance and Accounts; and the Head of Finance and Accounts of an MDA."},{"p":"**(c)** Faithfully representing the entity's financial performance and position; reflecting the economic substance of events rather than only their legal form; being free from material misstatement and bias; prudent preparation; and completeness in all material respects."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — statement of financial performance, Maiko State Government","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Maiko State Government's 31 December 2021 accounts included (₦m): land and buildings (cost) 155,000 [of which land 100,000]; long-term investments 75,000; equipment and furniture (cost) 27,000; motor vehicles (cost) 58,000; accumulated depreciation — buildings 16,000, motor vehicles 12,000, equipment/furniture 8,500; Federation Account allocation 115,000; VAT allocation 35,000; grants from Federal Government 13,500; internally generated fund 39,000; grant from donor agency 10,000; personal emolument 50,000; maintenance of premises 2,000; CRF charges 13,000; overhead expenses 10,000; miscellaneous expenditure/income 15,000 Dr / 24,500 Cr; long-term loans 100,000; Consolidated Revenue Fund (b/f) 32,500. Additional information: loan interest outstanding at year end was 5,000; depreciation is charged on cost at buildings 5%, motor vehicles 20%, equipment and furniture 15%; a building costing 5,000 with accumulated depreciation of 2,000 was sold for 4,500 and not yet adjusted; interest receivable amounted to 4,000. Prepare the statement of financial performance for the year."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Maiko State Government — statement of financial performance for the year ended 31 December 2021 (₦m)","align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["**Revenue**",""],["Federation Account allocation","115,000"],["VAT allocation","35,000"],["Grants from Federal Government","13,500"],["Internally generated revenue","39,000"],["Grant from donor agency","10,000"],["Miscellaneous income","24,500"],["Interest on investment (receivable)","4,000"],["Profit on sale of building","1,500"],["**Total revenue**","**242,500**"],["**Expenditure**",""],["Personal emolument","50,000"],["Maintenance of premises","2,000"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","13,000"],["Overhead expenses","10,000"],["Interest on loans (outstanding)","5,000"],["Depreciation — buildings","2,750"],["Depreciation — motor vehicles","11,600"],["Depreciation — equipment and furniture","4,050"],["Miscellaneous expenses","15,000"],["**Total expenditure**","**113,400**"],["**Surplus for the year**","**129,100**"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund b/f","32,500"],["**Consolidated Revenue Fund c/f**","**161,600**"]]}},{"note":"Workings: **depreciation** — buildings 5% × (155,000 − 100,000 land) = 2,750; motor vehicles 20% × 58,000 = 11,600; equipment/furniture 15% × 27,000 = 4,050. **Profit on disposal** — proceeds 4,500 less carrying amount (5,000 − 2,000) = 1,500."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Surplus for the year","tex":"\\text{Total revenue} - \\text{Total expenses}"},{"lb":"Accounting equation","tex":"\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities} = \\text{Net assets/Equity}"},{"lb":"Closing accumulated surplus","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Surplus for the year}"},{"lb":"Cash generated from operations","tex":"\\text{Surplus} + \\text{non-cash items} \\pm \\text{working capital changes}"},{"lb":"Net movement in cash","tex":"\\text{Operating} + \\text{Investing} + \\text{Financing}"}],"focus":"Together with Chapter 14, the core preparation chapter of the paper and increasingly the one set in Section B as accrual adoption beds in. Expect a list of balances from which two or three statements must be prepared. Present them in the proper IPSAS form with headings, subtotals and totals, and always perform the three articulation checks: net assets equals total net assets/equity; the closing accumulated surplus agrees with the changes statement; and closing cash in the cash flow statement equals cash in the position statement.","errors":["Treating capital expenditure as an expense in the Statement of Financial Performance; it is the acquisition of an asset, charged through depreciation.","Recognising a revaluation gain or a defined benefit remeasurement in surplus or deficit instead of in net assets/equity.","Omitting depreciation, which is the single largest accrual adjustment in most public sector statements.","Adding back a decrease in receivables and deducting an increase — the direction is the reverse for assets and for liabilities.","Classifying the purchase of property, plant and equipment as an operating rather than an investing cash flow.","Failing to reconcile closing cash in the cash flow statement to cash in the statement of financial position."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under accrual basis IPSAS, revenue is recognised when it is","o":["received in cash","earned","budgeted","assessed and collected","approved by the legislature"],"a":1,"w":"Revenue is recognised when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of when cash moves.","src":"Chapter 16.1","sec":"16.1"},{"q":"Total assets are ₦180,000,000 and total liabilities ₦52,000,000. Net assets are","o":["₦232,000,000","₦128,000,000","₦52,000,000","₦180,000,000","₦104,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"Net assets are total assets less total liabilities.","calc":"180{,}000{,}000 - 52{,}000{,}000 = ₦128{,}000{,}000","src":"Chapter 16.3","sec":"16.3"},{"q":"A gain on the revaluation of property is recognised in","o":["the Statement of Financial Performance","the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","the Statement of Cash Flows","the notes only","the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts"],"a":1,"w":"The gain goes to the revaluation reserve within net assets, not through surplus or deficit for the year.","src":"Chapter 16.4","sec":"16.4"},{"q":"In a cash flow statement prepared by the indirect method, an increase in receivables is","o":["added to the surplus","deducted from the surplus","shown as an investing outflow","shown as a financing outflow","ignored"],"a":1,"w":"Revenue has been recognised but the cash has not arrived, so cash is lower than the surplus suggests.","src":"Chapter 16.5","sec":"16.5"},{"q":"The purchase of property, plant and equipment is classified in the cash flow statement as","o":["an operating outflow","an investing outflow","a financing outflow","a non-cash item","an adjustment to net assets"],"a":1,"w":"Acquisition and disposal of non-current assets are investing activities.","src":"Chapter 16.5","sec":"16.5"},{"q":"Which item would appear in an accrual basis but NOT in a cash basis Statement of Financial Position?","o":["Cash and cash equivalents","Employee benefit obligations","Advances","Deposits","Investments"],"a":1,"w":"A pension obligation is incurred but unpaid, so it is recognised only under the accrual basis. The other four arise from cash transactions and appear under both.","src":"Chapter 16.3","sec":"16.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following balances were extracted from the accrual-based records of the Government of Ekiti State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000):\n\nRevenue: statutory allocation 27,400,000; VAT allocation 8,300,000; tax revenue 4,100,000; fees and licences 1,850,000; grants 650,000; investment income 300,000.\n\nExpenses: salaries and allowances 17,600,000; pension contributions 1,940,000; use of goods and services 7,250,000; depreciation 3,400,000; finance cost 1,260,000; grants and subsidies 900,000.\n\nAssets at 31 December 2026: property, plant and equipment 94,500,000; investments 2,100,000; inventories 1,480,000; receivables 3,920,000; cash and cash equivalents 2,760,000.\n\nLiabilities: payables 4,610,000; deposits 540,000; borrowings — current 2,400,000, non-current 19,300,000; employee benefit obligations 6,850,000. The revaluation reserve stands at 9,700,000.\n\n(a) Prepare the Statement of Financial Performance for the year. (b) Prepare the Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026. (c) Explain four advantages of accrual basis reporting over cash basis reporting for a state government.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Statement of Financial Performance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ekiti State — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**REVENUE**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",27400000],["Value added tax allocation",8300000],["Tax revenue",4100000],["Fees and licences",1850000],["Grants",650000],["Investment income",300000],["Total revenue@sub",42600000],["**EXPENSES**@t",null],["Salaries and allowances",-17600000],["Pension contributions",-1940000],["Use of goods and services",-7250000],["Depreciation",-3400000],["Grants and subsidies",-900000],["Finance cost",-1260000],["Total expenses@sub",-32350000],["SURPLUS FOR THE YEAR@tot",10250000]]}},{"h4":"(b) Statement of Financial Position"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ekiti State — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**NON-CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Property, plant and equipment",94500000],["Investments",2100000],["@sub",96600000],["**CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Inventories",1480000],["Receivables",3920000],["Cash and cash equivalents",2760000],["@sub",8160000],["TOTAL ASSETS@t",104760000],["**CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Payables",-4610000],["Deposits",-540000],["Current portion of borrowings",-2400000],["@sub",-7550000],["**NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Borrowings",-19300000],["Employee benefit obligations",-6850000],["@sub",-26150000],["TOTAL LIABILITIES@t",-33700000],["NET ASSETS@tot",71060000],["**NET ASSETS / EQUITY**@t",null],["Accumulated surplus (balancing figure)",61360000],["Revaluation reserve",9700000],["TOTAL NET ASSETS / EQUITY@tot",71060000]]}},{"p":"The accumulated surplus of ₦61,360,000 thousand is derived as net assets of ₦71,060,000 less the revaluation reserve of ₦9,700,000. Note also that total liabilities of ₦33.7 billion are equal to about 79% of a full year's revenue, of which the pension obligation of ₦6.85 billion would not have appeared at all under the cash basis."},{"h4":"(c) Advantages of accrual reporting"},{"ol":["**The full financial position is disclosed.** The statement above shows ₦94.5 billion of property, plant and equipment, ₦3.92 billion owed to the State and ₦33.7 billion owed by it, including a ₦6.85 billion pension obligation. Under the cash basis none of these would appear, and a reader could not tell whether the State was solvent. Disclosing accumulated liabilities is what makes it possible to hold a government to account for obligations it incurs but leaves to its successors.","**The true cost of services is measured.** Depreciation of ₦3.4 billion charges the consumption of assets to the year that benefited from them. Without it, a school built in 2010 appears to cost nothing to use in 2026, and no meaningful cost per pupil, per patient or per kilometre of road can be computed. Value for money cannot be assessed on information that omits the largest input.","**Manipulation by timing is removed.** Under the cash basis, deferring payment of contractors past the year end converts a deficit into a surplus without any change in substance, which is precisely how arrears accumulate. Under the accrual basis the expense is recognised when incurred and the unpaid amount appears as a payable, so the tactic achieves nothing.","**Asset management improves.** Recognising and depreciating assets requires an asset register that most governments have never maintained. The discipline of compiling and keeping it is frequently worth more than the accounting entries themselves, because it reveals what the State actually owns and what condition it is in.","**Comparability is improved**, with other states, with other countries and with the private sector, which strengthens the State's standing with lenders and rating agencies.","**Better decisions become possible** on buying against leasing, maintaining against replacing, and providing directly against contracting out — none of which can be evaluated on cash information alone."]},{"note":"A complete answer acknowledges the costs: accrual accounting is more expensive, requires skills scarce in the public service, and rests on judgements — asset lives, impairment, actuarial assumptions — that themselves create scope for manipulation. The conclusion is nevertheless that the information gained outweighs those costs, which is why IPSAS and Nigeria have both moved that way."}],"src":"Chapter 16.2–16.6","sec":"16.2"}]}},{"n":17,"t":"Accounting for Public Sector Organisations and Government Business Entities","brief":"Parastatals, corporations and Government Business Entities (GBEs): why they are set up, their classes, the accounting and reporting they follow, educational institution accounts, and their audit.","outcomes":["Discuss the Enabling Act that establishes a parastatal or a public company","Itemise the objectives of setting up public enterprises","Explain the accounting requirements of public enterprises","Discuss the sources of income and types of expenditure of public enterprises","Prepare the accounts of an educational institution","Describe how government enterprises are audited"],"secs":[{"n":"17.1","t":"Introduction — parastatals and public companies","b":[{"def":{"t":"Corporation/parastatal","d":"a special organisation set up by government to carry out certain projects or perform beneficial services to the nation — e.g. the **River Basin Authority** (harnessing river-basin agricultural benefits) or the **Federal Environmental Protection Agency (FEPA)** (safeguarding Nigeria's environment). Most corporations are **not-for-profit**, though some are expected to recover their operating costs and make a margin/surplus."}},{"p":"Each corporation/parastatal has its own **Enabling Act** — the law setting it up — which states in detail:"},{"ol":["The name of the corporation, its functions and objectives;","The principal officers of the board, their functions and mode of appointment;","The supervising ministry;","The place where the head office and branches will be sited;","The organogram of the organisation; and","The source of funds and the type of accounts it is expected to keep."]},{"key":"Parastatals/corporations are **not** governed by the Companies and Allied Matters Act, Cap. C20, LFN 2004 — hence a corporation's name does **not** end with \"Limited\" or \"Public Limited Company\"."},{"p":"State and Federal governments are free to set up their own corporations after due process. Such parastatals, boards or corporations differ from **ministries**: ministries and extra-ministerial departments share the same accounting system, unlike boards and corporations, and regulations applying to ministries may not apply to government agencies. \"Parastatal\" also refers to a government company, board, corporation, or a tertiary institution (e.g. Lagos State Polytechnic, University of Nigeria Nsukka, Ahmadu Bello University)."},{"p":"All corporations have **supervising ministries**. Regulations a corporation passes are called **bye-laws**. The supervising ministry and government must approve the following before they become operative:"},{"ol":["Increases in the prices of goods and services delivered — e.g. the Federal Ministry of Health approves any price increase by Federal Medical Centres before implementation;","All the bye-laws;","The corporation's annual budget; and","Any major foreign agreement."]},{"note":"The supervising ministry **recommends** — but does not itself make — the appointment of the managing director/general manager, executive director and key officers of a corporation; approval rests with the President or the National Assembly."}]},{"n":"17.2","t":"Main objectives of setting up corporations/parastatals/public enterprises","b":[{"ol":["Bring the means of production under public ownership;","Avoid high prices of goods normally charged by the private sector;","Avoid duplication of facilities;","Ensure close government control over certain \"key\" sectors of the economy;","Ensure the survival of the industries;","Avoid imitation of goods; and","Enhance the standard of living of the people."]}]},{"n":"17.3","t":"Classes of government enterprises","b":[{"def":{"t":"Public enterprises","d":"establishments owned and controlled partially or wholly by government, coming into existence through the promulgation of appropriate Federal or State laws."}},{"ul":["**Public utilities** — parastatals providing essential services to citizens either at \"nil\" cost or at subsidised rates, to balance social and economic objectives.","**Regulatory agencies** — government agencies or partially autonomous establishments executing general government policies within specified areas, e.g. the **National Communications Commission (NCC)** and the **Nigerian Copyright Commission**. May be fully or partially commercial, yet still look to government financial assistance to meet obligations.","**Commercial enterprises** — bodies established by government, in line with the country's laws, to create a competitive environment and make a profit — spanning mining, banking, insurance, manufacturing, trading and transportation. Autonomous in structure and operations, incorporated, and governed by laws such as the **Companies and Allied Matters Act, Cap C20, LFN 2004**, the **Insurance Act of 2000 (as amended)**, the **Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 1990 (as amended)** and the **Bankruptcy Act of 1979 (as updated)**.","**Government Business Enterprises (GBEs)** — an entity with **all** of these characteristics: (i) has the power to contract in its own name; (ii) has been assigned financial and operational authority to carry on a business; (iii) sells goods and services in the normal course of business to other entities, at a profit or full cost recovery; (iv) is not reliant on continuing government funding to be a going concern (other than purchases of outputs at arm's length); and (v) is controlled by a public sector entity. GBEs include trading enterprises (e.g. utilities) and financial enterprises, are, in substance, no different from private-sector entities doing similar work, and generally operate to make a profit, though some carry limited community-service obligations to supply goods/services at no or reduced charge."]}]},{"n":"17.4","t":"Public sector enterprises accounting","b":[{"p":"The nature and structure of accounting in public enterprises depends largely on their scope and objectives, so it varies from one enterprise to another. Despite this, any accounting system set up for a public enterprise should be able to:"},{"ol":["Provide detailed financial information adequate for policy formulation;","Facilitate extraction of relevant financial statements complying with the enabling law and the needs of information users;","Accommodate changes that become necessary; and","Facilitate the work of appointed auditors."]},{"h3":"17.4.1 Sources of income of parastatals"},{"p":"Corporations are set up mainly to render social services at the least possible cost, and are principally self-financing, but the appropriate government makes funds available to them as **subventions**. Money given by government to a corporation is income to it, usually classified into **recurrent** and **capital** grant/subvention."},{"h3":"17.4.2 Expenditure of parastatals"},{"p":"All corporations incur expenses such as staff salaries and facility maintenance. Expenditure is either **revenue** or **capital** in nature. Most corporations depreciate their assets using appropriate policies."},{"h3":"17.4.3 Financial statements"},{"p":"Financial statements must comply with normal accounting standards and the requirements of the laws regulating the enterprise. For **profit-making** public enterprises, they comprise:"},{"ol":["Statement of financial position;","Statement of financial performance;","Statement of changes in net assets/equity;","Statement of cash flow;","Accounting policies; and","Notes to the financial statements and other disclosures."]},{"p":"For **not-for-profit** public enterprises, the financial statements are expected to include the same six, in more detail:"},{"h4":"(a) Statement of financial position"},{"p":"Shows assets, liabilities and net assets/equity, both categorised as **current** and **non-current**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Includes"],"rows":[["**Current assets**","Cash and equivalents; receivables; inventories; prepayments; other current assets"],["**Non-current assets**","Loans granted (receivables); investments; infrastructure, plant and equipment; investment property, plant and equipment; intangible assets"],["**Current liabilities**","Deposits; short-term loans and advances; unremitted deductions; accrued expenses"],["**Non-current liabilities**","Public funds; long-term borrowings"]]}},{"key":"These sub-groupings must, **as a minimum**, be disclosed on the face of the statement of financial position. Assets are shown **net of all provisions**, with details disclosed in the notes."},{"h4":"(b) Statement of financial performance"},{"p":"(The income and expenditure account.) Shows income accrued from all sources and expenditure incurred during the period. As a **minimum**, the face of the statement should include:"},{"ol":["Revenue from operating activities;","Surplus or deficit from operating activities;","Finance costs;","Share of net surpluses/deficits of associates and joint ventures accounted for by the equity method;","Surplus or deficit from ordinary activities;","Extraordinary items;","Minority interest's share of net surplus or deficit;","Net surplus or deficit for the period."]},{"note":"Expenses are classified either **by nature** or **by function**. If classified by function, a presentation **by nature** must also be given in the notes."},{"h4":"(c) Statement of changes in net assets/equity"},{"def":{"t":"Net assets/equity","d":"assets less liabilities, financed by reserves, accumulated surpluses/deficits and minority interest."}},{"p":"Important because it lets users ascertain the causes of movement in net equity. Changes are normally caused by:"},{"ul":["Significant changes in accounting policies;","Correction of prior years' errors;","Revaluation of assets;","Surplus or deficit for the period; and","Changes in currency translation."]},{"h4":"(d) Statement of cash flow"},{"p":"Required by **IPSAS 1**; its preparation and presentation is covered by **IPSAS 2**. Identifies the sources of cash inflows, items on which cash was expended, and the closing cash balance. Reported under three activities:"},{"ul":["**Operating activities** — the entity's day-to-day activities, not investing or financing;","**Investing activities** — acquisition/disposal of long-term assets and other investments not included in cash equivalents;","**Financing activities** — changes in the size and composition of contributed capital and borrowings."]},{"h4":"(e) Accounting policies"},{"def":{"t":"Accounting policies","d":"the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices an entity adopts in preparing and presenting financial statements — themselves part of the financial statements. Entities select and apply accounting policies so their statements comply with every applicable IPSAS."}},{"h4":"(f) Notes to the financial statements and other disclosures"},{"p":"Additional information helping users understand and compare the financial statements — narrative descriptions or detailed schedules/analyses of the amounts shown, plus items such as contingent liabilities and commitments. IPSAS does not prescribe a note format, but as a **minimum**, notes are presented in this order:"},{"ol":["Statement of compliance with IPSAS;","Statement of the measurement basis (bases) and accounting policies applied;","Supporting information for items on the face of each statement, in the order each is presented; and","Other disclosures, including contingencies, commitments and other financial/non-financial disclosures."]},{"p":"**Non-financial disclosures** include:"},{"ul":["Domicile and legal form of the entity;","Nature of the entity's operations and principal activities;","Reference to relevant legislation governing its operations; and","The name of the controlling and ultimate controlling entity of the economic entity (where applicable)."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Statement of financial performance and position — Federal Airport Authority of Nigeria","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A typical format of the Statement of Financial Performance and Statement of Financial Position of the Federal Airport Authority of Nigeria (FAAN), showing comparative years, follows."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"FAAN — Statement of Financial Performance (illustrative format)","rows":[["**Income**@t",null],["Government subventions",null],["Licence fees",null],["Parking fees",null],["FAAN citizen tax",null],["Staff school income",null],["Guest house income",null],["Sundry income",null],["**Expenditure**@t",null],["Salary and allowances",null],["Postage, cable and telephone",null],["Airport maintenance",null],["Printing and stationery",null],["Books and periodicals",null],["Training",null],["(Deficit)/surplus for the year@sub",null],["Add/(deduct) balance brought forward",null],["(Deficit)/surplus carried forward@tot",null]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"FAAN — Statement of Financial Position (illustrative format)","rows":[["Non-current assets@t",null],["**Current assets**@t",null],["Inventory",null],["Trade receivables",null],["Bank",null],["Cash",null],["**Less: Current liabilities**@t",null],["Trade payables",null],["Other payables",null],["Total net assets@tot",null],["**Financed by**@t",null],["Capital fund",null],["Other funds",null],["Reserves",null]]}},{"note":"This is the study text's own line-item skeleton (all figures shown as \"x\" in the original), reproduced to show which revenue/expenditure heads and which net-asset financing items a public enterprise like FAAN discloses — see the fully worked illustrations at §17.8 for figures."}]}}]},{"n":"17.5","t":"Preparation of educational institutions' accounts","b":[{"p":"Educational institution accounts were formally maintained on the **cash basis**; however, following the **IPSAS accrual basis** introduced by the Federal Government of Nigeria from the **2016** financial year, all educational institutions now operate the accrual basis."},{"p":"An educational institution typically maintains **domestic** and **boarding** accounts. The **domestic account** deals with tuition fees, admission fees, fines, session charges and special fees (e.g. laboratory, library, sports fees); the **boarding account** deals with boarding house fees, cleaning, lighting, etc."},{"h3":"Books of account"},{"ul":["**Cash book** — a columnar cash book records all receipts and payments; each is analysed into its appropriate head, and the total of each analysis column gives the amount received/paid under that head. Periodic reconciliation should be made between fees collected, fees outstanding at the start/end of the period, and fees written off, against what should have been collected (given student numbers, free studentships, concessions, etc.); grants, scholarships and stipends should also be separately receipted — all recorded in the columnar cashbook.","**Personal ledger**, consisting of: **(i) Collection ledger/register** — separate records of tuition, admission, fines, session and special fee collections; **(ii) Students' ledger** — where these collections are credited to each student's account, including free studentship, concessions and write-offs of irrecoverable fees (which need higher-authority sanction); **(iii) Donors' register** — the amount provided by donors, amounts actually collected, and the amount outstanding; **(iv) Salaries and wages register** — amounts paid/payable to employees, with a monthly total also recorded in the cash book; **(v) Inventory book register** — all property purchased (buildings, furniture, investments, books, consumable inventories, etc.), first recorded in the cash book, then in the inventory books, and adjusted when an asset is sold so the inventory books stay up to date.","**Annual statement of accounts** — reveals actual income and expenditure for the year, classified under appropriate heads; must be audited by chartered accountants, and is submitted to the institution's governing body along with the auditor's report, becoming final upon approval. Prepared in **columnar form**: budgeted figures, actuals, and the variance between them."]}]},{"n":"17.6","t":"Audit of government enterprises","b":[{"p":"The laws setting up most Federal corporations state that:"},{"ol":["An **internal audit department** should be established, auditing the corporation and forwarding report copies to the Auditor-General for the Federation, for information only;","The **annual accounts** of the corporation must be audited by an **external auditor**;","Under **Section 85(125), sub-section 3(b)**, the Auditor-General may comment on the annual accounts and the external auditor's reports, and report to the legislature; and **Section 85(125), sub-section 4** empowers the Auditor-General to conduct **periodic checks** on all government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies, including all persons/bodies established by an Act of the National (or State) Assembly."]}]},{"n":"17.7","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the establishment of different types of corporations, their accounting policies, income receivable, expenditure incurred, and their peculiar reporting system."}]},{"n":"17.8","t":"End of chapter review questions","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is true of parastatals? (i) They have their enabling laws (ii) They are natural legal persons that can sue and be sued (iii) They are artificial legal persons that can sue and be sued. (A) I only  (B) II only  (C) III only  (D) I and II  (E) I and III","Why are public companies and corporations set up? (i) To beat down high prices charged by commercial businesses (ii) To raise the standard of living of the people (iii) To ensure the survival of some industries. (A) I only  (B) II only  (C) III only  (D) I and II  (E) I, II and III","The following are the main objectives of parastatals except (A) Bring the means of production under public ownership  (B) Avoid high prices of goods normally charged by the private sector  (C) Avoid duplication of facilities  (D) Maximisation of profits  (E) Ensure the survival of the industries","The financial statements of parastatals will include the following except (A) Statement of financial position  (B) Statement of financial performance  (C) Statement of monthly transcripts  (D) Statement of cash flow  (E) Accounting policies","Changes in net assets/equity are normally caused by the following except (A) The acquisition and disposal of non-current assets  (B) Correction of prior years' errors  (C) Revaluation of the assets  (D) Surplus or deficit for the period  (E) Changes in currency translation","Public companies and corporations are government agencies set up for … purposes.","The subventions which government gives may be … or … in nature.","Parastatals have … Ministries.","Collection of tuition fees, admission fees, fines, session charges and special fees e.g. laboratory fees, library fees, sports fees etc. should be separately recorded in …","The … has the power to conduct periodic checks of all government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities, agencies, including all persons and bodies established by an Act of the National (State) Assembly."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** E  **3.** D  **4.** C  **5.** A  **6.** Specific  **7.** Recurrent or Capital  **8.** Supervising  **9.** Collection register and ledger  **10.** Auditor-General"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — Bolus Electricity Board","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The following information was extracted from the books of Bolus Electricity Board for the year ended 31 December 2021 (N'000): accumulated depreciation b/f 45,224; sale of electricity 114,392; purchase of electricity 95,784; meter reading, billing and collection of electricity 1,624; non-current assets expenditure (at cost) 84,102; debtors for electricity consumption 12,006; training and welfare 692; stock and work in progress 1,234; rents, rates and insurance 2,126; electricity estimated unread consumption 7,222; administration and general expenses 1,476; Electricity Council grant 21,556; preparation of Electricity Council's expenses 362; bank and cash balance 1,284; depreciation for the year 3,634; hire purchase/deferred payment instalments not yet due 2,672; interest and financing expenses 2,434; creditors and accrued liabilities 13,926; profit on contracting and sale of appliance poles 534; reserves 23,116; rental of meters, etc. 556; distribution cost 4,476; customer service 1,810. Prepare, in vertical form, the Statement of Financial Performance and Statement of Financial Position of Bolus Electricity Board for the year ended 31 December 2021."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Bolus Electricity Board — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2021 (N'000)","rows":[["Sales of electricity",114392],["Less: Purchases of electricity",-95784],["Gross profit@sub",18608],["Profit on contracting and sale of appliance poles",534],["Rental of meter applications",556],["Total profit (A)@sub",19698],["Meter reading, billing and collection of electricity",-1624],["Training and welfare",-692],["Rent, rates and insurance",-2126],["Administration and general expenses",-1476],["Preparation of Electricity Council's expenses",-362],["Depreciation",-3634],["Interest and financing expenses",-2434],["Distribution cost",-4476],["Customer services",-1810],["Total (B)@sub",-18634],["Net income (A − B)@tot",1064]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Bolus Electricity Board — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2021 (N'000)","rows":[["At cost",84102],["Less: Accumulated depreciation (45,224 + 3,634)",-48858],["Net book value@sub",35244],["**Current assets**@t",null],["Inventory and work-in-progress",1234],["Trade receivables",12006],["Electricity estimated unread consumption",7222],["Hire purchase and deferred payment instalments",2672],["Bank balance and cash",1284],["@sub",24418],["Less: Current liabilities — trade and other payables",-13926],["Working capital@sub",10492],["Net total assets@tot",45736],["**Financed by**@t",null],["Electricity Council grant",21556],["Reserves brought forward",23116],["Retained profit for the year",1064],["@tot",45736]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — Okokomaiko State University","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The trial balance of Okokomaiko State University for the year ended 31 December 2021 showed (N'000): land and buildings at cost 55,000 Dr; long-term investments 25,000 Dr; accumulated depreciation — land and buildings 6,000 Cr, motor vehicles 2,000 Cr, equipment and furniture 1,500 Cr; motor vehicles at cost 28,000 Dr; grants from Central Government 15,000 Cr, State Government 5,000 Cr, Local Governments 3,500 Cr; endowment, donations and subventions 39,000 Cr; Computer Board grant 10,000 Cr; residences and catering operations 5,000 Dr / 7,500 Cr; academic fees and support grant 9,000 Cr; maintenance of premises 2,000 Dr; academic services 3,000 Dr; academic departments 10,000 Dr; general education expenditure 9,000 Dr; equipment and furniture 17,000 Dr; miscellaneous expenditure/income 5,000 Dr / 4,500 Cr; research grants and contracts 3,500 Dr / 4,000 Cr; long-term loans 50,000 Cr; current assets/liabilities 15,500 Dr / 4,500 Cr; appeal funds 5,000 Cr; general fund 10,500 Cr; reserves 1,000 Cr. Additional information: (i) loan interest outstanding at year-end was N5 million; (ii) depreciation on cost: buildings 5% (land cost N25 million, not depreciated), motor vehicles 20%, equipment and furniture 15%; (iii) a building costing N5 million with accumulated depreciation of N1 million was sold for N8 million, not yet adjusted; (iv) interest receivable was N6 million, and N5 million is to be transferred to reserves. Prepare the Statement of Financial Performance for the year and the Statement of Financial Position as at that date."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Okokomaiko State University — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2021 (N'000)","rows":[["Grants from Central Government",15000],["Grants from State Government",5000],["Grants from Local Government",3500],["Endowment, donations and subvention",39000],["Computer Board grant",10000],["Residences and catering operations",7500],["Academic fees and support grant",9000],["Research grants and contracts",4000],["Miscellaneous income",4500],["Profit on sale of building",4000],["Interest on investments",6000],["Total income@sub",107500],["Residence and catering operations",-5000],["Maintenance of premises",-2000],["Academic services",-3000],["Academic department",-10000],["General education expenditure",-9000],["Research grant and contract",-3500],["Interest on loans",-5000],["Depreciation — Buildings",-1250],["Depreciation — Motor vehicles",-5600],["Depreciation — Equipment and furniture",-2550],["Miscellaneous expenses",-5000],["Total expenditure@sub",-51900],["Surplus for the year@sub",55600],["Transfer to reserves",-5000],["Surplus after transfer@tot",50600]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Okokomaiko State University — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2021 (N'000)","rows":[["Land and buildings — cost 50,000, accum. dep. 6,250",43750],["Equipment and furniture — cost 17,000, accum. dep. 4,050",12950],["Motor vehicles — cost 28,000, accum. dep. 7,600",20400],["Total non-current assets (net)@sub",77100],["Long-term investments",25000],["Current assets (W1)",29500],["Current liabilities (W2)",-9500],["Net current assets@sub",20000],["Total net assets@tot",122100],["**Financed by**@t",null],["General funds",61100],["Appeal fund",5000],["Reserves",6000],["Long-term loan",50000],["@tot",122100]]}},{"p":"**Workings —** (W1) Current assets: b/f 15,500 + proceeds from sale of building 8,000 + interest received 6,000 = **29,500**. (W2) Current liabilities: b/f 4,500 + loan interest 5,000 = **9,500**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — accounting policies and objectives of parastatals","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What are the most common accounting policies adopted by parastatals and public companies? (b) Give the main objectives of setting up parastatals, departments and agencies."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a) Common accounting policies"},{"ol":["**Accrual basis of accounting** — income relating to a period is recognised in that period whether or not cash has been received; expenses are charged against profit when incurred, even if not yet paid.","Some corporations prepare **Income and Expenditure accounts**, showing a **surplus/deficit**; others (profit-oriented) prepare a **Statement of Financial Performance**.","The capital/proprietorship of the organisation is represented by a **\"fund account\"**.","Unlike the three tiers of government, parastatals show non-current assets at historical cost, accumulated depreciation to date, and net book value.","Government subventions/grants are usually stated as amounts received during the year (cash basis); some corporations instead credit them to income and expenditure and show them as current assets on an accrual basis.","Interest receivable on fixed deposits is usually accounted for on a cash basis; some corporations use the accrual method.","Foreign currency transactions: income/expenditure items are translated at the average rate; non-current asset translations use historical cost; other assets/liabilities are translated at the rate ruling at the reporting date, with the translation gain/loss shown in the Statement of Financial Performance yearly."]},{"h4":"(b) Main objectives of setting up parastatals, departments and agencies"},{"ol":["To bring the means of production under public ownership;","To avoid high prices of goods normally charged by private ownership;","To avoid duplication of facilities;","To ensure close government control over certain \"key\" sectors of the economy;","To ensure survival of industries;","To improve the standard of living of the people."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — Government College, Kangiwa bank reconciliation","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mallam Saani Magana, Bursar of Government College, Kangiwa for about 10 years, was solely in charge of collecting revenue, issuing receipts, custody and lodging of money, keeping tellers, collecting bank statements, posting the cash book, and preparing bank reconciliation statements in arrears. His lifestyle changed suddenly from \"low-profile\" to ostentatious spending, arousing suspicion. The Principal commissioned a firm of Chartered Accountants, whose investigation revealed: cheques issued but not presented N300,000; uncredited cheques N530,000; bank charges N100,000; cash collections not yet banked N30,000; direct POS transfers into the bank account N66,000; Bursar's cash float balance N750,000; e-subscriptions paid directly to the bank N2,000; a \"standing order\" for staff pension of N20,000 effected at the bank only; the bank statement showed a balance of N356,000. Required: (i) Identify four basic control barriers faced by the Bursar's operational system. (ii) Prepare the bank reconciliation statement and comment on any difference observed."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i) Four basic control barriers (any four)"},{"ol":["No segregation of duties.","No supervision control.","Poor management control.","Poor authorization and approval.","Poor organization control."]},{"stmt":{"t":"Government College, Kangiwa — Bank Reconciliation Statement (N'000)","rows":[["Balance as per cash float",750],["Add: Unpresented cheques",300],["Add: Receipts in bank statement (e-transfer)",66],["Add: e-Subscription paid directly to the bank",2],["@sub",1118],["Less: Bank charges",-100],["Less: Standing order",-20],["Less: Uncredited cheques",-530],["Less: Receipts in cash book not in bank statement",-130],["@sub",338],["Amount not accounted for/errors in cash book",18],["Balance as per bank statement@tot",356]]}},{"p":"**Comment:** The reconciled balance of N338,000 does **not** agree with the bank statement balance of N356,000 — a difference of N18,000 attributable to errors in the cash book, which the investigation should pursue further."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 5 — Bangura Local Government net assets","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Bangura Local Government has the following (N'000): cash 100,500; accounts receivable 200,200; property, plant and equipment 500,000; accounts payable 150,500; long-term debts 300,000. Calculate the Local Government's total assets, total liabilities and net assets/equity for the period."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Bangura Local Government — total assets, liabilities and net assets (N'000)","rows":[["Property, plant and equipment",500000],["Accounts receivable",200200],["Bank",100500],["Total assets@sub",800700],["Accounts payable",-150500],["Long-term debt",-300000],["Total liabilities@sub",-450500],["Net assets/equity@tot",350200]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 6 — United Schools bank balance","open":true,"q":[{"p":"United Schools, a government parastatal, reports for the year to 31 December 2024 (N'000): net cash flows from operating activities 400,000; from investing activities (150,000); from financing activities 200,000; beginning bank balance 300,000. Calculate the bank balance as at 31 December 2024."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"United Schools — bank balance as at 31 December 2024 (N'000)","rows":[["Beginning bank balance",300],["Net cash flows from operating activities",400],["Net cash flows from investing activities",-150],["Net cash flows from financing activities",200],["End of year bank balance@tot",750]]}}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"The examiner tests two things above all: the six-item list of what a not-for-profit public enterprise's statement of financial position and financial performance must disclose at minimum, and the distinction between a parastatal (its own Enabling Act, not under CAMA, name never ends \"Limited\") and a Government Business Entity (all five GBE characteristics must hold simultaneously). Bank reconciliations and simple net-asset calculations (assets − liabilities) recur as quick Section B marks.","errors":["Treating \"parastatal\" and \"ministry\" as accounted for the same way — ministries share one accounting system; boards/corporations do not, and government regulations for ministries need not apply to agencies.","Forgetting that a corporation's name never carries \"Limited\" or \"Plc\" — it is not governed by CAMA.","Missing any one of the five GBE characteristics (own-name contracting power; financial/operational authority; sells at profit or full cost recovery; not reliant on continuing government funding; controlled by a public sector entity) — all five must hold.","Confusing recurrent and capital subventions, or forgetting that expenditure, like income, splits into revenue and capital in nature."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A parastatal's \"bye-laws\", annual budget, price increases and major foreign agreements must be approved by","o":["The Auditor-General for the Federation","The supervising ministry and government","The Corporate Affairs Commission","The National Assembly alone","The parastatal's own board, with no external approval"],"a":1,"w":"These four items require approval from the supervising ministry and government before they become operative — the supervising ministry then recommends senior appointments to the President or National Assembly for approval.","src":"Chapter 17.1","sec":"17.1"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the five defining characteristics of a Government Business Enterprise (GBE)?","o":["It can contract in its own name","It sells goods/services at a profit or full cost recovery","It is reliant on continuing government funding to remain a going concern","It has financial and operational authority to carry on a business","It is controlled by a public sector entity"],"a":2,"w":"A GBE must specifically NOT be reliant on continuing government funding (other than arm's-length purchases of its outputs) to be a going concern — that is one of its five defining features, stated in the negative.","src":"Chapter 17.3","sec":"17.3"},{"q":"On the face of a not-for-profit public enterprise's Statement of Financial Position, current liabilities must, as a minimum, disclose","o":["Public funds and long-term borrowings","Loans granted, investments and intangible assets","Deposits, short-term loans and advances, unremitted deductions and accrued expenses","Cash, receivables, inventories and prepayments","Infrastructure, plant and equipment"],"a":2,"w":"Deposits, short-term loans/advances, unremitted deductions and accrued expenses are the minimum current-liability disclosures; public funds and long-term borrowings are non-current liabilities.","src":"Chapter 17.4.3(a)","sec":"17.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"State the contents of a corporation's Enabling Act, and explain why a corporation's name never ends in \"Limited\".","marks":8,"a":[{"ol":["The name of the corporation, its functions and objectives.","The principal officers of the board, their functions and mode of appointment.","The supervising ministry.","The location of the head office and branches.","The organogram of the organisation.","The source of funds and the type of accounts to be kept."]},{"p":"A corporation is not governed by the Companies and Allied Matters Act, Cap C20, LFN 2004 — so its name does not end with \"Limited\" or \"Public Limited Company\", unlike a registered commercial company."}],"src":"Chapter 17.1","sec":"17.1"},{"q":"List the minimum line items required on the face of a not-for-profit public enterprise's Statement of Financial Performance.","marks":8,"a":[{"ol":["Revenue from operating activities.","Surplus or deficit from operating activities.","Finance costs.","Share of net surpluses or deficits of associates and joint ventures accounted for using the equity method.","Surplus or deficit from ordinary activities.","Extraordinary items.","Minority interest share of net surplus or deficit.","Net surplus or deficit for the period."]}],"src":"Chapter 17.4.3(b)","sec":"17.4"},{"q":"Kagoma Water Board reports: property, plant and equipment N600,000,000; receivables N90,000,000; cash N40,000,000; payables N75,000,000; long-term borrowings N250,000,000. Calculate total assets, total liabilities and net assets.","marks":6,"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Kagoma Water Board — total assets, liabilities and net assets","rows":[["Property, plant and equipment",600000000],["Receivables",90000000],["Cash",40000000],["Total assets@sub",730000000],["Payables",-75000000],["Long-term borrowings",-250000000],["Total liabilities@sub",-325000000],["Net assets/equity@tot",405000000]]}}],"src":"Chapter 17.8, similar to Section B Q5","sec":"17.8"}]}},{"n":18,"t":"Financial Management Control System in the Public Sector","brief":"The three control structures over government revenue and expenditure — legislative, executive and treasury/departmental — the Vote Book, the Efficiency Unit, and finance/audit committees.","outcomes":["Appreciate what financial control in public sector organisations means","Identify the various executive and legislative controls exerted to ensure efficient use of taxpayers' money","Explain treasury and departmental controls, virement and cash management","Describe the purpose and format of the Vote Book","Explain the roles of the Efficiency Unit, Finance and General-Purpose Committee, and Audit Committee"],"secs":[{"n":"18.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Expenditure control","d":"the strings of coordinated actions taken to ensure all expenditure is \"wholly\", \"necessarily\", \"reasonably\" and \"exclusively\" incurred for the purposes for which it is meant. Control structures broadly ensure that all revenue generated by government is paid into the main government account or the **Consolidated Revenue Fund**."}},{"p":"Revenue-generating departments pay collections into \"**collection bank accounts**\" for onward transfer into government accounts with the Central Bank. Where a department is legislatively allowed to utilise some of its collections, it must follow the necessary procedures to properly account for the money used."},{"key":"Three main control structures exist to ensure government revenue is not misappropriated: **\"Parliamentary\" (Legislative)**, **\"Treasury\"** and **\"Departmental\"** controls."}]},{"n":"18.2","t":"The concept of financial control","b":[{"p":"Public sector financial control has to do with:"},{"ol":["Budgeting for public revenue and expenditure over an appropriate time span;","Recording the revenue collected and expenditure incurred;","Comparing actual results with the targets set; and","Establishing systems for information flow and control."]}]},{"n":"18.3","t":"Legislative control","b":[{"p":"The **National Assembly** is the supreme authority on the nation's finances. Legislative control is both **\"ante-natal\"** (considering and approving the estimates the President submits) and **\"post-natal\"** (reviewing transactions after payment). No public fund may be spent without National Assembly approval — though **Section 82 of the 1999 Constitution** empowers the President to spend from the Consolidated Revenue Fund to run the Federation for **not more than six (6) months**, or until the Appropriation Act takes effect, whichever is earlier."},{"p":"This control matters because Parliament is independent of the Executive (which the ruling party forms). Parliament achieves financial control through:"},{"h4":"(a) Public finance committee"},{"p":"A sub-committee of the National Assembly, responsible for receiving the **Appropriation Bill** (the annual revenue/expenditure estimates and other government proposals) for the National Assembly's consideration."},{"h4":"(b) Appropriation committee"},{"p":"Concerned with passing the Appropriation Bill into the **Appropriation Act**, authorising the estimates after examination by various sub-committees. This committee is, in effect, the **whole National Assembly** when it sits to pass the Appropriation Act."},{"h4":"(c) Examination sub-committees"},{"p":"Sub-committees responsible for examining individual organisations' estimates and monitoring their expenditure. Proper performance of this role is a major control mechanism known as the execution of **oversight functions**."},{"h4":"(d) Public Accounts Committee (PAC)"},{"def":{"t":"Public Accounts Committee (PAC)","d":"a body established by law to study and examine the Auditor-General's reports — especially fraud or embezzlement of public funds — expose waste, corruption or inefficiency, and make recommendations to the National/State Assembly. Empowered to examine the audited accounts of the Federation, public offices, and the Auditor-General's report thereon."}},{"p":"**Role of the PAC:**"},{"ol":["Examine the accounts showing appropriation of sums granted by the Legislature to meet public expenditure, together with the Auditor-General's report;","Ensure ministries spend in accordance with Legislative approval, and bring instances of extravagance, loss, infructuous expenditure and lack of financial integrity to the Assembly's notice;","Send for any person, papers and records, report to the Legislature from time to time, and sit notwithstanding an adjournment of the Assembly;","Examine any accounts/report of statutory corporations and boards after presentation to the House of Assembly, and report thereon;","Enquire into the Auditor-General's report on any pre-payment audit query overruled by a Chief Executive, and report it to the Assembly;","Has, in recent years, extended its scope to policy issues and accounts outside the appropriation accounts."]},{"p":"**Weaknesses of the PAC:**"},{"ul":["Heavy turnover of PAC membership;","Difficulty distinguishing \"policy\" issues from \"administration\" issues;","The unclear status of public servants before the PAC and in the accountability system generally;","Many members do not value the assignment, and lack appropriate background/experience;","Lack of rules surrounding public servants' testimony before the PAC;","Heavy reliance on the Office of the Auditor-General for its agenda and investigative resources, leaving it little capacity or desire for independent investigation;","A general lack of interest among legislators in accountability issues."]},{"h3":"18.3.1 Legislative control in Ghana"},{"p":"**(a) The Audit Service in Ghana** — responsible for examining public accounts to attest to the use of public funds sanctioned by Parliament at the year's start, assisting Parliament (via the Public Accounts Committee) by examining all government organisations' accounts."},{"p":"**(b) The concept of Appropriation in Ghana** — the procedure by which national revenue is made available annually to the Executive to finance its programmes. Every year, public sector undertakings prepare budgets (**supply estimates**) for legislative approval; an **Appropriation Bill** covers the combined estimates, passed into an **Appropriation Act** after examination and debate. Under **Section 25(2) of the FAA**, expenditure estimates are classified under programmes or activities in the Appropriation Bill, forwarded to Parliament to authorise the issue of money from the Consolidated Fund (or another appropriate fund)."},{"p":"Functions of an Appropriation Act:"},{"ol":["Authorise the organisations' estimates;","Give agreement to the **Ambit of the Vote**;","Act as a reference point for disagreements;","Denote, by implication, activities outside authorised areas as **ultra vires**."]},{"p":"Before a budget year starts, a **Provisional Appropriation Act** authorises finances for government organisations to operate (normally for the first three months) until examinations/debates finish — these finances are **Provisional Appropriations** or **Votes on account**, deductible from each organisation's final approved amount. **Article 180 of the 1992 Constitution of Ghana** requires the President to call for such Provisional Appropriations. Approved estimates become **Votes**; the description of purposes for which they may be used is the **Ambit of the Vote**."},{"p":"**(c) Appropriations-in-Aid** — any income a department receives apart from releases from the Consolidated Fund; deducted from the total supply estimates/appropriations the organisation needs, reducing the amount required from the Consolidated Fund."}]},{"n":"18.4","t":"The executive control","b":[{"p":"The **Executive** — the President and cabinet (or, at State level, the Governor and cabinet) — has responsibility for efficient, effective administration of the country, politically and economically. The Constitution creates the **Legislature** and the **Judiciary** as separate arms, for checks and balances; all Presidential measures/policies are subject to Legislative approval within the Constitution."},{"p":"Under **Section 81(1) of the Constitution**, \"The President shall cause to be prepared and laid before each House of the National Assembly, at any time in each financial year, estimates of revenue and expenditure of the Federation for the following financial year.\" The President appoints a **Cabinet Committee on Estimates** to advise on contemplated policy measures, transmitted to the **Budget Department in the Presidency**, leading to budget preparation guidelines and effective supervision of all budget-operation agencies. Any unit whose requirements exceed the issued \"control figures\" must defend the excess request."},{"p":"The Executive controls government expenditure through:"},{"ol":["Determination of monetary and fiscal policies in general;","Compilation and tentative approval of the Nation's budget;","Appointment of the Auditor-General at all tiers of government;","Issuance of budgetary guidelines;","Introduction of \"due process\" guidelines;","Introduction of e-payment, e-receipt and e-banking systems;","Introduction of the **Public Procurement and Fiscal Responsibility Act, 2007**."]}]},{"n":"18.5","t":"Ministry of Finance control","b":[{"p":"Ministries present the Minister of Finance with proposed expenditure budgets that are necessary, reasonable and economical. Beyond the annual activities, the Minister's approval is required for every item of new expenditure, any increase beyond the amount originally authorised, and any transfer of funds between heads/subheads."},{"note":"All warrants authorising the Accountant-General to release funds from the Consolidated Revenue Fund (recurrent expenditure) or the Capital Development Fund (capital expenditure) must be issued and signed by the **Minister of Finance**."},{"h4":"(a) Virement"},{"def":{"t":"Virement","d":"an authorised transfer from one **sub-head** to another **sub-head, within the same head**."}},{"p":"A virement warrant may be issued: **(i)** when circumstances unforeseen when the annual estimates were framed require it; **(ii)** when additional provision is needed under one sub-head while an equivalent saving can be made under another sub-head of the same head."},{"p":"**Conditions for virement:**"},{"ol":["The amount vired from a sub-head under overhead costs, within a financial year, must not exceed **10%** of the approved estimate for each such sub-head;","Virement must not create a new sub-head, or re-introduce items disallowed by the National Assembly during the budget process;","Virement does not apply to Auditor-General queries on improper expenditure already incurred by an MDA;","Approval is by the National Assembly, conveyed by the Minister of Finance to the MDA;","A register must be kept of all virements made;","Details of virements authorised must be forwarded within **one month** of approval to the ministry concerned, with copies to the Accountant-General and Auditor-General for the Federation."]},{"def":{"t":"Appropriation-in-Aid (A-in-A)","d":"income a department receives, apart from its normal release from the Consolidated Revenue Fund — e.g. fees, sales of forms or by-products, rent on official quarters — that it is permitted to apply directly against its own expenditure, instead of remitting the whole amount to the CRF and being released a matching sum back. Also called **appropriations-in-aid**, or simply **grants**, of the department's vote."}},{"h4":"(b) Cash management"},{"def":{"t":"Cash management","d":"a component of good financial governance — ensuring cash resources are spent to maximize citizen benefit, and are available in a predictable, smooth manner for effective, efficient service delivery."}},{"p":"**Requires:**"},{"ul":["Cash flow forecasting and planning;","Integrated cash and debt management;","Active liquidity management; and","Efficient banking and payment arrangements."]},{"p":"**Objectives:**"},{"ol":["Minimize costs within a prudent risk level;","Give a consolidated view of Government cash resources;","Ensure adequate cash is available to pay expenditures when due;","Maximize returns on idle cash;","Manage risk by investing temporary surpluses;","Develop good practice for managing daily cash balances;","Take advantage of modern banking systems and financial instruments;","Minimize borrowing."]},{"h4":"Structure and institutional arrangement for FGN cash management"},{"p":"**Federal Cash Management Policy Committee** — chaired by the Honourable Minister of Finance (HMF), with overall responsibility for FGN cash planning/management. Members:"},{"ol":["Minister of Finance — Chairperson;","Minister of Budget and National Planning;","Chief Economic Adviser to the President;","Permanent Secretary, Federal Ministry of Finance;","Deputy Governor (Operations), CBN;","Special Adviser to the President on National Assembly Matters;","Senior Special Assistant to the President on Millennium Development Goals;","Accountant-General of the Federation;","DG, Budget Office of the Federation;","DG, Bureau of Public Procurement;","DG, Debt Management Office;","A representative of the SGF."]},{"p":"The Committee meets **at least quarterly** to review performance and approve Cash Plans, and authorises publication of quarterly Cash Performance Reports."},{"p":"**Cash Management Technical Committee** — drawn from key MDAs, providing technical support to the FCMPC, coordinating MDA/stakeholder activities and undertaking analytical work ahead of ministerial meetings. Membership:"},{"ol":["Director Fund, OAGF — Chairperson;","Director, Cash Management Department, FMF;","Director Expenditure, BOF;","Director, Portfolio Management, DMO;","Director, Banking and Payment System, CBN;","Director, Revenue Accounting, FIRS;","Directors of Finance and Accounts of key MDAs (State House, FMWH, Power, Agriculture, Transport, etc.)."]},{"p":"**MDA cash management** — each MDA should:"},{"ol":["Have its own cash planning/forecasting mechanism, with the Accounting Officer overall responsible for cash resources;","Consider its Procurement Plan and Annual Cash Plan against budget objectives, needs and government priority;","Review actual budget out-turn and address mitigating factors; and","At a minimum: assign an officer to enter/review departmental needs into **GIFMIS**; consolidate MDA cash needs for the CMU in the OAGF Funds Department; confirm cash payment needs are supported by legal/financial commitments in GIFMIS; and assign a staff counterpart to the CMU."]},{"h4":"Other Ministry of Finance controls"},{"ol":["Issuance of financial authorities;","Compilation and tentative approval of the Nation's budget;","Issuance of budgetary guidelines;","Issuance of financial regulations;","Issuance of financial circulars."]}]},{"n":"18.6","t":"Treasury control — Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation (OAGF)","b":[{"p":"Responsibilities of the Accountant-General:"},{"ol":["Ensure government funds are used judiciously for the purposes for which they are earmarked;","Bear overall responsibility for total government expenditure;","Keep the necessary books of account recording all receipts and expenditure of the various ministries and departments; and","Exercise supervision and checks over the accounting records of **Non-Self-Accounting Units**, through the Treasury Department."]},{"h4":"(a) Treasury Inspection Questionnaires"},{"def":{"t":"Treasury Inspection Questionnaire","d":"a set of standard questions to be answered, and a list of documents to be made available, during a visit to an Accounts Department, used by the Accountant-General's representatives to perform appraisal/control functions."}},{"p":"**Advantages:**"},{"ol":["They help the inspector complete the assignment without omission;","They clearly state the required standard of performance;","They state the requirements to be met; and","They detail the level of assignment required."]},{"h4":"(b) Inspectorate department"},{"p":"Inspectorate Officers visit MDAs to evaluate the internal control system, ensuring the accounting system and books conform to approved regulations. Functions:"},{"ol":["Inspection of accounting records, documents and books of Federal MDAs, checking compliance with rules/regulations and internal controls;","Investigation of fraud and losses of funds/stores in Federal MDAs;","Permanent Board of Survey, and enquiry into cash/stores losses in Federal ministries;","Losses committee, handling cases and write-offs of losses."]},{"h4":"(c) Funds department"},{"ol":["Managing the Federation Account, Consolidated Revenue Fund and other public funds;","Cash backing and cash management;","Loans servicing;","Regular reconciliation of government accounts with banks, etc.;","Fiscal accounts; and","Maintenance of the Federal Government's expenditure pattern."]},{"h4":"(d) Department of audit monitoring"},{"p":"The OAGF dispatches Internal Auditors to Ministries and Self-Accounting departments to appraise the effectiveness of existing internal checks. The department carries out, on the Accountant-General's behalf:"},{"ol":["Control and supervision of the pool of Internal Auditors in Federal MDAs;","Performing and monitoring all Internal Audit duties in Federal ministries and Federal Pay Offices; and","Collating and analysing Internal Audit reports from all ministries and agencies."]}]},{"n":"18.7","t":"Departmental controls","b":[{"p":"Control measures instituted in the spending organisations, to ensure proper, optimal use of funds — accountability is enhanced by routine compliance with institutionalized internal controls, and specific controls exist for each type of financial transaction."},{"h4":"Revenue controls"},{"ol":["All revenue must be timely collected as it falls due;","The actual amount collected must be accounted for and remitted to the Treasury;","All revenues must be paid in gross to the **TSA (Treasury Single Account)**;","Monthly returns of revenue must be rendered to the appropriate institutions."]},{"h4":"Expenditure control variables"},{"p":"**(a)** Heads of departments (Vote Controllers) issue **Activity and Expenditure Initiation Forms** for expenditure activity, and issue departmental accounting manuals — codes of instruction regulating financial administration and accounting practices, containing accounting procedures, chart of accounts, keeping of accounts, and financial-statement formatting/preparation, approved by the Vote Controller, Accountant-General and Auditor-General."},{"p":"**(b)** Spending officers/Heads of Departments, as primary controllers of votes, are obliged (under the Financial Regulations) to keep accounts monitoring and rendering stewardship for approved/allocated funds, and to state commitments made against those funds."},{"p":"**(c)** Vote Controllers, for proper and tight expenditure control, must ensure:"},{"ol":["Every expenditure is a lawful charge against the public fund;","There are funds to meet the expenditure;","Stores/Services Received Advices (SRAs) are checked;","Payments are in order in all respects, conforming to regulations, with prices/rates fair and reasonable relative to current local rates;","Payment vouchers are duly signed with relevant supporting documents."]},{"h4":"Other departmental controls"},{"ul":["Relevant authorities must properly authorise all payments — an officer making an unauthorised payment can be charged for an offence.","All amounts payable must not exceed approved amounts; variations need cogent explanation and subsequent approval.","Payments go only to the right persons entitled to be paid (suppliers, contractors, staff), never to officials themselves or other insiders.","Correct classification is applied, showing the head/sub-head to which expenditure relates.","Payments are made only for the purpose for which funds are meant — no diversion.","All payments must be approved by the designated authority, supported by the appropriate payment voucher, and pre-audited.","Documents accompanying payment must support the validity and purpose of the claim.","Payments must be supported by: evidence of funds availability (warrants/AIEs); Certificate of No Objection, as appropriate; evidence of Tender Board approval; and evidence of Federal Executive Council approval for contracts of N50 million and above.","**Mobilisation** must not exceed **15%** of the contract price.","In National Competitive Bidding (NCB), mobilisation must be supported by an unconditional bank guarantee or insurance bond acceptable to the MDA.","In International Competitive Bidding, mobilisation must be supported by an unconditional bank guarantee from a banking institution acceptable to the MDA.","Where a contract requires a mobilisation fee, a performance guarantee is required of not less than **10%** of the contract value, or an amount equal to the mobilisation fee, whichever is higher.","For NCB and international bidding, the bank guarantee's validity remains until full recovery of the advance payment.","An interim performance certificate is required, approved by the Honourable Minister, before further payment beyond the initial mobilization payment."]}]},{"n":"18.8","t":"Vote book and expenditure control (DVEA)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Departmental Vote Expenditure Allocation (DVEA) book — the Vote Book","d":"a record kept by officers entrusted with total, express control of a Ministry/Department's expenditure vote, cross-referenced against the provision for expenditure in the approved annual estimate. It records all expenditure, including liabilities incurred, against the votes allocated. The **Accounting Officer** controls the Vote Book."}},{"h3":"18.8.1 Purposes of keeping a vote book"},{"ol":["To have absolute control over expenditure;","To prevent reckless spending or abuse of a vote in a Ministry/Department;","To ensure votes for a particular expenditure are not channelled to expenditures not provided for in the approved budget;","To reveal balances available;","To show all commitments and outstanding liabilities."]},{"h3":"18.8.2 Maintenance of the vote book"},{"p":"Maintained by an officer with integrity, experience, competence and dedication, not below the rank of **Senior Finance Assistant**, always supervised by another senior officer so its objectives are not neglected."},{"h3":"18.8.3 Format of a vote book"},{"p":"A DVEA book has 15 columns:"},{"ol":["**Line No.** — numbered serially;","**Date** — the date a voucher is recorded or a liability is incurred;","**Voucher No.** — from the Voucher Register;","**Particulars** — the payee's name;","**Payments** — the gross payment on the voucher;","**Cumulative payment** — total payments to date;","**Balance** — the authorised amount less column 6;","**Liability reference** — an LPO, job order or agreement entered into but not yet executed/supplied;","**Liability incurred** — the actual amount recognised once supply/execution occurs;","**Liability cleared** — posted once the liability in column 9 is cleared and entered in column 5;","**Outstanding liabilities** — cumulative liability to date;","**Remarks** — the indebtedness to the contractor/supplier in column 9;","**Uncommitted balance** — column 7 less all liabilities incurred;","**Line No.** — reference to the numbers of the lines cleared;","**Line No.** — repeating column 1."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Vote Book illustration — Ministry of Education, August 2021 (stationery)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Ministry of Education's DVEA book shows: authorised appropriation for the year N1,000,000. 2/8/2021 — paid N45,000 to Odunuga Bookshop, PV 004. 6/8/2021 — paid N50,000 for stencils to Abiola Bookshop, PV 005. 10/8/2021 — issued LPO 0044 to Lambus Bookshop for photocopying paper, N100,000. 18/8/2021 — settled Lambus on account, PV 006. 23/8/2021 — paid N20,000 for stapling pins/staplers to Orita Bookshop, PV 007. 26/8/2021 — PV 0008 raised for N120,000 for typing sheets, CSS Bookshop. 27/8/2021 — issued LPO 00045 for N300,000 to Olorus Stores Ltd for duplicating paper. 30/8/2021 — settled N300,000 on PV 009 for carbon paper, Dossy Bookshop. 31/8/2021 — settled Olorus Stores Ltd on account, PV 010. Prepare the Vote Book."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Ministry of Education — Vote Book (Stationery), August 2021 (N)","align":"lllllllll","head":["Line","Date","PV No.","Particulars","Payment","Cumulative payment","Balance","Liability incurred/cleared","Outstanding liability"],"rows":[["1","1/8","—","Auth. Appropriation","—","—","1,000,000","—","—"],["2","2/8","004","Odunuga Bookshop","45,000","45,000","955,000","—","—"],["3","6/8","005","Abiola Bookshop","50,000","95,000","905,000","—","—"],["4","10/8","—","LPO 0044 to Lambus Bookshop","—","95,000","905,000","Incurred: 100,000","100,000"],["5","18/8","006","Lambus Bookshop — settlement of LPO","100,000","195,000","805,000","Cleared (ref. line 4)","—"],["6","23/8","007","Orita Bookshop","20,000","215,000","785,000","—","—"],["7","26/8","008","CSS Bookshop","120,000","335,000","665,000","—","—"],["8","27/8","—","LPO 0045 to Olorus Stores Ltd","—","335,000","665,000","Incurred: 300,000","300,000"],["9","30/8","009","Dossy Bookshop","300,000","635,000","365,000","—","—"],["10","31/8","010","Olorus Stores Ltd — settlement","300,000","935,000","65,000","Cleared (ref. line 8)","—"]]}},{"note":"By the end of the month, cumulative payments of N935,000 leave a balance of N65,000 available against the N1,000,000 appropriation, with no outstanding liabilities — both LPOs (lines 4 and 8) were fully settled and cleared (lines 5 and 10)."}]}},{"h3":"18.8.4 Verification of entries in the vote book"},{"p":"Vouchers must be stamped \"Entered in the Vote Book\" by the officer controlling expenditure, and initialled. Entries can also be verified by ensuring: **(a)** totals in columns 6 and 7 agree with the authorised amount; **(b)** totals in columns 11 and 13 agree with the amount in column 7."},{"h3":"18.8.5 Functions of the officer controlling expenditure"},{"ol":["Ensure the Vote Book is properly maintained, entering all payments accurately;","Forward monthly returns to the vote-issuing ministry/department;","Investigate, report and act on any irregularity discovered in a payment voucher;","Ensure all expenditures are justified;","Ensure votes are applied for the purpose for which they are provided;","Ensure votes remain available throughout the fiscal year, by spreading them evenly;","Demand additional vote provision where incurred liabilities are likely to exceed the vote obtained."]}]},{"n":"18.9","t":"Efficiency Unit","b":[{"h3":"Necessity of the Efficiency Unit"},{"ul":["Nigeria's dependence on crude oil for foreign exchange/revenue makes the economy vulnerable to shocks in international oil markets, as seen in the impact of oil-price collapses on external reserves, the Naira exchange rate and revenues.","A disproportionate share of recurrent to capital expenditure has constrained infrastructure development — spending biased against capital projects is inimical to growth.","Public-sector work and procurement processes/practices inherently promote or support wastage and inefficiency.","The need to ensure Government resources are used most efficiently, so citizens get value for money and more funds are available for capital projects, has never been greater."]},{"h3":"Mandate of the Efficiency Unit"},{"ol":["Reviewing government spending patterns using budget implementation reports and other sources;","Working closely with MDAs to review work/procurement processes for specific expenditure lines;","Agreeing changes or process improvements to reduce wastage and make savings;","Continuously publishing achievements (savings) from such changes;","Identifying and migrating best practices in public spending from other countries to Nigeria;","Promoting a change in the culture of MDA public spending, towards prudence and savings;","Collaborating with government institutions whose responsibilities bear on the unit's;","Identifying and implementing Price Guidelines and Benchmarking of MDAs, offering \"Quick Wins\" and large savings;","Ensuring Government revenues are deployed efficiently, for Value for Money and Savings, complementing efforts to diversify revenue sources."]},{"h3":"Composition of the Steering Committee"},{"ol":["The Honourable Minister of Finance — Chairman;","The Head of Civil Service of the Federation — Member;","The Auditor-General for the Federation — Member;","The Accountant-General of the Federation — Member;","Four members from the private sector;","Head of the Efficiency Unit — Secretary."]},{"h3":"Benefits of the Efficiency Unit"},{"ol":["An internal government tool, saving costs, providing learning opportunities and long-term benefits;","Participatory (MDAs are involved) rather than top-down, easing acceptance and institutionalisation of an efficiency culture;","Has the potential to introduce other public-sector reforms for the public good."]}]},{"n":"18.10","t":"Finance and General-Purpose Committee (FGPC)","b":[{"h3":"Composition"},{"ol":["At least three Board members, appointed by the Board on the Chairman's recommendation;","One member also serves as the Committee's Chairman;","Members should aim to serve a term of at least three years;","Vacancies are filled by the Board, which may appoint members for a shorter term;","Members serve until their successors are elected, or earlier resignation/removal;","Any member may be removed/replaced, for any reason, by a majority Board vote."]},{"h3":"Responsibilities"},{"p":"The Committee periodically reviews and reports/recommends to the Board on:"},{"ol":["Management of the organisation's finances;","Control over the organisation's property, revenue and expenditure;","Direction of the deposit and investment of subscriptions, donations and other monetary income;","Appraisal of the financial implications of proposed activities;","Consideration of annual budgets and accounts;","Regular reports on performance against budget for a financial year;","Assessment of the organisation's relationships with other bodies, income/expenditure policies, and audited financial statements with the management letter;","Any other matters submitted by management, including capital expenditures, divestments, acquisitions, joint ventures and other capital transactions; leases, short/long-term borrowings and other financing transactions; interest rate swaps and similar debt-exposure management arrangements; and registration/issuance of debt or equity securities;","Performing other activities the Committee deems appropriate or the Board requests; reviewing and reassessing this Charter's adequacy; and conducting an annual performance evaluation, reporting regularly to the Board."]}]},{"n":"18.11","t":"Audit committee","b":[{"p":"Audit committees are a common private-sector feature, sometimes established for public-sector-owned enterprises, but much less a feature of non-market public sector organisations (ministries, local governments). Where established in the non-market public sector, \"audit committee\" describes committees of varying purpose and reporting line, often limited in scope, and typically lacking the **independent membership** regarded as crucial to a private-sector audit committee's success."},{"h3":"Composition"},{"p":"At least **3 members**, all non-executive directors, a majority of whom are independent. The Chairman is independent and **not** the Chairman of the Board. At least one member has relevant qualifications."},{"h3":"Roles"},{"p":"Set out in written terms of reference, including:"},{"ol":["Monitoring the integrity of the financial statements and formal announcements on financial performance, reviewing significant financial reporting judgements;","Reviewing internal financial controls, and (unless addressed by a separate risk committee or the board itself) reviewing internal control and risk management systems;","Monitoring and reviewing the effectiveness of the internal audit function;","Recommending to the board (for shareholder approval) the appointment, re-appointment and removal of the external auditor, and approving the auditor's remuneration and terms of engagement;","Reviewing and monitoring the external auditor's independence, objectivity and the effectiveness of the audit process;","Developing and implementing policy on engaging the external auditor for non-audit services, and reporting to the board on matters needing action or improvement."]}]},{"n":"18.12","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"Financial control in the public sector involves control of public revenue and expenditure, deciding on government objectives and priorities, and devising proper systems for information flow and control — as well as monitoring the outcome of finance usage. The chapter covered revenue and expenditure control techniques, with emphasis on the Executive, Legislative, Ministry of Finance, Treasury and Departmental controls, plus financial authorisation measures — expenditure authorisation procedures, warrants, financial encumbrance and drawing limits."}]},{"n":"18.13","t":"End of chapter review questions","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–10 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Issuance of financial authorities is one of the control measures adopted by: (i) The Treasury  (ii) The Ministry of Finance  (iii) The Extra-Ministerial Departments. (A) (i) only  (B) (ii) and (iii)  (C) (iii) only  (D) (i) and (ii)  (E) (ii) only","Which of the following organs approve the Nation's Budget? (A) The National Assembly  (B) The Executive  (C) The Minister of Finance  (D) The Accountant-General of the Federation  (E) The Auditor-General for the Federation","The Legislative body that performs a post-mortem function on financial matters is the (A) Audit Alarm Committee  (B) Treasury Board  (C) Public Accounts Committee  (D) The Joint Committee of the Senate and House of Representatives  (E) The Board of Survey","Which ONE of the following arms of government carries out monetary and fiscal policies as a means of control over government expenditure? (A) The Industries and Extra-Ministerial Departments  (B) The Ministry of Finance  (C) The Legislature  (D) The Executive  (E) The Judiciary","The independent organ of government that reviews all contracts awarded which are of significant value is (A) The procurement body  (B) The Board of enquiry  (C) The Board of Survey  (D) The Budget Monitoring and Price Intelligence Unit  (E) The Federal Executive Council","Issuance of budgetary guidelines is one of the ways by which the … arm of government controls expenditure.","What is the mechanism of government for ensuring strict compliance with the openness, competition and cost-accuracy rules and procedures in contract awards?","The officer saddled with the responsibility of performing purely regularity and compliance audit on government accounts and reporting to the National Assembly is …","The authority that confers on government officers controlling expenditure the power to incur expenditure votes is called …","The organ of government that issues guidelines on the preparation of budgets is the …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** A  **3.** C  **4.** B  **5.** D  **6.** Executive  **7.** Due process  **8.** Auditor-General for the Federation  **9.** Warrant  **10.** Ministry of Finance"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–4 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["What are the main roles of the National Assembly in planning and monitoring public expenditure?","List the basic controls exercised over government expenditure to prevent indiscriminate spending of funds.","The Legislative Control deals with various measures Parliament employs to safeguard public funds. Discuss five ways by which Parliament exercises such financial controls.","(a) Explain the purpose of the Efficiency Unit. (b) State the benefits of the Efficiency Unit for Nigeria."]}],"a":[{"h4":"1. Main roles of the National Assembly"},{"ol":["Ratification of the monetary and fiscal policies adopted by the Executive.","Compilation and ultimate approval of the Nation's budget.","Ratification of the appointment of the Auditor-General.","Appointment of the Public Accounts Committee.","Monitoring of the implementation of the Budget.","Guarding against extra-budgetary spending, and ensuring money is spent for which it is meant."]},{"h4":"2. Basic controls exercised over government expenditure"},{"ol":["Financial control (overall).","Legislative control.","Executive control.","Ministry of Finance control.","The Treasury control.","Departmental control."]},{"h4":"3. Five ways Parliament exercises legislative control"},{"ul":["**Public Finance Committee** — a sub-committee of Parliament, receiving the Appropriation Bill for the National Assembly's consideration.","**Appropriation Committee** — passes the Appropriation Bill into the Appropriation Act, in effect the whole House of Parliament sitting to authorise the estimates.","**Examination Sub-Committees** — examine individual organisations' estimates and monitor their expenditure (oversight functions).","**Public Accounts Committee** — receives audited public accounts and special audit reports, examines and debates them, and reports findings/recommendations to the whole house, which acts on that basis.","**The concept of Appropriation** — the procedure making national revenue available annually to the Executive to finance its programmes, including **Appropriations-in-Aid** (income departments receive apart from Consolidated Fund releases, which reduces the amount they need from that Fund)."]},{"h4":"4(a). Purpose of the Efficiency Unit"},{"p":"In broad terms, its mandate is to review the Federal Government's expenditure profile/pattern and work with MDAs to introduce more efficient processes and procedures, ensuring Government revenues are deployed efficiently for Value for Money and Savings — complementing efforts to diversify revenue sources."},{"h4":"4(b). Benefits of the Efficiency Unit for Nigeria"},{"ol":["An internal government tool, saving costs and providing learning opportunities and long-term benefits.","Participatory (MDAs involved) rather than top-down, easing acceptance and institutionalisation of an efficiency culture.","Potential to introduce other public-sector reforms for the public good."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"The examiner loves matching a control measure to the exact arm that exercises it — issuance of financial authorities and budgetary guidelines (Ministry of Finance), monetary/fiscal policy (Executive), post-mortem/PAC review (Legislature), inspection and audit monitoring (Treasury/OAGF). Memorise virement's 10% overhead-cost limit and its restriction to sub-heads within the same head, and the Vote Book's purpose (absolute expenditure control, revealing balances and outstanding liabilities) rather than its full 15-column layout.","errors":["Confusing virement (sub-head to sub-head, same head, max 10% of overhead-cost estimate) with a fresh budgetary allocation — virement can never create a new sub-head or reintroduce a disallowed item.","Treating the Public Accounts Committee as an executive body — it is a Legislative organ performing post-natal (after-the-fact) review of accounts already audited.","Mixing up the Efficiency Unit (reviews MDA spending patterns for savings) with the Audit Committee (monitors financial statement integrity and the external/internal audit process) — both are oversight bodies but with different mandates.","Forgetting that ante-natal control (approving estimates before spending) and post-natal control (reviewing transactions after payment) are both exercised by the Legislature, not split between Legislature and Executive."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"An authorised transfer of funds from one sub-head to another sub-head, but within the same head, is called","o":["A supplementary appropriation","Virement","A warrant","Appropriation-in-Aid","A vote on account"],"a":1,"w":"Virement moves money between sub-heads of the same head, subject to a 10% cap on overhead-cost sub-heads, and cannot create a new sub-head.","src":"Chapter 18.5(b)","sec":"18.5"},{"q":"Income a department receives, apart from its normal release from the Consolidated Revenue Fund, which it is permitted to apply directly against its own expenditure is called","o":["a supplementary appropriation","a vote on account","Appropriation-in-Aid","a virement","a warrant"],"a":2,"w":"Appropriation-in-Aid (A-in-A) — e.g. fees, sales of by-products, rent on official quarters — is applied directly by the department rather than remitted wholly to the CRF.","src":"Chapter 18.5(a)","sec":"18.5"},{"q":"Which committee is empowered to examine the Auditor-General's reports on fraud or embezzlement of public funds and report to the National/State Assembly?","o":["The Finance and General-Purpose Committee","The Audit Committee","The Public Accounts Committee (PAC)","The Efficiency Unit","The Cash Management Technical Committee"],"a":2,"w":"The PAC specifically examines audited accounts and the Auditor-General's report, exposing waste, corruption or inefficiency and recommending action to the Assembly.","src":"Chapter 18.3(d)","sec":"18.3"},{"q":"The officer below whose rank the Vote Book may not be maintained is a","o":["Senior Finance Assistant","Director of Finance and Accounts","Permanent Secretary","Accountant-General","Chief Treasury Officer"],"a":0,"w":"The Vote Book must be maintained by an officer of integrity and experience not below the rank of Senior Finance Assistant, always under a senior officer's supervision.","src":"Chapter 18.8.2","sec":"18.8"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish \"ante-natal\" from \"post-natal\" legislative control, and state the constitutional limit on the President's power to spend without an Appropriation Act.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Ante-natal control** occurs when the legislature considers and approves the estimates the President submits, before any spending. **Post-natal control** is the legislature's review of transactions after payment has been made. Under Section 82 of the 1999 Constitution, the President may spend from the Consolidated Revenue Fund to run the Federation's administration for not more than **six months**, or until the Appropriation Act comes into operation, whichever is earlier."}],"src":"Chapter 18.3","sec":"18.3"},{"q":"State the conditions under which an Accounting Officer may apply for virement.","marks":8,"a":[{"ol":["The amount vired from any sub-head, within a financial year, under overhead costs must not exceed 10% of the approved estimate for each such sub-head.","Virement must not create a new sub-head, or re-introduce items disallowed during the budget process by the National Assembly.","Virement does not apply to Auditor-General queries on improper expenditure already incurred.","Approval is by the National Assembly, conveyed by the Minister of Finance to the MDA.","A register must be kept of all virements made.","Details of authorised virements must be forwarded within one month of approval to the ministry concerned, and to the Accountant-General and Auditor-General for the Federation."]}],"src":"Chapter 18.5(b)","sec":"18.5"},{"q":"List any five purposes served by keeping a Departmental Vote Expenditure Allocation (Vote) Book.","marks":5,"a":[{"ol":["To have absolute control over expenditure.","To prevent reckless spending or abuse of a vote in a Ministry/Department.","To ensure votes for a particular expenditure are not channelled to items not provided for in the approved budget.","To reveal balances available.","To show all commitments and outstanding liabilities."]}],"src":"Chapter 18.8.1","sec":"18.8"}]}},{"n":19,"t":"Interpretation of Public Sector Financial Statements, Using Relevant and Appropriate Techniques","brief":"Interpreting public sector accounts through analytical review/variance analysis and ratio analysis — liquidity, solvency and other ratios, each with a worked illustration.","outcomes":["Interpret public sector accounts using analytical review","Interpret public sector accounts using ratio analysis","Compute and analyse liquidity and solvency ratios","Explain the advantages and limitations of ratio analysis"],"secs":[{"n":"19.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Interpretation of accounts","d":"the art and science of translating the figures shown in financial statements so as to reveal the strengths and weaknesses of an entity and the causes of the weaknesses — undertaken to measure the quality of management."}},{"p":"Interpretation must be undertaken by those conversant with its language; accounts must be drawn up in a form enabling full appreciation of the facts. Any financial statement — management, final, or interim accounts — can be critically analysed."},{"p":"Techniques for interpreting public sector accounts:"},{"ol":["Straightforward criticism or **analytical review**;","Performance reports using **variance analysis**;","**Ratio analysis**."]},{"p":"Accounts are perused by different interested persons — owners (shareholders), creditors, employees, researchers, bank managers — who form conclusions on matters such as the entity's **profitability, solvency, ownership, financial strength, trend of economic endeavours, and gearing and cover**."}]},{"n":"19.2","t":"Analytical review using variance analysis","b":[{"p":"Public and private sector organisations use **variance analysis** to compare financial performance changes month to month, quarter to quarter, or year to year — typically comparing actual results to budget, or budget to forecast. This process, and the resulting variances, help management identify problems, investigate issues, and make rapid changes."},{"key":"Comparing one month to another gives some insight; assembling a trend line across several months' data — **\"horizontal analysis\"** — more easily reveals sudden changes."},{"p":"Comparisons may be undertaken as:"},{"ol":["Previous years' figures against the current year's;","This year's statistics for a period against a similar period last year;","The year's 1st-quarter figures against the 4th quarter of the same year; and","Percentage representation within the year — e.g. salary expenses as a ratio of total expenses."]},{"p":"Other ways of analysing public sector financial statements:"},{"ul":["Share of capital expenditure as % of non-debt expenditure;","Share of capital expenditure as % of total expenditure;","Share of recurrent expenditure as % of total expenditure;","Share of statutory allocation as % of total revenue; and","Share of internally generated revenue as % of total revenue."]},{"p":"Under **IPSAS 24**, entities must present a comparison of the budgeted amount for which they are held publicly accountable against the actual amount, either as a separate additional statement or an additional budget column, presented separately for each level of legislative oversight, covering:"},{"ol":["The original and final budget amounts;","The actual amounts on a comparable basis; and","By note disclosure, an explanation of material differences (variances) between budget and actual, unless already explained elsewhere with a cross-reference given."]},{"table":{"cap":"Extract Statement of Financial Performance showing budget information, per IPSAS 24","align":"lllll","head":["Description","Previous year actual","Current year actual","Final budget for current year","Original budget for current year"],"rows":[["**Revenue:**","","","",""],["Statutory allocation","X","X","X","X"],["Value added tax","X","X","X","X"],["Independent revenue","X","X","X","X"],["Aid and grants","X","X","X","X"],["Capital receipts","X","X","X","X"],["**Total receipts**","XX","XX","XX","XX"],["**Expenditure:**","","","",""],["CRF charges — public debt charges","X","X","X","X"],["Internal loans repayment","X","X","X","X"],["External loans repayment","X","X","X","X"],["Personnel cost","X","X","X","X"],["CRF charges — pensions and gratuities","X","X","X","X"],["Overhead cost","X","X","X","X"],["Finance cost","X","X","X","X"],["**Total expenditure**","XX","XX","XX","XX"],["**Surplus/(Deficit) for the period**","XXX","XXX","XXX","XXX"]]}},{"h3":"19.2.1 Interpretation of variances"},{"ul":["**Positive (favourable) budget variance** — actual revenue exceeds budget, or expenses are lower than budget. Investigated relative to each line item — a positive income variance prompts research into whether higher-than-expected income caused it; expenses below budget prompt research into whether the cause was a one-time/infrequent event, a poor budget assumption, or an ongoing cost cut.","**Negative budget variance** — income falls below budget, or expenses exceed it. Both positive and negative variances can arise from controllable internal events or uncontrollable, often externally-driven, events. Depending on the organisation, variance analysis may focus only on the most relevant line items."]}]},{"n":"19.3","t":"Ratio analysis","b":[{"def":{"t":"Financial ratio analysis","d":"the process of calculating financial ratios — mathematical indicators from comparing key financial information in a public sector entity's statements — and analysing them to find the reasons behind the entity's current financial position and recent performance, and to develop expectations about its future outlook."}},{"p":"Ratio analysis expresses one figure as a ratio/percentage of another, to bring out strengths or weaknesses that the raw figures in a government department, ministry or corporation's statements would not sufficiently reveal for well-informed judgment."},{"key":"The three tiers of government operated the **cash basis** of accounting prior to **1 January 2016**; from that date, the three tiers, parastatals and government business entities operate the **accrual basis**. Federal/state/local government councils mostly use **liquidity ratios**, to measure the ease of meeting obligations due within the year."},{"h3":"19.3.1 Classification of ratios"},{"p":"Public sector financial ratios are broadly classified into **liquidity, solvency, profitability and efficiency (activity/asset utilisation)** ratios. Given the peculiarity of public sector activities, this chapter considers only the liquidity and solvency ratios, plus a few \"other\" ratios."},{"h3":"19.3.2 Liquidity ratios"},{"def":{"t":"Liquidity ratios","d":"assess an entity's ability to convert its assets to cash and pay off obligations without significant difficulty (delay or loss of value) — particularly useful for suppliers, employees and banks."}},{"h4":"(a) Quick ratio (acid-test ratio)"},{"p":"Measures the naira of liquid current assets available per naira of current liabilities. **Liquid current assets** — cash, marketable securities and receivables — can be quickly converted to cash without significant loss of value. A **stricter** liquidity measure than the current ratio, since it excludes illiquid current assets (e.g. inventory); most useful where illiquid current assets are a high proportion of total current assets, though less conservative than the cash ratio."},{"tex":"Quick\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Cash + Marketable\\ securities + Receivables}{Current\\ liabilities}","nt":"Cash = cash in hand and at bank. Marketable securities are quickly convertible with negligible value loss, e.g. government treasury bills, listed shares."},{"h4":"(b) Current ratio"},{"p":"The most fundamental liquidity ratio, measuring a business's ability to repay current liabilities with current assets. **Current assets** convert to cash within the normal operating cycle or one year (cash/equivalents, marketable securities, short-term investments, receivables, the short-term portion of notes receivable, inventories, short-term prepayments). **Current liabilities** are settled within the normal operating cycle or the next 12 months (payables, salaries/wages payable, current tax payable, sales tax payable, accrued expenses, etc.)."},{"tex":"Current\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Current\\ assets}{Current\\ liabilities}","nt":"A ratio of 1 or more means current assets exceed current liabilities and the entity should face no liquidity problem; below 1 may indicate liquidity problems. Higher is generally better, but should be judged against the relevant industry — retail tends to run high current ratios, service providers (e.g. accounting firms) tend to run low ones since their business model needs little current assets."},{"warn":"An **abnormally high** current ratio may indicate idle or under-utilised resources, since most current assets earn no or very low return compared with long-term projects — hurting profitability and efficiency, not helping it."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — current ratio, two electricity distribution companies","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Calculate and analyse current ratios for Okokomaiko Electricity Distribution Company (OEDC) and Erile Electricity Distribution Company (EEDC): OEDC — current assets N133,000,000 / 130,000,000 / 131,000,000 and current liabilities N130,000,000 / 110,000,000 / 120,500,000, for 2021 / 2020 / 2019 respectively. EEDC — current assets N100,000,000 / 110,000,000 / 96,000,000 and current liabilities N90,000,000 / 88,000,000 / 86,000,000, for the same three years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Entity","2021","2020","2019"],"rows":[["OEDC","133,000/130,000 = 1.02 : 1.00","130,000/110,000 = 1.18 : 1.00","131,000/120,500 = 1.09 : 1.00"],["EEDC","100,000/90,000 = 1.11 : 1.00","110,000/88,000 = 1.25 : 1.00","96,000/86,000 = 1.17 : 1.00"]]}},{"p":"EEDC has a higher current ratio than OEDC in each of the three years, meaning EEDC is better placed to meet short-term liabilities with short-term assets; OEDC's ratios have nonetheless stayed above 1 throughout, which is not bad. Both companies' liquidity improved and then this trend reversed across the three years shown."}]}},{"h3":"19.3.3 Solvency ratios"},{"def":{"t":"Solvency ratios","d":"assess a public sector entity's long-term financial viability — its ability to pay off long-term obligations (bank loans, bonds payable, etc.). Critical for banks, employees, owners, bondholders, institutional investors and government."}},{"h4":"(a) Debt ratio (debt-to-assets ratio)"},{"p":"Measures debt as a percentage of total assets, showing whether the entity's debt load is sustainable. If too high, it may indicate difficulty paying off debts and continuing operations."},{"tex":"Debt\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Total\\ debt}{Total\\ assets}","nt":"Total debt = long-term + short-term debt — narrower than total liabilities, since it excludes non-debt liabilities such as accounts payable and salaries payable (sometimes total liabilities is used instead). A very low debt ratio is safe but may indicate under-utilisation of a major finance source, restricting growth; a very high debt ratio signals high risk to debt-holders and equity investors, and may hamper obtaining finance on good terms."},{"h4":"(b) Debt-to-equity ratio"},{"p":"A leverage ratio measuring the degree to which assets are financed by debt versus net assets/equity (shareholders' equity), both taken from the statement of financial position."},{"tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}equity\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Total\\ liabilities}{Net\\ assets/equity}","nt":"A variation uses only interest-bearing long-term liabilities in the numerator. Lower values are favourable (less risk); a ratio of 1.00 means debt and equity finance the assets equally; above 1.00 means more assets are financed by debt than by equity, and vice versa."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — debt-to-equity ratio","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Calculate the debt-to-equity ratio of an entity with total liabilities of N120,000,000 and shareholders' equity of N150,000,000."}],"a":[{"tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}equity\\ ratio = \\dfrac{120{,}000{,}000}{150{,}000{,}000} = 0.80"}]}},{"h4":"(c) Debt-to-capital ratio"},{"p":"A refinement of the debt-to-assets ratio, measuring how much of the capital employed (resources on which the entity pays a cost) is debt. **Interest-bearing debt** includes bonds payable, bank loans and notes payable; **non-interest-bearing debt** includes trade payables and accrued expenses. Higher debt within capital employed means higher insolvency risk."},{"tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}capital\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Interest\\text{-}bearing\\ debt}{Interest\\text{-}bearing\\ debt + Equity}"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — debt-to-capital and debt-to-assets ratios, Eko State Bulk Purchase Company","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Eko State Bulk Purchase Company, FY2020 (N'000): short-term debt 3,120; accounts payable 3,500; accrued expenses 2,900; accrued advertising 1,100; deferred income 1,950; other accrued liabilities 3,600; long-term debt 13,200; long-term deferred tax liabilities 3,400; other long-term liabilities 3,700; total liabilities 36,470; total shareholders' equity 60,200; total assets 96,670. Calculate the debt-to-capital and debt-to-assets ratios."}],"a":[{"p":"Interest-bearing debt = short-term debt 3,120 + long-term debt 13,200 = **N16,320,000**."},{"tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}capital\\ ratio = \\dfrac{16{,}320}{16{,}320 + 60{,}200} = 0.213"},{"tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}assets\\ ratio = \\dfrac{16{,}320}{96{,}670} = 0.169"}]}},{"h3":"19.3.4 Other ratios"},{"h4":"(a) Receivables' payment period"},{"p":"Measures the average length of time it takes a corporation's debtors to pay — an estimated average, best calculated regularly to avoid distortions. The earlier debtors are encouraged to pay, the better the board/corporation's cash position."},{"tex":"Receivables\\ payment\\ period = \\dfrac{Receivables\\ for\\ goods\\ or\\ services}{Credit\\ sales} \\times 365\\ days"},{"h4":"(b) Payables' payment period"},{"p":"Measures the average length of time the parastatal takes to pay its creditors."},{"tex":"Payables\\ payment\\ period = \\dfrac{Trade\\ or\\ expense\\ creditors}{Credit\\ purchases} \\times 365\\ days"},{"h4":"(c) Inventory turnover period"},{"p":"Indicates the average number of days stock items are held for sale or in store. The shorter the period, the healthier the situation in making the best use of funds."},{"tex":"Inventory\\ turnover\\ period = \\dfrac{Cost\\ of\\ goods\\ sold}{(Opening\\ inventory + Closing\\ inventory)/2}","nt":"Average stock is the mean of the opening and closing stock figures."},{"h3":"19.3.5 Advantages and limitations of ratio analysis"},{"p":"**Advantages:**"},{"ol":["It simplifies the financial statements;","It helps compare entities of different size with each other;","It helps in trend analysis — comparing a single entity's performance over a period; and","It highlights important information in simple form quickly, letting a user judge an entity from a few numbers rather than the whole set of statements."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages:**"},{"ol":["Different entities operate in different geographical areas with different regulation, market structure, etc., so comparisons across areas can be misleading;","Financial accounting information is affected by estimates and assumptions, and accounting standards allow different policies, impairing comparability;","Ratio analysis explains relationships between **past** information, while users are more concerned about current and future information."]}]},{"n":"19.4","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"Interpretation of financial statements reveals the financial strengths and weaknesses of a business and their causes. The cash flow statement gives the details of all cash received and paid by the organisation during the year, making it a good reporting statement for cash management."}]},{"n":"19.5","t":"End of chapter review questions","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–12 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Opening inventory, purchases, carriage inwards and closing inventory are accounting information which can be used to compute (A) Working capital ratio  (B) Acid test ratio  (C) Trading account ratio  (D) Cost of sales ratio  (E) Stock turnover ratio","The ratio used to determine how much a government business enterprise is financed by borrowed funds is … (A) Working capital ratio  (B) Acid test ratio  (C) Efficiency ratio  (D) Gearing ratio  (E) Profitability ratio","One of the following is NOT in the class of liquidity ratio: (A) Quick asset ratio  (B) Working capital ratio  (C) Acid test ratio  (D) Cash ratio  (E) Gross profit margin","Which of the following is NOT among the class of profitability ratio? (A) Net profit margin  (B) Earnings per share  (C) Return on assets  (D) Debt to equity ratio  (E) Return on equity","The formula for calculating Days' Sale in Inventory is … (A) Sales/Total assets  (B) Current assets : Current liabilities  (C) 365 days/Inventory turnover  (D) Sales/Net working capital  (E) Net profit/Net sales","Ratio analysis explains relationships between … while users are more concerned about current and future information.","Public or private sector organisations use … to compare financial performance changes from one month to the next, or perhaps from one quarter to another, or year to year.","According to …, entities are required to present a comparison of the budgeted amount for which they are held publicly accountable and the actual amount, either as a separate additional financial statement or as an additional budget column.","Budget variance is … when actual revenue results are higher than budget expectations, or expenses are lower than budget.","… assess an entity's ability to convert its assets to cash and pay off its obligations without significant difficulty.","… is a solvency ratio that measures the proportion of interest-bearing debt to the sum of interest-bearing debt and shareholders' equity.","… assess the long-term financial viability of a public sector entity, i.e. its ability to pay off its long-term obligations such as bank loans, bonds payable, etc."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** D  **3.** E  **4.** D  **5.** C  **6.** Past information  **7.** Variance analysis  **8.** IPSAS 24  **9.** Positive (favourable)  **10.** Liquidity ratios  **11.** Debt-to-capital ratio  **12.** Solvency ratios"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — objectives and computation of five key ratios","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Define ratio analysis. (b) Explain the objective of the following ratios and state their mode of computation: (i) Current ratio; (ii) Quick (acid test) ratio; (iii) Debtors' payment period; (iv) Creditors' payment period; (v) Stock turnover period."}],"a":[{"p":"(a) **Ratio analysis** is the expression of one figure as a ratio of another, to determine the weakness or strength in an entity's financial affairs at a particular point in time."},{"ul":["**(i) Current ratio** — determines the extent to which an entity can discharge its current liabilities without affecting its current assets; a ratio in excess of 2 is generally required for an organisation.","**(ii) Quick (acid test) ratio** — designed to reveal the solid liquidity position of an entity.","**(iii) Receivables (debtors') payment period** — measures the average time it takes an entity's debtors to settle their debts; an estimated average.","**(iv) Payables (creditors') payment period** — measures the average time it takes an entity to pay its creditors; also an estimated average.","**(v) Stock turnover period** — indicates the average number of days that items of stock are held for sale or in the store."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — advantages and limitations of ratio analysis","open":true,"q":[{"p":"State the advantages and limitations of ratio analysis."}],"a":[{"h4":"Advantages"},{"ol":["It simplifies the financial statements.","It helps in comparing entities of different size with each other.","It helps in trend analysis — comparing a single entity's performance over a period.","It highlights important information in simple form quickly, so a user can judge an entity from a few numbers instead of reading the whole financial statements."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages"},{"ol":["Different entities operate in different geographical areas with different environmental conditions (regulation, market structure), so cross-area comparison may mislead.","Financial accounting information is affected by estimates/assumptions, and accounting standards permit different policies, impairing comparability.","Ratio analysis explains relationships between past information, while users are more concerned about current and future information."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — Danduala Local Government ratios","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Danduala Local Government, year 2021 (N'000): Recurrent Revenue — internal revenue: estimated 60,000,000, actual 54,000,000; allocation from Federation Account: estimated 67,500,000, actual 88,455,000; allocation from State Account: estimated 48,720,000, actual 18,949,320; others: estimated 7,500,000, actual 12,656,197.5 (totals: estimated 183,720,000, actual 174,060,517.5). Recurrent Expenditure — personnel costs: estimated 35,615,253, actual 52,115,400; overhead costs: estimated 57,989,898, actual 56,673,480; stabilization utilization: estimated 15,000,000, actual 15,870,000; outstanding liabilities: estimated 1,801,170, actual 3,626,370 (totals: estimated 110,406,321, actual 128,285,250). Capital Receipts — internal source: estimated 15,000,000, actual 1,800,000; external loans: estimated 8,250,000, actual 9,000,000; grants: estimated 3,000,000, actual 3,000,000 (totals: estimated 26,250,000, actual 13,800,000). Capital Expenditure — capital expenditure: estimated 58,570,743, actual 60,070,743; outstanding liabilities: estimated 54,000,000, actual 5,391,360. Calculate: (i) estimated internal revenue to estimated allocations from the Federation and State; (ii) actual outstanding recurrent liabilities to actual capital liabilities; (iii) actual personnel cost to actual internal revenue; (iv) estimated recurrent expenditure to actual recurrent expenditure; (v) actual internal revenue to estimated internal revenue; (vi) actual recurrent surplus to actual capital expenditure."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Ratio","Computation","Result"],"rows":[["(i) Est. internal revenue : Est. Federation + State allocation","60,000,000 / (67,500,000 + 48,720,000) = 60,000,000 / 116,220,000","0.52 : 1"],["(ii) Actual outstanding recurrent liabilities : actual capital liabilities","3,626,370 / 5,391,360","0.67 : 1"],["(iii) Actual personnel cost : actual internal revenue","52,115,400 / 54,000,000","0.97 : 1"],["(iv) Estimated recurrent expenditure : actual recurrent expenditure","110,406,321 / 128,285,250","0.86 : 1"],["(v) Actual internal revenue : estimated internal revenue","54,000,000 / 60,000,000","0.90 : 1"],["(vi) Actual recurrent surplus : actual capital expenditure","45,775,267.50 / 65,462,103.00","0.70 : 1"]]}},{"note":"As printed in the study text; the actual recurrent surplus (45,775,267.50) and actual capital expenditure (65,462,103.00) used in ratio (vi) are not both directly given in the question data above, and appear to be derived figures in the original solution — reproduced here exactly as printed."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — variance interpretation and going-concern factors","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) In the interpretation of variances, explain: (i) Positive budget variance; (ii) Negative budget variance. (b) State any five factors that show that a company's going concern is threatened."}],"a":[{"p":"(a)(i) **Positive (favourable) budget variance** — actual revenue exceeds budget expectations, or expenses are lower than budget; analysed relative to the line item, e.g. researching whether higher-than-expected income caused a positive income variance, or whether below-budget expenses were a one-time event, a poor budget assumption, or a genuine ongoing cost saving."},{"p":"(a)(ii) **Negative budget variance** — income falls below budget, or expenses exceed budget; may result from controllable internal events or uncontrollable, often externally driven, events."},{"h4":"(b) Factors threatening going concern (any five)"},{"ol":["Dependency on creditors to finance the company.","Persistent losses and the resultant loss of capital.","Dependence on few product lines.","Declining sales and stocks.","Loss of key management staff.","Low returns on huge investments.","Using short-term funds to finance long-term projects.","High labour turnover."]}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Quick (acid-test) ratio","tex":"Quick\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Cash + Marketable\\ securities + Receivables}{Current\\ liabilities}","nt":"A stricter liquidity measure than the current ratio."},{"lb":"Current ratio","tex":"Current\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Current\\ assets}{Current\\ liabilities}","nt":"1 or more indicates no obvious liquidity problem."},{"lb":"Debt ratio (debt-to-assets)","tex":"Debt\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Total\\ debt}{Total\\ assets}","nt":"Total debt = long-term + short-term debt (interest-bearing)."},{"lb":"Debt-to-equity ratio","tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}equity\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Total\\ liabilities}{Net\\ assets/equity}","nt":"1.00 means debt and equity finance the assets equally."},{"lb":"Debt-to-capital ratio","tex":"Debt\\text{-}to\\text{-}capital\\ ratio = \\dfrac{Interest\\text{-}bearing\\ debt}{Interest\\text{-}bearing\\ debt + Equity}","nt":""},{"lb":"Receivables' payment period","tex":"Receivables\\ payment\\ period = \\dfrac{Receivables}{Credit\\ sales} \\times 365","nt":""},{"lb":"Payables' payment period","tex":"Payables\\ payment\\ period = \\dfrac{Trade/expense\\ creditors}{Credit\\ purchases} \\times 365","nt":""},{"lb":"Inventory turnover period","tex":"Inventory\\ turnover\\ period = \\dfrac{Cost\\ of\\ goods\\ sold}{(Opening + Closing\\ inventory)/2}","nt":""}],"focus":"The examiner tests whether you can tell liquidity ratios (quick, current) apart from solvency ratios (debt, debt-to-equity, debt-to-capital) — both groups measure \"can this entity pay?\" but over different time horizons (short-term obligations vs long-term viability). Learn the \"365 days ×\" formula shape for all three period ratios (receivables, payables, inventory turnover) as one family. A very high current ratio is a trap answer — know it signals idle resources, not strength.","errors":["Treating a higher current ratio as always better — an abnormally high ratio signals idle or under-utilised current assets, hurting profitability.","Using total liabilities instead of total (interest-bearing) debt in the debt ratio — the debt ratio specifically excludes non-debt liabilities like accounts payable.","Confusing the quick ratio (excludes inventory and prepayments) with the current ratio (includes them) — the quick ratio is always the stricter, lower figure unless all current assets are liquid.","Forgetting that IPSAS 24's budget-vs-actual comparison must be shown at the level of each legislative oversight, with original AND final budget columns, not just one budget figure."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A ratio that excludes inventory from current assets before comparing them with current liabilities, giving a stricter test of liquidity, is the","o":["Current ratio","Quick (acid-test) ratio","Debt ratio","Debt-to-equity ratio","Inventory turnover ratio"],"a":1,"w":"The quick ratio uses only cash, marketable securities and receivables — the most liquid current assets — making it stricter than the current ratio, which includes inventory and prepayments.","src":"Chapter 19.3.2(a)","sec":"19.3"},{"q":"Under IPSAS 24, a comparison of budgeted and actual amounts must be presented separately for","o":["Each individual employee","Each level of legislative oversight","Each supplier","Each bank account","Each fixed asset category"],"a":1,"w":"IPSAS 24 requires the comparison — original budget, final budget, and actual on a comparable basis, with variance explanations — to be presented separately for each level of legislative oversight.","src":"Chapter 19.2","sec":"19.2"},{"q":"An abnormally high current ratio most likely indicates","o":["Excellent short-term liquidity with no downside","Idle or under-utilised resources, potentially hurting profitability","A high risk of insolvency","Understated current liabilities","A data entry error"],"a":1,"w":"Since most current assets earn little or no return compared with long-term projects, an abnormally high current ratio signals idle/under-utilised resources rather than pure strength.","src":"Chapter 19.3.2(b)","sec":"19.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish liquidity ratios from solvency ratios, giving one example of each and stating what each measures.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Liquidity ratios** (e.g. the current ratio) assess an entity's ability to convert assets to cash and meet its short-term obligations without significant delay or loss of value. **Solvency ratios** (e.g. the debt ratio) assess an entity's long-term financial viability — its ability to meet long-term obligations such as bank loans and bonds payable."}],"src":"Chapter 19.3.2, 19.3.3","sec":"19.3"},{"q":"A Local Government reports current assets of N45,000,000 and current liabilities of N30,000,000, of which N12,000,000 is inventory. Calculate the current ratio and the quick ratio, and comment on the difference.","marks":8,"a":[{"tex":"Current\\ ratio = \\dfrac{45{,}000{,}000}{30{,}000{,}000} = 1.5 : 1"},{"tex":"Quick\\ ratio = \\dfrac{45{,}000{,}000 - 12{,}000{,}000}{30{,}000{,}000} = \\dfrac{33{,}000{,}000}{30{,}000{,}000} = 1.1 : 1"},{"p":"The quick ratio (1.1:1) is lower than the current ratio (1.5:1) because it excludes the N12,000,000 of inventory, which is less readily convertible to cash — both ratios remain above 1, so the Local Government should not face short-term liquidity difficulty even without selling inventory."}],"src":"Chapter 19.3.2, worked example","sec":"19.3"},{"q":"State the advantages and limitations of ratio analysis (any three of each).","marks":6,"a":[{"h4":"Advantages (any three)"},{"ul":["Simplifies the financial statements.","Helps compare entities of different size.","Helps in trend analysis over time.","Highlights important information quickly, in simple form."]},{"h4":"Limitations (any three)"},{"ul":["Cross-area comparisons can mislead, given different regulation/market structures.","Different accounting policies impair comparability.","Ratios explain past relationships, not current/future ones."]}],"src":"Chapter 19.3.5","sec":"19.3"}]}},{"n":20,"t":"Investment/Project Appraisal in the Public Sector","brief":"Why public sector investment needs formal appraisal, and the three examinable methods — Accounting Rate of Return, Payback Period and Net Present Value — each fully worked.","outcomes":["State the reasons for investment/project appraisal in the public sector","Compute and apply the Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)","Compute and apply the Payback Period (PBP)","Compute and apply the Net Present Value (NPV)","Advise on project selection using each method"],"secs":[{"n":"20.1","t":"Rationale for investment appraisal","b":[{"p":"Where investment resources are scarce, as in most developing countries, rational allocation of capital is of the greatest importance. For a developing country to be assured that resources used on a given project have no better alternative use, in terms of the country's objectives, practical project appraisal is required. National governments guide investment patterns either by direct public sector investment, or by controlling private investment."},{"p":"To provide rational guidance, government must formulate and evaluate investment projects so that alternatives can be compared in terms of their contribution to national objectives. Evaluation is needed because a choice must be made among alternatives, and a clearly stated objective is essential — both as a basis for rational choice, and because without one there is no criterion to test whether a proposed action is desirable."},{"key":"Public sector project appraisal is **not** a set of techniques applied mechanically — it is an approach that must be interpreted analytically."}]},{"n":"20.2","t":"Methods of investment appraisal","b":[{"def":{"t":"Investment appraisal","d":"a technique aimed at finding the least possible cost of an investment and the maximum economic benefits that may accrue from committing resources to it."}},{"p":"Appraisal techniques broadly classify into **discounted cash flow (DCF)** and **non-discounted cash flow (NDCF)** methods. This syllabus covers only three:"},{"ol":["**Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)**;","**Pay-Back Period (PBP)**;","**Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) using Net Present Value (NPV)**."]}]},{"n":"20.3","t":"Accounting rate of return (ARR)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)","d":"the return on initial outlay, or return on average capital."}},{"tex":"ARR = \\dfrac{Average\\ Annual\\ Accounting\\ Profit}{Average\\ Investment}","nt":"Average investment = (Initial investment + Residual value) / 2. Profit is \"accounting profit\" — income less all necessary expenses incurred in earning it, including depreciation."},{"key":"**Decision rule:** pick the option with the **highest** rate of return."},{"p":"**Advantages of ARR:**"},{"ol":["Considers the profits of a project throughout its useful life;","Simple to calculate and understand;","Facilitates expenditure follow-up, due to more readily available data on accounting records."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages of ARR:**"},{"ol":["Does not take into account the time value of money;","Ignores that profits from different projects may accrue at an uneven rate;","Fails to cater for risks and uncertainties."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 1 — ARR, Agbede Local Government Council","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Agbede Local Government Council supplies the following data on three projects:"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Year","X1 (N)","X2 (N)","X3 (N)"],"rows":[["1","1,000","500","5,000"],["2","2,000","1,500","4,000"],["3","4,000","2,000","500"],["4","5,000","2,500","1,000"],["Initial investment","15,000","10,000","15,000"],["Residual value","1,000","1,000","2,000"]]}},{"p":"Using the Accounting Rate of Return, in which project should Agbede Local Government Council invest?"}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Computation of ARR","align":"llll","head":["","X1","X2","X3"],"rows":[["Total accounting profit (4 years)","N12,000","N6,500","N10,500"],["Average annual accounting profit (÷4)","N3,000","N1,625","N2,625"],["Initial investment + residual value","N16,000","N11,000","N17,000"],["Average investment (÷2)","N8,000","N5,500","N8,500"],["ARR = Avg. profit / Avg. investment","0.38","0.30","0.31"],["ARR (%)","**38%**","30%","31%"]]}},{"p":"**Agbede Local Government Council should invest in Project X1**, which has the highest accounting rate of return of 38%."}]}}]},{"n":"20.4","t":"Pay-back period (PBP) method","b":[{"def":{"t":"Pay-back period","d":"the time an investment takes to recoup the amount of money invested in a project, focusing only on **cash flow**, not profit."}},{"key":"The **shorter** the payback period, the more preferable the project. A project is undertaken only if its payback period is shorter than, or at worst equal to, the maximum set standard. For a single project, payback is compared with a set standard; for **mutually exclusive** projects, they are ranked and the one with the **shortest** payback period is selected."},{"p":"**Advantages of PBP:**"},{"ol":["A useful measure of liquidity, since it selects projects offering the hope of immediate cash recoupment;","May be used as a safeguard against risk, particularly if risk increases as payback lengthens;","Simple to calculate and understand;","Popular for public project evaluation, where liquidity predominates over profitability;","Serves as a useful screen for evaluating all projects; and","Uses cash flows rather than accounting profits to appraise."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages of PBP:**"},{"ol":["Does not consider the time value of money;","Ignores variations in the timing of cash inflows within the payback period;","Cash inflows outside the payback period are ignored; and","Does not take risks and uncertainties into consideration."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 2 — payback period, Omidan Local Government Council","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Omidan Local Government Council is considering three projects:"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","B1 (N)","B2 (N)","B3 (N)"],"rows":[["Initial investment","100,000","150,000","180,000"],["Year 1 inflow","70,000","10,000","50,000"],["Year 2 inflow","30,000","20,000","60,000"],["Year 3 inflow","20,000","10,000","80,000"],["Year 4 inflow","10,000","40,000","90,000"]]}},{"p":"Using the payback period method, advise which project should be selected."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Cumulative recovery of initial investment","align":"llll","head":["","B1","B2","B3"],"rows":[["Initial investment","(100,000)","(150,000)","(180,000)"],["After Year 1 inflow","(30,000)","(140,000)","(130,000)"],["After Year 2 inflow","NIL — recovered","(120,000)","(70,000)"],["After Year 3 inflow","—","(110,000)","10,000 — recovered"],["After Year 4 inflow","—","(70,000) unrelieved","—"]]}},{"p":"**Project B1** paid off its initial capital in **Year 2**. **Project B2** could not generate enough cash inflows across all four years, leaving N70,000 unrelieved — it should be **dropped completely**. **Project B3** paid off in approximately **2.88 years**. B1 is therefore more profitable than B3."}]}}]},{"n":"20.5","t":"Net present value (NPV)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Net Present Value (NPV)","d":"the present-value equivalent of the cash inflows and outflows from a project, discounted at a particular (given) cost of capital — the firm/corporation's **opportunity cost of capital**, equal to its required rate of return."}},{"key":"**Decision rule:** a project is acceptable if it has a **positive** NPV, and rejected if it has a **negative** NPV — in total, the present value of cash inflows should exceed that of cash outflows. For **mutually exclusive** projects, rank them and select the one with the **highest** NPV."},{"tex":"NPV = \\dfrac{C_1}{(1+K)} + \\dfrac{C_2}{(1+K)^2} + \\dfrac{C_3}{(1+K)^3} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{C_n}{(1+K)^n} - C_0","nt":"C = cash inflows in each year; K = the opportunity cost of capital (discount rate); C₀ = the initial cost of the investment; n = the project's expected life."},{"p":"**Advantages of NPV:**"},{"ol":["Timing of cash flows is considered; and","Cash flows over the entire life of the project are taken into consideration."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages of NPV:**"},{"ol":["Management is obliged to determine the appropriate cost of capital to use;","Not suitable where a capital rationing situation exists; and","Assumes cash inflows will occur as predicted, which may not necessarily be so."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3 — NPV, Yabus Local Government Council","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Yabus Local Government Council is choosing among Projects T, M and X, cost of capital 10%:"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Project T (N)","Project M (N)","Project X (N)"],"rows":[["Initial investment","100,000","180,000","150,000"],["Year 1","40,000","10,000","50,000"],["Year 2","50,000","30,000","10,000"],["Year 3","60,000","50,000","90,000"],["Year 4","70,000","70,000","10,000"],["Year 5","80,000","120,000","40,000"]]}},{"p":"Discount factors at 10%: Year 1 — 0.9090; Year 2 — 0.8264; Year 3 — 0.7513; Year 4 — 0.6830; Year 5 — 0.6209. Advise which project is most viable."}],"a":[{"table":{"cap":"Project T — NPV computation","align":"llll","head":["Year","Cash flow (N)","DF (10%)","DCF (N)"],"rows":[["0","(100,000)","1.0000","(100,000)"],["1","40,000","0.9090","36,360"],["2","50,000","0.8264","41,320"],["3","60,000","0.7513","45,078"],["4","70,000","0.6830","47,810"],["5","80,000","0.6209","49,672"],["**NPV**","","","**120,240**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"Project M — NPV computation","align":"llll","head":["Year","Cash flow (N)","DF (10%)","DCF (N)"],"rows":[["0","(180,000)","1.0000","(180,000)"],["1","10,000","0.9090","9,090"],["2","30,000","0.8264","24,792"],["3","50,000","0.7513","37,565"],["4","70,000","0.6830","47,810"],["5","120,000","0.6209","74,508"],["**NPV**","","","**13,765**"]]}},{"table":{"cap":"Project X — NPV computation","align":"llll","head":["Year","Cash flow (N)","DF (10%)","DCF (N)"],"rows":[["0","(150,000)","1.0000","(150,000)"],["1","50,000","0.9090","45,450"],["2","10,000","0.8264","8,264"],["3","90,000","0.7513","67,617"],["4","10,000","0.6830","6,830"],["5","40,000","0.6209","24,836"],["**NPV**","","","**2,997**"]]}},{"p":"**Yabus Local Government Council is advised to invest in Project T**, which has the highest NPV of N120,240. Projects M and X have NPVs of N13,765 and N2,997 respectively."}]}}]},{"n":"20.6","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"It is essential for an organisation, public or private, to make investment decisions where it faces limited resources with alternative uses. Different techniques of investment appraisal — ARR, PBP and NPV — can be employed to arrive at the best course of action."}]},{"n":"20.7","t":"End of chapter review questions","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–8 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The following are advantages of the Accounting Rate of Return: (i) It considers the profits of a project throughout its useful life (ii) It is simple to calculate and understand (iii) It facilitates expenditure follow-up due to more readily available data on accounting records (iv) It takes into account the time value of money. (A) (i) & (iii)  (B) (i) & (ii)  (C) (ii) & (iii)  (D) (i)–(iv)  (E) (i)–(iii)","The following are disadvantages of Pay-back period, EXCEPT ONE: (A) It does not consider the time value of money  (B) It ignores variations in the timing of cash inflow within the payback period  (C) The approach uses cash flows rather than accounting profits to appraise  (D) Cash inflows outside the payback period are ignored  (E) It does not take into consideration risks and uncertainties","The formula for the computation of the Net Present Value is NPV = C1/(1+K) + C2/(1+K)² + C3/(1+K)³ + … + Cn/(1+K)ⁿ − C0. What does C represent? (A) The opportunity cost of capital  (B) The initial cost of investment  (C) The project's expected life  (D) Cash inflows  (E) None of the above","The disadvantages associated with the use of net present value include the following: (i) timing of cash flows is considered (ii) there is the obligation for management to determine the appropriate cost of capital to use (iii) there is the assumption that the cash inflows will come as predicted, which may not necessarily be so. (A) (i)–(iii)  (B) (i) & (ii)  (C) (i)–(iv)  (D) (ii)–(iv)  (E) None of the above","In using the Accounting Rate of Return as an investment appraisal method, the decision rule is to pick the option which gives the ……………….","When using the Pay-back period method, for mutually exclusive projects, they are ranked and the one with the ………….. period is selected.","The appropriate discount rate chosen for the Net Present Value method is the firm's or corporation's …………","The decision criterion applicable under the NPV method is that a project is acceptable if it has a ………….. NPV."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** E  **2.** C  **3.** D  **4.** D  **5.** Highest rate of return  **6.** Shortest pay-back  **7.** Opportunity cost of capital  **8.** Positive"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — Omuro Local Government Council: PBP, NPV and ARR compared","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Omuro Local Government Council is considering Projects A, B and C:"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Project A (N)","Project B (N)","Project C (N)"],"rows":[["Initial investment","15,000","20,000","20,000"],["Residual value","1,000","1,000","1,000"],["Year 1 inflow","6,000","10,000","1,000"],["Year 2 inflow","7,000","10,000","6,000"],["Year 3 inflow","8,000","1,000","10,000"],["Year 4 inflow","9,000","1,000","20,000"]]}},{"p":"Cost of capital is 10%. Using the Payback Period, Net Present Value and Accounting Rate of Return methods, advise which project should be selected under each."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i) Payback period method"},{"p":"**Project B** was able to offset its initial capital injected in **2 years**, so it should be selected in preference to A. **Project C should be rejected** — it does not recover its outlay within the years shown."},{"h4":"(ii) Net Present Value method"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Year","Project A DCF (N)","Project B DCF (N)","Project C DCF (N)"],"rows":[["0","(15,000)","(20,000)","(20,000)"],["1","5,454","9,090","909"],["2","5,785","8,264","4,958"],["3","6,010","751","7,513"],["4","6,147","683","13,660"],["Residual value","683","683","683"],["**NPV**","**9,079**","**(529)**","**7,723**"]]}},{"p":"The Council is advised to invest in **Project A**, with the highest NPV of N9,079. **Project B has a negative NPV of N(529)** and is completely rejected. **Project C**, with a positive NPV of N7,723, is acceptable after A."},{"h4":"(iii) Accounting rate of return method"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Project A","Project B","Project C"],"rows":[["Depreciation p.a. = (Cost − Residual)/4","N3,500","N4,750","N4,750"],["Average annual accounting profit","N4,000","N750","N5,000"],["Average investment = (Cost + Residual)/2","N8,000","N10,500","N10,500"],["ARR","0.50 or **50%**","0.0714 or 7.14%","0.4762 or 47.62%"]]}},{"p":"The Council should invest in **Project A**, with the highest ARR of approximately 50%. **Project C** ranks second, **Project B** third."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)","tex":"ARR = \\dfrac{Average\\ Annual\\ Accounting\\ Profit}{Average\\ Investment}","nt":"Average investment = (Initial investment + Residual value) / 2. Pick the option with the highest ARR."},{"lb":"Net Present Value (NPV)","tex":"NPV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{C_t}{(1+K)^t} - C_0","nt":"K = opportunity cost of capital; C0 = initial outlay. Accept if NPV > 0; for mutually exclusive projects, pick the highest NPV."}],"focus":"Practise all three methods on the same set of cash flows until switching between them is automatic — ARR wants average accounting profit over average investment (pick highest); PBP wants the year cumulative inflows first cover the outlay (pick shortest); NPV wants discounted cash flows minus the initial outlay (pick highest, but only if positive). Watch for questions that quietly include a **residual value**, discounted in its own right in the NPV working and added into the \"investment\" figure for ARR.","errors":["Forgetting to discount the residual value in an NPV computation — it is a cash inflow in the final year (or its own row) and must be multiplied by that year's discount factor, not added at face value.","Ranking projects on payback period alone when the question asks for NPV or ARR — each method can give a different preferred project, and the decision rule differs (shortest time vs highest return vs highest discounted value).","Using total inflows instead of average annual accounting profit in the ARR numerator, or forgetting to add the residual value into the average investment denominator.","Treating a negative NPV project as merely \"less good\" — under the NPV decision rule it must be rejected outright, not just ranked lower."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the Accounting Rate of Return method, average investment is calculated as","o":["Initial investment only","(Initial investment + Residual value) / 2","Total accounting profit / number of years","Initial investment − Residual value","Average annual cash inflow"],"a":1,"w":"Average investment averages the initial outlay and what is left over (residual value) at the end of the project's life.","src":"Chapter 20.3","sec":"20.3"},{"q":"A project with an initial outlay of N200,000 recovers N90,000 in Year 1, N80,000 in Year 2 and N70,000 in Year 3. Under the payback period method, in which year is the outlay recovered?","o":["Year 1","Year 2","Year 3","It is never recovered","Cannot be determined"],"a":2,"w":"Cumulative inflows: Year 1 = 90,000 (200,000 − 90,000 = 110,000 outstanding); Year 2 = 170,000 (30,000 outstanding); Year 3 brings cumulative inflows to 240,000, exceeding the 200,000 outlay partway through the year — so payback falls within Year 3.","src":"Chapter 20.4","sec":"20.4"},{"q":"Under the Net Present Value decision rule, a project should be","o":["Accepted only if its IRR exceeds its ARR","Accepted if its NPV is positive, rejected if negative","Accepted regardless of the sign of its NPV, provided the payback is short","Ranked only by its undiscounted total cash inflow","Accepted only if its NPV equals exactly zero"],"a":1,"w":"A positive NPV means the discounted inflows exceed the discounted outflows (including the initial outlay), making the project acceptable; a negative NPV means it should be rejected.","src":"Chapter 20.5","sec":"20.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"A Local Government invests N50,000 in a project with a residual value of N5,000 after 4 years, generating accounting profits of N8,000, N10,000, N12,000 and N14,000 in years 1–4. Calculate the Accounting Rate of Return.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"Total accounting profit = 8,000 + 10,000 + 12,000 + 14,000 = **N44,000**. Average annual accounting profit = 44,000 / 4 = **N11,000**."},{"p":"Average investment = (50,000 + 5,000) / 2 = **N27,500**."},{"tex":"ARR = \\dfrac{11{,}000}{27{,}500} = 0.40\\ or\\ 40\\%"}],"src":"Chapter 20.3","sec":"20.3"},{"q":"A project costs N120,000 and is expected to generate cash inflows of N50,000, N45,000, N40,000 and N35,000 in years 1–4. Using discount factors of 0.9091, 0.8264, 0.7513 and 0.6830 at 10%, calculate the NPV and advise whether the project should be accepted.","marks":10,"a":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Year","Cash flow (N)","DF (10%)","DCF (N)"],"rows":[["0","(120,000)","1.0000","(120,000)"],["1","50,000","0.9091","45,455"],["2","45,000","0.8264","37,188"],["3","40,000","0.7513","30,052"],["4","35,000","0.6830","23,905"],["**NPV**","","","**16,600**"]]}},{"p":"Since the NPV is **positive (N16,600)**, the project should be **accepted**."}],"src":"Chapter 20.5","sec":"20.5"},{"q":"State any three advantages and three disadvantages of the Payback Period method of investment appraisal.","marks":6,"a":[{"h4":"Advantages (any three)"},{"ul":["A useful measure of liquidity, selecting projects with hope of quick cash recoupment.","Simple to calculate and understand.","Popular in public project evaluation, where liquidity predominates over profitability.","Uses cash flows, not accounting profits, to appraise."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages (any three)"},{"ul":["Does not consider the time value of money.","Ignores variations in the timing of cash inflows within the payback period.","Cash inflows outside the payback period are ignored.","Does not take risk and uncertainty into account."]}],"src":"Chapter 20.4","sec":"20.4"}]}},{"n":21,"t":"Emerging Issues in the Nigerian Public Sector","brief":"The information-technology reforms transforming Nigerian public financial management: the Treasury Single Account, ATRRS, IPPIS, GIFMIS, the Open Treasury Portal, the Federal Treasury e-Receipt, NRS e-invoicing, and TMRAS.","outcomes":["Explain the concept, design, models and benefits of the Treasury Single Account (TSA)","Describe the e-payment and e-collection components of TSA and the role of each stakeholder","Explain the purpose and benefits of ATRRS, IPPIS and GIFMIS","Describe the scope and financial-management functions of GIFMIS","Explain the Open Treasury Portal, the Federal Treasury e-Receipt and NRS e-invoicing","Describe the Treasury Management and Revenue Assurance System (TMRAS)"],"secs":[{"n":"21.1","t":"Background: the public financial management reforms","b":[{"p":"From **July 2003** the Federal Government pursued deliberate reforms to strengthen the economy, governance and the fight against corruption. The first **Economic Reform and Governance Project (ERGP)**, approved in December 2004, aimed to strengthen governance and accountability, reduce corruption and deliver services more effectively — and gave rise to the public-sector reforms in this chapter."}]},{"n":"21.2","t":"The Treasury Single Account (TSA)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Treasury Single Account (TSA)","d":"part of the 2004 Public Financial Management reforms — a bank account, or set of linked accounts, through which government transacts its financial operations, giving a **unified, consolidated view of government cash resources** to strengthen budget implementation, curb idle balances, ease planning and support decision-making."}},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["Ensure sufficient cash is available when needed to meet payment commitments.","Control aggregate cash flow within fiscal, monetary and legal limits.","Improve management of government's domestic borrowing programmes.","Enhance operating efficiency through high-quality service at minimal cost.","Invest excess or idle cash.","Ensure greater accountability in public expenditure."]},{"h4":"Models"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Model","Structure"],"rows":[["**Pure TSA (Model 1)**","No account sub-structure — every deposit and payment is processed through a **single** bank account. Relatively rare."],["**Decentralised TSA (Model 2)**","Separate accounts (in commercial banks or the CBN), **zero-balanced overnight (ZBAs)**; each MDA operates its own account under the TSA system and makes its own payments, while the Ministry of Finance sets cash-disbursement limits by unit of appropriation for control"]]}},{"h4":"Why TSA was introduced"},{"ul":["Government could not determine its cash position at any point in time.","MDAs maintained unlimited commercial bank accounts.","Domestic debt and borrowing grew without regard to actual need.","Idle/unspent balances sat in MDA accounts.","Excessive use of Ways and Means advances to finance the budget.","No effective cash planning as the Fiscal Responsibility Act required.","Warrants/AIEs were not based on a cash plan, causing delayed execution and year-end unspent balances.","Over **₦100 billion** was lost in failed commercial banks."]},{"h4":"Benefits"},{"ul":["Unifies banking arrangements and ensures efficient use of funds for approved projects.","Promotes transparency and accountability.","Reduces the amount and cost of government borrowing.","Gives centralised control over revenue through effective cash management.","Lets government know, daily, how much is accruing to it.","Reduces fiscal criminality and helps tame corruption."]},{"h4":"Components: e-payment and e-collection"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Component","What it is"],"rows":[["**E-payment**","Started April 2012 — direct electronic-transfer payment to an individual or organisation using ICT"],["**E-collection**","Started January 2015 (first circular 19 March 2015, guidelines September 2015) — a comprehensive electronic solution for remitting, managing and reporting all Federal Government receipts (revenue, donations, transfers, refunds, grants, fees, taxes, duties, tariffs) into the TSA and its CBN sub-accounts"]]}},{"p":"**E-payment objectives**: avoid borrowing/extra charges while MDAs sit on idle funds; aggregate cash control; minimise transaction costs; speed up payment; ease reconciliation; monitor MDA funds efficiently; support monetary-policy implementation. **E-collection objectives**: comply with ss.162/80 of the 1999 Constitution; collect and remit all revenue due to the Federation Account/CRF; block leakages in revenue generation, collection and remittance; entrench transparency and accountability; improve funds available for development; align with the CBN cashless policy; and ease the burden on revenue payers."},{"p":"**E-payment benefits**: complete, timely cash-resource information; better budget-execution control; efficient cash management; lower bank fees; efficient payment mechanisms; better reconciliation and fiscal data; improved liquidity; warrants/AIEs issued on a cash-plan basis; no more MDA commercial accounts; a sharp fall in Ways and Means requirements; support for budget execution; and elimination of the risks of stolen cheques, forged signatures and figure/words mismatches."},{"p":"**E-collection benefits**: controls and monitors FGN receipts/payments; prevents and detects fraud; improves MTEF-based planning; avoids double payments and payment arrears; produces accurate cash-flow statements supporting credit lines; accelerates cash collection; channels resources to budget priorities; minimises deficits/borrowing within set limits; and improves transparency of all FGN receipts."},{"def":{"t":"Public-Private Partnership (PPP)","d":"an arrangement in which the private sector is co-opted into financing socio-economic infrastructure that would otherwise be provided exclusively by government — e.g. toll roads, or the international passport contract referenced in the table below. Government and the private partner share the financing, risk and revenue of the project."}},{"h4":"Scope: how different MDA categories operate under TSA"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["MDA category","How it operates under TSA"],"rows":[["Fully budget-funded (e.g. ministries)","All collections paid directly into CRF/TSA; expenditure drawn from CRF/TSA per the approved budget"],["Partially funded, generate extra revenue (e.g. teaching hospitals)","Collections to CRF/TSA, except extra-budgetary receipts to a linked CBN sub-account; access to funds is budget-based"],["Not budget-funded, remit a surplus/25% of earnings (e.g. CBN, NPA, FAAN, NDIC)","Collections into linked CBN sub-accounts; budget-based access"],["Funded from the Federation Account (e.g. NNPC, FIRS/NRS, NCS, DPR)","Federation revenue to the Federation Account at CBN; independent revenue to CRF/TSA; FGN's Federation Account share to CRF/TSA; approved cost of collection to a linked sub-account"],["Funded through special levies (e.g. PTDF, NPA, RMRDC)","Linked CBN sub-accounts; all IGR paid directly to CRF/TSA"],["Profit-oriented corporations (e.g. BOI, NEXIM, BOA)","Dividends paid into CRF/TSA"],["Public-Private Partnership revenue (e.g. international passports)","FGN's portion to CRF/TSA; partners' portion transferred to their own accounts"],["Revolving/project-fund and donor-fund MDAs","A linked CBN project/donor sub-account; IGR collections to CRF/TSA; spending per the approved budget"]]}},{"h4":"Roles of the stakeholders"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stakeholder","Key roles"],"rows":[["**Office of the Accountant-General (OAGF)**","Implements and monitors e-collection; reconciles collections promptly; reports to MDAs; supports users; keeps guidelines current; and honours the stakeholder MoU"],["**MDA Directors of Finance and Accounts**","Maintain proper revenue records; issue e-collection receipts promptly; prevent diversion of IGR; render revenue-performance returns; stop collusive sharp practices; invest idle funds and remit interest to the CRF; and monitor revenue at FPOs, companies and parastatals"],["**Central Bank of Nigeria**","Deploys and secures the payment gateway (Remita); designs the payment/collection process; maintains the TSA; and issues bank guidelines"],["**Deposit Money Banks**","Give prompt attention to government payments; remit collections promptly and report complaints; liaise with OAGF/CBN; and honour the MoU"],["**Service provider**","Articulates system requirements with CBN/OAGF; runs a stable processing platform; supports and trains users; and supplies reports"]]}}]},{"n":"21.3","t":"Automated Accounting Transaction Recording and Reporting System (ATRRS)","b":[{"def":{"t":"ATRRS","d":"an ICT-based accounting software application, developed by the Treasury (OAGF), that automates input of accounting transactions, reconciliations and standard Treasury reports in line ministries, agencies and parastatals — a **bridge** towards full GIFMIS implementation."}},{"ul":["Familiarises staff with IT early, easing the transition to **GIFMIS**.","Reduces GIFMIS training time and implementation cost.","Shortens the business-process re-engineering period — faster from semi-automated than from fully manual.","Eases reconciliation of the various bank accounts.","Ensures clean, accurate data is ready for migration into GIFMIS."]}]},{"n":"21.4","t":"Integrated Personnel and Payroll Information System (IPPIS)","b":[{"def":{"t":"IPPIS","d":"a system conceived to improve the effectiveness and efficiency of storing personnel records and administering the monthly payroll, so as to give confidence in staff-emolument costs and budgeting."}},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["Facilitates manpower planning from a centralised database.","Aids budgeting for an accurate yearly emolument budget.","Monitors monthly staff payment against the budget.","Ensures database integrity — correct, intact personnel data.","Eliminates payroll fraud such as the **\"ghost workers\" syndrome**.","Eases storage, updating and retrieval of records for administration and pensions."]},{"h4":"Components and their functions"},{"ul":["Biometric **data-capture equipment** (fingerprint scanners, cameras) lets each MDA capture, update and process personnel records.","Public servants' records and biometric data are verified and stored in the **centralised IPPIS database**.","Salaries are paid **directly into enrolled staff' bank accounts**.","Third parties — FIRS/NRS, State Boards of Internal Revenue, PenCom, cooperative societies — also receive their deductions directly."]}]},{"n":"21.5","t":"Government Integrated Financial Management Information System (GIFMIS)","b":[{"def":{"t":"GIFMIS","d":"a sub-component of the ERGP — an IT-based budget-management and accounting system modernising fiscal processes to improve public expenditure management and strengthen accountability and transparency across MDAs, using modern ICT to plan and use financial resources more efficiently."}},{"p":"It targets four persistent weaknesses: budgets not enacted before the financial year begins; budgets not based on realistic cash-availability forecasts; poor cash management from multiple Treasury/MDA bank accounts (worsened by no cash forecasting, leading to unplanned borrowing); and weak integration across financial management functions. GIFMIS is a **tool that facilitates change**, not a driver of it — it must be paired with real business-process reform, and it enables the move to TSA, fewer processing stages and lower fiduciary risk."},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["Central control and monitoring of MDA expenditure and receipts.","Access to financial and operational performance information.","Stronger internal controls to prevent/detect fraud.","Visibility of government's cash position and economic performance.","Better medium-term planning through the **Medium-Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF)**.","Understanding the cost of groups of activities and tasks.","Demonstrable accountability and transparency to the public and partners."]},{"h4":"Scope — the financial management cycle it covers"},{"p":"Budget preparation; budget maintenance and management; budget execution and treasury management; the general ledger; procurement (commitments, a central suppliers' register, e-procurement support); receipting, receivables and revenue management; payments and payables; inventory and stock control; asset management; budget-execution and financial reporting; project accounting; and loans and advances."},{"p":"**Interfaces with third-party systems:** Human Manager (payroll, OAGF — payroll costs/loan repayments); ASYCUDA (Customs, NCS — revenue); SAP (Taxation, NRS — revenue); CS-DRMS (Debt Management, DMO — debt payments); Oracle ERP (banking, CBN — bank statements); Oracle 9i (medium-term budget, Budget Office — annual budgets and ceilings)."}]},{"n":"21.6","t":"The Open Treasury Portal","b":[{"p":"The Minister of Finance launched the **Financial Transparency Policy / Open Treasury Portal on 9 December 2019**, timed with United Nations Anti-Corruption Day. The OAGF must publish a **Daily Treasury Statement** of what came into and went out of the national purse — the Treasury publishes payments of **₦10 million and above**, and every MDA publishes payments **above ₦5 million**, showing the MDA's name, the beneficiary, the purpose and the amount."},{"ul":["Opens government's books to the public, raising confidence in its operations.","Keeps MDAs \"on their toes\".","Is monitored by a **peer-review mechanism** of the OAGF, the Auditor-General, EFCC and ICPC, among others.","Aims at improved transparency and combating corruption."]}]},{"n":"21.7","t":"The Federal Treasury e-Receipt (FTeR) and NRS e-invoicing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Federal Treasury e-Receipt (FTeR)","d":"a mandatory digital-receipt system giving digital proof of payment for taxes, levies and other government revenue, so as to record and track payments accurately, cut revenue leakage and improve compliance."}},{"p":"**Features:** digital proof of payment; a **Unique Reference Number (URN)** per payment; real-time updates; mandatory for every government payment; enhanced security; improved compliance; and efficient reconciliation."},{"p":"**Penalties for non-compliance:** ₦1 million for the first day, ₦10,000 for each subsequent day; ₦200,000 administrative penalty for failing to process taxable supplies through the system, plus 100% of the tax due, plus interest at 2% above the CBN monetary policy rate."},{"def":{"t":"NRS e-invoicing","d":"Nigeria's mandatory digital invoicing system — businesses generate, validate and transmit invoices electronically through the **Merchant Buyer Solution (MBS)** platform, involving VAT-registered suppliers, buyers and licensed **Access Point Providers (APPs)** — mandated under the Nigeria Tax Administration Act and Nigeria Tax Act, with penalties for non-compliance."}},{"p":"**Purpose:** enhance tax compliance, reduce evasion and increase revenue by accurately tracking transactions."},{"ul":["**Invoice generation** — a unique invoice number, date and transaction details.","**Real-time validation** by NRS for regulatory compliance.","**Digital record-keeping**, cutting paper storage.","**Automated reporting** to NRS, simplifying compliance.","**Secure authentication** (digital signatures) and **tracking/verification** by unique invoice number.","**Integration** with businesses' accounting systems.","**Compliance alerts** for filing requirements.","**Standardised formats** reducing errors, with a clear **audit trail** for assessments."]}]},{"n":"21.8","t":"Treasury Management and Revenue Assurance System (TMRAS)","b":[{"def":{"t":"TMRAS","d":"an integrated digital platform linking government treasuries, revenue-generating agencies and the central bank to pool cash in real time, track every naira from collection through allocation, and close leakages before they happen — streamlining and managing federal revenue collection and payment across MDAs with transparency, accountability and efficiency."}},{"h4":"Composition (modules)"},{"ul":["**Treasury management** — cash management, forecasting, banking transactions, liquidity.","**Revenue management** — collection, tracking and reporting of tax, customs and other revenue streams.","**Accounting and reconciliation** — financial transactions, payables, and data accuracy.","**Budget management** — planning, execution and analytics.","**Reporting and analytics** — dashboards and performance metrics for decisions."]},{"h4":"Powers, functions and benefits"},{"p":"**Powers:** centralise financial oversight; automate financial processes; enforce financial controls and compliance; provide real-time data insights; and enhance transparency and accountability."},{"p":"**Functions:** cash management and forecasting; revenue collection and tracking; financial reporting and reconciliation; budget planning and execution; financial analytics; and compliance/risk management."},{"p":"**Purpose in practice:** cash consolidation (eliminating idle agency balances, giving Treasury a live view); revenue assurance (matching receipts to taxpayer records, spotting under-remittance, auto-reconciling); transparency and control (an immutable audit trail, less manual intervention); liquidity optimisation (forecasting funding gaps, faster payments); and policy enforcement (budget ceilings, Cash Reserve Ratio rules and earmarks built into payment workflows)."},{"p":"**Benefits:** improved financial transparency; enhanced revenue collection; better cash management; and increased efficiency."},{"p":"**Implementation challenges:** integrating with existing systems; change management and user training; and data quality/security. **Best practice:** define clear objectives and scope; conduct a thorough needs assessment; select an experienced vendor; and provide adequate training and support."}]},{"n":"21.9","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–11 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is NOT an objective of the Treasury Single Account (TSA)? (A) Ensure cash availability  (B) Eliminate operational inefficiency and cost of maintaining multiple bank accounts  (C) Block loopholes in revenue management  (D) Ensure job stability  (E) Establish efficient collection and disbursement mechanisms for government funds","GIFMIS is an acronym for (A) General Integrated Financial Management Information System  (B) Government Instituted Financial Management Information System  (C) Government Information Financial Management Information System  (D) Government Integrated Financial Management Information System  (E) Government Institution Financial Management Information System","Which of the following is NOT one of the objectives of GIFMIS? (A) Increases the ability to access information on financial and operational performance  (B) Increases internal controls to prevent and detect potential and actual fraud  (C) Improves medium-term planning through the MTEF  (D) Increases the ability to demonstrate accountability and transparency  (E) Ensuring excessive use of ways and means in financing budget expenditure","Which of the following is NOT among the benefits of IPPIS? (A) It facilitates manpower planning since all civil service records are centralised  (B) It aids budgeting since an accurate recurrent expenditure budget becomes possible  (C) It eliminates payroll fraud such as the \"ghost workers\" syndrome  (D) It ensures payment of extra allowances to members of the executive, legislature and judiciary  (E) It ensures database integrity through accurate storage of personnel information","One of the following is NOT among the benefits of ATRRS accounting software: potentially reduces GIFMIS training period and requirements; facilitates ease of reconciliation of bank accounts; potentially reduces GIFMIS implementation costs; ensures clean, accurate data for migration into GIFMIS; makes accountants depend more on the efficiency of Auditor-General staff auditing public accounts","One of the following is the implementation timeline for the Mandatory Federal Treasury e-Receipt (FTeR): (A) 31 December 2025  (B) 1 December 2025  (C) 31 January 2026  (D) 1 January 2026  (E) 31 March 2026","… is a unified structure that gives a consolidated view of government cash resources to strengthen budget implementation, check idle cash balances, ease planning and support decision-making.","… is an ICT-based accounting software application facilitating the input of accounting transactions, reconciliations and generation of standard Treasury reports.","The Federal Government introduced … to improve the effectiveness and efficiency of storing personnel records and administering the monthly payroll.","… is a sub-component of the ERGP supporting public resource management and anti-corruption initiatives by modernising fiscal processes with better methods, techniques and IT.","The … initiative was approved to strengthen governance and accountability, reduce corruption and deliver services more effectively."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** D  **2.** D  **3.** E  **4.** D  **5.** E  **6.** D  **7.** Treasury Single Account (TSA)  **8.** Automated Accounting Transaction Recording and Reporting System (ATRRS)  **9.** Integrated Personnel and Payroll Information System (IPPIS)  **10.** Government Integrated Financial Management Information System (GIFMIS)  **11.** Economic Reform and Governance Project (ERGP)"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — e-payment","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What is e-payment? (b) List ten benefits of e-payment. (c) State two types of transaction covered by e-payment. (d) Itemise the content of an e-payment voucher/teller."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** E-payment is a subset of e-governance — the application of electronic means to government-to-citizen and government-to-business interaction. It is direct payment and banking without physical attendance at MDAs or banks, via electronic, interactive communication channels and technology."},{"p":"**(b) Benefits:**"},{"ol":["Complete, timely information on government cash resources.","Better operational control on budget execution.","Efficient cash management.","Lower bank fees and transaction costs.","Efficient payment mechanisms.","Better bank reconciliation and fiscal-data quality.","Improved government liquidity.","Warrants/AIEs issued on a cash-plan basis.","No more commercial bank accounts maintained by MDAs.","A drastic fall in the Ways and Means (overdraft) requirement from the CBN, supporting budget execution and eliminating the risks of stolen cheques, forged signatures and figure/words mismatches."]},{"p":"**(c) Transactions covered:** payments to contractors and service providers; and payments to staff, PHCN, FIRS and other government agencies."},{"p":"**(d) Contents of an e-payment teller:** account name and number of the beneficiary; the beneficiary's bank and branch; sort code (if not part of the account number); the amount payable; the purpose of payment; and the signature/thumbprint of the accounts signatories."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — the Treasury Single Account","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Explain the concept of the Treasury Single Account. (b) Discuss any five benefits of TSA."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** TSA was established to reduce the proliferation of bank accounts operated by MDAs and promote financial accountability among government agencies, though compliance has faced resistance from many MDAs."},{"p":"**(b)** Assists efficient use of funds for approved projects; unifies banking arrangements; promotes transparency and accountability; reduces the amount and cost of government borrowing; and ensures centralised revenue control through effective cash management (also: enhances accountability, and reduces fiscal criminality)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — GIFMIS","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) What is the full meaning of the acronym GIFMIS? (b) State the main objective of GIFMIS. (c) Enumerate six factors critical to GIFMIS's success."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Government Integrated Financial [and] Management Information System."},{"p":"**(b)** To implement an efficient, effective and user-friendly computerised financial management information system for the FGN."},{"p":"**(c)** Central control/monitoring of MDA expenditure and receipts; access to financial and operational performance information; stronger internal controls against fraud; visibility of government's cash position and economic performance; better MTEF-based medium-term planning; and the ability to understand the cost of groups of activities and tasks."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — ATRRS","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Briefly explain the term ATRRS. (b) State the benefits of ATRRS."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** ATRRS is an ICT-based accounting software application, developed by the Treasury (OAGF), that automates the manual recording of accounting transactions in line ministries, agencies and parastatals — enabling prompt, accurate financial returns and complex analytical reports."},{"p":"**(b)** Familiarises staff with IT ahead of GIFMIS; reduces GIFMIS training time; shortens business-process re-engineering; eases bank reconciliation; and ensures clean data is ready for migration into GIFMIS."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 5 — the Federal Treasury e-Receipt","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Explain the concept of the Federal Treasury e-Receipt (FTeR). (b) State the penalties for non-compliance."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** FTeR is a digital receipt system enhancing transparency and accountability in Nigeria's revenue collection, with features including digital proof of payment, a Unique Reference Number, real-time updates, mandatory use, enhanced security, improved compliance and efficient reconciliation."},{"p":"**(b)** ₦1 million penalty for the first day of non-compliance with FTeR deployment; ₦10,000 for each subsequent day; and ₦200,000 administrative penalty for failing to process taxable supplies through the system, plus 100% of the tax due, plus interest at 2% above the CBN's monetary policy rate."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 6 — NRS e-invoicing","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Briefly explain the meaning, composition, benefits and power of NRS e-invoicing. (b) State the features of e-invoicing and explain any five."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) Meaning:** Nigeria's mandatory digital invoicing system, requiring businesses to generate, validate and transmit invoices electronically through the Merchant Buyer Solution (MBS) platform. **Composition:** VAT-registered suppliers, buyers and licensed Access Point Providers (APPs). **Benefits:** improves tax compliance, reduces revenue leakage, enhances transparency. **Power:** mandated under the Nigeria Tax Administration Act and Nigeria Tax Act, with penalties for non-compliance."},{"p":"**(b)** Invoice generation with a unique number/date/details; real-time NRS validation for compliance; digital record-keeping; automated reporting to NRS; secure authentication; tracking/verification by invoice number; integration with accounting systems; compliance alerts; reduced errors from standardised formats; and enhanced audit trails."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 7 — TMRAS","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(i) What is the full meaning of the acronym TMRAS, and what does it mean? (ii) Briefly explain the purpose of TMRAS."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i)** Treasury Management and Revenue Assurance System — an integrated digital platform linking government treasuries, revenue-generating agencies and the central bank to pool cash in real time, track every naira from collection through allocation, and close leakages before they happen."},{"p":"**(ii)** Cash consolidation (eliminating idle balances, giving Treasury a live view); revenue assurance (matching receipts to taxpayer records, catching under-remittance, auto-reconciling); transparency and control (an audit trail, less manual intervention); liquidity optimisation (forecasting funding gaps, faster payment); and policy enforcement (embedding budget ceilings, CRR rules and earmarks into payment workflows)."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"A newer chapter, examined increasingly as the underlying reforms bed in. Section A tests the acronyms (TSA, ATRRS, IPPIS, GIFMIS, TMRAS) and their objectives; Section B most often asks for the concept and benefits of TSA, or the objectives of GIFMIS/IPPIS. Learn the two TSA components (e-payment/e-collection) and their separate objective and benefit lists — examiners test whether you can tell them apart.","errors":["Confusing ATRRS (an interim manual-to-digital bridge) with GIFMIS (the full integrated system it leads into).","Mixing up the objectives of e-payment with those of e-collection — they are listed separately in the study text for a reason.","Saying TSA eliminates all MDA bank accounts everywhere; some categories still operate linked CBN sub-accounts under the TSA umbrella.","Describing GIFMIS as the cause of reform rather than a tool that must be paired with business-process change.","Omitting the Unique Reference Number (URN) when describing the Federal Treasury e-Receipt."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Treasury Single Account (TSA) model with no account sub-structure, where all transactions pass through one bank account, is called","o":["the decentralised TSA","the pure TSA (Model 1)","a zero-balanced account","a sub-self-accounting model","the GIFMIS model"],"a":1,"w":"Model 1, the \"pure\" TSA, has no sub-structure at all; Model 2 (decentralised) uses zero-balanced overnight accounts per MDA.","src":"Chapter 21.2","sec":"21.2"},{"q":"The TSA component that commenced in April 2012, involving direct electronic-transfer payments, is","o":["e-collection","e-payment","e-invoicing","IPPIS","ATRRS"],"a":1,"w":"E-payment began in April 2012; e-collection followed in January 2015.","src":"Chapter 21.2","sec":"21.2"},{"q":"The acronym PPP, in the context of government expenditure, means","o":["Principal Partners Plan","Private Public Plan","Public-Private Partnership","Public Practice Partners","Perfect Public Partners"],"a":2,"w":"A Public-Private Partnership co-opts the private sector into financing socio-economic infrastructure otherwise provided exclusively by government, sharing financing, risk and revenue.","src":"Chapter 21.2","sec":"21.2"},{"q":"ATRRS is best described as","o":["the final IT system replacing all manual accounting permanently","an interim ICT bridge automating manual accounting ahead of GIFMIS","a payroll system for eliminating ghost workers","a public disclosure portal for government payments","a tax e-invoicing platform"],"a":1,"w":"ATRRS eases the workforce and data into IT before the full GIFMIS rollout, reducing GIFMIS training time and cost.","src":"Chapter 21.3","sec":"21.3"},{"q":"A core objective of IPPIS is to","o":["increase the number of MDA bank accounts","eliminate the \"ghost workers\" syndrome","replace the Treasury Single Account","set procurement thresholds","issue the Certificate of No Objection"],"a":1,"w":"A centralised, verified personnel database removes fictitious (\"ghost\") names from the payroll.","src":"Chapter 21.4","sec":"21.4"},{"q":"Under the Open Treasury Portal, an MDA must publish payments above","o":["₦1 million","₦5 million","₦10 million","₦50 million","₦100 million"],"a":1,"w":"The Treasury itself publishes payments of ₦10 million and above; every MDA must publish its own payments above ₦5 million.","src":"Chapter 21.6","sec":"21.6"},{"q":"The unique identifier assigned to every payment under the Federal Treasury e-Receipt (FTeR) is the","o":["NCOA code","Unique Reference Number (URN)","Treasury Form 15","Certificate of No Objection","TIN"],"a":1,"w":"The URN allows each e-Receipt payment to be tracked and verified.","src":"Chapter 21.7","sec":"21.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Explain the concept of the Treasury Single Account (TSA) and state five reasons for its introduction in Nigeria. (b) Distinguish between the e-payment and e-collection components of TSA, stating two objectives of each. (c) Explain the purpose of GIFMIS and state five financial-management functions it covers. (d) Briefly explain IPPIS and TMRAS.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) The TSA concept and reasons for its introduction"},{"p":"The TSA is a bank account, or set of linked accounts, through which government transacts its financial operations, giving a unified, consolidated view of government cash resources to strengthen budget implementation, curb idle balances, ease planning and support decision-making. It was part of the Public Financial Management reforms approved in 2004."},{"ol":["Government could not determine its cash position at any point in time.","MDAs maintained an unlimited number of commercial bank accounts.","Domestic debt and borrowing grew without regard to actual need.","Substantial idle/unspent balances sat in MDA accounts.","Warrants and AIEs were not based on a cash plan, delaying budget execution."]},{"h4":"(b) E-payment and e-collection"},{"p":"**E-payment** (from April 2012) is direct electronic-transfer payment to individuals or organisations; its objectives include avoiding unnecessary borrowing while MDAs hold idle funds, and achieving aggregate cash control. **E-collection** (from January 2015) is the electronic remittance, management and reporting of all Federal Government receipts into the TSA and its CBN sub-accounts; its objectives include compliance with sections 162 and 80 of the 1999 Constitution, and blocking leakages in revenue collection and remittance."},{"h4":"(c) GIFMIS"},{"p":"GIFMIS is an IT-based budget-management and accounting system implemented to improve public expenditure management and strengthen accountability and transparency across MDAs, using modern ICT to plan and use financial resources more efficiently. It covers: budget preparation; budget maintenance and management; budget execution and treasury management; the general ledger; procurement; and receipting/revenue management (also payments/payables, inventory and asset management, financial reporting, project accounting, and loans and advances)."},{"h4":"(d) IPPIS and TMRAS"},{"p":"**IPPIS** is a centralised personnel and payroll database that captures biometric staff records, pays salaries directly into staff bank accounts, and eliminates payroll fraud such as the \"ghost workers\" syndrome. **TMRAS** (Treasury Management and Revenue Assurance System) is an integrated digital platform linking treasuries, revenue-generating agencies and the central bank to pool cash in real time, track every naira from collection through allocation, and close revenue leakages before they happen."}],"src":"Chapter 21.2–21.8","sec":"21.2"}]}},{"n":22,"t":"Ethical Considerations in Public Sector Accounting in Managing Economic Crimes","brief":"The institutions Nigeria has established to enforce ethical standards and combat corruption and financial crime: the EFCC, the ICPC, the Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal, the Public Complaints Commission, the Money Laundering (Prevention) Act, and the role of the Judiciary.","outcomes":["Identify and discuss the institutions responsible for ethical compliance in the public sector","State the duties, powers and offences/penalties under the EFCC Act","State the composition, duties, offences/penalties under the ICPC Act, and distinguish it from the EFCC","Explain the Code of Conduct for public officers and the roles of the Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal","Describe the mandate of the Public Complaints Commission (Ombudsman)","State the key prohibitions and obligations under the Money Laundering (Prohibition) Act","Describe the characteristics and functions of the Judiciary"],"secs":[{"n":"22.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"To stamp out corruption, fraud, greed and avarice, and to repair Nigeria's image before accountability bodies such as **Transparency International**, the Federal Government has introduced a suite of regulatory laws and institutions — each dealt with in turn in this chapter."}]},{"n":"22.2","t":"Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC)","b":[{"def":{"t":"EFCC","d":"empowered to prevent, investigate, prosecute and sanction economic and financial crimes, and to enforce related laws — the Money Laundering Act 1995, the Advance Fee Fraud and Other Related Offences Act 1995, the Failed Banks (Financial Malpractices in Banks) Act 1994, the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 1991, and the Miscellaneous Offences Act."}},{"h4":"Duties (Part II of the Act)"},{"ul":["Enforce and administer the Act, and investigate financial crimes — advance fee fraud, money laundering, counterfeiting, illegal charge transfers, futures-market fraud, fraudulent encashment of negotiable instruments, computer/credit-card fraud, contract scams and the like.","Co-ordinate all economic/financial crime enforcement functions conferred on any other person or authority, and adopt measures to **eradicate** such crimes.","Identify, trace, freeze, confiscate or seize proceeds of terrorism or economic/financial crime, or property of equivalent value.","Adopt coordinated preventive/regulatory measures and investigative/control techniques; facilitate rapid exchange of technical information and joint operations.","Examine reported cases to identify the individuals, corporate bodies or groups involved, and determine the extent of loss suffered.","Collaborate with government bodies in Nigeria and abroad on identifying suspects, tracing proceeds, exchanging personnel/experts, monitoring international crime, and maintaining data/statistics/records.","Research the manifestation, extent and effect of such crimes, and advise government on intervention.","Take charge of, supervise, control and coordinate investigation/prosecution of all such offences, in consultation with the **Attorney-General of the Federation**."]},{"h4":"Powers (paragraph 6 of the Act)"},{"ul":["Cause investigation of any suspected offence under the Act, or of a person whose lifestyle/property is unjustified by his income.","Enforce the Money Laundering Act 1995, the Advance Fee Fraud Act 1995, the Failed Banks Act 1994, the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act 1991, the Miscellaneous Offences Act, and any other relevant law."]},{"h4":"Offences and penalties (Part IV)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["Financial malpractice","5 years' imprisonment or a ₦50,000 fine, or both"],["Terrorism-related offences","Imprisonment for life"],["Offences by public officers","15 to 25 years' imprisonment"],["Retaining proceeds of criminal conduct","Not less than 5 years, or a fine of 5 times the value of the proceeds, or both"],["Economic/financial crime generally","Not less than 15 and not more than 25 years"]]}},{"warn":"Paragraph 20 of the Act provides that all property of a convicted person shown to derive from the illegal act, and already subject to an interim order, is **forfeited to the Federal Government** — no further assurance beyond the Act itself is needed."}]},{"n":"22.3","t":"Independent Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Commission (ICPC)","b":[{"def":{"t":"ICPC","d":"established by the Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Act 2000 — a body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal, able to sue and be sued in its own name."}},{"h4":"Composition"},{"p":"A Chairman (who has held, or is qualified to hold, office as a judge of a superior court of record) and **twelve other members**, two from each of the six geopolitical zones: a legal practitioner (10+ years post-call); a retired judge of a superior court; a retired police officer not below Commissioner rank; a retired public servant not below Director rank; a woman; a chartered accountant; and a youth aged 21–30 at appointment."},{"p":"**Appointment/tenure:** members must be of unquestionable integrity, appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate; they may not begin duties until they have declared their assets/liabilities. The **Chairman serves 5 years**, renewable once; **other members serve 4 years**, renewable once."},{"h4":"Duties"},{"ul":["Receive and investigate reports of conspiring to commit, attempting, or committing an offence under the Act or any anti-corruption law, and prosecute offenders.","Examine public bodies' practices/systems/procedures that aid fraud or corruption, and direct/supervise their review.","Instruct, advise and assist officers/agencies/parastatals on eliminating or minimising fraud/corruption.","Advise Heads of Public Bodies on practice/procedure changes to reduce bribery/corruption.","Enlist and foster public support in combating corruption."]},{"h4":"Offences and penalties"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty on conviction"],"rows":[["Accepting/soliciting gratification (self or another)","7 years"],["Giving/accepting gratification through an agent","7 years"],["Gratification where the purpose was never carried out","7 years"],["Fraudulent acquisition, or fraudulent receipt, of property","7 years"],["Offence via the postal system (felony)","3 years — 7 years if the item obtained was postal matter or a chattel/money/security inside it"],["Deliberately frustrating an ICPC investigation (destroying/altering documents)","7 years"],["Making a false statement/return on money or property in one's care","7 years"],["Gratification by/through an agent","5 years"],["Bribery of a public officer","5 years with hard labour"],["Using office/position to gratify oneself or an associate","5 years, no option of a fine"],["Inflating the price of goods/services above market/professional rates","7 years plus a ₦1,000,000 fine"]]}},{"h4":"EFCC compared with ICPC"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","EFCC","ICPC"],"rows":[["Scope","Banking, money laundering, Advance Fee Fraud (\"419\") and miscellaneous offences","Fraud, corruption and embezzlement in public services"],["Time limit","None — any crime, whenever committed","Limited to offences committed from **year 2000** onward"],["Prosecution","Can prosecute **directly**, without recourse to the Attorney-General","Can prosecute **only through** the Attorney-General's office"]]}}]},{"n":"22.4","t":"Code of Conduct Bureau (CCB)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Code of Conduct Bureau","d":"established by Part I of the Third Schedule to the 1999 Constitution. Composition: a Chairman and **nine other members**, none under 50 at appointment, vacating office at 70; the Bureau opens state offices as it needs."}},{"h4":"Powers"},{"ul":["Receive public officers' asset declarations under the Fifth Schedule, Part I, paragraph 12.","Examine declarations against the Code or any law; retain custody and allow citizen inspection on National Assembly-prescribed terms.","Ensure compliance with, and enforce, the Code of Conduct.","Receive and investigate complaints of breach, referring appropriate matters to the **Code of Conduct Tribunal**.","Carry out any other function the National Assembly confers."]},{"h4":"The Code of Conduct for public officers (Fifth Schedule, Part I)"},{"p":"The governing rule: **\"a public officer shall not put himself in a position where his personal interest conflicts with his duties and responsibilities.\"**"},{"ul":["**No double emoluments** — except part-time officers not running a private business (farming is always permitted).","**No foreign bank accounts** for the President, Vice-President, Governors, Deputy Governors, federal/state ministers/commissioners, and members of the National/State Assemblies.","**Retired officers** may hold at most one remunerative position (chairman/director/staff) in a government-controlled company in addition to pension, and the President, VP, CJN, Governor and Deputy Governor may never work for a foreign company/enterprise.","**No gifts/benefits** in the discharge of duty, other than customary personal gifts from relatives/friends, and **no bribes**.","**No abuse of office**, and **no membership** of a society incompatible with the dignity of one's office.","**Asset declaration** — within 3 months of the Code taking effect or of taking office, and thereafter every 4 years and at the end of one's term, covering the officer and unmarried children under 18. A false statement breaches the Code, as does any unexplained increase in assets after a declaration, unless the contrary is proved."]}]},{"n":"22.5","t":"Code of Conduct Tribunal","b":[{"p":"Comprises a **Chairman and two other persons**. Where it finds a public officer guilty of contravening the Code, it may impose:"},{"ol":["Vacation of office or of a seat in any Legislative House.","Disqualification from Legislative House membership and from any public office, for up to **10 years**.","Seizure and forfeiture to the State of property acquired through abuse/corruption of office.","Any penalty a law imposes where the conduct is also a criminal offence.","Prosecution of the officer in a court of law."]},{"p":"A finding may be **appealed to the Court of Appeal**; the **prerogative of mercy does not apply** to a Tribunal punishment."}]},{"n":"22.6","t":"Public Complaints Commission (PCC) — Nigeria's Ombudsman","b":[{"p":"Established by **Decree No. 31 of 1975** (amended by Decree No. 21 of 1979), now **Cap. P37 LFN 2004**. A **Chief Commissioner** (Abuja head office) and State **Commissioners** are appointed by the National Assembly for **3 years**, renewable once, after which they must vacate; the National Assembly may remove a commissioner at any time, and a commissioner may hold no other remunerated office."},{"h4":"Mandate"},{"ul":["Investigate and research MDAs, private companies and their officials, and the administrative procedures of any court.","Report crimes discovered during or after investigation, and report erring officers for discipline.","Interpret government policy and advise government and companies; publish reports after investigation; make recommendations.","Have access to all information necessary for its work."]},{"h4":"Powers and duties of a commissioner"},{"ul":["Investigates, on his own initiative or on complaint, administrative action by any federal/state department, statutory corporation, CAMA-incorporated company (public or private), or their officers.","Determines how complaints are lodged and, at his discretion, whether/how to notify the public of action taken.","May visit and inspect any relevant premises, and investigates with special care any action that is contrary to law, mistaken/arbitrary, unreasonable/unfair/oppressive, or otherwise objectionable.","May also investigate the administrative procedures of **any court** in Nigeria.","Where concurrent complaints reach more than one commissioner, the **Chief Commissioner** decides who handles it, finally.","Must keep matters designated secret confidential (save what must be disclosed to support a report's conclusions), and acts **free of any external direction or control**.","A person required to furnish information must comply within **30 days**."]},{"warn":"A commissioner may **not** investigate: matters clearly outside his terms of reference; matters pending before the National Assembly, the National Council of State/Ministers, or any court; or matters concerning Armed Forces/Police personnel under the Army, Navy, Air Force or Police Acts."}]},{"n":"22.7","t":"The Money Laundering (Prohibition) Act","b":[{"def":{"t":"Money laundering","d":"exchanging criminally obtained money/assets for \"clean\" money/assets with no obvious link to crime — the term also covers money used to fund terrorism, regardless of how it was obtained."}},{"h4":"Prevention: limits, reporting and identification"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Rule","Detail"],"rows":[["**Cash payment limit** (outside a financial institution)","₦500,000 for an individual; ₦2 million for a body corporate"],["**International transfer reporting**","Any transfer of funds/securities above US$10,000 to/from a foreign country must be reported to the **CBN**, with the amount, nature, and sender/receiver details"],["**Over-the-counter exchange dealers**","Must declare their activity to the CBN before starting business; identify any customer transacting above US$100,000 by photo ID; and keep a numbered, chronological register for **at least 10 years**"],["**Casinos**","Must verify a gambler's identity by document, and keep a similar 10-year register"],["**Customer identification (KYC)**","A financial institution verifies identity/address before opening any account or relationship; individuals show photo ID and a recent utility bill, bodies corporate their certificate of incorporation; a casual customer is identified once a transaction (or connected transactions) exceeds ₦500,000; suspected drug-money transactions require identification regardless of amount; and the true principal must be identified if the customer may be acting for someone else"],["**Special surveillance**","Triggered by a transaction above ₦500,000 (individual) or ₦2 million (corporate) that is unusually/unjustifiably complex and has no clear economic or lawful purpose — if the institution proceeds, it must file a written report and send a copy to the CBN"],["**Record preservation**","Identification records — 10 years after the account closes; transaction/report records — 10 years after the transaction/report"],["**Staff awareness**","Institutions must appoint compliance officers at HQ and every branch, run ongoing staff training, centralise collected information, and maintain an internal audit unit"],["**Mandatory disclosure**","A single transaction above ₦500,000 (individual) or ₦2 million (corporate) must be reported in writing to the Agency within **7 days**; a stop notice may defer the transaction up to **72 hours**, after which it may proceed unless the Federal High Court orders the funds blocked"]]}},{"h4":"Offences and penalties"},{"ul":["**Money laundering itself** (converting/transferring, or helping conceal, proceeds of illicit drug trafficking): **15–25 years'** imprisonment.","**Tipping off, destroying records, using a false identity, breaching the cash limits, or failing to report an international transfer**: for an individual, a fine of ₦250,000–₦1,000,000 and/or 15–25 years; for a corporate body (bureau de change, casino, financial institution), a fine of ₦250,000–₦1,000,000, plus possible **permanent or 5-year debarment** from the profession involved.","**Conspiracy, aiding, abetting, inciting or being an accessory** attracts the same punishment as the underlying offence.","A body corporate's offence attributable to a director's or manager's instigation, connivance or neglect makes that individual **personally** liable too.","Banking secrecy is **no defence** against surveillance measures or a duty to testify under this Act."]}]},{"n":"22.8","t":"The Judiciary","b":[{"p":"The judiciary — judges and courts — is one of the institutions of constitutional government, alongside the legislature, the executive and the electoral system."},{"h4":"Characteristics"},{"ul":["**Impartiality** and **political neutrality** — judges do not engage in partisan politics.","**Permanence** — inherited unchanged by successive governments.","**Independence** from the executive and legislature, staffed by legal **professionals**.","**Stable tenure** — judges serve until retirement unless found guilty of corrupt practice, protected by **judicial immunity**, and bound by due process and a code of conduct."]},{"h4":"Functions"},{"ol":["**Interprets the law/Constitution**, upholding constitutional supremacy and shaping major political decisions.","**Settles disputes** between the Executive and Legislature, and between parties, individuals and organisations.","**Protects citizens' rights and liberties**, including ordering the release of anyone unjustly detained.","**Advises the Executive and Legislature** on sensitive matters — e.g. the prerogative of mercy, or constitutional amendments.","**Punishes law-breakers**, ensuring the law is obeyed.","**Determines election petitions**, ascertaining the true winner of an election."]}]},{"n":"22.9","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–11 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The Commission responsible for compelling any person or government institution to disclose information relating to public revenue and expenditure is (A) Economic and Financial Crimes Commission  (B) Fiscal Responsibility Commission  (C) Audit Committee  (D) Public Complaints Commission  (E) Security Service Commission","Which of the following tiers of government funds the Fiscal Responsibility Commission? (A) The Local Government  (B) The State Government  (C) The Federal Government  (D) The National Assembly  (E) The Local Council Legislature","Who serves as Chairman of the National Council on Public Procurement? (A) The Auditor-General for the Federation  (B) The Accountant-General of the Federation  (C) The Revenue Collector  (D) The Minister of Finance  (E) The Budget Officer","Which of the following Ministries has a representative on the Board of the EFCC? (A) Ministry of Education  (B) Ministry of Power and Steel  (C) Ministry of Works  (D) Ministry of Agriculture  (E) Ministry of Justice","Which of the following is NOT true about the ICPC? (A) It is established by an Act  (B) It is a body corporate  (C) It has perpetual succession  (D) It can sue and be sued in its own name  (E) It can prosecute without recourse to the Attorney-General","The body established by Act of Parliament No. 5 of December 2002 with power to enforce the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act is called …","The ICPC's composition has a Chairman and twelve other members, two of whom come from each …","Under the Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Act 2000, the Chairman of the ICPC may hold office for a maximum aggregate of … years on re-election.","The body with power to prosecute directly without recourse to the Attorney-General's office is …","The body that receives copies of annual reports and financial statements of public interest entities from preparers within 60 days of Board approval is …","The means of effecting payment for, and receipt of, government business transactions through online transfer is called …"]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** B  **2.** C  **3.** D  **4.** E  **5.** E  **6.** EFCC  **7.** the six geo-political zones of the nation  **8.** 10 years  **9.** EFCC  **10.** Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria  **11.** E-payment"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — the Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Part I of the Third Schedule to the 1999 Constitution established the Code of Conduct Bureau. State six powers of the Bureau. (b) Outline the punishments the Code of Conduct Tribunal may impose where a public officer is found guilty of contravening the Code."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** See §22.4 above — receiving asset declarations; examining them against the Code; retaining custody and allowing inspection; ensuring compliance/enforcement; receiving and investigating complaints of breach; and referring appropriate matters to the Code of Conduct Tribunal."},{"ol":["Vacation of office or of a seat in any legislative house.","Prosecution of the officer in a court of law.","Disqualification from legislative house membership and from holding any public office, for up to ten years.","Any penalty a law imposes where the conduct is also a criminal offence.","Seizure and forfeiture to the State of property acquired through abuse/corruption of office."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — functions and powers of the EFCC","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The EFCC was established by Act No. 5 of 2002. (i) Enumerate eight functions of the Commission. (ii) In line with paragraph 6 of the Act, state three powers of the Commission."}],"a":[{"ol":["Enforcement and due administration of the Act.","Investigation of reported financial crimes — Advance Fee Fraud (\"419\"), money laundering, counterfeiting, illegal charge transfers, contract scams, forgery of financial instruments, issuance of dud cheques.","Adopting measures to identify, trace, freeze, confiscate or seize proceeds of terrorism.","Adopting measures to identify, trace, freeze and seize proceeds of financial crime.","Adopting measures to eradicate and prevent economic/financial crime, identifying the individuals, corporate bodies or groups involved.","Determining the extent of financial and other losses to government, individuals and organisations.","Collaborating with government bodies within and outside Nigeria in discharging the Act's functions, including on extradition, deportation and mutual legal assistance.","Collecting, analysing and disseminating reports on suspicious financial transactions, and running public-enlightenment campaigns against economic and financial crime."]},{"p":"**(ii)** Investigate, or cause investigation of, any suspected offence under the Act; investigate the properties of a person whose lifestyle/property is unjustified by his income; and enforce the Banks and Other Financial Institutions Act, the Failed Banks Act, the Advance Fee Fraud Act, the Money Laundering Act and the Miscellaneous Offences Act."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — the ICPC","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Act 2000 established the ICPC. (a) State eight functions of the Commission. (b) State six offences and penalties. (c) Compare and contrast the EFCC and the ICPC."}],"a":[{"ol":["Receive and investigate any report of conspiring to commit, attempting, or committing an offence under the Act.","Prosecute those found, on investigation, to have committed an offence.","Examine the systems, practices and procedures of public bodies (ministries, states, local governments, parastatals).","Give supervisory advice where practices/systems/procedures are susceptible to fraud or corruption.","Advise, educate and assist officers, agencies, boards or parastatals on eliminating or minimising fraud and corruption.","Notify Accounting Officers of changes to administrative procedures/systems affecting their ministries, parastatals or departments.","Educate public bodies on detecting, preventing and arresting fraud, bribery, corruption and related offences.","Educate and involve the general public in the fight against corruption."]},{"p":"**(b)** See the offences/penalties table in §22.3 above — e.g. accepting gratification (7 years), bribery of a public officer (5 years with hard labour), using office for personal gratification (5 years, no fine option), and inflating the price of goods/services (7 years plus a ₦1,000,000 fine)."},{"p":"**(c)** The EFCC enforces banking, money-laundering and Advance Fee Fraud law with no time limitation and can prosecute directly; the ICPC enforces fraud/corruption/embezzlement law in public services, only for offences from the year 2000 onward, and can prosecute only through the Attorney-General's office."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) State any ten members of the Board of the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria. (b) Explain the circumstances in which a Board seat becomes vacant."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** A Chairman (a professional accountant of considerable standing); two representatives each from ANAN and ICAN; and one representative each from the OAGF, the Office of the Auditor-General for the Federation, the CBN, the Chartered Institute of Stockbrokers, the Chartered Institute of Taxation of Nigeria, the Corporate Affairs Commission, the FIRS, the Federal Ministry of Finance, NACCIMA, the NDIC, the Nigerian Institute of Estate Surveyors and Valuers, SEC, the Nigerian Stock Exchange, NAICOM, the Nigerian Accounting Association, the Federal Ministry of Commerce, and PenCom."},{"p":"**(b)** A seat becomes vacant if the member resigns; becomes disqualified under section 4 of the Act; ceases to hold the office by virtue of which he became a member; or is absent, without leave (from the Minister for the Chairman, or the Chairman for any other member), from consecutive meetings or three-quarters of the Board's meetings in a financial year."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Section A tests names, dates and composition — the year each body was established, the monetary thresholds under the Money Laundering Act, and the tenure of ICPC/CCB/PCC members. Section B most often asks for the functions/powers of the EFCC or ICPC, or a compare-and-contrast between them — learn that comparison table cold, since it is asked almost every diet in one form or another.","errors":["Confusing the EFCC (banking/money-laundering/419, no time limit, prosecutes directly) with the ICPC (public-sector corruption, offences from 2000 only, prosecutes through the Attorney-General).","Saying the Code of Conduct Tribunal's decisions are final; they may be appealed to the Court of Appeal.","Giving the Money Laundering Act's individual cash limit as the same as the corporate limit; they are ₦500,000 and ₦2 million respectively.","Treating the Public Complaints Commission as able to investigate matters already before a court or the National Assembly; both are expressly excluded.","Forgetting that write-off/prosecution decisions under the EFCC/ICPC Acts do not discharge an officer already surcharged, or vice versa — they are independent consequences."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following can prosecute directly, without recourse to the Attorney-General's office?","o":["ICPC","EFCC","Code of Conduct Bureau","Public Complaints Commission","Code of Conduct Tribunal"],"a":1,"w":"The EFCC has direct prosecutorial power; the ICPC must prosecute through the Attorney-General.","src":"Chapter 22.3","sec":"22.3"},{"q":"The ICPC is limited in time to offences committed","o":["at any time, with no limitation","from the year 2000 onward","within the last five years only","before independence in 1960","only during a state of emergency"],"a":1,"w":"Unlike the EFCC, which has no time limitation, the ICPC covers only offences from 2000 onward.","src":"Chapter 22.3","sec":"22.3"},{"q":"Under the Money Laundering (Prohibition) Act, the cash payment limit outside a financial institution for an individual is","o":["₦200,000","₦500,000","₦1,000,000","₦2,000,000","₦5,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"The limit is ₦500,000 for an individual and ₦2 million for a body corporate.","src":"Chapter 22.7","sec":"22.7"},{"q":"A body established under Part I of the Third Schedule to the 1999 Constitution to receive and examine public officers' asset declarations is the","o":["Independent Corrupt Practices Commission","Code of Conduct Bureau","Public Complaints Commission","Economic and Financial Crimes Commission","Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Code of Conduct Bureau receives, examines and keeps custody of asset declarations, and refers breaches to the Code of Conduct Tribunal.","src":"Chapter 22.4","sec":"22.4"},{"q":"Nigeria's \"Ombudsman\", empowered to investigate administrative action by MDAs and public bodies on its own initiative or on complaint, is the","o":["Code of Conduct Tribunal","Public Complaints Commission","ICPC","EFCC","Judiciary"],"a":1,"w":"The Public Complaints Commission, established by Decree No. 31 of 1975, is Nigeria's Ombudsman.","src":"Chapter 22.6","sec":"22.6"},{"q":"A decision of the Code of Conduct Tribunal","o":["is final and cannot be appealed","may be appealed to the Court of Appeal","may be pardoned under the prerogative of mercy","may only be appealed to the ICPC","is subject to review by the EFCC"],"a":1,"w":"An officer found guilty may appeal to the Court of Appeal, but the prerogative of mercy does not apply to a Tribunal punishment.","src":"Chapter 22.5","sec":"22.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State the composition and duties of the Independent Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Commission (ICPC). (b) Compare and contrast the EFCC and the ICPC. (c) Explain the Code of Conduct for public officers and state the punishments the Code of Conduct Tribunal may impose. (d) Describe the mandate of the Public Complaints Commission.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) ICPC composition and duties"},{"p":"The ICPC consists of a Chairman (a person who has held or is qualified to hold office as a judge of a superior court) and twelve other members, two from each of the six geopolitical zones, drawn from specified categories (a legal practitioner, a retired judge, a retired senior police officer, a retired senior public servant, a woman, a chartered accountant, and a youth aged 21–30). Members are appointed by the President, confirmed by the Senate, and must declare their assets before assuming duty. The Chairman serves 5 years (renewable once); other members serve 4 years (renewable once)."},{"p":"Its duties: receive and investigate reports of corruption offences and prosecute offenders; examine and direct the review of public bodies' practices and procedures that facilitate fraud; advise and assist agencies on eliminating corruption; advise Heads of Public Bodies on procedural changes; and enlist public support against corruption."},{"h4":"(b) EFCC compared with ICPC"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","EFCC","ICPC"],"rows":[["Scope","Banking, money laundering, Advance Fee Fraud and miscellaneous offences","Fraud, corruption and embezzlement in public services"],["Time limitation","None","Offences committed from 2000 onward only"],["Prosecution","Directly, without the Attorney-General","Only through the Attorney-General's office"]]}},{"h4":"(c) The Code of Conduct and Tribunal punishments"},{"p":"The Code of Conduct (Fifth Schedule, Part I, 1999 Constitution) rests on the principle that a public officer must not place himself where personal interest conflicts with duty. It bars double emoluments, foreign bank accounts for named senior officers, more than one remunerative position after retirement, gifts or benefits (beyond customary ones from relatives/friends), bribery, abuse of office, and incompatible society membership — and requires periodic asset declaration."},{"ol":["Vacation of office or of a legislative seat.","Disqualification from legislative membership and public office for up to 10 years.","Seizure and forfeiture to the State of property acquired through abuse of office.","Any penalty a law separately imposes where the conduct is also a criminal offence.","Prosecution of the officer in a court of law."]},{"h4":"(d) The Public Complaints Commission"},{"p":"The PCC (Nigeria's Ombudsman, Cap. P37 LFN 2004) investigates and researches MDAs, private companies and their officials, and the administrative procedures of any court; reports crimes and erring officers for discipline; interprets and advises on government policy; publishes reports and recommendations; and has access to all information needed for its work — save for matters already before the National Assembly, a court, or concerning the Armed Forces/Police, which it may not investigate."}],"src":"Chapter 22.2–22.6","sec":"22.2"}]}},{"n":23,"t":"Public Sector Audit","brief":"The legal framework for auditing government accounts, the objectives and types of audit, the steps in a government audit, the Auditor-General/Public Accounts Committee relationship, internal audit, the INTOSAI code of ethics, and the codified sanctions for audit-query offences under the Financial Regulations.","outcomes":["State the constitutional and statutory basis for auditing public accounts","State the objectives of audit and the factors that make an audit effective","Distinguish compliance, financial and performance (value-for-money) audit, and describe the other recognised types of audit","Outline the steps in auditing government financial statements","Explain the relationship between the Auditor-General and the Public Accounts Committee","State the objectives and scope of internal audit and how it supports external audit","State the INTOSAI code of ethics and basic postulates for auditing standards","State the sanctions prescribed for audit-query offences under the Financial Regulations"],"secs":[{"n":"23.1","t":"Introduction and legal framework","b":[{"def":{"t":"Auditing","d":"an independent appraisal process, often governed by statute, for examining, investigating and verifying an organisation's financial statements. The auditor forms an opinion on the truth, accuracy, validity, reliability and fairness of the statements and the records behind them, and on whether they comply with statutory or other requirements, reporting that opinion to the statements' users."}},{"h4":"Constitutional basis"},{"p":"Sections **85–87** of the 1999 Constitution (ss.125–128 for a State) require the accounts of the Federation/State and all its offices and courts to be audited and reported to the **Auditor-General**, who reports to the **National (or State) Assembly**; the Auditor-General or anyone he authorises has access to every book, record, return or document relating to those accounts. Under section 85 (or 125), the **Auditor-General for the Federation** (or for the State) is the **external auditor** of the Federation (or State)."},{"h4":"Audit of government enterprises (parastatals)"},{"ul":["An **Internal Audit Department** must be established, auditing the corporation and forwarding reports to the Auditor-General for information.","The board must appoint an **external auditor** annually (s.85(3)).","Section 85(3): the Auditor-General provides a **list of qualified external auditors**, **guidelines on the fees** payable to them, and **comments on the annual accounts** and auditors' reports.","Section 85(4): the Auditor-General may conduct periodic checks of **all** government statutory corporations, commissions, authorities and agencies established by an Act of the National Assembly."]}]},{"n":"23.2","t":"Objectives of audit and factors for an effective audit","b":[{"h4":"General audit objectives"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Objective","What it tests"],"rows":[["**Completeness**","All transactions relevant to the year have been recorded"],["**Occurrence**","Every recorded transaction actually occurred and belongs to the year"],["**Measurement**","Transactions are correctly valued/calculated, consistently, per established accounting policy"],["**Regularity**","Transactions comply with primary/secondary legislation and other required authorities"],["**Disclosure**","Transactions are properly classified and disclosed where required"]]}},{"h4":"Factors making for an effective audit"},{"ol":["**Independence of the auditor** — free of management's control.","**Adequacy and scope of the auditor's power** — guaranteed authority to discharge his responsibilities.","**Expertise and professionalism** of the auditor and staff.","**Resources at the auditor's disposal** — sufficient funds to do the job.","**Freedom of reporting and quality of reports** — promptly acted on, per professional audit standards.","**Unrestricted access** to employees, property and records.","**Stakeholder support** — the audit's legitimacy understood and backed by officials, media and citizens."]}]},{"n":"23.3","t":"Types of audit","b":[{"p":"Public sector auditing systematically and objectively gathers and evaluates evidence to test whether information or actual conditions meet established criteria. There are **three main types**: compliance, financial and performance (value-for-money) audit."},{"h4":"Compliance audit"},{"p":"Assesses whether a chosen **subject matter** (e.g. procurement, non-financial reporting, freedom-of-information compliance, staff conduct) complies with **authorities** — parliamentary decisions, laws, legislative acts, codes and agreed terms — looking for material deviations from law/regulation or from **propriety** (sound financial management and conduct). It promotes transparency (reliable reports on fund administration and due process), accountability (reporting violations so corrective action follows) and good governance (identifying weaknesses and assessing fraud risk)."},{"h4":"Financial audit"},{"p":"Tests whether an entity's financial information is presented per the applicable financial reporting/regulatory framework, looking for **material** misstatements and errors. Per **ISSAI 1250 §10**, the objectives are: obtaining sufficient evidence of compliance with laws/regulations that directly affect material amounts and disclosures; performing procedures to identify non-compliance with other laws that could have a material effect; and responding appropriately to any (suspected) non-compliance found. Broader compliance responsibilities in the public sector are covered instead by **ISSAI 4000**."},{"h4":"Performance (value-for-money) audit"},{"p":"An independent, objective, reliable examination of whether government undertakings/systems/operations are run **economically, efficiently and effectively**, with recommendations for improvement. **Value for money (VFM)** — using resources in the best way to achieve intended objectives — rests on the **\"3 Es\"**:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["3 Es","Meaning","Measured by"],"rows":[["**Economy**","Doing it cheaply — not paying more than necessary for inputs","Money spent, compared with inputs acquired"],["**Efficiency**","Doing it well — the greatest output from the resources used","Inputs used, compared with output achieved"],["**Effectiveness**","Doing the right thing — achieving the intended objectives","Output achieved, compared with objectives"]]}},{"h4":"Steps in a value-for-money audit"},{"ol":["Initial analysis of the financial statements.","Review the management system.","Plan and control the engagement.","**Compliance test** — check approvals and authority limits.","**Substantive tests**, in three parts: **economy test** (oral interview, circularisation — are inputs bought at the cheapest price?); **efficiency assessment** (physical asset verification, third-party checks, review of computations/extensions, circularisation, oral interview, internal audit report review — is waste minimised?); and **effectiveness review** (circularisation, oral interview — is the policy adopted actually delivering?).","Write the report."]},{"h4":"Other recognised types of audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","What it is"],"rows":[["**Annual/statutory audit**","A yearly, statute-required examination of stewardship"],["**Ad-hoc/special audit**","A one-off assignment on special request — e.g. a suspected fraud"],["**Pre-payment audit**","Carried out before payment (e.g. before contractors are paid), to guard against unreasonable expenditure, confirm funds are available, ensure compliance with budgetary/civil-service/legal requirements, and confirm goods/services meet the prescribed standard"],["**Post-payment audit**","Carried out after payment, by internal or external auditors, to complement pre-payment audit"],["**Interim audit**","By the external auditor, during the earlier months of the year, reducing year-end workload and catching fraud/errors early"],["**Final audit**","After the year end, completing what the interim audit began"],["**Management audit**","A review of management's performance over a period (\"operational audit\")"],["**Operational/systems audit**","Focuses on the operational efficiency/effectiveness of management practice in delivering services"],["**Vouching audit**","Checks that supporting documents for a transaction are relevant and adequate, tracing receipts and other evidence to the ledgers"],["**Verification audit**","Confirms the existence and ownership of assets, by physical verification and evidence review"]]}}]},{"n":"23.4","t":"Steps in auditing government financial statements","b":[{"steps":["**Scheduling and planning** — the audit's objective, timing (agreed with management), team independence, programme and scope are settled up front (ISA 300). Good planning focuses effort on important areas, resolves problems early, organises the engagement efficiently, matches team members to risk, and coordinates work with component auditors/experts.","**Engagement letter** — notifies management of the pending audit, requesting preliminary documentation and setting out the terms of reference and scope (ISA 210). For government audits the terms come from statute; management must acknowledge its responsibility for the statements and for internal control, and give the auditor unrestricted access. A scope limitation the law does not permit should lead to a qualified opinion if not removed.","**Entrance conference** — a meeting with the department to discuss the audit's purpose and scope and hear the auditee's concerns.","**Regularity (financial) audit work** — attesting the financial accountability of the entity and of government as a whole; auditing financial systems/transactions for compliance; auditing internal control and internal audit; auditing the probity/propriety of administrative decisions; and reporting any other matter the Supreme Audit Institution considers should be disclosed.","**Fieldwork** — mostly done at the auditee's site: understanding the entity's activities, identifying risks and the controls that mitigate them, examining evidence, questioning staff, inspecting records and tangible assets, reviewing significant estimates, obtaining written confirmations (e.g. from debtors), and testing controls — to form a judgement on whether the statements give a true and fair view.","**Draft report** — issues and recommendations are discussed with management as the work proceeds; the draft is shared for review and comment, and management's response must include a corrective-action plan, who is responsible, and a completion timetable. Reports must be clear, unambiguous, evidence-based, independent, objective, fair and constructive; the Auditor-General decides finally what action follows any fraud or serious irregularity found.","**Exit conference** — a closing meeting (or informal exchange) with the auditee.","**Report distribution** — the final report goes to the Legislature and the Accountant-General and is published for public consumption."]},{"note":"**ISA** = **International Standards on Auditing** — the numbered standards referred to throughout this chapter. Two ISAs govern how findings are communicated: **ISA 260** (communication with those charged with governance — the audit's scope/timing, information needed from them, significant observations, and two-way dialogue) and **ISA 265** (communicating internal-control deficiencies — where a control is badly designed/operated, or missing, such that misstatements are not prevented or timely detected/corrected)."}]},{"n":"23.5","t":"The Auditor-General and the Public Accounts Committee (PAC)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Public Accounts Committee","d":"a body established by law to study and examine the Auditor-General's reports — especially on fraud or embezzlement of public funds — and make appropriate recommendations to the National Assembly. The Auditor-General's certification and findings go to the Assembly, where the PAC deliberates on them."}},{"p":"During a PAC session, the Auditor-General should:"},{"ul":["Guide members on the proper conduct of the session, and shed further light on issues raised.","Assist the Committee with the correct approach to problems, offering expert, independent, professional advice.","Help select areas needing less attention versus those needing deep probing, and identify weaknesses in financial administration.","Interrogate officials during the deliberation, if the chairman permits, and assess the answers they give.","Give a professional assessment of the acceptability/relevance of evidence, and assist (if allowed) in compiling the Committee's final report from the minutes of evidence."]}]},{"n":"23.6","t":"Internal audit","b":[{"def":{"t":"Internal audit","d":"an independent appraisal activity within an organisation, reviewing accounting, financial and other operations as a service to management. In government, audit certificates are issued before contractors and suppliers are paid."}},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["Determine the adequacy of the existing internal control system.","Investigate compliance with the Financial Memorandum, laws and Financial Regulations.","Check the adequacy of monthly activity returns.","Verify the physical existence of assets and liabilities."]},{"h4":"Typical scope"},{"p":"Pre-audit; vouching of payroll and third-party claims; auditing store movements and records; internal investigations/evaluations for management; and constant review of internal control measures — though the precise duties are at management's discretion."},{"h4":"Role in a democracy"},{"p":"The internal auditor renders continuous **exception reports** on government financial activity to the accounting officer, copied to the Auditor-General, supporting budget discipline through ongoing monitoring of receipts and payments — while the legislature retains power to question the Auditor-General on actions the executive has already taken with his endorsement."},{"h4":"Fostering the internal/external audit relationship"},{"ul":["Assign responsibility for maintaining an effective relationship with each audited body's internal audit unit.","Hold liaison meetings at least half-yearly, with a record of key decisions, and discuss audit plans jointly to avoid duplicated effort.","Review internal audit reports and working papers, and agree where external audit will place reliance on internal audit's (satisfactory) work as part of the certification audit.","Discuss any shortcomings revealed by evaluations, and share planning documents/working papers with internal audit in confidence where it must cooperate with external audit."]},{"h4":"Where internal audit can assist external audit"},{"ul":["Attending stock-takes and cash counts the external auditor cannot attend himself.","Conducting staff pay parades.","Contributing relevant specialist expertise.","A closer working relationship brings accounting/auditing issues found during internal work to the external auditor's attention sooner."]}]},{"n":"23.7","t":"Where specialists can assist the Auditor-General","b":[{"p":"An external auditor may need to rely on a specialist's work to form his opinion, for example:"},{"ul":["Valuation of assets — land, buildings, machinery, minerals.","Measuring work completed, in progress, or to be completed, on long-term contracts (architects, engineers).","Legal interpretation of an agreement or statute.","Physical stock-taking of specialised stores — e.g. drugs or chemicals.","Determining amounts by specialised technique — e.g. an actuarial valuation."]},{"p":"Before relying on an expert, the auditor considers: the engagement team's own knowledge and experience of the matter; the risk of material misstatement given its nature, complexity and materiality; the quantity/quality of other evidence expected; the expert's **professional competence**; and the expert's **objectivity** — which may be impaired if he is employed by, or related to, the auditee."}]},{"n":"23.8","t":"INTOSAI — code of ethics and basic postulates","b":[{"h4":"Code of ethics"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Principle","What it requires"],"rows":[["**Integrity**","High standards of behaviour — honesty and candour — in the auditor's work and dealings with audited-entity staff"],["**Independence, objectivity, impartiality**","No personal/external interest may impair independence; conclusions rest solely on evidence gathered per the SAI's own auditing standards"],["**Professional secrecy**","No disclosure of information obtained in auditing, except to meet the SAI's own statutory responsibilities"],["**Competence**","Never undertake work one is not competent to perform"]]}},{"h4":"Basic postulates for auditing standards"},{"ul":["The **Supreme Audit Institution (SAI)** — the Auditor-General — should apply INTOSAI standards wherever material, determining which standards fit non-audit work or Courts-of-Account-type SAIs.","The SAI exercises its own **judgement** across the diverse situations of government auditing.","Growing public accountability demands an effective accountability process.","Good information/control/evaluation/reporting systems within government support that process; **management** is responsible for the correctness and sufficiency of financial reports.","Authorities should promulgate acceptable accounting standards, and audited entities should set specific, measurable objectives and performance targets.","Consistent application of accounting standards yields fair presentation.","An adequate **internal control system** minimises errors/irregularities — it is the audited entity's job to build and maintain it; the auditor recommends improvements where controls are missing or weak.","Legislation should require audited entities to cooperate and give access to relevant data.","All audit activity must stay within the SAI's mandate.","SAIs should keep improving techniques for auditing the validity of performance measures, and avoid any conflict of interest with the audited entity."]}]},{"n":"23.9","t":"Codified offences and sanctions arising from audit queries (Financial Regulations 2009, ch.31)","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Audit query","Time to respond","Sanction"],"rows":[["Inflation of contracts","5 days","Accounting officer reported to the President; any other officer surcharged, removed from duty, dismissed and prosecuted; a Tenders Board involved is collectively and severally sanctioned"],["Unauthorised contract variation","21 days","Same as inflation of contracts"],["Payment to a contractor for a job not executed","30 days","Contractor completes the job or refunds the money; contractor blacklisted and reported to the EFCC"],["Payment on a false certificate of completion","21 days","Same as above"],["Payment for a job not executed, due to an officer's fraud","N/A","Officer refunds the money, is removed from duty and reported to the EFCC"],["Poor quality of work","42 days","Contractor rectifies the defect or refunds the money and is blacklisted/reported to the EFCC; the certifying officer is demoted"],["Irregular or wrong payment","21 days","Officer refunds the money and is removed from duty"],["Shortage/loss of stores by the storekeeper","14 days","Officer surcharged for the loss and removed from duty"],["Shortage/loss of cash by the cashier","7 days","Officer surcharged for the loss and removed from duty"],["Assets paid for but not supplied","21 days","Contractor blacklisted/reported to the EFCC; officer removed from duty and disciplined"],["Payment for ghost workers","N/A","Officer removed from duty, charged with misconduct and reported to the EFCC"],["Overpayment of salaries/allowances","21 days","Officer disciplined and reported to the Police"],["Failure to collect government revenue","21 days","Officer removed from duty and surcharged"],["No satisfactory reply on failure to account for revenue","7 days","Officer surcharged in full and reported to the EFCC/ICPC"],["Non-payment for use of government property","30 days","Officer surcharged in full and seriously warned"],["Non-rendition of returns","30 days","Officer surcharged for the resulting loss and seriously warned"],["Non-rendition of the monthly transcript","21 days","The MDA's allocation is suspended indefinitely"],["Non-retirement of an advance/imprest","21 days","Officer surcharged and the full amount recovered"],["Offences under the Public Procurement Act 2007","N/A","Not less than 5 years' imprisonment with no option of a fine; summary dismissal; debarment from public procurement for not less than 5 years; a fine of 25% of the procurement value"],["An organisation/officer paying by cash/cheque against the rules","N/A","The organisation's budget allocation is suspended; the officer is treated as having committed gross misconduct and disciplined"]]}},{"note":"[This response-time ladder, alongside every other numeric rule in the syllabus →](guides/ps-numbers.html) — years of service, thresholds and deadlines from all 23 chapters, in one place."}]},{"n":"23.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"h3":"Section A"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Questions 1–12 with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["The duties of the Auditor-General do NOT include (A) Financial audit  (B) Appropriation audit  (C) Direct audit of public quoted companies  (D) Value for money audit  (E) Financial control audit","Instituting a sound internal control system in the public sector is one of the functions of (A) Officer controlling expenditure  (B) Sub-accounting officer  (C) Role player  (D) Accounting officer  (E) Internal auditor","Effective audit can be made possible through the following EXCEPT (A) The independence of the auditor  (B) Adequacy and scope of the auditor's powers  (C) The expertise and professionalism of the auditor  (D) The academic qualifications of the auditor  (E) The resources at the auditor's disposal","The auditor is expected to consider the following when determining the use of an expert, EXCEPT (A) The engagement team's knowledge and previous experience of the matter  (B) The risk of material misstatement based on nature, complexity and materiality  (C) The quantity and quality of other audit evidence expected  (D) Evaluating the professional competence of the expert  (E) Ignoring the objectivity of the expert, which may be impaired if he is employed by or related to the auditee","The Auditor-General could rely on the internal auditor's work in the following circumstances, EXCEPT (A) The degree of independence of the internal auditor  (B) The scope and objectives of internal audit as defined by management  (C) Due professional care — whether internal audit work is properly planned, recorded and reviewed  (D) The technical competence of the internal auditor  (E) The quality and quantity of internal audit reports, and how far they are influenced by management, are of interest to the external auditor","The independent assessment of whether a particular subject matter is in compliance with applicable authorities identified as criteria, in auditing, is known as …","The officer who attests to the integrity and accuracy of financial statements at the end of the year is ….","… is an independent appraisal activity within an organisation for the review of accounting, financial and other operations as a basis for services to management.","That auditors shall have full, free and unrestricted access to all activities, records, property, infrastructure and personnel is contained in ….","… is a review of the performance of management during a period; it is synonymous with the investigation or performance review of management, otherwise called \"operational audit\".","The documentary evidence used by an auditor when conducting an audit, which can be tendered in court in case of litigation, is called ….","An examination of suspected irregular activities within an entity, public or private, to obtain evidence usable in support of court proceedings, is called …."]}],"a":[{"p":"**1.** C  **2.** E  **3.** D  **4.** E  **5.** E  **6.** Compliance audit  **7.** Auditor-General  **8.** Internal audit  **9.** Audit rules and regulations  **10.** Management audit  **11.** Audit working paper  **12.** Forensic audit"}]}},{"h3":"Section B"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 1 — audit objectives of treasury, agency and enterprise accounts","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Office of the Auditor-General for the Federation audits the accounts of all accounting officers and all persons entrusted with government money, stamps, securities, stores or other property, and certifies the annual accounts of government. State the audit objectives of (a) Treasury accounts; (b) Agency accounts; (c) Government enterprise accounts."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) Treasury accounts:** ensure money is received when due and properly accounted for; ensure the cash book and memorandum accounts are maintained; confirm compliance with the 2009 Financial Regulations and other relevant legislation; guard against misappropriation; ensure the safety of departmental assets; guarantee the accuracy of records; and confirm the existence and ownership of assets."},{"p":"**(b) Agency accounts:** ensure compliance with the legislation setting up the agency and with the accounting manual; minimise misappropriation; ensure the safety of the agency's assets; ensure records/returns are reliable; ensure payments and receipts follow the agency's trust deed; ensure returns are rendered when due; and confirm the existence and ownership of agency assets."},{"p":"**(c) Government enterprise accounts:** confirm compliance with the laws establishing the enterprise and with the accounting manual; ensure the safety of enterprise assets; ensure receipts/payments follow the approved budget; guard against misappropriation; and confirm the existence and ownership of assets."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 2 — testing a revenue collector and reviewing imprest accounts","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) State eight tests to carry out when auditing a revenue collector. (b) List seven internal-control questions for evaluating imprest accounts."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)**"},{"ol":["Review the system for revenue collection.","Review, and cast, the cash book.","Check the type of receipts issued, to confirm they are genuine.","Review and continue preparing the bank reconciliation.","Carry out a spot check on the revenue collector.","Review internal and external audit reports.","Confirm all money due has been collected and accounted for."]},{"p":"**(b)**"},{"ol":["Is a bank account opened for an imprest over ₦20,000?","Was the cash book properly kept?","Were vouchers properly kept and classified?","Was the imprest account correctly accounted for at the time of the check?","Was the LPO register kept, and cash disbursed for LPOs by the imprest holder?","Were receipts issued for unspent cash returned?","Was the reimbursement request checked, and were classifications made to the correct Head and Subhead of expenditure?"]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 3 — audit objectives and internal control","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(a) Differentiate between the primary and secondary objectives of a public sector audit. (b)(i) What is internal control? (ii) Outline its important features, with a brief note on each."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)** The **primary objective** is to enable the auditor to report whether the financial statements present a true and fair view of the entity's financial affairs. The **secondary objectives** are the detection of fraud, errors and omissions, and the prevention of fraud and errors."},{"p":"**(b)(i)** Internal control is the whole system of controls, financial or otherwise, established by management to run the entity in a way that protects its assets and places reliance on its records."},{"p":"**(b)(ii) Features:**"},{"ol":["**Segregation of duty** — dividing work between departments/people.","**Established organisation structure** — clearly defined duties and powers.","**Perusal of records** — checking earlier work and timely correction of anomalies.","**Acknowledgement of deed** — a signature/initial confirming work is done.","**Asset protection** — records/documents under lock and key, assets insured.","**Formality** — every store movement backed by an authorised, signed voucher.","**Verification** — purchased items checked for completeness, accuracy and specification.","**Internal audit** — continuous review of operations and records by specialist staff."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Question 4 — value-for-money audit","open":true,"q":[{"p":"(i) Define value-for-money (VFM) audit. (ii) Identify and explain its three major components. (iii) Explain five steps towards a successful VFM audit. (iv) Identify five factors contributing to an effective VFM audit. (v) Explain the precise roles of the internal audit unit in a Government Business Entity's VFM audit."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i)** VFM audit means using resources in the best way to achieve intended objectives — reviewing financial transactions to confirm the organisation received adequate benefit for the money spent."},{"p":"**(ii)** Economy (spending carefully, not overpaying for inputs); efficiency (the greatest output from the resources used); effectiveness (using resources to achieve the desired objectives)."},{"ol":["Initial analysis of project areas via the annual budget and financial statements.","Adequate planning, coordination and control — the annual programme, staff and resources needed, attention to important areas, and proper assignment/coordination of team work.","Introducing the main study to the auditee — its purpose, timetable, team, objectives/questions/criteria, data-collection methods, information needed from the auditee, the contact person, and the purpose of the exit meeting.","Compliance testing, to gauge the strength of internal control.","Substantive testing for economy, efficiency and effectiveness, then reporting (cleared through review, quality control and an exit meeting, then submitted to the legislature/government/auditee) and follow-up (documenting impact, the auditee's progress, and feedback to the auditor and legislature)."]},{"p":"**(iv)** The independence of the auditor; the adequacy/scope of his powers; his expertise and professionalism; the resources at his disposal; and freedom of reporting/quality of reports (also: unrestricted access, and stakeholder support)."},{"p":"**(v)** Guard against unreasonable or extravagant expenditure; ensure sufficient funds are available for payment; ensure compliance with budgetary, civil-service, financial-memorandum and other legal requirements; ensure goods/services meet prescribed standards before payment; and confirm whether the organisation has committed its resources economically, efficiently and effectively."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Section A tests definitions (compliance/forensic/management audit) and the Auditor-General's functions and limits. Section B most often asks for the objectives of a VFM audit and its 3 Es, or for the audit-query sanctions table — learn the sanctions by category (contractor default → blacklist/EFCC; officer default → surcharge/removal) rather than trying to memorise every time limit exactly.","errors":["Saying the Auditor-General directly audits private/publicly quoted companies; his remit is government accounts and the bodies the Constitution/statute names.","Treating \"efficiency\" and \"effectiveness\" as synonyms; efficiency is input-to-output, effectiveness is output-to-objective.","Confusing internal audit (management's own continuous review) with external/statutory audit (independent, reporting to the Legislature).","Giving management audit and operational audit as different things; they are the same audit under two names.","Missing that a sanction table entry usually has TWO tracks — one for the contractor/third party (blacklist, EFCC referral) and one for the officer (surcharge, removal, discipline)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Auditor-General for the Federation is established as external auditor of the Federation under which section of the 1999 Constitution?","o":["Section 80","Section 85","Section 162","Section 7","Section 4"],"a":1,"w":"Section 85 (section 125 for a State) makes the Auditor-General the external auditor of the Federation (or State).","src":"Chapter 23.1","sec":"23.1"},{"q":"Value-for-money audit rests on the \"3 Es\": economy, efficiency and","o":["equity","effectiveness","evaluation","ethics","expenditure"],"a":1,"w":"Economy (cheap inputs), efficiency (output per input) and effectiveness (achieving the objective).","src":"Chapter 23.3","sec":"23.3"},{"q":"An audit carried out before payment is made to a contractor, to check economy, efficiency and compliance, is called a","o":["post-payment audit","pre-payment audit","interim audit","final audit","management audit"],"a":1,"w":"Pre-payment audit is common in government service and guards against unreasonable or non-compliant expenditure before money leaves the treasury.","src":"Chapter 23.3","sec":"23.3"},{"q":"Under the INTOSAI code of ethics, an auditor who does not disclose information obtained during an audit to third parties (except to meet the SAI's own statutory duties) is observing","o":["independence","professional secrecy","competence","objectivity","neutrality"],"a":1,"w":"Professional secrecy restricts disclosure of audit information to what the SAI's own statutory responsibilities require.","src":"Chapter 23.8","sec":"23.8"},{"q":"Under the Financial Regulations' codified sanctions, a shortage or loss of cash by the cashier must be responded to within","o":["30 days","21 days","14 days","7 days","5 days"],"a":3,"w":"Cash losses by a cashier carry a 7-day response limit — the shortest in the table besides the false-reply case, reflecting how urgent a cash shortfall is treated.","src":"Chapter 23.9","sec":"23.9"},{"q":"A body established by law to examine the Auditor-General's reports, especially on fraud or embezzlement, and recommend action to the National Assembly, is the","o":["Code of Conduct Tribunal","Public Accounts Committee","Fiscal Responsibility Commission","Bureau of Public Procurement","Federal Losses Committee"],"a":1,"w":"The Public Accounts Committee deliberates on the Auditor-General's reports and reports its recommendations to the National Assembly.","src":"Chapter 23.5","sec":"23.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State the objectives of audit and five factors that contribute to an effective audit. (b) Distinguish compliance audit, financial audit and performance (value-for-money) audit. (c) Explain the relationship between the Auditor-General and the Public Accounts Committee. (d) State the objectives of internal audit and how it assists external audit.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Objectives of audit and factors for effectiveness"},{"p":"The general audit objectives are completeness, occurrence, measurement, regularity and disclosure — see the table in §23.2."},{"ol":["Independence of the auditor from management.","Adequacy and scope of the auditor's power.","The expertise and professionalism of the auditor and staff.","The resources at the auditor's disposal.","Freedom of reporting and unrestricted access to employees, property and records, backed by stakeholder support."]},{"h4":"(b) Compliance, financial and performance audit"},{"p":"**Compliance audit** tests whether a chosen subject matter follows applicable authorities (laws, codes, agreed terms). **Financial audit** tests whether financial information is presented per the applicable reporting framework, free of material misstatement. **Performance (value-for-money) audit** tests whether operations are run economically, efficiently and effectively, and recommends improvement — the only one of the three primarily forward-looking rather than an opinion on a past statement."},{"h4":"(c) The Auditor-General and the PAC"},{"p":"The PAC is established by law to study the Auditor-General's reports, especially on fraud/embezzlement, and recommend action to the National Assembly. The Auditor-General's certification and findings feed the PAC's deliberations; during PAC sessions he guides the Committee, clarifies issues, advises on approach, helps prioritise areas for scrutiny, may question officials if the chairman permits, assesses their answers, gives a professional view on the evidence, and may help compile the final report."},{"h4":"(d) Internal audit's objectives and support role"},{"p":"Internal audit determines the adequacy of internal control; checks compliance with the Financial Memorandum, laws and Financial Regulations; checks the adequacy of monthly returns; and verifies the physical existence of assets and liabilities. It supports external audit by attending stock-takes/cash counts the external auditor cannot attend, running pay parades, contributing specialist expertise, and — through regular liaison and agreed reliance on its work — letting the external auditor avoid duplicating procedures already performed competently."}],"src":"Chapter 23.1–23.9","sec":"23.1"}]}}]});
